/* Question bank for lab/java-backend-guide.html. One object per question. */
window.JAVA_GUIDE = [{"id":1,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Compare List.of() with new ArrayList<>(). When would you use each?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`List.of()` gives a compact, unmodifiable, null-rejecting list for constants and API return values; `new ArrayList<>()` is a mutable, null-tolerant working buffer you fill locally and then expose as an unmodifiable copy.","answer":["`List.of(...)` (Java 9+) returns an unmodifiable list: `add`, `remove`, `set` and `sort` throw `UnsupportedOperationException`. It also rejects `null` eagerly, so `List.of(a, null)` throws `NullPointerException` at creation, and even `contains(null)` throws. `new ArrayList<>()` is a resizable, array-backed list that accepts `null` and duplicates and is not thread-safe.","Declaring a field `final` does not make the list immutable; it only stops reassignment of the reference. Immutability comes from the list implementation. That matters at API boundaries: if a service returns its internal `ArrayList`, any caller can mutate your state, and if it stores a caller's list, the caller can change it later. Returning or storing `List.copyOf(...)` removes that whole class of aliasing bugs and makes the value safe to share across threads without locking, provided the elements are themselves immutable.","In practice: use `ArrayList` as a local accumulator inside a method (building a result, batching rows, sorting) and for JPA entity collections, which must be mutable. Use `List.of` for constants, test fixtures and small fixed lists, and `List.copyOf` to freeze a list at the edge (constructor, record compact constructor, return value). The production payoff is fail-fast behavior: an accidental write or a stray `null` blows up where the bug is, instead of corrupting shared state or leaking into a JSON response three layers later."],"keyPoints":["List.of is immutable and rejects null","ArrayList is mutable and allows null","immutable collections are safer at API boundaries and for constants"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable buffer inside, unmodifiable copy at the boundary","lang":"java","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic final class Order {\n    private final String orderId;\n    private final List<OrderLine> lines; // final reference only; the list decides mutability\n\n    public Order(String orderId, List<OrderLine> lines) {\n        this.orderId = orderId;\n        this.lines = List.copyOf(lines); // GOOD: snapshot, rejects null lines\n    }\n\n    public List<OrderLine> lines() {\n        return lines; // safe to hand out: callers cannot mutate it\n    }\n}\n\nclass OrderAssembler {\n    Order assemble(String orderId, List<CartItem> cart) {\n        List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>(cart.size()); // mutable local buffer\n        for (CartItem item : cart) {\n            if (item.quantity() > 0) {\n                lines.add(new OrderLine(item.sku(), item.quantity()));\n            }\n        }\n        return new Order(orderId, lines);\n    }\n}","note":"Build with a presized `ArrayList` inside the method, then freeze with `List.copyOf` in the constructor. A caller doing `order.lines().add(...)` gets an immediate `UnsupportedOperationException` instead of silently changing the order."},{"title":"Unmodifiable view versus real copy","lang":"java","code":"List<String> source = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"PENDING\", \"AUTHORIZED\"));\n\nList<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(source);\nList<String> copy = List.copyOf(source);\n\nsource.add(\"CAPTURED\");\n\nSystem.out.println(view);  // [PENDING, AUTHORIZED, CAPTURED]: the view sees the change\nSystem.out.println(copy);  // [PENDING, AUTHORIZED]: independent snapshot\n\nview.add(\"REFUNDED\");      // UnsupportedOperationException\nList.of(\"A\", null);        // NullPointerException at creation\nnew ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"A\", null)); // fine: ArrayList accepts null","note":"`Collections.unmodifiableList` only blocks writes through the wrapper; whoever holds `source` can still change what readers of `view` see. `List.copyOf` detaches completely."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How is List.of different from Collections.unmodifiableList()?","a":"`Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` is a read-only view that wraps and delegates to the original list: it blocks writes through the wrapper, but anyone holding the original reference can still modify it, and it allows `null`. `List.of` and `List.copyOf` create a new unmodifiable list with its own storage, reject `null`, and can never change afterwards. Use the view only when you deliberately want callers to observe live changes; use `List.copyOf` when you want a stable snapshot."},{"q":"What happens if the backing list changes?","a":"The unmodifiable view reflects the change immediately, because every read delegates to the backing list, so a list you returned as read-only can change size under the caller. If the backing list is structurally modified while someone iterates the view, the iterator can throw `ConcurrentModificationException`, and a concurrent writer without synchronization can cause stale or inconsistent reads. A list created by `List.of` or `List.copyOf` is unaffected because it holds no reference to the source."}],"extra":["**Stream.toList() vs Collectors.toList():** `stream.toList()` (Java 16) returns an unmodifiable list that still permits `null` elements, `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()` rejects nulls, and `Collectors.toList()` makes no mutability promise (it is an `ArrayList` today, but do not rely on it).","**Arrays.asList is a third behavior:** it is fixed-size and writes through to the backing array, so `set` works and changes the array, while `add` and `remove` throw `UnsupportedOperationException`.","**List.copyOf is cheap when already immutable:** passing a list created by `List.of` or `List.copyOf` returns the same instance without copying, so defensive copying at every layer costs almost nothing.","**Shallow only:** an unmodifiable list of mutable `Order` objects still lets callers mutate each order; real immutability needs immutable elements too (records with immutable components, `BigDecimal`, `String`, `Instant`).","**JPA collections must stay mutable:** initialize `@OneToMany` fields with `new ArrayList<>()` or `new HashSet<>()`, because your domain methods and Hibernate both mutate them; initialize with `List.of()` and the first `order.addLine(...)` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`."],"redFlags":["Thinks final makes a collection immutable","does not know List.of rejects null","recommends mutable lists by default"]},{"id":2,"category":"Java Collections","question":"Compare ArrayList and LinkedList for a backend service.","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Default to `ArrayList`: O(1) indexed access, compact contiguous storage and cache-friendly iteration; `LinkedList` pays a node object per element and O(n) access, and for queues or stacks `ArrayDeque` beats both.","answer":["`ArrayList` stores references in one contiguous `Object[]`. `get(i)` is O(1), appends are amortized O(1) (the array grows by about 50% when full), and iteration walks memory sequentially, which CPU caches and prefetchers love. Inserting or removing in the middle is O(n), but it is a single `System.arraycopy`, which is very fast for the sizes backend code usually handles.","`LinkedList` is a doubly linked list: every element lives in a separate `Node` with `item`, `next` and `prev`, roughly 24 extra bytes per element with compressed oops, scattered across the heap. `get(i)` is O(n) (it walks from the nearer end) and each hop is a likely cache miss. The famous O(1) insertion only holds once you already hold a position through a `ListIterator`; finding that position is O(n). So the claim that LinkedList is faster for inserts is usually false in practice, and the extra allocations add GC pressure.","For FIFO queues and stacks, use `ArrayDeque`: a resizable circular array with O(1) `offerLast`, `pollFirst`, `push` and `pop` and no per-element nodes. It replaces both `LinkedList` used as a queue and the legacy synchronized `Stack`. The production rule: default to `ArrayList`, presize it when the size is known, and reach for `LinkedList` only with a benchmark that proves it wins for your access pattern."],"keyPoints":["ArrayList has O(1) indexed access and good cache locality","LinkedList has O(n) access and object overhead","LinkedList is rarely preferable","ArrayDeque is usually better for queues"],"examples":[{"title":"Indexed loop turns quadratic on LinkedList","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: looks linear, is O(n^2) because get(i) walks the nodes every time\nList<Payment> payments = new LinkedList<>(repository.findSettledToday());\nBigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;\nfor (int i = 0; i < payments.size(); i++) {\n    total = total.add(payments.get(i).amount());\n}\n\n// GOOD: array-backed, presized copy, O(1) access and sequential memory\nList<Payment> settled = new ArrayList<>(repository.findSettledToday());\nBigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;\nfor (Payment p : settled) {\n    sum = sum.add(p.amount());\n}","note":"With a six-figure list the BAD loop performs billions of pointer hops, and it often hides behind a method that accepts `List` and uses an index loop. Code against `List`, but never assume `get(i)` is cheap unless the list implements `RandomAccess`."},{"title":"ArrayDeque as a retry queue and a stack","lang":"java","code":"Deque<RetryTask> retryQueue = new ArrayDeque<>(); // GOOD: circular array, no nodes\n\nretryQueue.offerLast(new RetryTask(\"pay-1001\", 1));\nretryQueue.offerLast(new RetryTask(\"pay-1002\", 1));\n\nRetryTask next;\nwhile ((next = retryQueue.pollFirst()) != null) {\n    boolean ok = gateway.retry(next.paymentId());\n    if (!ok && next.attempt() < 3) {\n        retryQueue.offerLast(next.nextAttempt());\n    }\n}\n\nDeque<String> steps = new ArrayDeque<>(); // GOOD: replaces legacy java.util.Stack\nsteps.push(\"validate\");\nsteps.push(\"authorize\");\nString top = steps.pop(); // \"authorize\"","note":"`ArrayDeque` rejects `null` (null means empty for `poll`) and is not thread-safe, which is exactly right for single-threaded work queues and stacks."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When can LinkedList actually be useful?","a":"When you already hold a `ListIterator` positioned in a long list and perform many insertions or removals at that position while traversing, since each `add` or `remove` through the iterator is O(1) with no shifting. It is also the only standard `List` that is simultaneously a `Deque` and accepts `null` elements. Even then, measure: for most sizes an `ArrayList` with `removeIf` or a rebuilt list, or an `ArrayDeque`, still wins because of cache locality."},{"q":"Why is ArrayDeque usually faster for queue operations?","a":"`ArrayDeque` keeps elements in a contiguous circular array and only moves head and tail indices, so `offer` and `poll` are a couple of array writes with no allocation. `LinkedList` allocates a `Node` on every `offer`, links three references, and turns every `poll` into garbage, which costs allocation, GC work and cache misses. The array only grows occasionally (amortized O(1)), and unlike `Stack` it takes no lock on every call."}],"extra":["**Memory math:** a million-element `ArrayList` holds about 4 MB of references (compressed oops) in one array, while a `LinkedList` adds a million 24-byte `Node` objects on top, roughly 24 MB of extra heap and a million more objects for the GC to trace.","**Java 21 sequenced collections:** `List` now has `getFirst`, `getLast`, `addFirst` and `removeFirst`; on `ArrayList`, `addFirst` and `removeFirst` are O(n) shifts, so seeing them in a hot loop is a hint you actually want an `ArrayDeque`.","**Presizing:** `new ArrayList<>(expectedSize)` avoids repeated grow and copy cycles when you know the batch size (for example a page of 500 rows), and `trimToSize()` releases slack on long-lived lists.","**Bulk removal:** removing many elements with `iterator.remove()` on an `ArrayList` is O(n^2) because of repeated shifting; `removeIf` compacts the array in one pass, which removes the main argument people make for `LinkedList`.","**Concurrent queues are a different choice:** across threads use `ArrayBlockingQueue` (bounded, gives backpressure), `LinkedBlockingQueue` (optionally bounded, one node per element) or `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` (lock-free, unbounded); unbounded queues under sustained load are a classic cause of `OutOfMemoryError`."],"redFlags":["Claims LinkedList is generally faster for insertion without considering traversal","ignores memory and cache locality"]},{"id":3,"category":"Java Collections","question":"How does HashMap handle collisions, and what can make lookups unexpectedly slow?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`HashMap` picks a bucket from the key's spread `hashCode()` and finds the key with `equals()`; collisions chain into a list that may treeify, and bad hashes, mutable keys or expensive equality degrade lookups.","answer":["On `put` and `get`, `HashMap` computes `h ^ (h >>> 16)` from `hashCode()` to mix high bits into low bits, then indexes the table with `(n-1) & hash`; the table size is a power of two (default 16) and doubles when size exceeds capacity times the load factor (0.75). Inside the bucket it compares the cached hash first, then `==` or `equals()`. So `hashCode` only chooses the bucket and `equals` decides whether the key matches. Hash codes are not unique: unequal keys sharing a bucket is normal.","Colliding entries chain as a linked list. Since Java 8, when one bucket reaches 8 entries and the table has at least 64 slots, the chain becomes a red-black tree of `TreeNode`s, so worst-case lookup is O(log n) instead of O(n); on smaller tables the map resizes instead. Trees order by hash, then by `compareTo` when keys are mutually `Comparable`, so non-comparable keys with identical hashes still degrade toward a linear search.","What makes lookups slow in production: a weak `hashCode` (constant, or built from a low-cardinality field like country or status) that funnels keys into few buckets; expensive `hashCode` or `equals` computed on every lookup; repeated resizing when the map is not presized; and adversarial keys crafted to collide when keys come from user input. Mutable keys are worse than slow: if a hashed field changes after insertion, the entry becomes unreachable. The fix is immutable keys with cheap, well-distributed hashes, typically records, `String` or `Long` ids."],"keyPoints":["hashCode selects a bucket, equals finds the key","collisions form a list and may treeify in Java 8+","poor hash distribution, mutable keys and many collisions degrade performance"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable key makes an entry unreachable","lang":"java","code":"final class AccountKey {\n    private String iban; // BAD: mutable field that drives hashCode\n\n    AccountKey(String iban) { this.iban = iban; }\n    void setIban(String iban) { this.iban = iban; }\n\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof AccountKey k && iban.equals(k.iban);\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return iban.hashCode(); }\n}\n\nrecord AccountId(String iban) { } // GOOD: immutable, equals and hashCode generated\n\nclass BalanceCacheDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<AccountKey, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>();\n        AccountKey key = new AccountKey(\"SE3550000000054910000003\");\n        balances.put(key, new BigDecimal(\"100.00\"));\n\n        key.setIban(\"SE4550000000058398257466\"); // hash changes, entry stays in the old bucket\n\n        System.out.println(balances.get(key));         // null\n        System.out.println(balances.containsKey(key)); // false\n        System.out.println(balances.size());           // 1: unreachable entry, a slow leak\n    }\n}","note":"Each node caches the hash computed at insertion, so even if the new hash lands in the same bucket, the cached hash no longer matches and the lookup misses."},{"title":"Poor distribution and presizing","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: all merchants in one country share a hash, so a few buckets hold thousands of keys\nrecord MerchantKey(String merchantId, String country) {\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return country.hashCode(); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: the generated hashCode combines all components\nrecord MerchantRef(String merchantId, String country) { }\n\nclass MerchantRegistry {\n    // GOOD: sized for the expected number of mappings, no resize while loading (Java 19+)\n    private final Map<MerchantRef, MerchantConfig> configs = HashMap.newHashMap(50_000);\n\n    void load(List<MerchantConfig> all) {\n        for (MerchantConfig c : all) {\n            configs.put(new MerchantRef(c.merchantId(), c.country()), c);\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD key still honors the contract (equal keys have equal hashes), which is why this bug survives code review: correctness is fine, only latency and CPU degrade as the map grows."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if a key's hashCode changes after insertion?","a":"The entry stays in the bucket chosen by the old hash, and the node also caches that old hash value. A later `get`, `containsKey` or `remove` with the same, now mutated, object computes the new hash, looks in a different bucket or fails the cached hash comparison, and misses. The result is an unreachable entry: `get` returns `null`, `size()` still counts it, iteration still shows it, and you can `put` a logical duplicate, which is a quiet memory leak in caches."},{"q":"When does a bucket become a tree?","a":"In Java 8 and later, when adding an entry makes a bucket's chain reach `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD` (8) and the table has at least `MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY` (64) buckets; otherwise the map resizes instead. The bucket becomes a red-black tree of `TreeNode`s ordered by hash, then by `compareTo` for comparable keys, then by a tie-breaker. During a resize, a split bucket with 6 or fewer entries is converted back into a plain list."}],"extra":["**Treeify numbers:** `TREEIFY_THRESHOLD` is 8, `UNTREEIFY_THRESHOLD` is 6 (a split bin shrinks back to a list during resize) and `MIN_TREEIFY_CAPACITY` is 64; below 64 slots a long chain triggers a resize instead of a tree.","**Hash flooding:** strings like `\"Aa\"` and `\"BB\"` share a hash code, so an attacker can generate many colliding keys in request parameters or JSON field names; treeification limits the damage to O(log n) because `String` is `Comparable`, but capping input size is still your job.","**Presizing correctly:** `new HashMap<>(n)` takes a capacity, not an expected size, so it still resizes at 75% load; `HashMap.newHashMap(expectedMappings)` (Java 19+) computes the right capacity for you.","**Resize cost:** a resize allocates a doubled table and splits each bucket into a low and high half, which is O(n); a large map built without presizing on a hot path shows up as latency spikes and allocation churn.","**Hash caching:** `String` caches its hash after the first computation, so repeated lookups are cheap, whereas a record key containing a `List` recomputes the whole list hash on every `get`."],"redFlags":["Believes hashCode is unique","omits equals","cannot explain mutable-key bugs or treeification"]},{"id":4,"category":"Java Collections","question":"ConcurrentHashMap vs Collections.synchronizedMap: which one scales better and why?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`Collections.synchronizedMap` puts one lock around every call so all threads serialize; `ConcurrentHashMap` locks only single bins on writes, reads without locking, and offers atomic `compute`, `merge` and `putIfAbsent` for compound updates.","answer":["`Collections.synchronizedMap(map)` wraps every method in `synchronized` on a single mutex. Each call is thread-safe, but every `get` and `put` from every thread queues on that one lock, so throughput flattens or even drops as you add cores. Iteration must be wrapped manually in `synchronized (map) { ... }` or you risk `ConcurrentModificationException`, and every check-then-act sequence needs that same external lock.","`ConcurrentHashMap` (Java 8+) has no global lock. Inserting into an empty bin is a CAS; updating a non-empty bin synchronizes only on that bin's first node; `get` takes no lock at all and relies on volatile reads, so readers never block writers or each other. Resizing is cooperative, with writer threads helping transfer bins, and the size is tracked with striped counters. Iterators are weakly consistent: they never throw `ConcurrentModificationException` but may or may not reflect concurrent updates.","Thread-safe single operations do not make compound logic atomic: `if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)` is a race in either map. Use `putIfAbsent`, `computeIfAbsent`, `compute`, `merge` or `replace(key, expected, newValue)`, which are atomic per key. Keep the remapping functions short and free of side effects: they run while holding the bin lock, and modifying the same map from inside them is forbidden (it can deadlock or throw `IllegalStateException`). Also, `ConcurrentHashMap` rejects `null` keys and values, which breaks code ported from `HashMap` that used `null` as a marker."],"keyPoints":["synchronizedMap serializes operations with one lock","ConcurrentHashMap uses finer-grained coordination and mostly non-blocking reads","compound operations should use compute/merge/putIfAbsent"],"examples":[{"title":"Check-then-act race versus computeIfAbsent","lang":"java","code":"private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, MerchantThrottle> throttles = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n// BAD: two threads can both see \"absent\" and create two throttles\nMerchantThrottle throttleBad(String merchantId) {\n    MerchantThrottle throttle = throttles.get(merchantId);\n    if (throttle == null) {\n        throttle = MerchantThrottle.forMerchant(merchantId);\n        throttles.put(merchantId, throttle); // the second put overwrites the first\n    }\n    return throttle;\n}\n\n// GOOD: created at most once per key, atomically\nMerchantThrottle throttle(String merchantId) {\n    return throttles.computeIfAbsent(merchantId, MerchantThrottle::forMerchant);\n}","note":"In the BAD version two requests for the same merchant can end up with different throttle instances, so the rate limit is silently doubled. `computeIfAbsent` guarantees one instance per key."},{"title":"Atomic per-key counters","lang":"java","code":"private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Long> declinesByReason = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\nprivate final ConcurrentHashMap<String, LongAdder> requestsByMerchant = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\nvoid recordDecline(String reasonCode) {\n    declinesByReason.merge(reasonCode, 1L, Long::sum); // atomic read, modify, write per key\n}\n\nvoid recordRequest(String merchantId) {\n    requestsByMerchant.computeIfAbsent(merchantId, id -> new LongAdder())\n                      .increment();                  // scales for very hot keys\n}\n\nlong requests(String merchantId) {\n    LongAdder adder = requestsByMerchant.get(merchantId);\n    return adder == null ? 0 : adder.sum();\n}\n\n// BAD: lost updates, two threads read 5 and both write 6\n// declinesByReason.put(code, declinesByReason.getOrDefault(code, 0L) + 1);","note":"`merge` is enough for moderate traffic; `LongAdder` spreads increments over internal cells so a hot key does not serialize on one bin lock, at the cost of a slightly more expensive `sum()`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is get() locked?","a":"No. `get` reads the table and node fields with volatile (acquire) semantics and never takes a lock, so it returns the most recently completed update for that key, and a completed `put` happens-before a `get` that observes it. During a resize, `get` follows forwarding nodes into the new table, and in treeified bins readers walk the linked nodes instead of blocking when a writer holds the tree."},{"q":"Why are null values forbidden?","a":"Because `null` would be ambiguous: `get` returning `null` could mean absent or mapped to null, and in a concurrent map you cannot resolve that with a follow-up `containsKey`, since another thread may change the map between the two calls. Forbidding `null` keeps `get`, `putIfAbsent`, `computeIfAbsent` and `merge` unambiguous, because a `null` result always means no mapping. If you need a known-missing marker, store an explicit sentinel value object instead."},{"q":"How would you implement an atomic counter update?","a":"Use `counts.merge(key, 1L, Long::sum)`, which atomically inserts 1 or adds to the existing value for that key. For very hot keys, store a `LongAdder` via `counts.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new LongAdder()).increment()`, which spreads contention across cells and is read with `sum()`. Never write `put(key, get(key) + 1)`: two threads read the same value and one increment is lost."}],"extra":["**Java 7 versus 8 internals:** Java 7 split the map into `Segment` locks (16 by default, the `concurrencyLevel`); Java 8 replaced segments with per-bin locking plus CAS, and `concurrencyLevel` survives only as a sizing hint.","**size() is an estimate under load:** with concurrent writers, `size()` and `isEmpty()` are momentary snapshots; `mappingCount()` returns a `long` and is preferred for large maps, and neither should drive correctness decisions.","**Not a loading cache:** the `computeIfAbsent` function blocks other writers to that bin (including unrelated keys that collide with it), so a slow HTTP or database call inside it serializes traffic; for loading caches with expiry use Caffeine, which is built on these primitives.","**Concurrent sets and bulk operations:** `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` gives a scalable concurrent `Set`, and `forEach`, `reduceValues` and `search` with a `parallelismThreshold` argument run bulk operations safely while the map is being updated.","**Treeified bins exist here too:** heavily colliding bins convert to a balanced `TreeBin` at the same threshold of 8, so bad key hashes cost O(log n) rather than O(n), but they still concentrate lock contention on that bin."],"redFlags":["Treats both as equivalent","performs check-then-act using separate get and put","expects null keys or values"]},{"id":5,"category":"Java Collections","question":"When is CopyOnWriteArrayList appropriate, and when is it dangerous?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"`CopyOnWriteArrayList` gives lock-free reads and snapshot iterators by copying the whole array on every write, so it shines for small, read-mostly lists like listeners and becomes a CPU and memory hog under frequent writes.","answer":["Every mutation (`add`, `set`, `remove`) takes a lock, copies the entire backing array, applies the change and publishes the new array through a volatile field. Reads and iteration take no lock; they simply read the current array. An iterator captures the array at creation, so it never throws `ConcurrentModificationException`, never blocks writers and never sees later changes; `iterator.remove()` is unsupported.","That makes it ideal for small collections that are read constantly and written rarely: event listeners, interceptor or filter chains, a list of downstream endpoints refreshed from configuration, subscribers registered at startup. Readers on hot paths pay no synchronization cost, and you can iterate while other threads register or remove listeners without extra locking.","It becomes dangerous when writes are frequent or the list is large. Each write is an O(n) copy plus a new array allocation, so appending n elements one by one is quadratic work and garbage; with thousands of elements and steady writes you get allocation churn and GC pressure, and writers serialize on one lock. Snapshot semantics can surprise you too: an iteration in progress keeps calling a listener that was just removed, and a reader holding an old snapshot may act on stale data. Use it as a registry, never as a buffer, queue or growing log."],"keyPoints":["Excellent for small read-heavy collections with rare writes and snapshot iteration","each write copies the array, causing CPU and memory cost"],"examples":[{"title":"Listener registry: the intended use","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class PaymentEventPublisher {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(PaymentEventPublisher.class);\n\n    private final CopyOnWriteArrayList<PaymentListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\n    public void register(PaymentListener listener) {  // rare: startup or plugin reload\n        listeners.addIfAbsent(listener);\n    }\n\n    public void unregister(PaymentListener listener) {\n        listeners.remove(listener);\n    }\n\n    public void publish(PaymentSettled event) {        // hot path: every settled payment\n        for (PaymentListener listener : listeners) {   // snapshot: no lock, no CME\n            try {\n                listener.onSettled(event);\n            } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n                log.warn(\"Listener {} failed for {}\", listener, event.paymentId(), e);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Writes happen a handful of times per deployment while `publish` runs thousands of times per second, which is exactly the ratio copy-on-write is designed for."},{"title":"Write-heavy misuse and a better structure","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one full array copy per request; at 10k entries every add copies 10k references\nprivate final List<UsageEvent> usageBuffer = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\nvoid onRequest(UsageEvent event) {\n    usageBuffer.add(event);\n}\n\n// GOOD: bounded queue for concurrent producers, drained in batches\nprivate final BlockingQueue<UsageEvent> usageQueue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(50_000);\n\nvoid onRequestQueued(UsageEvent event) {\n    if (!usageQueue.offer(event)) {\n        droppedEvents.increment(); // explicit backpressure instead of unbounded growth\n    }\n}\n\n@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 1000)\nvoid flush() {\n    List<UsageEvent> batch = new ArrayList<>(500);\n    usageQueue.drainTo(batch, 500);\n    if (!batch.isEmpty()) {\n        usageRepository.saveAll(batch);\n    }\n}","note":"The queue gives O(1) inserts, a bounded memory footprint and batch draining, while the copy-on-write buffer degrades quadratically as the buffer grows."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Give a production use case. What alternatives would you consider for frequent writes?","a":"A classic use case is a registry of `PaymentListener`s or HTTP interceptors: registered at startup or on a rare reload, iterated on every request, so reads outnumber writes by orders of magnitude and iteration must never block. For frequent writes, choose by access pattern: `ConcurrentHashMap` or `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` for keyed membership; `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` or a bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue` for producer and consumer buffers; an `ArrayList` guarded by a `ReentrantReadWriteLock` (or `Collections.synchronizedList` with explicit locking during iteration) when you need list semantics with moderate writes; and an immutable list swapped through an `AtomicReference` when updates arrive as whole batches."}],"extra":["**Batch your writes:** `addAll`, `addAllAbsent` and `removeIf` copy the array once for the whole batch, so rebuilding a listener list from configuration should be one bulk call, not a loop of `add`.","**CopyOnWriteArraySet:** it is backed by a `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, so `contains` and `add` are linear scans; fine for a dozen listeners, wrong for thousands of ids.","**Humongous allocations:** in G1, an array larger than half a region is allocated as a humongous object, so copying a large array on every write can trigger frequent humongous allocations and extra GC cycles.","**Immutable snapshot alternative:** for configuration that is replaced wholesale, a `volatile List<Route>` or `AtomicReference<List<Route>>` holding a `List.copyOf(...)` gives the same lock-free reads with one explicit swap and no per-element write API to misuse.","**Only references are snapshotted:** if the elements are mutable objects, concurrent changes to their fields are not protected by the list at all."],"redFlags":["Uses it for write-heavy workloads","ignores memory amplification and stale iterators"]},{"id":6,"category":"Java Core","question":"What is the difference between == and equals()?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`==` compares primitive values or object references (identity), while `equals()` expresses logical equality defined by the class; value types must override `equals` and `hashCode` together.","answer":["For primitives, `==` compares values (with numeric promotion, so `1 == 1.0` is true). For references, `==` asks whether both variables point to the same object. `equals()` is a method: `Object.equals` defaults to identity, while `String`, `Integer`, `LocalDate`, `UUID` and every record override it to compare content. Two distinct `String` objects with the same characters are therefore `equals` but usually not `==`.","The nasty production bugs come from `==` appearing to work. String literals are interned, so `==` passes in unit tests but fails for strings read from JSON or the database. Autoboxing caches `Integer`, `Long` and friends in the range -128 to 127, so comparing two boxed `Long` ids with `==` works for small test ids and fails for real ones. And `==` between a wrapper and a primitive unboxes, throwing `NullPointerException` when the wrapper is null.","When you define a value type (`Money`, `AccountId`, an order line key) you override `equals` and `hashCode` together, because hash-based collections use `hashCode` to find the bucket and `equals` to confirm the match; records do this for you. Use `==` deliberately only for identity, primitives and enums (where it is null-safe and preferred), and `Objects.equals(a, b)` for null-safe logical comparison."],"keyPoints":["== compares primitive values or object references","equals defines logical equality","value objects should override equals and hashCode together"],"examples":[{"title":"Boxed ids, strings and BigDecimal: == passes in tests, fails in production","lang":"java","code":"Long testId = 42L;\nLong sameTestId = 42L;\nSystem.out.println(testId == sameTestId);        // true: both come from the Long cache\n\nLong orderId = 1_000_000L;\nLong lookedUpId = 1_000_000L;\nSystem.out.println(orderId == lookedUpId);       // false: two objects (BAD)\nSystem.out.println(orderId.equals(lookedUpId));  // true (GOOD)\n\nString status = new String(\"SETTLED\");           // like a value read from a DB row\nSystem.out.println(status == \"SETTLED\");         // false: different objects (BAD)\nSystem.out.println(\"SETTLED\".equals(status));    // true, and null-safe in this order (GOOD)\n\nBigDecimal a = new BigDecimal(\"10.0\");\nBigDecimal b = new BigDecimal(\"10.00\");\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));                 // false: equals includes scale\nSystem.out.println(a.compareTo(b) == 0);         // true: numeric comparison\n\nSystem.out.println(Long.valueOf(7).equals(7));   // false: 7 autoboxes to Integer","note":"Every line compiles and most would pass a test with small ids and literals. The last two lines are why money and id comparisons need `compareTo` and consistent id types."},{"title":"equals without hashCode breaks HashSet","lang":"java","code":"final class Money {\n    private final BigDecimal amount;\n    private final String currency;\n\n    Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {\n        this.amount = amount;\n        this.currency = currency;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Money m\n                && amount.compareTo(m.amount) == 0\n                && currency.equals(m.currency);\n    }\n    // BAD: hashCode not overridden, equal Money objects get identity hash codes\n    // FIX: return Objects.hash(amount.stripTrailingZeros(), currency);\n}\n\nclass MoneyDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Money> seen = new HashSet<>();\n        seen.add(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"));\n        System.out.println(seen.contains(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"))); // almost always false\n        seen.add(new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\"));\n        System.out.println(seen.size()); // 2: logical duplicate stored\n    }\n}","note":"The fix hashes `amount.stripTrailingZeros()`, not `amount`, because `equals` uses `compareTo` and treats 9.99 and 9.990 as equal while `BigDecimal.hashCode` does not; hashing must follow whatever notion of equality `equals` uses."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens when equals is overridden but hashCode is not?","a":"Two logically equal objects keep `Object`'s identity-based hash codes, so they almost always land in different buckets. `HashMap.get` and `HashSet.contains` then fail to find an equal key, and `add` or `put` store duplicates, because `equals` is only consulted for entries in the same bucket with the same hash. It violates the rule that equal objects must have equal hash codes, which is why IDEs, static analysis and records exist to prevent it."},{"q":"Give an example using HashSet.","a":"With `Money` overriding only `equals`, adding `new Money(new BigDecimal(\"9.99\"), \"SEK\")` to a `HashSet` and then calling `contains` with another identical instance returns false, and a second `add` makes `size()` equal 2. Add a consistent `hashCode` (for example `Objects.hash(amount.stripTrailingZeros(), currency)`) or turn the class into a record, and `contains` returns true while the duplicate `add` returns false."}],"extra":["**Integer cache is tunable:** the upper bound of the `Integer` cache can be raised with `-XX:AutoBoxCacheMax`, so `==` on boxed values can even behave differently between environments; never rely on it.","**Enums and ==:** for enums, `==` is preferred: there is exactly one instance per constant, it is null-safe, and comparing an enum with an unrelated type fails at compile time instead of quietly returning false.","**Cross-type equals:** `Long.valueOf(7).equals(7)` is false, so `map.get(intId)` on a `Map<Long, Order>` silently returns `null`; keep id types consistent or wrap them in records like `OrderId`.","**BigDecimal in value objects:** `equals` is scale-sensitive (10.0 versus 10.00), which bites money comparisons and `HashSet<BigDecimal>`; compare with `compareTo` or normalize the scale once in the value object's constructor.","**Objects.equals:** it handles nulls on both sides and is the right building block for comparing fields inside your own `equals` implementations."],"redFlags":["Says both compare content","forgets reference equality or the hashCode contract"]},{"id":7,"category":"Java Core","question":"Explain the equals/hashCode contract and how violating it breaks HashMap or HashSet.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"If `a.equals(b)` then `a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()` must hold; unequal objects may collide, but breaking the rule, or changing hashed fields after insertion, makes hash collections miss entries and store duplicates.","answer":["The contract has two halves. `equals` must be reflexive (`x.equals(x)`), symmetric (`x.equals(y)` exactly when `y.equals(x)`), transitive, consistent across repeated calls while the objects do not change, and return false for `null`. `hashCode` must stay consistent while the fields used by `equals` do not change, and equal objects must return equal hash codes. The reverse is not required: unequal objects may share a hash code, and such collisions only cost performance.","To see why it matters, follow a `HashMap` lookup: hash the key, pick a bucket, compare the stored hash, then call `equals` on candidates in that bucket. If equal objects produce different hashes, the lookup goes to the wrong bucket and `equals` is never called: `get` returns `null`, `HashSet.add` accepts a duplicate and `remove` does nothing. If `equals` is not symmetric or transitive, results depend on which object is the argument and on insertion order, producing flaky, data-dependent bugs.","Practical rules: compute both methods from the same immutable fields; prefer `final` classes or records so subclasses cannot break symmetry; use `instanceof` pattern matching for the type check (or `getClass()` when subclasses must never equal their parent); and never mutate a field that feeds `hashCode` while the object sits in a hash-based collection, because it becomes unreachable in its old bucket."],"keyPoints":["Equal objects must have equal hash codes","equals should be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent and null-safe","unequal objects may share a hash"],"examples":[{"title":"Broken symmetry versus a normalized record","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: tries to be equal to plain Strings, which breaks symmetry\nfinal class CurrencyCode {\n    private final String code;\n\n    CurrencyCode(String code) { this.code = code; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (o instanceof CurrencyCode c) return code.equalsIgnoreCase(c.code);\n        if (o instanceof String s) return code.equalsIgnoreCase(s); // one-way equality\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() { return code.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT).hashCode(); }\n}\n\n// new CurrencyCode(\"sek\").equals(\"SEK\")  is true\n// \"SEK\".equals(new CurrencyCode(\"sek\"))  is false: symmetry broken, so\n// list.contains(x) and set lookups depend on which side calls equals\n\n// GOOD: normalize once, compare only with the same type\nrecord CurrencyId(String code) {\n    CurrencyId {\n        code = code.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);\n    }\n}","note":"Trying to be clever across types is the most common way to break symmetry. Normalizing in a compact constructor gives the same case-insensitive behavior with generated, contract-safe `equals` and `hashCode`."},{"title":"Mutating a hashed field after insertion","lang":"java","code":"class Customer {\n    private String email; // BAD: mutable and used in equals and hashCode\n\n    Customer(String email) { this.email = email; }\n    void changeEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }\n\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        return o instanceof Customer c && email.equals(c.email);\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return email.hashCode(); }\n}\n\nclass NotificationDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Customer> notified = new HashSet<>();\n        Customer c = new Customer(\"ana@example.com\");\n        notified.add(c);\n        c.changeEmail(\"ana.b@example.com\");\n        System.out.println(notified.contains(c)); // false: searched under the new hash\n        System.out.println(notified.add(c));      // true: same object stored twice\n        System.out.println(notified.size());      // 2\n    }\n}","note":"`remove(c)` now only removes the copy under the new hash; the old one leaks forever. Base equality on immutable identity (a customer id), not on editable attributes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can two unequal objects have the same hashCode?","a":"Yes, and it is unavoidable: `hashCode` returns an `int`, so there are only about 4 billion values for an unbounded number of objects. For example, the strings `\"Aa\"` and `\"BB\"` both hash to 2112. The contract only requires equal objects to have equal hashes; collisions among unequal objects are resolved by `equals` inside the bucket and only hurt performance when they are frequent."},{"q":"What bug appears when a field used in hashCode changes?","a":"The object stays in the bucket chosen by its old hash, so `contains`, `get` and `remove` look in the new hash's bucket and miss it. You get phantom entries that still count in `size()` and show up in iteration, and a later `add` stores the same object a second time. In caches this appears as a slow memory leak plus stale data; the fix is to hash only immutable fields, or to remove, mutate and reinsert."}],"extra":["**Consistency with compareTo:** `TreeSet` and `TreeMap` use `compareTo`, not `equals`, so `BigDecimal` values 1.0 and 1.00 are two elements in a `HashSet` but one in a `TreeSet`; keep `compareTo` consistent with `equals` or document the difference.","**Records and arrays:** a record's generated `equals` compares array components by reference, so `record Batch(byte[] payload)` is almost never equal to another instance; use a `List`, or override both methods with `Arrays.equals` and `Arrays.hashCode`.","**Inheritance trap:** a subclass that adds a field to `equals` cannot keep both symmetry and transitivity with a parent that uses `instanceof`; prefer composition over inheritance for value types.","**Cost of Objects.hash:** `Objects.hash(a, b, c)` allocates a varargs array per call; that is fine in general, but for keys hashed on very hot paths a hand-written `31 * h + field.hashCode()` avoids relying on escape analysis.","**Test it once, properly:** the EqualsVerifier library checks symmetry, transitivity, null handling and hashCode consistency in a single assertion and catches most hand-written mistakes."],"redFlags":["Claims hash codes must be unique","cannot describe lookup flow or duplicate behavior"]},{"id":8,"category":"Java Core","question":"Should a JPA entity use its generated database ID in equals/hashCode?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Not naively: a generated id is null until persist and then changes, so a `hashCode` built on it corrupts hash collections; prefer an immutable business key or assigned UUID, or id-based `equals` with a constant `hashCode`.","answer":["With `@GeneratedValue`, a new entity has `id == null` until `persist` (for `IDENTITY`, until the insert runs). If `hashCode` is derived from the id, its value changes during the entity's lifecycle, so any `HashSet` or `HashMap` that already holds the entity, including the `Set` behind a `@OneToMany`, now has it in the wrong bucket. And if `equals` treats two null ids as equal, two different new order lines collapse into one set element before they are saved.","Three approaches work. First, a stable business key set at construction and never changed (payment reference, external order number), mapped with Hibernate's `@NaturalId` and a unique constraint. Second, an application-assigned UUID generated in the constructor and used as the id, so identity exists before persistence. Third, if you must use the generated id, make `equals` compare ids only when non-null and return a constant `hashCode` per class; that stays correct across the lifecycle, and child entity sets are small enough that one bucket does not matter.","Hibernate adds two more concerns. Lazy references are proxies (runtime subclasses), so `getClass() != o.getClass()` fails when a proxy meets the real entity; use `instanceof` or `Hibernate.getClass`, and read the other side through getters because a proxy's own fields are empty. And never generate entity equality with Lombok `@Data` or `@EqualsAndHashCode` over all fields: it touches lazy associations, causing extra queries, `LazyInitializationException` or infinite recursion across bidirectional links."],"keyPoints":["Generated IDs are null before persistence and change during lifecycle","changing hashCode while inside a set is dangerous","stable business keys or carefully designed identity semantics are preferred"],"examples":[{"title":"Business key equality that is proxy-safe","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\n@Table(name = \"payments\")\npublic class Payment {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)\n    private Long id;\n\n    @NaturalId\n    @Column(name = \"payment_reference\", nullable = false, unique = true, updatable = false)\n    private String paymentReference;\n\n    protected Payment() { } // required by JPA\n\n    public Payment(String paymentReference) {\n        this.paymentReference = Objects.requireNonNull(paymentReference);\n    }\n\n    public Long getId() { return id; }\n    public String getPaymentReference() { return paymentReference; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof Payment other)) return false; // also matches Hibernate proxies\n        return paymentReference.equals(other.getPaymentReference()); // getter, not field\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return paymentReference.hashCode(); // known before persist, never changes\n    }\n}","note":"The reference exists from construction, so the hash is identical before and after `persist`, across sessions and for detached copies. Accessing `other` through its getter makes the comparison work when `other` is an uninitialized proxy."},{"title":"Generated id only: the safe variant and what the naive one breaks","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class OrderLine {\n\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE)\n    private Long id;\n\n    public Long getId() { return id; }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof OrderLine other)) return false;\n        return id != null && id.equals(other.getId()); // transient lines equal only themselves\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return OrderLine.class.hashCode(); // GOOD: constant across persist and merge\n    }\n\n    // BAD alternative: return Objects.hash(id);\n    // With it:\n    //   order.getLines().add(line);       hashed while id is null\n    //   entityManager.persist(order);     cascade assigns line.id\n    //   order.getLines().contains(line);  false, it sits under the old hash\n}","note":"The constant hash puts all lines of one order in a single bucket, which is irrelevant for the small collections entities hold, and in exchange the set never loses an element when ids are assigned."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if the entity is already in a HashSet before persist?","a":"It was placed in the bucket computed from the null-id hash. After `persist` assigns the id, `hashCode` returns a different value, so `contains` and `remove` look in another bucket and miss it, `add` can insert it again, and Hibernate's `PersistentSet`, which wraps that same `HashSet`, inherits the corruption: orphans are not removed or children appear twice. With a business key or a constant `hashCode`, the bucket never changes and the problem disappears."},{"q":"How do Hibernate proxies affect equality?","a":"A lazy `@ManyToOne` or `getReference()` returns a runtime subclass of your entity, so `getClass()` comparisons fail between a proxy and a loaded instance of the same row, and direct field access like `other.id` reads the proxy's own empty fields. Use `instanceof` (or `Hibernate.getClass(o)`) for the type check and getters such as `other.getId()` for values; calling the identifier getter on a proxy normally does not even initialize it. Within one persistence context Hibernate hands out a single instance per row, so the problem appears mainly with detached objects and cross-session comparisons."}],"extra":["**Persistence context guarantee:** within one `EntityManager`, Hibernate returns the same Java instance for the same row, so identity equality already works inside a single transaction; custom `equals` matters when you compare detached entities, mix sessions or keep entities in sets across requests.","**Assigned UUIDs and Spring Data:** if you assign the id in the constructor, `SimpleJpaRepository.save` sees a non-null id, treats the entity as existing and calls `merge`, which costs an extra `SELECT`; implement `Persistable<UUID>` with an `isNew` flag, or add a `@Version` field, to get a plain `persist`.","**Time-ordered UUIDs:** random version 4 UUID primary keys scatter inserts across a PostgreSQL B-tree index; time-ordered version 7 UUIDs keep inserts mostly append-only while still being generated in the application.","**Keep equals and hashCode non-final:** a proxy cannot intercept `final` methods, so a `final equals` runs against the proxy's empty fields; if you make them final, access state only through getters.","**Natural id lookups:** with `@NaturalId`, `session.bySimpleNaturalId(Payment.class).load(reference)` resolves the entity by its business key through Hibernate's natural id resolution, so the key used for equality doubles as an efficient lookup path."],"redFlags":["Always uses ID without considering transient entities","ignores proxy and lifecycle issues"]},{"id":9,"category":"Java Core","question":"Checked vs unchecked exceptions: how do you choose in a service codebase?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use unchecked exceptions for most service code (bugs, domain rule violations, infrastructure failures), reserve checked ones for rare recoverable conditions the direct caller must handle, and translate at layer boundaries while always preserving the cause.","answer":["Checked exceptions make the compiler force callers to handle or declare them. That fits a boundary condition the direct caller can realistically recover from, like a file that may be missing in a batch job or a parser signaling malformed input. In a layered Spring service, though, most failures cannot be fixed by the immediate caller, and checked exceptions leak through every signature, do not compose with lambdas and streams, and tempt people into empty `catch` blocks.","Unchecked exceptions fit three cases: programming errors (`IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`), domain rule violations (an `InsufficientFundsException extends RuntimeException` carrying the account and amount), and infrastructure failures that should propagate to a single handler. Spring follows this design: `DataAccessException` and the HTTP client exceptions are all unchecked. For expected business outcomes that are not errors, such as a declined credit application, a return type like a sealed `CreditDecision` is often better than any exception.","The non-negotiables: never swallow, and always chain the cause (`new PaymentGatewayException(msg, e)`) so the root stack trace survives. Translate at boundaries: outbound adapters turn `SQLException`, `RestClientException` or Kafka errors into domain or application exceptions, and the web edge maps those to HTTP responses with `@RestControllerAdvice` and `ProblemDetail`. Log once, where the exception is handled, not at every layer that rethrows it."],"keyPoints":["Checked exceptions force handling and may fit recoverable boundary conditions","unchecked exceptions fit programming, domain and non-recoverable failures","preserve cause and translate at boundaries"],"examples":[{"title":"Adapter translating HTTP client failures","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass CreditBureauAdapter implements CreditBureauPort {\n\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n\n    CreditBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.internal\").build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId) {\n        try {\n            return restClient.get()\n                    .uri(\"/v1/scores/{id}\", customerId.value())\n                    .retrieve()\n                    .body(CreditScore.class);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.NotFound e) {\n            throw new UnknownCustomerException(customerId, e);         // GOOD: domain meaning, cause kept\n        } catch (RestClientException e) {\n            throw new CreditBureauUnavailableException(customerId, e); // GOOD: retryable dependency failure\n        }\n        // BAD: catch (Exception e) { return null; }                     swallowed\n        // BAD: throw new RuntimeException(\"bureau error\");              cause lost\n    }\n}","note":"The domain only sees `UnknownCustomerException` and `CreditBureauUnavailableException`; the Spring HTTP exception survives as the cause, so logs still show status codes and the original stack trace."},{"title":"Translating to HTTP once, at the web edge","lang":"java","code":"@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(UnknownCustomerException.class)\n    ProblemDetail unknownCustomer(UnknownCustomerException e) {\n        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, e.getMessage());\n    }\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(CreditBureauUnavailableException.class)\n    ProblemDetail bureauDown(CreditBureauUnavailableException e) {\n        log.warn(\"Credit bureau unavailable\", e); // logged once, with the full cause chain\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(\n                HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE, \"Credit check temporarily unavailable\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"retryable\", true);\n        return pd;\n    }\n}","note":"Controllers and services stay free of try/catch; the advice is the single place that knows about status codes and response bodies, and it is also the single place that logs."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where should exceptions be translated?","a":"At architectural boundaries, once per boundary. Outbound adapters (repositories, HTTP clients, message producers) translate technical exceptions into domain or application exceptions that carry business meaning and retryability; the inbound edge (`@RestControllerAdvice`, Kafka error handlers) translates those into protocol results such as status codes, `ProblemDetail` bodies or dead-letter routing. The domain layer in between should neither catch infrastructure exceptions nor know about HTTP status codes."},{"q":"Would you expose SQLException or HTTP client exceptions to domain code?","a":"No. It couples domain logic to JDBC or a specific HTTP library, leaks vendor details such as SQL states and URLs into business code, and makes swapping a client or adding retries ripple through the codebase. Spring already translates `SQLException` into the unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy (for example `DuplicateKeyException`); the adapter should go one step further and map it to something like `DuplicatePaymentException`, keeping the original as the cause for logs and debugging."}],"extra":["**@Transactional rollback rules:** Spring rolls back by default only on `RuntimeException` and `Error`; a checked exception thrown from a `@Transactional` method commits the transaction unless you declare `rollbackFor`, a classic source of half-applied payments.","**InterruptedException:** if you catch it and cannot rethrow it, restore the flag with `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()` before translating, otherwise shutdown and cancellation silently stop working.","**Checked exceptions in lambdas:** when an `IOException` must cross a lambda, wrap it in the purpose-built `UncheckedIOException` (or your own unchecked type) instead of sneaky-throw tricks that hide it from the compiler.","**Stack trace cost:** exceptions used for high-volume control flow spend CPU in `fillInStackTrace`; a subclass can call the protected `RuntimeException(message, cause, enableSuppression, writableStackTrace)` constructor, but usually the better fix is a result type.","**Suppressed exceptions:** try-with-resources attaches failures from `close()` through `addSuppressed`, so the primary exception survives; a `finally` block that throws would replace it and hide the real failure."],"redFlags":["Says checked is always good or RuntimeException is always bad","swallows exceptions","loses original cause"]},{"id":10,"category":"Java Core","question":"Why is String immutable, and what practical benefits does that provide?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`String` is immutable (final class, private final internal array, no mutators), which makes it thread-safe, poolable, a stable hash key and safe to pass through security checks; the price is a new object on every modification.","answer":["`String` is a `final` class whose content lives in a private `final byte[]` (compact strings since Java 9, Latin-1 or UTF-16) that is never exposed or modified. Methods like `concat`, `replace` and `toUpperCase` return new strings. The class being `final` matters too: no subclass can add mutable behavior while posing as a `String`.","The benefits are concrete. Thread safety: strings can be shared across threads without synchronization, and final fields give safe publication. Pooling: literals and `intern()` share one instance per value, which is only safe because nobody can change it. Stable hashing: the hash is computed once, cached in the object and can never go stale, which is why `String` is the ideal `HashMap` key. Security: a file path, class name, JDBC URL or permission string validated by one component cannot be altered by the caller afterwards, closing time-of-check versus time-of-use holes. Sharing: APIs can store or return strings without defensive copies.","The cost: every modification allocates. `s += x` in a loop copies the entire accumulated string on each iteration, which is quadratic time and a lot of garbage when building CSV exports or log payloads. Use `StringBuilder`, `String.join` or `Collectors.joining` for loops; a single-expression concatenation is fine because since Java 9 `javac` compiles `+` to an optimized `invokedynamic` call. And remember that a `final String` variable only fixes the reference; immutability comes from the `String` class itself."],"keyPoints":["Immutability supports thread safety, string pooling, stable hashing, security and safe sharing","concatenation creates new objects, so builders help in loops"],"examples":[{"title":"Building a settlement CSV","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: each += copies everything built so far (quadratic for large batches)\nString csvBad = \"\";\nfor (Settlement s : settlements) {\n    csvBad += s.merchantId() + \";\" + s.amount() + \";\" + s.currency() + \"\\n\";\n}\n\n// GOOD: one growable buffer, presized\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(settlements.size() * 48);\nfor (Settlement s : settlements) {\n    sb.append(s.merchantId()).append(';')\n      .append(s.amount().toPlainString()).append(';')\n      .append(s.currency()).append('\\n');\n}\nString csv = sb.toString();\n\n// GOOD: declarative version for simple joins\nString csv2 = settlements.stream()\n        .map(s -> String.join(\";\", s.merchantId(), s.amount().toPlainString(), s.currency()))\n        .collect(Collectors.joining(\"\\n\"));","note":"With 100k settlements the BAD loop copies gigabytes of characters in total; the builder appends in place and copies only when it grows."},{"title":"final reference versus immutable object","lang":"java","code":"final StringBuilder note = new StringBuilder(\"PENDING\");\nnote.append(\"_REVIEW\");                 // allowed: final only blocks reassignment\n// note = new StringBuilder();          // compile error\n\nString status = \"PENDING\";\nstatus.concat(\"_REVIEW\");               // BAD: result discarded, status is still \"PENDING\"\nstatus = status.concat(\"_REVIEW\");      // GOOD: rebind the variable to the new String\n\nMap<String, Integer> retries = new HashMap<>();\nString key = \"pay-1001\";\nretries.put(key, 1);\n// No code path can change the characters of key, so its cached hash and bucket stay valid","note":"A final reference to a mutable object is still mutable, and an immutable object behind a non-final variable is still immutable: the two concepts are independent."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is String safe as a HashMap key?","a":"Its `equals` and `hashCode` depend only on its characters, and those characters can never change after construction, so the bucket chosen at `put` time stays correct forever. The hash is also cached after the first computation, which makes repeated lookups cheap. Mutable keys have neither guarantee, which is why they get lost in maps."},{"q":"StringBuilder vs StringBuffer?","a":"Both are mutable character buffers with the same API; `StringBuffer` is the Java 1.0 version with every method `synchronized`, while `StringBuilder` (Java 5) is unsynchronized and faster. Builders are almost always method-local, so use `StringBuilder`; sharing one buffer across threads is a design smell even with `StringBuffer`, because a sequence of `append` calls is still not atomic as a whole."}],"extra":["**Compact strings:** since Java 9 (JEP 254), Latin-1 strings use one byte per character instead of two, roughly halving heap for ASCII-heavy data such as ids, JSON keys and log messages.","**Hash caching detail:** the hash is computed lazily on the first `hashCode()` call and stored in a field; Java 13 added a flag so strings whose hash really is 0 are not recomputed on every call.","**Passwords in char[]:** a `String` cannot be wiped and may linger in the heap or a heap dump until GC, which is why security APIs such as `KeyStore.load` take a `char[]` that you can zero after use.","**substring no longer leaks:** since Java 7u6, `substring` copies the characters instead of sharing the parent's array, so keeping a small substring of a huge payload no longer pins the whole payload in memory.","**String deduplication:** `-XX:+UseStringDeduplication` lets the GC make equal strings share one backing array, which can noticeably cut heap in services that hold many duplicate values (currency codes, statuses) loaded from databases."],"redFlags":["Only says 'for security' without mechanisms","confuses final reference with immutable object"]},{"id":11,"category":"Java Core","question":"What does final mean for variables, methods and classes? Does it guarantee immutability?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`final` means cannot be reassigned (variables and fields), cannot be overridden (methods) or cannot be subclassed (classes); it freezes a reference, not the object behind it, so on its own it does not guarantee immutability.","answer":["On a local variable or field, `final` means it is assigned exactly once, enforced by the compiler's definite assignment rules; lambdas and inner classes may only capture final or effectively final locals. On a method, subclasses cannot override it. On a class, nobody can extend it, as with `String`, `Integer` and every record.","`final` does not make the referenced object immutable. A `private final List<OrderLine> lines` still accepts `lines.add(...)`, and the elements of a `final` array can be overwritten. Immutability is a property of the whole object graph: the class must close every path to changing state, including references it hands out and references it received from callers.","`final` fields still matter beyond documentation: the Java Memory Model guarantees that once a constructor completes without leaking `this`, every thread sees the correctly initialized values of final fields, even if the object was published without synchronization. That is the foundation of thread-safe immutable objects. Also keep the keywords apart: `finally` is the block that runs after `try` and `catch`, while `finalize()` is the deprecated GC callback that should not be used at all."],"keyPoints":["Final variable cannot be reassigned, final method cannot be overridden, final class cannot be extended","referenced object's internal state may still mutate"],"examples":[{"title":"Final fields, mutable object, and the immutable fix","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: every field is final, yet the invoice is mutable through its list\npublic final class Invoice {\n    private final String invoiceNo;\n    private final List<InvoiceLine> lines;\n\n    public Invoice(String invoiceNo, List<InvoiceLine> lines) {\n        this.invoiceNo = invoiceNo;\n        this.lines = lines;        // the caller keeps a reference and can change it\n    }\n\n    public List<InvoiceLine> lines() {\n        return lines;              // callers can add or clear lines\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: final class, final fields, defensive copy, immutable component types\nrecord ImmutableInvoice(String invoiceNo, List<InvoiceLine> lines, Instant issuedAt) {\n    ImmutableInvoice {\n        Objects.requireNonNull(invoiceNo);\n        Objects.requireNonNull(issuedAt);\n        lines = List.copyOf(lines); // unmodifiable snapshot; InvoiceLine is itself a record\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD class would pass a review that only checks for `final` keywords. The GOOD version also relies on `Instant` and `InvoiceLine` being immutable; one mutable component breaks the guarantee."},{"title":"finally versus finalize for resource cleanup","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: relies on the GC to close the connection someday (deprecated for removal)\nclass LegacyExporter {\n    private final Connection connection;\n\n    LegacyExporter(Connection connection) { this.connection = connection; }\n\n    @Override\n    @SuppressWarnings(\"removal\")\n    protected void finalize() throws Throwable {\n        connection.close();\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: deterministic cleanup; try-with-resources compiles to finally blocks\nclass PaymentExporter {\n    void export(DataSource dataSource) throws SQLException {\n        try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection();\n             PreparedStatement ps = connection.prepareStatement(\"select id, amount from payments\");\n             ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {\n            while (rs.next()) {\n                write(rs.getLong(\"id\"), rs.getBigDecimal(\"amount\"));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void write(long id, BigDecimal amount) { /* append to the export file */ }\n}","note":"Under load the finalizer version exhausts the connection pool long before the GC runs finalizers; the try-with-resources version returns the connection the moment the block exits, even on exceptions."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you design a truly immutable class?","a":"Make the class `final` (or give it private constructors and static factories), make all fields `private final`, provide no setters, and do not leak `this` from the constructor. Defensively copy mutable inputs on the way in (`List.copyOf`, or better, use immutable types such as `Instant` and `BigDecimal`), never return internal mutable state, and make state-changing operations return new instances, for example `withStatus(...)`. In modern Java a record with a compact constructor that copies collections covers most of this, as long as every component type is itself immutable."},{"q":"Why should finalize not be used?","a":"There is no guarantee when, or even whether, `finalize` runs, so file handles and connections can be exhausted long before the GC gets to them. Finalizable objects need at least two GC cycles to be reclaimed, are processed by a finalizer thread that can fall behind and cause memory pressure, can resurrect `this`, silently ignore exceptions, and have been used in security attacks. Use try-with-resources with `AutoCloseable` for deterministic cleanup and `java.lang.ref.Cleaner` only as a safety net."}],"extra":["**finalize is on its way out:** `Object.finalize` was deprecated in Java 9 and deprecated for removal in Java 18 (JEP 421); running with `--finalization=disabled` tells you whether anything in your application still depends on it.","**Compile-time constants are inlined:** a `static final` primitive or `String` initialized with a constant expression is copied into every class that uses it, so changing it in a shared library without recompiling dependents leaves stale values in production.","**Sealed classes as a middle ground:** Java 17 `sealed` classes allow extension only by the subclasses listed in `permits`, which is often what you want instead of the all-or-nothing choice of `final`.","**final is not a performance tool:** HotSpot does not need `final` on locals or parameters to optimize, and it inlines non-final methods using class hierarchy analysis; use `final` for design intent and safe publication, not speed.","**Leaking this voids the guarantee:** registering `this` as a listener or starting a thread from the constructor lets other threads see the object before its final fields are frozen, which breaks the safe-publication guarantee."],"redFlags":["Says final object is immutable","confuses final, finally and finalize"]},{"id":12,"category":"Java Core","question":"When would you use a Java record instead of a regular class?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Use a record when a type is transparent, shallowly immutable data (DTOs, value objects, events, projections); it generates the constructor, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString`, but it does not fit mutable entities or behavior-heavy models.","answer":["A record (final since Java 16) declares its state in the header, and the compiler generates private final fields, a canonical constructor, accessors named after the components (`amount()`, not `getAmount()`), and `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` over all components. Records are implicitly final, extend `java.lang.Record` and cannot declare extra instance fields, but they can implement interfaces and have static members, instance methods and additional constructors.","They fit request and response DTOs, value objects like `Money` or `CustomerId`, domain events and Kafka payloads, query projections (JPQL constructor expressions and Spring Data projections) and composite map keys. In Java 21, records plus sealed interfaces and record patterns make algebraic data types practical, for example an exhaustive `switch` over a `CreditDecision`.","Two limits matter. Records are only shallowly immutable: a `List` component can still be mutated by whoever holds it, so copy it in a compact constructor with `List.copyOf`. And records are the wrong tool for JPA entities, which need a no-arg constructor, mutable state, proxyable (non-final) classes and identity that is not value-based, and for rich models whose invariants evolve through state transitions. Validation belongs in the compact constructor, so an invalid instance can never exist; Jackson and Bean Validation (`@Valid` on a `@RequestBody`) both work with records in Spring Boot 3."],"keyPoints":["Records are concise shallowly immutable data carriers with generated accessors, equals/hashCode and toString","useful for DTOs/value types, not mutable entities or behavior-heavy models"],"examples":[{"title":"DTO with validation, normalization and a defensive copy","lang":"java","code":"public record CreditApplication(\n        String applicationId,\n        String customerId,\n        BigDecimal requestedAmount,\n        String currency,\n        List<String> productCodes) {\n\n    public CreditApplication {                        // compact canonical constructor\n        Objects.requireNonNull(applicationId, \"applicationId\");\n        Objects.requireNonNull(customerId, \"customerId\");\n        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, \"currency\");\n        if (requestedAmount == null || requestedAmount.signum() <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"requestedAmount must be positive\");\n        }\n        currency = currency.toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);  // reassigns the parameter, not the field\n        productCodes = List.copyOf(productCodes);       // shallow immutability made real\n    }                                                   // fields are assigned here implicitly\n\n    public boolean requiresManualReview() {\n        return requestedAmount.compareTo(new BigDecimal(\"500000\")) > 0;\n    }\n}","note":"Every construction path, including Jackson deserialization of a request body, runs through the compact constructor, so an invalid or externally mutable `CreditApplication` cannot exist."},{"title":"Sealed hierarchy with record patterns (Java 21)","lang":"java","code":"public sealed interface CreditDecision permits Approved, Declined, Referred { }\n\npublic record Approved(String applicationId, BigDecimal limit) implements CreditDecision { }\npublic record Declined(String applicationId, String reasonCode) implements CreditDecision { }\npublic record Referred(String applicationId, String queue) implements CreditDecision { }\n\nclass DecisionMessages {\n    static String toCustomerMessage(CreditDecision decision) {\n        return switch (decision) {                    // exhaustive: no default branch needed\n            case Approved(var id, var limit) -> \"Approved with a limit of \" + limit;\n            case Declined(var id, var reason) -> \"Declined (\" + reason + \")\";\n            case Referred(var id, var queue) -> \"Your application is under review\";\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Adding a fourth decision type makes this `switch` fail to compile until it is handled, which is the safety net you want in credit decision code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can a record contain a mutable list?","a":"Yes. The field is final, so the reference cannot change, but the `List` it points to can be modified by anyone holding it, including the caller who passed it in, and the accessor returns that same reference. Make it effectively immutable by copying in the compact constructor with `lines = List.copyOf(lines);`, which also rejects `null` elements, so the generated accessor returns an unmodifiable list."},{"q":"How do compact constructors work?","a":"A compact constructor is the canonical constructor declared without a parameter list, for example `public Money { ... }`. Its body runs with the component parameters in scope, where you validate, normalize or reassign the parameters (assigning `this.field` there is a compile error), and the compiler assigns all fields from the parameters at the end. It is the idiomatic place for invariants because every construction path, including Jackson and Java serialization, goes through it."}],"extra":["**Safer serialization:** a serializable record is always deserialized through its canonical constructor, so compact constructor validation runs on deserialization too, unlike classic `Serializable` classes that bypass constructors.","**Array components:** generated `equals` and `hashCode` compare arrays by reference, so a `byte[]` component breaks value semantics; prefer a `List` or override both methods.","**BigDecimal components:** generated `equals` uses `BigDecimal.equals`, which is scale-sensitive, so 10.0 and 10.00 make two different `Money` records unless you normalize the scale in the compact constructor.","**JPA fit:** records cannot be entities but work well as DTO projections (`select new com.acme.OrderSummary(o.id, o.total) from Order o`) and, with Hibernate 6.2 or later, as `@Embeddable` value types.","**Local records:** you can declare a record inside a method, for example `record MerchantTotal(String merchantId, BigDecimal total)`, to carry intermediate stream results instead of abusing `Map.Entry` or `Object[]`."],"redFlags":["Assumes deep immutability","uses records blindly for JPA entities","cannot explain custom validation"]},{"id":13,"category":"Java Core","question":"Explain generics invariance and PECS (Producer Extends, Consumer Super).","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Generics are invariant, so `List<Integer>` is not a `List<Number>`; use `? extends T` when a parameter only produces values you read, `? super T` when it only consumes values you write, and never use raw types.","answer":["Arrays are covariant: `Integer[]` is a `Number[]`, and storing a `Double` into it compiles but throws `ArrayStoreException` at runtime. Generics were designed to catch that at compile time, and because type arguments are erased there is nothing to check at runtime, so parameterized types are invariant: `List<Integer>` is not a subtype of `List<Number>`. If it were, you could add a `Double` to a list of integers through the `List<Number>` alias.","Wildcards restore flexibility at the use site. `List<? extends Number>` accepts a `List<Integer>` or `List<BigDecimal>`: you can read elements as `Number` but cannot add anything except `null`, because the compiler does not know the actual element type. `List<? super Integer>` accepts `List<Integer>`, `List<Number>` or `List<Object>`: you can add `Integer`s, but reads only give you `Object`. That is PECS: a parameter that produces values for you uses `extends`, one that consumes your values uses `super`, and one that does both stays exact as `List<T>`.","This is why JDK signatures look like `Collections.copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)` and `List.sort(Comparator<? super E>)`, and why a `Comparator<Object>` can sort a `List<Payment>`. Put wildcards in parameters, not return types, where they only push complexity onto callers. Avoid raw types entirely: `List raw = orders` switches off type checking, allows heap pollution and turns a wrong insert into a `ClassCastException` far away from the actual bug."],"keyPoints":["List<Integer> is not a subtype of List<Number>","use ? extends T for reading/producing and ? super T for writing/consuming","avoid raw types"],"examples":[{"title":"PECS in a payment API","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface Payment permits CardPayment, BankTransfer {\n    BigDecimal amount();\n}\nrecord CardPayment(BigDecimal amount, String maskedPan) implements Payment { }\nrecord BankTransfer(BigDecimal amount, String iban) implements Payment { }\n\nfinal class PaymentOps {\n\n    // Producer: we only read Payments out, so accept a list of any subtype\n    static BigDecimal total(Collection<? extends Payment> payments) {\n        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;\n        for (Payment p : payments) {\n            sum = sum.add(p.amount());\n        }\n        return sum;\n    }\n\n    // Consumer: we only put CardPayments in, so accept a sink of any supertype\n    static void extractCards(Collection<? extends Payment> source, Collection<? super CardPayment> sink) {\n        for (Payment p : source) {\n            if (p instanceof CardPayment card) {\n                sink.add(card);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    static void demo() {\n        List<CardPayment> cards = List.of(new CardPayment(new BigDecimal(\"25.00\"), \"****4242\"));\n        BigDecimal t = total(cards);     // compiles only because of \"? extends\"\n        List<Payment> audit = new ArrayList<>();\n        extractCards(cards, audit);      // compiles only because of \"? super\"\n    }\n}","note":"With `Collection<Payment>` as the parameter types, both calls in `demo` would fail to compile, forcing callers into copies or casts. Wildcards make the API accept exactly what is type-safe."},{"title":"Covariant arrays, invariant generics and raw types","lang":"java","code":"Number[] amounts = new Integer[2];\namounts[0] = 1.5;                         // compiles, throws ArrayStoreException at runtime\n\nList<Integer> ints = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2));\n// List<Number> nums = ints;              // does not compile: generics are invariant\nList<? extends Number> view = ints;       // OK: read elements as Number\nNumber first = view.get(0);\n// view.add(3);                           // does not compile: actual element type unknown\n\nList<? super Integer> sink = new ArrayList<Number>();\nsink.add(42);                             // OK: an Integer fits any supertype list\nObject out = sink.get(0);                 // reads come back as Object\n\n// BAD: a raw type silences the compiler and the failure surfaces far away\nList raw = ints;\nraw.add(\"oops\");                          // only an unchecked warning\nint boom = ints.get(2);                   // ClassCastException here","note":"The array bug is caught at runtime, the generic one at compile time, and the raw type defeats both, so the exception appears at a read site that has nothing to do with the faulty write."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can you not add to List<? extends Number>?","a":"Because the wildcard means some unknown subtype of `Number`, and the list might actually be a `List<Integer>`, `List<Double>` or `List<BigDecimal>`. The compiler cannot prove that any value you add matches that unknown type, so it rejects every `add` except `null`. Reading is still safe, since whatever the element type is, it is a `Number`."},{"q":"Write a method that copies values between lists.","a":"`static <T> void copyInto(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dest) { for (T item : src) { dest.add(item); } }` The source only produces `T`s, so it uses `extends`; the destination only consumes them, so it uses `super`. This lets you copy a `List<CardPayment>` into a `List<Payment>` or a `List<Object>`, which a signature with plain `List<T>` for both parameters would reject."}],"extra":["**Wildcard capture:** a method taking `List<?>` cannot call `list.set(i, list.get(j))`; delegate to a private generic helper such as `<T> void swap(List<T> list, int i, int j)` so the compiler captures the unknown type.","**Recursive bounds:** the shape of `Collections.max`, `<T extends Comparable<? super T>> T max(Collection<? extends T> c)`, is PECS applied twice; the `super` lets a subclass rely on its parent's `compareTo`, as `java.sql.Timestamp` does through `java.util.Date`.","**Erasure consequences:** you cannot write `new T[]`, `instanceof List<String>` or overload `process(List<Order>)` with `process(List<Refund>)`; pass a `Class<T>` or a type token (like Jackson's `TypeReference`) when runtime type information is needed.","**Use-site versus declaration-site variance:** Java expresses variance at each use with wildcards, while Kotlin declares it once on the type (`out T`, `in T`); the PECS reasoning is identical.","**@SafeVarargs:** generic varargs create a generic array and a possible heap pollution warning; methods that only read their varargs, like `List.of`, can be annotated with `@SafeVarargs` when they are static, final or private."],"redFlags":["Believes generic collections are covariant","cannot safely explain get/add behavior"]},{"id":14,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"map vs flatMap: explain with a practical example.","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`map` applies a one-to-one transformation, while `flatMap` maps each element to a stream (or `Optional`) and flattens the results into one level, which is what you need for nested collections and chained optional lookups.","answer":["`map(Function<T, R>)` produces exactly one output per input: `orders.stream().map(Order::orderId)` gives a stream of ids. If the function itself returns a collection or a stream, `map` gives you a nested structure such as `Stream<List<LineItem>>`, which is rarely what you want.","`flatMap(Function<T, Stream<R>>)` lets each element produce zero, one or many results and concatenates them into a single `Stream<R>`. Turning a `List<Order>` into all of its line items is `flatMap(o -> o.lineItems().stream())`. Returning `Stream.empty()` drops an element, so `flatMap` can also act as a combined map and filter.","The same idea applies to `Optional`. When the mapping function already returns an `Optional`, `map` yields `Optional<Optional<T>>`, while `flatMap` keeps it flat: `findCustomer(id).flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod)`. That lets you chain lookups that may each be absent without null checks. `flatMap` is about shape, not performance: it creates a stream per element, so it is slightly more expensive than `map`, and for small fan-out on hot paths `mapMulti` (Java 16) avoids those intermediate streams."],"keyPoints":["map transforms one element to one result","flatMap transforms and flattens nested streams/optionals","useful for collections of collections or optional pipelines"],"examples":[{"title":"Orders to line items and revenue per SKU","lang":"java","code":"record LineItem(String sku, int quantity, BigDecimal unitPrice) {\n    BigDecimal total() { return unitPrice.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(quantity)); }\n}\nrecord Order(String orderId, List<LineItem> lineItems) { }\n\nclass RevenueReport {\n    Map<String, BigDecimal> revenueBySku(List<Order> orders) {\n        // map: one result per order\n        List<String> orderIds = orders.stream().map(Order::orderId).toList();\n\n        // map with a list-returning function gives nesting\n        Stream<List<LineItem>> nested = orders.stream().map(Order::lineItems);\n\n        // flatMap: one flat stream of every line item across all orders\n        return orders.stream()\n                .flatMap(order -> order.lineItems().stream())\n                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                        LineItem::sku,\n                        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, LineItem::total, BigDecimal::add)));\n    }\n}","note":"`flatMap` is the only step that changes the element type from `Order` to `LineItem`; everything after it works on a flat stream, so grouping and summing need no nested loops."},{"title":"Optional pipelines with flatMap","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass PaymentMethodService {\n\n    private final CustomerRepository customerRepository; // findById returns Optional<Customer>\n\n    PaymentMethodService(CustomerRepository customerRepository) {\n        this.customerRepository = customerRepository;\n    }\n\n    public Optional<String> cardTokenFor(String customerId) {\n        return customerRepository.findById(customerId)   // Optional<Customer>\n                .flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod) // Optional<PaymentMethod>, not nested\n                .filter(PaymentMethod::isActive)\n                .map(PaymentMethod::token);              // plain value: map is enough\n    }\n\n    // BAD: map with an Optional-returning function nests the result\n    // Optional<Optional<PaymentMethod>> nested = repo.findById(id).map(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod);\n\n    public List<String> activeTokens(List<String> customerIds) {\n        return customerIds.stream()\n                .map(this::cardTokenFor)                 // Stream<Optional<String>>\n                .flatMap(Optional::stream)               // keep present values only (Java 9+)\n                .toList();\n    }\n}","note":"If any step is empty the chain short-circuits to `Optional.empty()`, so there is no null check anywhere, and `flatMap(Optional::stream)` bridges from optionals back into a stream."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you turn List<Order> into a flat list of line items?","a":"`List<LineItem> items = orders.stream().flatMap(o -> o.lineItems().stream()).toList();` Each order maps to a stream of its items, and `flatMap` concatenates them in encounter order. If you also need the parent, map inside the lambda into a small record, for example `o.lineItems().stream().map(li -> new OrderLineRef(o.orderId(), li))`. With JPA entities, make sure the line items were fetched with the orders (join fetch or an entity graph), or this innocent stream triggers N+1 lazy loads."},{"q":"flatMap with Optional?","a":"`Optional.flatMap` is for functions that already return an `Optional`: `findCustomer(id).flatMap(Customer::defaultPaymentMethod)` yields `Optional<PaymentMethod>` instead of `Optional<Optional<PaymentMethod>>`. It short-circuits: if any step is empty, the rest of the chain is skipped and you get `Optional.empty()`. In streams, `flatMap(Optional::stream)` drops empty optionals and unwraps the present ones."}],"extra":["**Optional.stream():** since Java 9, `flatMap(Optional::stream)` turns a `Stream<Optional<T>>` into only the present values, replacing the older `filter(Optional::isPresent).map(Optional::get)` idiom.","**Stream.ofNullable:** `flatMap(o -> Stream.ofNullable(o.couponCode()))` (Java 9) maps a nullable field to zero or one elements without an explicit null filter.","**Laziness fix in Java 10:** before JDK 10, `flatMap` fully consumed each inner stream even under short-circuiting operations like `findFirst`, which was costly and could hang with infinite inner streams; since Java 10 it respects short-circuiting.","**Reactive flatMap is different:** in Project Reactor, `flatMap` subscribes to inner publishers concurrently and interleaves their results with no order guarantee, while `concatMap` preserves order; assuming `Stream.flatMap` semantics there is a common source of reordering bugs in WebFlux code.","**flatMap for validation:** mapping each input to a stream of errors and flattening (`flatMap(row -> validate(row).stream())`) collects all validation errors across a batch without any shared mutable state."],"redFlags":["Cannot explain flattening","uses flatMap as a generic performance optimization"]},{"id":15,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"What makes streams lazy, and when is work actually executed?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Intermediate operations only describe a pipeline; nothing runs until a terminal operation pulls elements through the fused stages one at a time, short-circuiting operations can stop early, and a stream can be consumed only once.","answer":["Calling `filter`, `map`, `sorted` or `peek` processes nothing: each call returns a new stage linked to the previous one and captures the lambda. When a terminal operation such as `collect`, `forEach`, `reduce`, `count`, `findFirst` or `anyMatch` runs, the stages are fused into a chain of sinks and each element flows through the whole chain before the next one starts, rather than stage by stage over the full collection. That is why `filter` then `map` over a million elements does not allocate an intermediate list.","Short-circuiting operations exploit this: `findFirst`, `findAny`, `anyMatch`, `allMatch`, `noneMatch`, `limit` and `takeWhile` stop pulling from the source as soon as the answer is known, which also makes infinite sources like `Stream.iterate` usable. Stateful operations such as `sorted` and `distinct` behave differently: `sorted` must see every element before it emits the first one, which removes most of the short-circuit benefit.","Streams are single-use: once a terminal operation has run, or once another stage has been chained from it, reusing the stream throws `IllegalStateException` (stream has already been operated upon or closed). To traverse twice, keep the source collection or a `Supplier<Stream<T>>`. The practical consequences: side effects placed in `peek` or `map` may run fewer times than you expect or never, and a pipeline without a terminal operation silently does nothing."],"keyPoints":["Intermediate operations build a pipeline","terminal operations trigger traversal","short-circuiting operations may stop early","streams are single-use"],"examples":[{"title":"Watching when work actually happens","lang":"java","code":"List<String> paymentIds = List.of(\"p-1\", \"p-2\", \"p-3\", \"p-4\");\n\nStream<Payment> pipeline = paymentIds.stream()\n        .filter(id -> {\n            System.out.println(\"filter \" + id);\n            return !id.equals(\"p-1\");\n        })\n        .map(id -> {\n            System.out.println(\"load \" + id);\n            return paymentRepository.load(id);   // expensive call\n        });\n\nSystem.out.println(\"pipeline built, nothing printed yet\");\n\nOptional<Payment> first = pipeline.findFirst();\n// Output: filter p-1, filter p-2, load p-2   (p-3 and p-4 are never touched)\n\npipeline.count(); // IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon or closed","note":"Only one expensive `load` call happens, and the elements are processed vertically (filter p-2 is immediately followed by load p-2). Reusing `pipeline` fails because streams are single-use."},{"title":"Lazy streaming from PostgreSQL with Spring Data","lang":"java","code":"public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {\n    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = \"org.hibernate.fetchSize\", value = \"500\"))\n    Stream<Order> streamByStatus(OrderStatus status);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderExportService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final CsvWriter csv;\n\n    OrderExportService(OrderRepository orders, CsvWriter csv) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.csv = csv;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional(readOnly = true)             // the cursor needs an open transaction\n    public void exportPending() {\n        try (Stream<Order> stream = orders.streamByStatus(OrderStatus.PENDING)) {\n            stream.map(OrderCsvRow::from)       // rows are fetched as the terminal op pulls\n                  .forEach(csv::write);         // terminal operation drives the JDBC cursor\n        }                                       // close releases the ResultSet\n    }\n}","note":"Laziness here spans all the way to the database: rows arrive in fetch-size batches as `forEach` pulls them, so a multi-million row export runs in bounded memory, provided the stream is closed and the transaction stays open."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does findFirst short-circuit?","a":"`findFirst` is a short-circuiting terminal operation: its sink records the first element that reaches it and then reports that cancellation is requested. The source is traversed with `Spliterator.tryAdvance` one element at a time, checking that flag before each pull, so traversal stops right after the first element survives the upstream `filter` and `map` chain. Upstream stages ran only for the elements actually pulled, unless a barrier such as `sorted` forced the whole source to be consumed first."},{"q":"What happens without a terminal operation?","a":"Nothing happens: the lambdas are never invoked, no elements are read and the stream object is eventually garbage collected. That is a common bug when someone uses `peek` or `map` for logging or saving and forgets the `collect` or `forEach`. For I/O-backed streams such as files or repository streams, the underlying resource was already opened when the stream was created, so it leaks until someone calls `close()`."}],"extra":["**count() may skip the pipeline:** since Java 9, `count()` can compute the size directly from a sized source without executing `map` or `peek`, so `List.of(1, 2, 3).stream().peek(System.out::println).count()` prints nothing.","**PostgreSQL cursors:** the PostgreSQL JDBC driver only streams rows in fetch-size batches when auto-commit is off; otherwise it loads the whole result set into memory, so a lazy `Stream<Order>` can still cause an `OutOfMemoryError` outside a transaction.","**Persistence context growth:** streamed entities stay managed until the transaction ends, so for large exports detach or clear the `EntityManager` periodically, or stream DTO projections instead of entities.","**findFirst versus findAny in parallel:** `findFirst` must respect encounter order and may keep earlier segments working, while `findAny` can return whichever match any thread finds first and is cheaper when order does not matter.","**Lazy sources hold resources:** `Files.lines` and `BufferedReader.lines` read as elements are pulled, so the file stays open until the stream is closed; always use try-with-resources with I/O-backed streams."],"redFlags":["Thinks each map/filter executes immediately","reuses a consumed stream"]},{"id":16,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"reduce vs collect: when should each be used?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`reduce` folds elements into one immutable value with an associative operation and a true identity; `collect` performs mutable reduction into containers via supplier, accumulator and combiner, and `Collectors` is the tool for lists, maps and grouping.","answer":["`reduce(identity, accumulator, combiner)` treats values as immutable: each step returns a new value, as in `reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)` or `reduce(Integer::max)`. The operation must be associative and the identity must be a real neutral element, because a parallel stream is split, each part is reduced separately starting from the identity, and partial results are combined in an unspecified grouping. Subtraction, or a sum that starts at 100 instead of 0, silently produces different results in parallel.","`collect` is mutable reduction: a `supplier` creates a fresh container (one per parallel segment), the `accumulator` adds each element into it in place, and the `combiner` merges containers. That is efficient for building a `List`, `Map`, `StringBuilder` or statistics object, and it is parallel-safe because each thread owns its container. The `Collector` interface packages these functions together with a `finisher` and characteristics such as `CONCURRENT` and `UNORDERED`.","In practice, use `reduce`, or better the primitive specializations like `mapToLong(...).sum()`, for scalar results, and `collect` with `Collectors` (`toList`, `toMap`, `groupingBy`, `partitioningBy`, `joining`, `teeing`) for anything container-shaped. Using `reduce` with an `ArrayList` identity is the classic bug: it mutates one shared object and breaks under parallelism."],"keyPoints":["reduce combines immutable values associatively","collect performs mutable reduction into containers using supplier/accumulator/combiner","collectors are preferred for lists/maps/grouping"],"examples":[{"title":"Scalar reduction versus mutable reduction","lang":"java","code":"// reduce: immutable values, associative operation, true identity\nBigDecimal settledTotal = payments.stream()\n        .filter(Payment::isSettled)\n        .map(Payment::amount)\n        .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);\n\nlong totalMinorUnits = payments.stream()\n        .mapToLong(Payment::amountMinorUnits)   // primitive stream: no boxing\n        .sum();\n\n// BAD: the identity is one shared ArrayList, mutated by every accumulator call\nList<String> idsBad = payments.parallelStream()\n        .reduce(new ArrayList<String>(),\n                (list, p) -> { list.add(p.id()); return list; },\n                (l1, l2) -> { l1.addAll(l2); return l1; });\n\n// GOOD: collect gives each segment its own container\nList<String> ids = payments.parallelStream()\n        .map(Payment::id)\n        .collect(Collectors.toList());\n\n// GOOD: the same thing with an explicit supplier, accumulator and combiner\nArrayList<String> ids2 = payments.parallelStream()\n        .map(Payment::id)\n        .collect(ArrayList::new, ArrayList::add, ArrayList::addAll);","note":"The BAD version may even pass tests sequentially, but in parallel several threads call `add` on the same `ArrayList` and the combiner merges the list with itself, producing duplicates, lost elements or exceptions."},{"title":"groupingBy, toMap and teeing in a settlement report","lang":"java","code":"// groupingBy: many values per key, a downstream collector shapes each value\nMap<String, BigDecimal> totalByMerchant = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Payment::merchantId,\n                TreeMap::new,                                    // sorted report order\n                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Payment::amount, BigDecimal::add)));\n\nMap<PaymentStatus, Long> countByStatus = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(\n                Payment::status,\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(PaymentStatus.class),\n                Collectors.counting()));\n\n// toMap: exactly one value per key, duplicates must be resolved explicitly\nMap<String, Payment> latestByOrder = payments.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(\n                Payment::orderId,\n                Function.identity(),\n                (a, b) -> a.createdAt().isAfter(b.createdAt()) ? a : b));\n\n// teeing (Java 12): two reductions in one pass\nrecord Summary(long count, BigDecimal total) { }\nSummary summary = payments.stream().collect(Collectors.teeing(\n        Collectors.counting(),\n        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Payment::amount, BigDecimal::add),\n        Summary::new));","note":"Each collector owns its intermediate containers, so the same code is correct sequentially and in parallel; the choice between `groupingBy` and `toMap` states whether duplicates per key are expected."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is using reduce to mutate an ArrayList unsafe?","a":"`reduce` assumes the identity is an immutable neutral value, so in a parallel stream every segment starts from the same identity object: all threads call `add` on one unsynchronized `ArrayList`, causing lost elements, duplicates or `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, and the combiner may then add the list to itself. Even sequentially it violates the contract, because after the first call the identity is no longer an identity. `collect` exists precisely for this case: its supplier creates a fresh container per segment."},{"q":"Explain groupingBy and toMap duplicate keys.","a":"`groupingBy` expects many elements per key: by default it builds a `Map<K, List<T>>`, or applies a downstream collector (`counting`, `reducing`, `mapping`, `toSet`) per key, so duplicates are the whole point. `toMap` expects exactly one value per key and throws `IllegalStateException` on the first duplicate unless you pass a merge function, such as keeping the newest or summing. Both reject some nulls with `NullPointerException`: `groupingBy` a null classifier key, `toMap` a null value."}],"extra":["**Floating point is not associative:** summing `double` values with `reduce` in parallel can give slightly different results from run to run; `DoubleStream.sum()` may use compensated summation, and money should be `BigDecimal` or long minor units anyway.","**Three-argument reduce:** it lets the result type differ from the element type, but a `map` followed by the two-argument `reduce` is usually clearer and just as fast; if you need a container, you want `collect`.","**Downstream collectors:** inside `groupingBy`, `reducing`, `summingLong`, `summarizingLong` and `mapping` compute per-key aggregates in the same pass instead of grouping first and iterating again.","**toList flavors:** `Stream.toList()` (Java 16) returns an unmodifiable list, `Collectors.toList()` makes no mutability guarantee, and `Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new)` is the explicit way to get a mutable one.","**groupingByConcurrent:** in parallel streams, merging many per-segment `HashMap`s is expensive; `groupingByConcurrent` accumulates into one `ConcurrentHashMap` when you do not need encounter order."],"redFlags":["Uses reduce with mutable containers","ignores associativity and parallel behavior"]},{"id":17,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"When can parallelStream hurt performance or correctness?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`parallelStream` runs on the JVM-wide common `ForkJoinPool`, so blocking I/O, small or badly splitting data, ordering, expensive merges and shared mutable state can make it slower or incorrect; benchmark CPU-bound work before using it.","answer":["Parallel streams split the source with its `Spliterator` and run the pieces on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`, which is shared by every parallel stream in the JVM and by `CompletableFuture` async methods called without an executor. Its parallelism defaults to the number of available processors minus one, with the calling thread helping. In a server, several requests running parallel streams at once compete for those few workers, so you get contention and latency spikes instead of speedup, and there is no isolation between features.","Performance suffers when per-element work is tiny or the dataset is small (splitting and task overhead dominate), when the source splits badly (`LinkedList`, `Stream.iterate`, I/O-backed streams), when ordering must be preserved (`forEachOrdered`, `limit`, `findFirst`, `sorted` on ordered streams), when merging is costly (combining many per-thread `HashMap`s in `groupingBy`), or when boxing dominates. Blocking I/O is the worst case: an HTTP or JDBC call inside the stream parks a common-pool worker and starves unrelated work across the whole JVM.","Correctness breaks with shared mutable state (adding to an `ArrayList` from `forEach`), non-associative reductions, code that relies on encounter order in `forEach`, and anything based on thread-locals: MDC trace ids, Spring's `SecurityContextHolder` and the transaction-bound `EntityManager` do not follow work onto pool threads. Use parallel streams only for large, CPU-bound, stateless computations, benchmark first with JMH under realistic concurrent load, and for I/O fan-out prefer virtual threads or a dedicated, bounded executor."],"keyPoints":["Common ForkJoinPool contention, small datasets, blocking I/O, ordering cost, split/merge overhead and shared mutable state can make it slower or incorrect","benchmark first"],"examples":[{"title":"Blocking fan-out: parallelStream versus virtual threads","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: blocking HTTP calls on the JVM-wide common pool, inside a request thread\npublic List<Quote> quotesBad(List<String> skus) {\n    return skus.parallelStream()\n            .map(pricingClient::fetchQuote)   // parks a common pool worker per call\n            .toList();\n}\n\n// GOOD: virtual threads for blocking I/O, bounded to protect the pricing service\nprivate final Semaphore pricingPermits = new Semaphore(20);\n\npublic List<Quote> quotes(List<String> skus) throws InterruptedException {\n    try (ExecutorService executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n        List<Future<Quote>> futures = skus.stream()\n                .map(sku -> executor.submit(() -> fetchWithPermit(sku)))\n                .toList();\n        List<Quote> result = new ArrayList<>(futures.size());\n        for (Future<Quote> f : futures) {\n            try {\n                result.add(f.get());\n            } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n                throw new PricingUnavailableException(\"Quote lookup failed\", e.getCause());\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n\nprivate Quote fetchWithPermit(String sku) throws InterruptedException {\n    pricingPermits.acquire();\n    try {\n        return pricingClient.fetchQuote(sku);\n    } finally {\n        pricingPermits.release();\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD version competes with every other parallel stream and default `CompletableFuture` in the JVM and gets at most a few workers in a small container. The GOOD version gives each call its own cheap virtual thread and makes the downstream concurrency limit explicit."},{"title":"When a parallel stream helps and when it hurts","lang":"text","code":"Scenario                                       Verdict\nSumming 50M longs from an array                Likely faster: large, CPU-bound, splits evenly\nHashing 10k JSON payloads in a batch job       Maybe: measure with JMH first\nMapping 200 DTOs in a request handler          No: overhead exceeds the work\nCalling a REST API or database per element     No: blocks common pool workers\nReading lines from a socket via BufferedReader No: splits poorly, I/O bound\ngroupingBy into HashMap on 1M elements         Often slower: map merging cost\nforEach adding to a shared ArrayList           Wrong: data race, lost elements\nCode relying on MDC or SecurityContextHolder   Wrong: thread-locals not propagated","note":"Only the first row is a clear win; parallel streams are a tool for bulk CPU work in batch jobs, not a default for request-path code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What pool does it use?","a":"`ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`, a static JVM-wide pool whose parallelism defaults to `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()` minus one, with the calling thread also doing work. It is shared with all other parallel streams and with default `CompletableFuture` async tasks, and it can only be tuned globally, via `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N`. Starting the stream from inside a task of a custom `ForkJoinPool` makes it run there, but that relies on undocumented behavior."},{"q":"How would blocking I/O affect other parallel streams?","a":"Each blocking call parks a common-pool worker, and since the pool has roughly one worker per core, a few slow HTTP or JDBC calls can occupy all of them. Every other parallel stream and default `CompletableFuture` in the JVM then queues behind them, so unrelated endpoints slow down or time out: a noisy neighbor problem with no bulkhead. Move I/O fan-out to virtual threads (`Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`) or a dedicated bounded executor, and cap concurrency toward the downstream service with a semaphore."}],"extra":["**CompletableFuture shares the pool:** `supplyAsync` without an executor uses the same common pool, so parallel streams and async code interfere; and when the common pool parallelism is below 2 (for example a container with 1 or 2 CPUs), `CompletableFuture` falls back to creating a new thread for every task.","**Container CPU limits:** the JVM derives `availableProcessors()` from the container's CPU quota, so a pod with a 2 CPU limit gets a common pool with a single worker; code that looked fast on a 16-core laptop gains nothing in Kubernetes.","**No compensation for blocking:** the common pool only adds threads for blocked workers when the blocking code goes through `ForkJoinPool.managedBlock`, which ordinary JDBC and HTTP clients do not, so blocked workers simply reduce parallelism.","**unordered() can help:** calling `.unordered()` on a parallel pipeline lets operations like `limit` and `distinct` skip encounter-order bookkeeping, which can make them much cheaper when order does not matter.","**Custom pool trick:** running the stream inside `customPool.submit(() -> list.parallelStream()...).get()` makes it use that pool, but this relies on implementation behavior rather than a documented contract, so treat it as a last resort."],"redFlags":["Assumes parallel is always faster","uses it around blocking calls or inside server request paths without pool awareness"]},{"id":18,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"Why are side effects inside map/filter/forEach risky?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Stream lambdas should be pure: side effects in `map`, `filter` or `forEach` make behavior depend on laziness, short-circuiting and parallel scheduling, cause races on shared state and hide intent; compute results first, then apply side effects explicitly.","answer":["The stream API requires behavioral parameters to be non-interfering and, ideally, stateless. A `map` or `filter` that writes to a field, list, counter or database is no longer a function of its input: whether and how often it runs depends on laziness, short-circuiting (`findFirst` stops early), optimizations (`count()` may skip the pipeline) and, in parallel streams, on which thread handles which element in what order. The code loses referential transparency, so you can no longer read or test the pipeline as a pure transformation.","Under parallel execution, side effects on shared non-thread-safe state are data races: `ArrayList.add` from several threads loses elements, stores nulls or throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, and a plain counter under-counts. A synchronized collection fixes the crash but serializes the work and still yields a nondeterministic order. `forEach` itself gives no ordering guarantee in parallel, even for an ordered source such as a `List`.","The pattern: transform with pure functions, let a collector build the result, then perform side effects explicitly, either in a terminal `forEach` whose only job is the effect (publishing events, writing rows) or in a plain `for` loop when the logic needs checked exceptions, early exit or readable stack traces. `peek` exists for debugging, not business logic."],"keyPoints":["They reduce readability, break referential transparency, complicate parallel execution and may cause races","prefer pure transformations and explicit terminal side effects"],"examples":[{"title":"Collect results instead of mutating shared state","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: side effects inside the pipeline, broken under parallel execution\nList<String> rejected = new ArrayList<>();\nAtomicInteger approvedCount = new AtomicInteger();\napplications.parallelStream()\n        .filter(app -> {\n            boolean ok = scoring.approve(app);\n            if (!ok) rejected.add(app.id());      // race: ArrayList is not thread-safe\n            else approvedCount.incrementAndGet();\n            return ok;\n        })\n        .forEach(notifier::sendApproval);          // order not guaranteed\n\n// GOOD: pure classification, one collector, explicit side effect at the end\nMap<Boolean, List<CreditApplication>> byOutcome = applications.parallelStream()\n        .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(scoring::approve));\n\nList<String> rejectedIds = byOutcome.get(false).stream()\n        .map(CreditApplication::id)\n        .toList();\nint approved = byOutcome.get(true).size();\nbyOutcome.get(true).forEach(notifier::sendApproval); // deliberate, sequential effect","note":"The GOOD version is deterministic in content, safe in parallel, and makes the only real side effect, sending notifications, visible as its own step."},{"title":"Collecting validation errors safely","lang":"java","code":"record ValidationError(String rowId, String field, String message) { }\n\nclass PaymentFileValidator {\n\n    List<ValidationError> validate(PaymentRow row) {\n        List<ValidationError> errors = new ArrayList<>();   // local, confined to this call\n        if (row.amount() == null || row.amount().signum() <= 0) {\n            errors.add(new ValidationError(row.id(), \"amount\", \"must be positive\"));\n        }\n        if (row.iban() == null || row.iban().isBlank()) {\n            errors.add(new ValidationError(row.id(), \"iban\", \"is required\"));\n        }\n        return errors;\n    }\n\n    Map<String, List<ValidationError>> errorsByField(List<PaymentRow> rows) {\n        return rows.parallelStream()\n                .flatMap(row -> validate(row).stream())     // pure: each row maps to its errors\n                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(ValidationError::field));\n    }\n}","note":"Mutating a list that is created and returned inside one call is fine; the problem is only state shared between elements or threads, and here there is none."}],"followUps":[{"q":"forEach vs forEachOrdered?","a":"`forEach` makes no ordering promise: in a parallel stream elements are processed in whatever order threads reach them, even when the source is an ordered `List`. `forEachOrdered` processes elements in encounter order for ordered streams, which forces coordination and gives up much of the parallel speedup. On a sequential stream both behave the same in practice, but if order matters, say so with `forEachOrdered`, or better, collect first and iterate the result."},{"q":"How would you collect validation errors safely?","a":"Make validation a pure function from an input to a list (or stream) of errors, then `flatMap` and collect: `rows.stream().flatMap(r -> validate(r).stream()).toList()`. If you need valid and invalid rows separately, use `partitioningBy`, or map each row to a sealed result type and group by it. This works identically in sequential and parallel streams, because each call builds its own local list and no thread writes to shared state."}],"extra":["**Side effects may not run at all:** since Java 9, `list.stream().peek(log::info).count()` can skip `peek` entirely because the size is known, and short-circuiting means `map` may run for only part of the data, so business logic hidden in those lambdas is unreliable.","**Hidden database calls:** touching a lazy JPA association inside `map` issues queries as a side effect; in a parallel stream those queries run on pool threads sharing a non-thread-safe Hibernate `Session`, which is undefined behavior.","**Stateful lambdas:** deduplicating with `filter(seen::add)` on a shared `HashSet` is both racy and order dependent; use `distinct()` or a collector keyed by id instead.","**Partial failure:** a side effect that throws halfway through a pipeline leaves some rows written and others not, with no clear boundary, whereas collecting first lets you validate everything and then apply effects in one transaction.","**Concurrent collectors when needed:** if you genuinely need to accumulate from parallel threads, use `Collectors.toConcurrentMap` or `groupingByConcurrent`, which are designed for it, rather than hand-rolled synchronized collections."],"redFlags":["Mutates shared lists inside parallel streams","treats forEach order as guaranteed"]},{"id":19,"category":"Streams & Functional","question":"What are common production problems with Collectors.toMap()?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"`Collectors.toMap` throws on duplicate keys unless you pass a merge function, throws on null values, and returns an unspecified map type (a `HashMap` today) with no ordering guarantee, so choose the merge policy and map supplier deliberately.","answer":["The two-argument `toMap(keyMapper, valueMapper)` assumes keys are unique; the first duplicate throws `IllegalStateException` with a duplicate key message (since Java 9 it names both conflicting values). That is a classic production incident: the code works on test data, then a customer with two active addresses or a feed with a repeated SKU crashes a batch job. The three-argument version takes a merge function, and the policy (keep first, keep newest, sum, or fail with a meaningful domain error) is a business decision, not a way to silence the exception.","Null values throw `NullPointerException` in every `toMap` variant, because the implementation rejects them the way `Map.merge` does, so filter first or map missing values to an explicit default. Null keys depend on the target map: `HashMap` accepts one, while `TreeMap` and `ConcurrentHashMap` throw. `toUnmodifiableMap` (Java 10) rejects null keys, null values and duplicates.","The returned map's type, mutability and iteration order are unspecified. In practice you get a `HashMap`, so iteration order is effectively arbitrary and can change as data grows, which surfaces as flaky tests, reshuffled JSON fields and nondeterministic reports. Use the four-argument overload with `LinkedHashMap::new` for insertion order, `TreeMap::new` for sorted keys, or an `EnumMap` supplier for enum keys. And if duplicates are legitimate, you probably wanted `groupingBy`, not `toMap`."],"keyPoints":["Duplicate keys throw unless a merge function is supplied","null handling and map type matter","encounter order is not always guaranteed","choose merge policy deliberately"],"examples":[{"title":"Duplicate keys: crash, silent overwrite, deliberate policy","lang":"java","code":"record PriceUpdate(String sku, BigDecimal price, Instant updatedAt) { }\n\nList<PriceUpdate> feed = priceFeed.fetch(); // may contain the same SKU twice\n\n// BAD: throws IllegalStateException on the first repeated SKU\nMap<String, BigDecimal> crashes = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, PriceUpdate::price));\n\n// BAD: silently keeps whichever came last in the feed, with no business reason\nMap<String, BigDecimal> lastWins = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, PriceUpdate::price, (a, b) -> b));\n\n// GOOD: the newest update wins, first-seen key order preserved for the report\nMap<String, PriceUpdate> latestBySku = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(\n                PriceUpdate::sku,\n                Function.identity(),\n                BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(PriceUpdate::updatedAt)),\n                LinkedHashMap::new));\n\n// GOOD: duplicates are a data error here, so fail with business context\nMap<String, PriceUpdate> strict = feed.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, Function.identity(), (a, b) -> {\n            throw new DuplicateSkuException(a.sku(), a.updatedAt(), b.updatedAt());\n        }));","note":"Both GOOD versions make the duplicate policy explicit and reviewable; which one is right depends on whether duplicates are expected in the feed or indicate an upstream bug."},{"title":"Null values, map types and legitimate duplicates","lang":"java","code":"record Customer(String id, String email, String segment) { }\n\n// BAD: NullPointerException as soon as one customer has no email\nMap<String, String> emailById = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Customer::id, Customer::email));\n\n// GOOD: decide explicitly what a missing email means\nMap<String, String> knownEmails = customers.stream()\n        .filter(c -> c.email() != null)\n        .collect(Collectors.toMap(Customer::id, Customer::email));\n\n// GOOD (Java 10+): unmodifiable, rejects null keys, null values and duplicates\nMap<String, Customer> byId = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(Customer::id, Function.identity()));\n\n// GOOD: many customers per segment is a grouping, not a map\nMap<String, List<Customer>> bySegment = customers.stream()\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Customer::segment, TreeMap::new, Collectors.toList()));","note":"The null value failure surprises people because `HashMap` itself accepts null values; the collector is stricter than the map it builds."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you keep the newest value?","a":"Pass a merge function that compares timestamps or versions: `toMap(PriceUpdate::sku, Function.identity(), BinaryOperator.maxBy(Comparator.comparing(PriceUpdate::updatedAt)))`. Do not rely on `(a, b) -> b` to mean newest, since that only reflects stream order, which may not match time order and means little in unordered or parallel streams. If ties are possible, add a tie-breaker such as a sequence number to the comparator."},{"q":"How would you preserve insertion order?","a":"Use the four-argument overload and supply the map: `toMap(keyFn, valueFn, mergeFn, LinkedHashMap::new)`; the merge function is mandatory in that overload, so choose a real policy rather than a throwaway. Also make sure the stream itself is ordered (a `List` source, not a `HashSet`), because the map can only preserve the encounter order it receives. For sorted keys instead of insertion order, pass `TreeMap::new`."}],"extra":["**Measure merges:** when a merge function resolves duplicates, count them with a metric (for example a Micrometer counter) so upstream data quality problems stay visible instead of being silently absorbed.","**Parallel streams:** `toMap` in a parallel stream merges per-thread maps, which is expensive for large maps; `toConcurrentMap` accumulates into one `ConcurrentHashMap` but gives up encounter order, so its merge function must not depend on which element came first.","**Java 21 SequencedMap:** a `LinkedHashMap` result is now a `SequencedMap`, so `firstEntry()`, `lastEntry()` and `reversed()` are available directly when the order carries meaning.","**Index-by-id pattern:** `toMap(Entity::getId, Function.identity())` after a batch `findAllById` is the standard fix for N+1 lookups; ids are unique there, so the two-argument form is right and its exception acts as a useful assertion.","**Mutability is unspecified:** do not rely on being able to `put` into the result of the default `toMap`; if you need a mutable map, pass `HashMap::new` explicitly so the contract lives in the code."],"redFlags":["Does not know duplicate-key behavior","silently overwrites without business justification"]},{"id":20,"category":"Concurrency","question":"What guarantees does volatile provide, and what does it not provide?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`volatile` gives visibility and ordering (a write happens-before every later read of that field) but no atomicity for compound actions and no mutual exclusion, so use it for flags and single-writer state.","answer":["`volatile` is a Java Memory Model tool, not a lock. A write to a volatile field **happens-before** every subsequent read of that same field, so the reader sees the new value and everything the writer did before writing it. It also restricts reordering: ordinary writes before a volatile write cannot move after it, and ordinary reads after a volatile read cannot move before it. Without it the JIT may keep a field in a register or hoist the read out of a loop, and a thread can spin forever on a stale value. Volatile `long` and `double` accesses are also atomic, which plain 64-bit fields are not guaranteed to be.","What it does not give you is atomicity of compound actions. `count++` is read, add, write: two threads can both read 41 and both write 42, losing an update even though every single access is volatile. There is no mutual exclusion either, so it cannot protect an invariant spanning several fields, such as `balance` and `lastUpdated` changing together.","The sweet spot is a status flag (`running`, `shutdownRequested`), a single writer publishing a value many threads read (a config snapshot reference, a leader flag), and the field in double-checked locking. For counters use `AtomicLong` or `LongAdder`; for multi-field invariants use a lock or an immutable snapshot swapped through one volatile reference. Calling volatile a \"lightweight lock\" is the classic red flag."],"keyPoints":["Visibility and ordering around reads/writes","it does not make compound actions such as count++ atomic","suitable for flags and single-writer state"],"examples":[{"title":"Lost updates with a volatile counter, and the fix","lang":"java","code":"public class PaymentStats {\n\n    // BAD: volatile makes the value visible, not the increment atomic\n    private volatile long processedBad;\n\n    public void onPaymentBad() {\n        processedBad++;              // read, add, write: concurrent increments are lost\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: one atomic read-modify-write\n    private final AtomicLong processed = new AtomicLong();\n\n    public void onPayment() {\n        processed.incrementAndGet();\n    }\n\n    public long processed() {\n        return processed.get();\n    }\n}","note":"Run the BAD version from 16 threads doing a million increments each and the total comes up short. `incrementAndGet` is a single atomic operation that HotSpot compiles to one locked CPU instruction on x86."},{"title":"Correct shutdown flag for a worker loop","lang":"java","code":"public class OutboxRelay implements Runnable {\n\n    private volatile boolean running = true;   // one writer (stop), one reader (the loop)\n    private final OutboxRepository outbox;\n    private final EventPublisher publisher;\n\n    public OutboxRelay(OutboxRepository outbox, EventPublisher publisher) {\n        this.outbox = outbox;\n        this.publisher = publisher;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void run() {\n        while (running && !Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {\n            List<OutboxEvent> batch = outbox.fetchUnpublished(100);\n            batch.forEach(publisher::publish);\n            if (batch.isEmpty()) {\n                LockSupport.parkNanos(Duration.ofMillis(200).toNanos());\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void stop() {\n        running = false;   // seen by the relay thread on its next loop check\n    }\n}","note":"Without `volatile` the JIT may hoist the `running` read out of the loop and the thread never stops. A flag is only checked between iterations, so for loops that block also interrupt the thread."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is count++ unsafe?","a":"`count++` is three steps: read the field, add one, write it back. Two threads can both read 41, both compute 42 and both write 42, so one increment disappears; `volatile` only guarantees each read and write goes to memory, not that nothing happens in between. Use `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()`, `LongAdder.increment()` or a `synchronized` block, which make the read-modify-write one atomic step."},{"q":"Give a correct shutdown-flag example.","a":"Declare `private volatile boolean running = true;`, loop with `while (running) { ... }` in the worker, and have `stop()` set `running = false` from another thread. There is one writer and the only operations are a plain write and a plain read, which is exactly what volatile covers. If the loop can block (a queue `take()`, a socket read), also interrupt the thread, or use `ExecutorService.shutdownNow()`, and check `Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()` in the loop condition."}],"extra":["**Arrays and references:** a `volatile` array or object reference only makes the reference volatile; writes to elements or fields are plain. Use `AtomicLongArray`, `AtomicReference` or a `VarHandle` for per-element semantics.","**Piggybacking publication:** because a volatile write publishes all earlier writes, assigning a fully built immutable object to a volatile field is a safe way to hot-reload configuration or routing tables.","**VarHandle access modes:** since Java 9, `VarHandle` offers weaker modes such as `setRelease`/`getAcquire` and `getOpaque`; they are for library authors, and application code should stay with volatile or the atomic classes.","**Double-checked locking:** the lazily initialized field must be volatile, otherwise another thread can see a non-null reference to a partially constructed object; the holder class idiom avoids the question entirely.","**Cost model:** on x86 a volatile read is about as cheap as a normal load, while a volatile write needs a store-load fence; heavy write contention on the same cache line is what actually hurts."],"redFlags":["Calls volatile a lightweight lock","uses it for counters or multi-field invariants"]},{"id":21,"category":"Concurrency","question":"synchronized vs ReentrantLock: what trade-offs matter?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Both give reentrant mutual exclusion with the same memory visibility; use `synchronized` by default for simplicity and automatic release, and `ReentrantLock` when you need tryLock, timeouts, interruptible waits, fairness or several conditions.","answer":["Both are reentrant mutexes with the same memory semantics: releasing the lock happens-before the next acquisition, so state written inside the critical section is visible to the next owner. `synchronized` is block-structured: the monitor is released automatically when the block exits, including on exceptions, and the JIT can optimize it (lock elision through escape analysis, lock coarsening). Thread dumps show monitor owners directly.","`ReentrantLock` adds capabilities: `tryLock()` and `tryLock(timeout)` for bounded waiting, `lockInterruptibly()` so a waiting thread can be cancelled, an optional fairness policy, several `Condition` objects per lock (separate `notFull` and `notEmpty` queues in a buffer), and introspection such as `getQueueLength()`. The price is manual release: `unlock()` must sit in a `finally` block, or one exception leaves the lock held forever and every later caller hangs.","A sensible default is `synchronized` for short critical sections and `ReentrantLock` when you need one of those features. Java 21 adds a reason: a virtual thread that blocks inside `synchronized` pins its carrier thread, while `ReentrantLock` lets it unmount; JDK 24 (JEP 491) removed that limitation. `synchronized` is not obsolete, and neither lock rescues a design that holds locks across I/O. For read-heavy data consider `ReentrantReadWriteLock` or `StampedLock`."],"keyPoints":["Both provide mutual exclusion and memory visibility","ReentrantLock adds tryLock, interruptible lock, fairness and multiple conditions","synchronized is simpler and auto-releases"],"examples":[{"title":"Forgetting finally: the lock leak","lang":"java","code":"public class AccountLedger {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private long balanceCents;\n\n    // BAD: if applyFee throws, the lock is never released and every later caller hangs\n    public void debitBad(long amountCents) {\n        lock.lock();\n        balanceCents -= amountCents;\n        applyFee();\n        lock.unlock();\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: lock() just before try, unlock() in finally\n    public void debit(long amountCents) {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            balanceCents -= amountCents;\n            applyFee();\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void applyFee() {\n        // may throw, for example when the fee schedule is missing\n    }\n}","note":"Call `lock()` outside the `try`: if it were inside and failed, the `finally` would call `unlock()` on a lock you do not hold and throw `IllegalMonitorStateException`. With `synchronized` this whole class of bug cannot happen."},{"title":"tryLock to coalesce a cache refresh","lang":"java","code":"public class FxRateCache {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock refreshLock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final FxRateClient client;\n    private volatile Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = Map.of();\n\n    public FxRateCache(FxRateClient client) {\n        this.client = client;\n    }\n\n    // Only one thread refreshes; the others keep serving the current snapshot\n    public void refreshIfIdle() {\n        if (!refreshLock.tryLock()) {\n            return;                                   // a refresh is already running\n        }\n        try {\n            rates = Map.copyOf(client.fetchLatest());\n        } finally {\n            refreshLock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public BigDecimal rate(String pair) {\n        return rates.get(pair);\n    }\n}","note":"`synchronized` cannot express \"skip if busy\"; with it, every caller would queue behind the slow HTTP refresh. Readers never take the lock because the map is immutable and published through a volatile field."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is tryLock useful?","a":"When waiting is worse than skipping or failing. Typical cases: coalescing work (only one thread refreshes a cache while others serve the current value), avoiding deadlock when two locks are needed (take the first, `tryLock` the second, release both and back off with jitter on failure), and bounding latency on a request path with `tryLock(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)` so you return a retryable error instead of piling up threads. Handle the `false` branch deliberately and only call `unlock()` if the acquire succeeded."},{"q":"What is lock fairness and what is its cost?","a":"A fair lock (`new ReentrantLock(true)`) grants access to the longest-waiting thread, which prevents starvation and gives more predictable tail latency. The cost is throughput: a running thread cannot barge in when the lock is free, so almost every handoff wakes a parked thread and pays a context switch, and the Javadoc warns fair locks are often much slower under contention. Use fairness only when starvation is an observed problem; `synchronized` makes no fairness guarantee at all."}],"extra":["**Biased locking is gone:** it was deprecated in JDK 15 and removed in JDK 18, so old benchmarks comparing uncontended `synchronized` with `ReentrantLock` no longer apply; measure on your JDK.","**Untimed tryLock barges:** `tryLock()` without a timeout ignores the fairness setting and takes a free lock even if threads are queued; `tryLock(0, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` honors fairness.","**Diagnostics:** in a thread dump, monitors appear as `waiting to lock <0x...>`, while `ReentrantLock` waiters appear as `parking to wait for` a `ReentrantLock$NonfairSync`; `jstack -l <pid>` also lists the ownable synchronizers each thread holds.","**Conditions need loops:** `Condition.await()`, like `Object.wait()`, can wake spuriously, so always wait in a `while (!predicate)` loop; separate conditions avoid waking every waiter with `notifyAll`.","**Private lock objects:** synchronizing on `this`, a `String` literal or a boxed `Integer` lets unrelated code contend on or deadlock your monitor; lock on a private final object instead."],"redFlags":["Always prefers ReentrantLock","forgets unlock in finally","claims synchronized is obsolete"]},{"id":22,"category":"Concurrency","question":"AtomicLong vs LongAdder: which would you choose for a high-contention metric?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"For a hot, write-heavy metric choose `LongAdder`: it stripes updates across cells to avoid CAS contention, but `sum()` is not an atomic snapshot, so exact values and conditional updates stay on `AtomicLong`.","answer":["`AtomicLong` holds one value updated with atomic instructions (`incrementAndGet`, `compareAndSet`, `updateAndGet`). Every update targets the same cache line, so with many cores hammering it the line bounces between CPUs and CAS retries pile up; throughput flattens or even drops as you add threads. In exchange you get an exact value at every moment and conditional updates.","`LongAdder` (built on `Striped64`) keeps a `base` value plus a lazily created array of padded cells. Uncontended updates go to `base`; once a CAS on it fails, threads are hashed to separate cells, so increments rarely collide. `sum()` adds base and cells without locking, so it is **not an atomic snapshot**: concurrent increments may or may not be included, and there is no `compareAndSet`. It also costs more memory per counter.","For a high-contention metric (requests per endpoint, bytes processed, cache hits) that is incremented constantly and read occasionally by a scraper, `LongAdder` is the right choice. For sequence numbers, rate-limit budgets, or anything where you check a value and then act on it, use `AtomicLong`, a lock or the database. Using `LongAdder` for an account balance is the red flag. Under low contention the difference is negligible, so change existing code only when a profiler or JMH benchmark shows the counter is a hotspot."],"keyPoints":["AtomicLong provides exact atomic updates/CAS","LongAdder spreads contention across cells and is faster for frequent increments, but sum is not an atomic snapshot"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-endpoint request metric with LongAdder","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class EndpointMetrics {\n\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, LongAdder> requests = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    // Hot path: called on every request from hundreds of threads\n    public void record(String endpoint) {\n        requests.computeIfAbsent(endpoint, k -> new LongAdder()).increment();\n    }\n\n    // Cold path: called by the metrics scraper, an approximate total is fine\n    public Map<String, Long> snapshot() {\n        Map<String, Long> result = new TreeMap<>();\n        requests.forEach((endpoint, adder) -> result.put(endpoint, adder.sum()));\n        return result;\n    }\n}","note":"`computeIfAbsent` makes counter creation atomic, and the increment itself rarely contends. The snapshot may miss increments that are in flight, which is irrelevant for a metric scraped every few seconds."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: an invariant needs CAS, not an adder","lang":"java","code":"public class CreditLine {\n\n    private final long limitCents;\n\n    public CreditLine(long limitCents) {\n        this.limitCents = limitCents;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: sum() is not a snapshot and check-then-add is not atomic, so two threads can overdraw\n    private final LongAdder usedBad = new LongAdder();\n\n    public boolean reserveBad(long amountCents) {\n        if (usedBad.sum() + amountCents <= limitCents) {\n            usedBad.add(amountCents);\n            return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: CAS loop enforces used <= limit atomically\n    private final AtomicLong used = new AtomicLong();\n\n    public boolean reserve(long amountCents) {\n        while (true) {\n            long current = used.get();\n            long next = current + amountCents;\n            if (next > limitCents) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            if (used.compareAndSet(current, next)) {\n                return true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"In a real system the authoritative credit line lives in the database behind a row lock or version column; an in-memory CAS like this fits a per-instance budget or rate limiter."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is LongAdder suitable for metrics but not a financial balance?","a":"A metric only needs every increment to be counted eventually and a roughly current value when it is read; increments are commutative, so it does not matter which cell received them. A balance has invariants (never below zero, never above a limit) that require reading the exact current value and updating it conditionally in one atomic step. `LongAdder` offers neither: `sum()` can miss in-flight updates and there is no `compareAndSet`, so a check-then-add allows overdrafts. Real balances belong in the database with transactional guarantees, or at minimum behind `AtomicLong.compareAndSet` or a lock."}],"extra":["**Same trick inside the JDK:** `ConcurrentHashMap` counts its size with a base count plus counter cells adapted from `LongAdder`, which is why `size()` is only an estimate while updates are in flight.","**False sharing and memory:** each cell is annotated `@Contended` so it sits on its own cache line; that padding is why an adder costs much more memory than an `AtomicLong`, which matters if you create millions of them.","**LongAccumulator:** generalizes striping to any associative, commutative function, for example `new LongAccumulator(Long::max, Long.MIN_VALUE)` to track peak latency without contention.","**sumThenReset caveat:** it is only exact when no updates race with it, so for interval metrics under load prefer cumulative counters and let Prometheus compute rates.","**Per JVM only:** both classes are in-process; across pods the metric is aggregated by the monitoring system, and a business counter that must survive restarts or be exact belongs in the database."],"redFlags":["Uses LongAdder for account balance or invariants","cannot explain contention trade-off"]},{"id":23,"category":"Concurrency","question":"How do race conditions and deadlocks differ? How do you prevent each?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A race condition is wrong results from an unlucky interleaving, a deadlock is a cycle of threads waiting on each other; prevent races with atomicity and immutability, deadlocks with lock ordering, timeouts and small lock scopes.","answer":["A **race condition** means the result depends on thread timing: check-then-act (`if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)`), read-modify-write (`count++`), or reading a half-built object. Each call may even be thread-safe on its own; the bug is that the compound action is not atomic. A **data race** is the narrower JMM term: two unsynchronized accesses to the same variable, at least one a write. Races corrupt data silently: double charges, lost updates, oversold stock.","A **deadlock** is a cycle in the wait-for graph: thread A holds lock 1 and waits for lock 2, while B holds 2 and waits for 1. It needs four conditions at once: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait. The symptom is the opposite of a race: nothing happens, threads sit BLOCKED and throughput drops to zero.","Prevent races by making compound actions atomic (`ConcurrentHashMap.compute`, `AtomicLong.compareAndSet`, a lock, a unique constraint or `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`), by preferring immutable objects, and by confining mutable state to one thread. Prevent deadlocks by breaking the cycle: acquire locks in one global order (for example by account id), use `tryLock` with a timeout and backoff, keep lock scopes small, and never do I/O or call foreign code while holding a lock. Diagnose with thread dumps and `ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()`. Adding `Thread.sleep` or more locks fixes neither."],"keyPoints":["Race conditions are incorrect interleavings","deadlocks are cyclic waiting","use atomicity/immutability and consistent lock ordering, timeouts, reduced lock scope and diagnostics"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: transfer deadlock fixed by lock ordering","lang":"java","code":"public class TransferService {\n\n    // BAD: T1 moves A to B while T2 moves B to A; each holds one monitor and waits for the other\n    public void transferBad(Account from, Account to, long cents) {\n        synchronized (from) {\n            synchronized (to) {\n                from.debit(cents);\n                to.credit(cents);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: always lock in a global order (account id), so no cycle can form\n    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, long cents) {\n        Account first = from.id() < to.id() ? from : to;\n        Account second = first == from ? to : from;\n        synchronized (first) {\n            synchronized (second) {\n                from.debit(cents);\n                to.credit(cents);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The same rule applies in PostgreSQL: lock rows in a consistent order (for example `SELECT ... ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE`), otherwise the database detects the cycle and aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`."},{"title":"What the deadlock looks like in a thread dump","lang":"text","code":"$ jcmd 41872 Thread.print\n...\nFound one Java-level deadlock:\n=============================\n\"transfer-worker-1\":\n  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f2a6c003f80 (object 0x0000000712a4b6e8, a com.acme.bank.Account),\n  which is held by \"transfer-worker-2\"\n\n\"transfer-worker-2\":\n  waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f2a6c006a10 (object 0x0000000712a4b700, a com.acme.bank.Account),\n  which is held by \"transfer-worker-1\"\n\nJava stack information for the threads listed above:\n===================================================\n\"transfer-worker-1\":\n        at com.acme.bank.TransferService.transferBad(TransferService.java:7)\n        - waiting to lock <0x0000000712a4b6e8> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n        - locked <0x0000000712a4b700> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n\"transfer-worker-2\":\n        at com.acme.bank.TransferService.transferBad(TransferService.java:7)\n        - waiting to lock <0x0000000712a4b700> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n        - locked <0x0000000712a4b6e8> (a com.acme.bank.Account)\n\nFound 1 deadlock.","note":"Read it as a wait-for graph: worker 1 waits for the object worker 2 locked and vice versa. The JVM reports cycles over monitors and `java.util.concurrent` locks, but not pool starvation or database waits."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you identify a deadlock from a thread dump? What is livelock?","a":"Take a dump with `jcmd <pid> Thread.print` or `jstack -l <pid>`; HotSpot prints a `Found one Java-level deadlock` section naming each thread, the monitor or `ReentrantLock` it waits for and which thread owns it, which is the wait-for cycle. Without that banner, look for BLOCKED threads `waiting to lock <0x...>` whose address shows up as `locked <0x...>` in another stuck thread, and take two or three dumps a few seconds apart to separate a real deadlock from heavy contention. A **livelock** is the opposite symptom: threads stay RUNNABLE and keep reacting to each other (for example both release and retry `tryLock` in lockstep) but make no progress; randomized backoff with jitter breaks the symmetry."}],"extra":["**Thread starvation deadlock:** a task in a bounded pool that submits a subtask to the same pool and blocks on `get()` can hang with no locks at all once every thread is waiting; the JVM deadlock detector will not report it.","**Connection pool deadlock:** a thread holding one Hikari connection that opens a `REQUIRES_NEW` transaction needs a second one; under load every connection is held by a thread waiting for another, visible as threads parked in `HikariPool.getConnection`.","**Database deadlocks:** PostgreSQL checks for lock cycles after `deadlock_timeout` (1s by default) and aborts one transaction; the application should retry the whole transaction, and consistent row ordering removes most occurrences.","**Races across instances:** with several pods, in-JVM locks do nothing; use a unique constraint, optimistic locking with `@Version`, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` or an idempotency key table.","**Testing races:** they rarely show in unit tests; use stress tests with many iterations and threads, `jcstress` for memory-model level behavior, and review specifically for check-then-act patterns."],"redFlags":["Defines only symptoms","proposes sleeping or adding more locks","cannot describe a wait-for cycle"]},{"id":24,"category":"Concurrency","question":"How would you size a thread pool for CPU-bound vs I/O-bound work?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Size CPU-bound pools near the core count; I/O-bound pools can be larger in proportion to wait versus compute time, but downstream capacity is the real ceiling, so bound the queue and validate under load.","answer":["For **CPU-bound** work (scoring, encryption, transforming large payloads), more threads than cores only add context switches and cache misses, so size near `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()`, perhaps plus one. In a container this value follows the CPU quota, and with Kubernetes CPU limits too many busy threads cause CFS throttling and latency spikes.","For **I/O-bound** work the formula from Java Concurrency in Practice is `threads = cores * targetUtilization * (1 + wait / compute)`: a task that computes for 5 ms and waits 95 ms on HTTP can keep about 20 threads per core busy. Little's law gives the same answer from the load side: required concurrency equals throughput times latency, so 400 requests per second at 100 ms needs about 40 in flight.","Formulas give an upper bound; downstream capacity sets the real one. If the pool calls PostgreSQL through a Hikari pool of 20 connections, 200 threads just queue on `getConnection`. The same applies to HTTP client connection limits and a partner's rate limit. Use a bounded queue with an explicit rejection policy, separate pools per dependency (bulkheads) so one slow partner cannot starve everything, and then measure queue wait, active threads, p99 latency and downstream saturation in a load test. Tune from data, not a big round number."],"keyPoints":["CPU-bound near core count","I/O-bound may use more threads based on wait/compute ratio","consider downstream capacity, queue bounds, latency and measurement"],"examples":[{"title":"CPU-bound and I/O-bound executors sized differently","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass ExecutorConfig {\n\n    // CPU-bound: in-memory risk scoring\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ExecutorService scoringExecutor() {\n        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();   // follows the container CPU quota\n        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(cores, cores, 0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(cores * 4),\n                new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());\n    }\n\n    // I/O-bound: credit bureau calls, about 5 ms CPU and 95 ms waiting each\n    // formula: cores * (1 + 95 / 5), then capped by the HTTP client's connection limit\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ExecutorService bureauExecutor(@Value(\"${bureau.max-connections:20}\") int maxConnections) {\n        int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();\n        int threads = Math.min(cores * (1 + 95 / 5), maxConnections);\n        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(threads, threads, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100),\n                new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy());\n    }\n}","note":"The I/O pool is capped by what the downstream accepts, not by the formula. Both queues are bounded, so saturation shows up as rejections you can count and map to 503, not as silent latency."},{"title":"Worked sizing for one dependency","lang":"text","code":"Sizing the credit bureau pool (4 vCPU pods, 6 replicas)\n\nMeasured per call:   compute ~4 ms, wait ~96 ms at p50, p99 latency 300 ms\nFormula bound:       4 cores * (1 + 96/4) = 100 threads per pod\nLittle's law:        peak 300 calls/s over 6 pods = 50 calls/s per pod\n                     50/s * 0.1 s = 5 in flight at p50, 50/s * 0.3 s = 15 at p99\nDownstream limit:    contract allows 120 concurrent calls in total = 20 per pod\n\nDecision:            core = max = 20 threads, ArrayBlockingQueue(100), AbortPolicy to 503 + Retry-After\n                     HTTP client max connections per route = 20\n                     alert on queue wait p99 > 50 ms and on any rejections","note":"The formula says 100, the load needs about 15, the partner allows 20: the smallest meaningful limit wins. Revisit when latency or traffic changes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if executor threads greatly exceed DB connections?","a":"The extra threads block in `getConnection()` until a connection frees up or Hikari's `connectionTimeout` (30 seconds by default) expires, then fail with `SQLTransientConnectionException`. Throughput does not improve because the database is the bottleneck, while latency, thread stack memory and timeout errors all grow, and the waiting hides the real saturation point. It can even deadlock if a thread already holding a connection needs a second one. Size the executor to the connection pool, or cap DB work with a semaphore, and size the pool to what the database can execute in parallel."},{"q":"Why are bounded queues important?","a":"An unbounded queue turns overload into invisible, growing latency and eventually an `OutOfMemoryError`, and tasks may run long after the client timed out, which is pure wasted work. A bound converts overload into an immediate, explicit signal (a rejection mapped to 503 or 429, or caller-runs throttling), which is backpressure the system can act on. With `ThreadPoolExecutor` it also matters mechanically: threads above `corePoolSize` are only created when the queue is full, so with an unbounded queue `maximumPoolSize` is never used."}],"extra":["**Container awareness:** since JDK 10 the JVM reads cgroup CPU limits, so `availableProcessors()` in a pod limited to 2 CPUs returns 2; `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount` overrides it when the detection is wrong for your workload.","**Common pool:** `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` defaults to parallelism of cores minus one and is shared by parallel streams and default `CompletableFuture` async methods, so never run blocking I/O there.","**Database side:** the HikariCP pool sizing guidance starts around `(core_count * 2) + effective_spindle_count` connections per database server, and PostgreSQL `max_connections` is shared by every replica, so pool size times pod count must fit.","**Virtual threads change the question:** for blocking I/O on Java 21 you can use one virtual thread per task and cap concurrency with a `Semaphore` sized to the downstream limit instead of tuning a pool size.","**Bulkheads:** giving each downstream its own bounded executor (or a Resilience4j bulkhead) means a slow dependency exhausts only its own threads, not the servlet container's request threads."],"redFlags":["Uses an arbitrary large pool","ignores DB/HTTP connection pools and unbounded queues"]},{"id":25,"category":"Concurrency","question":"Explain ThreadPoolExecutor queue and rejection choices.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"ThreadPoolExecutor fills core threads, then the queue, then grows to max, then rejects; choose a bounded queue and a deliberate rejection policy so overload becomes visible backpressure, not growing latency and memory.","answer":["On `execute()` the executor follows a fixed order: fewer than `corePoolSize` threads running, start a thread; otherwise offer the task to the queue; if the queue refuses because it is full, start a thread up to `maximumPoolSize`; if that is reached too, call the `RejectedExecutionHandler`. So the queue type decides how core and max interact. `SynchronousQueue` hands off directly and grows threads at once (with an unlimited max, as in `newCachedThreadPool`, that means unbounded threads). An unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue`, as in `newFixedThreadPool`, never fills, so max is ignored. A bounded `ArrayBlockingQueue` gives you both limits.","Unbounded queues hide overload: latency grows first, then heap, and callers time out while their tasks still wait. A bounded queue plus a deliberate rejection policy is backpressure. `AbortPolicy` (the default) throws `RejectedExecutionException`, which you map to 503 or a retry. `CallerRunsPolicy` runs the task on the submitting thread, which slows the producer naturally. `DiscardPolicy` and `DiscardOldestPolicy` drop work silently; they are only acceptable for genuinely lossy work, with a metric on every drop.","Also name the threads, set `keepAliveTime` for burst threads, and check Spring defaults: a plain `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor` and Spring Boot's auto-configured `applicationTaskExecutor` both have an unbounded queue capacity unless you set it."],"keyPoints":["Core/max sizes interact with queue type","unbounded queues can hide overload","bounded queues plus a deliberate rejection policy create backpressure","CallerRuns can slow producers"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: bounded, observable executor","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass PaymentCallbackExecutorConfig {\n\n    // BAD: Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8) uses an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue,\n    // so a slow acquirer grows the backlog, latency and heap with no signal\n\n    // GOOD: bounded queue, explicit rejection that is counted\n    @Bean(destroyMethod = \"shutdown\")\n    ThreadPoolExecutor paymentCallbackExecutor(MeterRegistry registry) {\n        Counter rejected = registry.counter(\"payment.callback.rejected\");\n\n        RejectedExecutionHandler rejectAndCount = (task, executor) -> {\n            rejected.increment();\n            throw new RejectedExecutionException(\"payment callback pool saturated\");\n        };\n\n        ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(\n                8,                                  // core threads\n                16,                                 // max, only used once the queue is full\n                30, TimeUnit.SECONDS,               // idle burst threads retire after 30s\n                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),\n                Thread.ofPlatform().name(\"payment-callback-\", 0).factory(),\n                rejectAndCount);\n\n        new ExecutorServiceMetrics(executor, \"payment.callback\", List.of()).bindTo(registry);\n        return executor;\n    }\n}","note":"Micrometer's `ExecutorServiceMetrics` exposes pool size, active threads, queued tasks and remaining queue capacity; the JDK does not count rejections, so the handler does."},{"title":"Bounding Spring Boot's default async executor","lang":"yaml","code":"# Auto-configured applicationTaskExecutor, used by @Async\nspring:\n  task:\n    execution:\n      thread-name-prefix: async-\n      pool:\n        core-size: 8\n        max-size: 16\n        queue-capacity: 500        # default is unbounded, which makes max-size meaningless\n        keep-alive: 30s\n      shutdown:\n        await-termination: true\n        await-termination-period: 20s","note":"With no completions, tasks 9 to 508 queue, tasks 509 to 516 start burst threads, and task 517 is rejected with Spring's `TaskRejectedException`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When would CallerRunsPolicy help?","a":"When the producer is something you want to slow down and it is safe to block: a Kafka listener or batch job submitting work, or a file import loop. Once the pool and queue are full, the producer spends its time running a task instead of submitting more, which throttles intake without dropping anything (for Kafka it also delays the next poll, so stay well under `max.poll.interval.ms`). It is a poor fit on a Tomcat request thread, where it adds unpredictable latency to that request, and it is fatal on a Netty event loop. Remember that the task then runs with the caller's ThreadLocals and transaction context."},{"q":"What metrics should be monitored?","a":"Pool size against max, active threads, queue size and remaining capacity, completed tasks, and a rejection counter (count it in the handler, since the JDK does not). Add timers for queue wait and execution time, because rising queue wait is the earliest sign of saturation. Alert on sustained queue growth, any rejections and queue wait p99, and correlate with downstream metrics such as `hikaricp.connections.pending` or HTTP client pool usage."}],"extra":["**CallerRuns during shutdown:** `CallerRunsPolicy` silently discards the task if the executor is already shut down, so do not rely on it to guarantee execution during a rolling restart.","**Exceptions vanish with submit:** a task passed to `submit()` stores its exception in the returned `Future`, so if nobody calls `get()` the failure is invisible; `execute()` reaches the uncaught exception handler. Override `afterExecute` or wrap tasks to log failures.","**Prestart and core timeout:** `prestartAllCoreThreads()` avoids a cold start on the first burst, and `allowCoreThreadTimeOut(true)` lets a rarely used pool shrink to zero threads.","**Queue wait is the early warning:** time from submit to start rises before errors do; Micrometer's `ExecutorServiceMetrics.monitor(...)` wrapper records idle (queued) time and execution time as timers.","**Graceful shutdown:** call `shutdown()`, then `awaitTermination` with a deadline shorter than the pod's termination grace period, then `shutdownNow()`, so in-flight callbacks finish during a Kubernetes rolling update."],"redFlags":["Uses unbounded queue by default","ignores rejection or drops tasks silently"]},{"id":26,"category":"Concurrency","question":"What are common CompletableFuture mistakes in backend code?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Common mistakes: blocking work on the common pool, missing timeouts and error handling, join mid-pipeline, lost MDC and security context, unbounded fan-out; fix with explicit bounded executors, composed stages and deliberate failure handling.","answer":["`supplyAsync(fn)` without an executor runs on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`, sized to cores minus one and shared by parallel streams across the whole JVM. Put blocking HTTP or JDBC calls there and a few slow requests stall every other async user. Pass a dedicated, bounded executor per dependency, or on Java 21 a virtual-thread executor with a semaphore as the concurrency limit.","Failures are easy to lose. An exception completes the future exceptionally, wrapped in `CompletionException`; if no stage calls `exceptionally`, `handle` or `whenComplete` and nobody joins, it simply vanishes. There is no default timeout either: add `orTimeout` or `completeOnTimeout` (Java 9+) plus client-level timeouts, because completing the future does not stop the underlying call. Calling `join()` or `get()` inside a stage blocks a pool thread and can starve the pool; compose with `thenCompose`, `thenCombine` and `allOf`, and block once, at the edge, with a timeout. Nesting futures with `thenApply` instead of `thenCompose` is a common red flag.","Context does not follow the task: MDC, `SecurityContextHolder`, tracing spans and transactions are ThreadLocal-based, so logs lose the correlation id and a `@Transactional` boundary does not extend into async stages. Propagate with a `TaskDecorator` or Micrometer context propagation. Finally, one future per item for 10,000 orders floods the executor and the downstream service; batch or cap in-flight work."],"keyPoints":["Using the common pool for blocking work, forgetting exception handling/timeouts, blocking with join, losing context and creating uncontrolled fan-out","use explicit executors and compose stages"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: credit decision pipeline","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: common pool, blocking HTTP, no timeout, nested futures, failures lost\nCompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<Decision>> bad =\n        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> bureauClient.fetchReport(customerId))\n                .thenApply(report -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> scorer.score(report)));\n\n// GOOD: explicit executors, flat composition, timeout and a deliberate fallback\nCompletableFuture<Decision> decision =\n        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> bureauClient.fetchReport(customerId), bureauExecutor)\n                .orTimeout(800, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)\n                .thenCompose(report ->\n                        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> scorer.score(report), scoringExecutor))\n                .exceptionally(ex -> {\n                    log.warn(\"Credit decision degraded for customer {}\", customerId, ex);\n                    return Decision.manualReview(customerId);\n                });","note":"`orTimeout` only fails the future; the bureau client still needs its own read timeout so the socket and thread are actually released. The fallback is a business decision (manual review), not a silent default."},{"title":"Bounded fan-out with MDC propagation","lang":"java","code":"@Bean\nThreadPoolTaskExecutor pricingExecutor() {\n    ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();\n    executor.setCorePoolSize(16);\n    executor.setMaxPoolSize(16);\n    executor.setQueueCapacity(200);\n    executor.setThreadNamePrefix(\"pricing-\");\n    executor.setTaskDecorator(task -> {\n        Map<String, String> mdc = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();     // captured on the caller thread\n        return () -> {\n            if (mdc != null) MDC.setContextMap(mdc);\n            try { task.run(); } finally { MDC.clear(); }\n        };\n    });\n    return executor;\n}\n\nCompletableFuture<List<Price>> priceAll(List<String> skus) {\n    List<CompletableFuture<Price>> futures = skus.stream()\n            .map(sku -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> pricingClient.price(sku), pricingExecutor)\n                    .completeOnTimeout(Price.unavailable(sku), 300, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)\n                    .exceptionally(ex -> Price.unavailable(sku)))\n            .toList();\n    return CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new))\n            .thenApply(done -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());\n}","note":"The `join()` calls run only after `allOf` completed, so they never block. If a request brings more SKUs than the queue holds, `supplyAsync` throws `TaskRejectedException` immediately: chunk large lists instead of fanning out without limit."}],"followUps":[{"q":"thenApply vs thenCompose?","a":"`thenApply` maps the result with a synchronous function `T -> U`, like `map` on a stream. If that function itself returns a future, you get `CompletableFuture<CompletableFuture<U>>`. `thenCompose` takes `T -> CompletionStage<U>` and flattens, like `flatMap`, so it is the right choice for dependent async calls such as loading a customer and then fetching that customer's credit report."},{"q":"How would you cancel or timeout a dependency call?","a":"Put the primary timeout in the client itself (HTTP connect and read timeouts, JDBC `setQueryTimeout` or PostgreSQL `statement_timeout`), because only that frees the socket and the thread. On the future, use `orTimeout(800, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)` to fail with `TimeoutException`, or `completeOnTimeout(fallback, ...)` to degrade, and add a circuit breaker for repeated failures. `cancel(true)` on a `CompletableFuture` does not interrupt the worker; if you need interruption, submit to an `ExecutorService` and cancel the returned `Future`, or use a structured concurrency scope that interrupts its subtasks."}],"extra":["**Which thread runs a stage:** `thenApply` runs on whichever thread completes the previous stage (or the caller if it is already complete), while `thenApplyAsync` without an executor jumps to the common pool; pass your executor to the `*Async` variants too.","**cancel does not interrupt:** `CompletableFuture.cancel(true)` just completes the future with `CancellationException`; the `mayInterruptIfRunning` flag has no effect, so the running HTTP or SQL call continues.","**Unwrap before matching:** inside `handle` and `exceptionally` the throwable is usually a `CompletionException`; check `getCause()` before mapping to domain exceptions or HTTP status codes.","**Java 19+ helpers:** `Future.resultNow()`, `exceptionNow()` and `state()` inspect already completed futures after `allOf` without checked exceptions or blocking.","**Consider plain blocking code:** with virtual threads, straightforward blocking calls in `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` are often clearer than long future chains; `StructuredTaskScope` adds sibling cancellation but is still a preview API in Java 21."],"redFlags":["Calls supplyAsync without executor everywhere","no timeout or exceptionally/handle","uses nested futures instead of thenCompose"]},{"id":27,"category":"Concurrency","question":"What problem do Java virtual threads solve, and what do they not solve?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Virtual threads make blocking thread-per-request code scale to huge concurrency cheaply, but they add no CPU capacity, do not raise database or partner limits, and still suffer from pinning and heavy ThreadLocal use.","answer":["Platform threads map one to one to OS threads, reserve a large stack and are expensive to create, so servers cap them at a few hundred and waste them while waiting on I/O. Virtual threads (final in Java 21, JEP 444) are scheduled by the JVM onto a small pool of carrier threads. When a virtual thread blocks on a socket, lock or sleep, it unmounts and its stack is parked on the heap, freeing the carrier. You can create millions, so plain blocking code (JDBC, `RestClient`) handles high concurrency without reactive frameworks. In Spring Boot 3.2+, `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` switches Tomcat and `@Async` to them.","They do not make anything faster per request. CPU-bound work still needs cores, and more threads only add contention. Downstream limits stay: a Hikari pool of 20 connections still allows 20 concurrent queries, and 10,000 virtual threads will queue on it. Since you should not pool virtual threads, limit concurrency explicitly with a `Semaphore` or the connection pool.","Two gotchas remain. **Pinning:** on JDK 21, blocking inside `synchronized` or a native call keeps the carrier occupied, and with few carriers the application can stall (JDK 24 fixed the `synchronized` case). **ThreadLocal:** every virtual thread gets its own copy, so ThreadLocal caches of expensive objects multiply memory; pass context explicitly or use scoped values."],"keyPoints":["They make thread-per-task practical for blocking I/O and simplify code","they do not increase CPU capacity or downstream limits","pinning and ThreadLocal usage still matter"],"examples":[{"title":"Thread per task with an explicit downstream limit","lang":"java","code":"public class BureauBatchFetcher {\n\n    private final Semaphore bureauPermits = new Semaphore(50);   // partner allows 50 concurrent calls\n    private final BureauClient bureauClient;\n\n    public BureauBatchFetcher(BureauClient bureauClient) {\n        this.bureauClient = bureauClient;\n    }\n\n    public List<CreditReport> fetchAll(List<String> customerIds)\n            throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {\n        try (ExecutorService executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n            List<Future<CreditReport>> futures = customerIds.stream()\n                    .map(id -> executor.submit(() -> fetchWithPermit(id)))\n                    .toList();\n            List<CreditReport> reports = new ArrayList<>();\n            for (Future<CreditReport> future : futures) {\n                reports.add(future.get());\n            }\n            return reports;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private CreditReport fetchWithPermit(String customerId) throws InterruptedException {\n        bureauPermits.acquire();\n        try {\n            return bureauClient.fetch(customerId);   // blocking HTTP: the virtual thread unmounts\n        } finally {\n            bureauPermits.release();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Ten thousand ids create ten thousand cheap virtual threads, but only 50 talk to the bureau at once. `ExecutorService` is `AutoCloseable` since Java 19, and `close()` waits for submitted tasks."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: pinning on JDK 21","lang":"java","code":"public class BureauTokenProvider {\n\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final AuthClient authClient;\n    private AccessToken token;\n\n    public BureauTokenProvider(AuthClient authClient) {\n        this.authClient = authClient;\n    }\n\n    // BAD on JDK 21 to 23: an HTTP call inside synchronized pins the carrier thread\n    public synchronized AccessToken currentTokenPinned() {\n        if (token == null || token.expiresSoon()) {\n            token = authClient.fetchToken();\n        }\n        return token;\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: ReentrantLock lets the virtual thread unmount while it waits\n    public AccessToken currentToken() {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            if (token == null || token.expiresSoon()) {\n                token = authClient.fetchToken();\n            }\n            return token;\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Find pinning with the JFR event `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned`, or on JDK 21 with `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full`. In real code pick one of the two locks for the `token` field, never both."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Would you replace every executor? What is pinning?","a":"No. Replace thread-per-request and blocking I/O executors, but keep bounded platform pools for CPU-bound work, and turn pools that were really concurrency limits into semaphores rather than pools of virtual threads; event loops such as Netty stay as they are. **Pinning** means a virtual thread cannot unmount from its carrier: on JDK 21 this happens when it blocks inside a `synchronized` block or method, or during a native call, so the carrier is blocked too. Enough pinned threads exhaust the carriers and throughput collapses; fix it with `ReentrantLock` around the blocking section or by moving to JDK 24+."},{"q":"How do connection pools limit concurrency?","a":"A pool such as HikariCP hands out at most `maximumPoolSize` connections; any further caller waits in `getConnection()` until one is returned or `connectionTimeout` expires. With virtual threads the pool effectively becomes the semaphore for database work: 10,000 concurrent requests still run only N queries at a time and the rest wait with growing latency. Size the pool from database capacity (PostgreSQL `max_connections` is shared by all replicas), keep transactions short so connections return quickly, and use a sensible `connectionTimeout` to fail fast instead of letting waiters pile up."}],"extra":["**Carrier pool:** the default scheduler is a `ForkJoinPool` whose parallelism equals the number of available processors, tunable with the system property `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism`.","**Throughput, not latency:** by Little's law virtual threads raise how many requests can be in flight; a single request's latency is unchanged, so a benchmark at low load shows no difference.","**Do not pool them:** creating a virtual thread is cheap, so use `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` and express limits with semaphores; a fixed pool of virtual threads recreates the old problem.","**Scoped values:** `ScopedValue` is a preview API in Java 21 and final in JDK 25; it is an immutable, bounded alternative to ThreadLocal for request context such as tenant or principal.","**Library readiness:** older JDBC drivers and client libraries guarded socket I/O with `synchronized`, so upgrade dependencies and check for pinning under load before switching production traffic."],"redFlags":["Treats virtual threads as automatic performance improvement","ignores DB connection limits or CPU-bound work"]},{"id":28,"category":"Concurrency","question":"How do immutability and safe publication reduce concurrency bugs?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Immutable objects cannot be corrupted by concurrent writes, so they need no locking, and safe publication (final fields, volatile, locks or concurrent collections) guarantees other threads see them fully initialized.","answer":["Most concurrency bugs involve shared **mutable** state. An immutable object (all fields `final`, no setters, no leaked mutable internals, no `this` escape) has exactly one state, so any number of threads can share, cache and use it as a map key without synchronization. Updates become \"build a new object and swap the reference\", which turns a multi-field invariant into a single reference write.","Visibility is the other half. Finishing a constructor does not by itself make an object's state visible to other threads: store a reference to a mutable object in a plain field and another thread may see the reference but stale or default field values, because writes can be reordered. **Safe publication** creates the happens-before edge: initialize in a static initializer, store the reference in a `volatile` field or `AtomicReference`, store it in a `final` field of a properly constructed object, publish under a lock that readers also take, or hand it over through a concurrent collection or `BlockingQueue`.","`final` fields get special treatment in the JMM: if `this` does not escape during construction, any thread that sees the reference sees the final fields, and objects reachable through them, as initialized. Records give you final fields for free but are shallow: a `List` component still needs `List.copyOf`. In practice: immutable value objects and config snapshots, published through a volatile reference, with defensive copies at the boundaries."],"keyPoints":["Immutable objects avoid synchronization for state changes","safe publication through final fields, synchronization, volatile or concurrent collections ensures other threads see initialized state"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: publishing FX rates to request threads","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: mutable object published through a plain field\nclass MutableFxRates {\n    Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = new HashMap<>();\n}\n\nclass FxRatesHolderBad {\n    MutableFxRates current = new MutableFxRates();     // no volatile, no lock\n\n    void reload(Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh) {\n        MutableFxRates next = new MutableFxRates();\n        next.rates.putAll(fresh);                       // readers may see the reference before these writes\n        current = next;\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: immutable snapshot published through a volatile reference\nrecord FxRates(Map<String, BigDecimal> rates, Instant loadedAt) {\n    FxRates {\n        rates = Map.copyOf(rates);                      // defensive, unmodifiable copy\n    }\n}\n\nclass FxRatesHolder {\n    private volatile FxRates current = new FxRates(Map.of(), Instant.EPOCH);\n\n    void reload(Map<String, BigDecimal> fresh) {\n        current = new FxRates(fresh, Instant.now());    // one swap of a complete snapshot\n    }\n\n    BigDecimal rate(String currencyPair) {\n        return current.rates().get(currencyPair);\n    }\n}","note":"In the BAD version a reader can observe the new holder with a partially filled or even null map. In the GOOD version readers never lock and always see one consistent snapshot, rates and timestamp together."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: this escaping from a constructor","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: this escapes before construction finishes\nclass SettlementListener implements Consumer<SettlementEvent> {\n    private final Map<String, SettlementEvent> pending;\n\n    SettlementListener(EventBus bus) {\n        bus.subscribe(this);                         // another thread may call accept() right now\n        this.pending = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();    // still null as seen by that thread\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void accept(SettlementEvent event) {\n        pending.put(event.paymentId(), event);       // NullPointerException in the race window\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: finish construction first, then publish from a factory method\nfinal class SafeSettlementListener implements Consumer<SettlementEvent> {\n    private final Map<String, SettlementEvent> pending = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    private SafeSettlementListener() { }\n\n    static SafeSettlementListener subscribeTo(EventBus bus) {\n        SafeSettlementListener listener = new SafeSettlementListener();\n        bus.subscribe(listener);\n        return listener;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void accept(SettlementEvent event) {\n        pending.put(event.paymentId(), event);\n    }\n}","note":"`EventBus` stands for any in-house event dispatcher. The final field guarantee only holds for objects whose `this` did not escape, so the private constructor plus factory is the robust fix."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is defensive copying?","a":"Copying mutable data at the boundary so outside code cannot change your object's state after you validated it: copy inputs in the constructor (`this.lines = List.copyOf(lines)`) and never return internal mutable structures from getters. Copy first, then validate the copy, otherwise a caller can change the data between your check and its use. With immutable components (`java.time` types, `List.of`, records of value types) no copy is needed, and `List.copyOf` may return the same instance when given an already unmodifiable list."},{"q":"How can this escape during construction?","a":"By registering `this` as a listener or callback, starting a thread in the constructor that uses the object, storing `this` in a static field or shared collection, or creating an inner class or lambda that captures `this` and handing it out. Calling an overridable method from a constructor is a related trap: the subclass override runs before the subclass fields are initialized. Once `this` escapes, the final field guarantee no longer holds for other threads; fix it with a private constructor plus a static factory, or a separate `start()` step (in Spring, work that needs a finished bean goes in `@PostConstruct` or an `ApplicationReadyEvent` listener)."}],"extra":["**Unmodifiable view vs copy:** `Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` is a read-only view that still changes when the backing list changes; `List.copyOf` and `Map.copyOf` create real immutable copies and reject nulls.","**Records are shallow:** a record with a `List`, array or `Date` component is only as immutable as that component; normalize in the compact constructor and prefer `java.time` types over `Date`.","**Effectively immutable objects:** an object never modified after publication, such as a fully built `HashMap` handed to readers through a volatile field, is safe to read concurrently as long as the publication itself is safe.","**this-escape lint:** JDK 21 added `javac -Xlint:this-escape`, which warns when a constructor can leak `this` to subclass-overridable code before the subclass is initialized.","**Copy-on-write for read-mostly data:** an immutable snapshot in an `AtomicReference` updated with `updateAndGet`, or a `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, suits routing tables, feature flags and listener lists where reads vastly outnumber writes."],"redFlags":["Assumes constructor completion alone always guarantees visibility","exposes mutable internals"]},{"id":29,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Composition vs inheritance: which should be the default?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Default to composition, which couples you only to a public contract and can change at runtime; use inheritance only for a genuine, substitutable is-a relationship with a stable base class designed for extension.","answer":["Inheritance couples a subclass to its parent's implementation, not just its API. When the base class changes how its methods call each other, subclasses break: the **fragile base class** problem. The classic case is overriding `add` and `addAll` on a `HashSet` to count insertions and getting double counts, because the inherited `addAll` calls `add`. Inheritance also fixes behavior at compile time and exposes every inherited public method, including ones that make no sense for the subtype.","Composition holds a reference to a collaborator and forwards to its public contract. You choose which operations to expose, can swap implementations at runtime (strategy, decorator), test with a simple fake, and the collaborator can change internally without breaking you. That is why Effective Java says to favor composition over inheritance, and to design and document for inheritance or else prohibit it by making classes `final`.","Inheritance is right when the subtype is truly substitutable (it honors every contract of the base), the base contract is stable, and the base class is designed for extension, typically a template method with protected hooks, as in Spring's `OncePerRequestFilter` or `AbstractRoutingDataSource`. Inheriting just to reuse a few helper methods is the red flag. In modern Java, sealed interfaces with records model closed hierarchies (payment methods, decision outcomes) through interface subtyping rather than implementation inheritance."],"keyPoints":["Composition is more flexible and reduces coupling","inheritance is appropriate for a true substitutable is-a relationship with stable base contracts","prefer composition by default"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: fragile base class vs composition","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: inheriting for reuse couples you to HashSet's internal use of add()\nclass CountingOrderSet extends HashSet<Order> {\n    private int added;\n\n    @Override public boolean add(Order order) { added++; return super.add(order); }\n\n    @Override public boolean addAll(Collection<? extends Order> orders) {\n        added += orders.size();\n        return super.addAll(orders);     // inherited addAll calls add(): every order counted twice\n    }\n\n    int added() { return added; }\n}\n\n// GOOD: compose, and expose only what the domain needs\nfinal class OrderBatch {\n    private final Set<Order> orders = new HashSet<>();\n    private int added;\n\n    boolean add(Order order) {\n        boolean changed = orders.add(order);\n        if (changed) added++;\n        return changed;\n    }\n\n    void addAll(Collection<Order> batch) { batch.forEach(this::add); }\n\n    int added() { return added; }\n\n    Set<Order> view() { return Collections.unmodifiableSet(orders); }\n}","note":"The BAD class also exposes `remove`, `clear` and `retainAll`, which the domain never wanted. The GOOD class does not care how `HashSet` is implemented and could switch to a `LinkedHashSet` without affecting callers."},{"title":"Subtyping that is appropriate: a sealed hierarchy","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface PaymentMethod permits Card, BankTransfer, Wallet {}\n\nrecord Card(String token, YearMonth expiry) implements PaymentMethod {}\nrecord BankTransfer(String iban) implements PaymentMethod {}\nrecord Wallet(String provider, String walletId) implements PaymentMethod {}\n\nfinal class FeeCalculator {\n\n    BigDecimal feeFor(PaymentMethod method, BigDecimal amount) {\n        return switch (method) {\n            case Card card -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.014\"));\n            case BankTransfer transfer -> new BigDecimal(\"0.20\");\n            case Wallet wallet -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.019\"));\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Each record truly is a payment method and shares no implementation, only a type. Because the hierarchy is sealed, the `switch` is exhaustive and adding a new method is a compile error until every switch handles it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Give an example where inheritance violates LSP. When is an abstract base class appropriate?","a":"A `ReadOnlyAccount extends Account` that throws on `withdraw` breaks every caller written against `Account`; in the JDK, `java.util.Stack extends Vector` lets callers insert at any index and break LIFO, and `java.sql.Timestamp extends Date` has an `equals` that is not symmetric with `Date`. An abstract base class is appropriate when subclasses share state and a fixed algorithm that you control, such as a `final charge()` template method calling a protected `doCharge()` hook, all within one module or team and with the extension points documented. Otherwise use an interface plus composition, and keep concrete classes `final`."}],"extra":["**Default methods are not free reuse:** interface default methods share behavior without state, but a default added later is silently overridden by any implementation that already has a method with that signature, possibly with different semantics.","**JPA inheritance cost:** `SINGLE_TABLE`, `JOINED` and `TABLE_PER_CLASS` mappings add nullable columns, joins or unions to every polymorphic query; many teams prefer composition with an embedded type or a type column plus a strategy.","**Decorators beat subclasses for cross-cutting concerns:** caching, retries or metrics around a `PaymentGateway` are cleaner as a decorator implementing the same interface than as a subclass of one concrete gateway.","**Spring proxies:** Spring AOP uses composition (JDK dynamic proxies) or generated subclasses (CGLIB); the subclass approach is why `final` classes and methods cannot be advised through CGLIB proxies."],"redFlags":["Uses inheritance only for code reuse","cannot discuss substitutability or fragile base classes"]},{"id":30,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Explain the Single Responsibility Principle without saying 'one method per class'.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"SRP means a module has one reason to change because it serves one actor or business responsibility; it is about cohesion around who requests changes, not about making classes small.","answer":["Robert Martin's refined wording is that a module should be responsible to one, and only one, **actor**: the group of stakeholders whose requests change it. The useful question is not \"does this class do one thing?\" but \"who would ask me to change this code, and for what reasons?\" If pricing rules come from the commercial team, the settlement file format from finance and the email template from marketing, putting them in one class means a change for one actor risks breaking the others, and their changes collide in merges and releases.","The goal is **high cohesion**: things that change together live together; things that change for different reasons are separated. A 400-line `CreditPolicy` class encoding one coherent set of lending rules can satisfy SRP perfectly, while ten tiny classes that must always change together violate its spirit and cause shotgun surgery. Size is a symptom, not the rule.","Practical signals of a violation: a class named with \"And\" or \"Manager\", tests that need many unrelated mocks, git history where one file changes for unrelated tickets, and several teams editing the same service. The fix is to split along change axes (domain rules, persistence, integration, notification) with a thin application service that orchestrates the use case."],"keyPoints":["A module should have one coherent reason to change and align with one actor/business responsibility","high cohesion matters more than class size"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: one service changed by three different actors","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: validation rules (product), SQL (DBA) and customer emails (CRM) in one class\n@Service\npublic class OrderServiceBad {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;\n    private final JavaMailSender mailSender;\n\n    public OrderServiceBad(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate, JavaMailSender mailSender) {\n        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;\n        this.mailSender = mailSender;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {\n        if (cmd.lines().isEmpty()) throw new InvalidOrderException(\"order has no lines\");\n        Order order = Order.from(cmd);\n        jdbcTemplate.update(\"insert into orders (id, customer_id, total) values (?, ?, ?)\",\n                order.id(), order.customerId(), order.total());\n        mailSender.send(confirmationEmail(order));   // SMTP failure rolls back a valid order\n        return order;\n    }\n\n    private SimpleMailMessage confirmationEmail(Order order) {\n        SimpleMailMessage message = new SimpleMailMessage();\n        message.setSubject(\"Order \" + order.id() + \" confirmed\");\n        return message;\n    }\n}","note":"Beyond mixed responsibilities, the failure semantics are wrong: an email outage now blocks order placement."},{"title":"GOOD: orchestrating use case plus focused collaborators","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class PlaceOrderService {\n    private final OrderValidator validator;           // business rules\n    private final OrderRepository orders;             // persistence\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;   // integration boundary\n\n    public PlaceOrderService(OrderValidator validator, OrderRepository orders,\n                             ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.validator = validator;\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {\n        validator.validate(cmd);\n        Order order = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.id(), order.customerId()));\n        return order;\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass OrderConfirmationNotifier {\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void on(OrderPlaced event) {\n        // render the template and send the email; failures are retried, never roll back the order\n    }\n}","note":"`AFTER_COMMIT` sends the email only for committed orders. If the notification must never be lost, write an outbox row in the same transaction instead of relying on an in-memory event."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Would validation, persistence and notification belong in one service?","a":"Not as implementation, because they change for different actors and have different failure semantics: validation rules change with the business, persistence with the schema, notification with channels and templates, and a failed email must not roll back a valid order. One application service can still **orchestrate** them, because running the \"place order\" use case is itself a single responsibility. The smell is a service that contains the rule details, the SQL and the email HTML itself."},{"q":"How would you split them?","a":"Put validation in the domain (invariants in the `Order` aggregate and value objects, with Bean Validation for request shape at the controller), persistence behind an `OrderRepository`, and notification in a separate component triggered by an `OrderPlaced` domain event. Publish the event after commit with `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, or through a transactional outbox if delivery must be guaranteed. The application service stays thin: validate, save, publish, inside one transaction."}],"extra":["**The actor test:** ask who requests changes to this code and who approves them; if the answer is two different teams or roles, you likely have two responsibilities in one module.","**SRP scales up:** the same reasoning applies to packages and services; a microservice owned by one team but changed by three is an SRP problem at the architecture level, closely tied to Conway's law.","**Do not split what changes together:** the Common Closure Principle is SRP applied to packages; spreading one cohesive rule set across many classes means every business change touches five files.","**AFTER_COMMIT listener detail:** when a `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` runs, the transaction has already committed, so database writes it makes need `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)` or they will not be committed.","**Testing payoff:** once split, validation rules get fast unit tests with no mocks, persistence gets `@DataJpaTest` or Testcontainers tests, and the orchestrator needs only a few collaborators."],"redFlags":["Equates SRP with tiny classes","cannot identify actors or change reasons"]},{"id":31,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"How does the Open/Closed Principle affect extensible business rules?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"OCP means designing stable extension points, such as policy or strategy interfaces, so a new business rule is added as new code rather than another branch in a central if/else, without abstracting what never varies.","answer":["The principle says code should be open for extension but closed for modification. In business systems it matters most where variation is frequent: discount types, credit policies per product or market, fee schedules, payment providers. If every new rule means editing the same `switch` in a central `PricingService`, each change risks breaking existing rules, forces retesting all of them and creates merge conflicts between teams.","The usual tool is a stable abstraction at the point of variation: a `DiscountPolicy` or `CreditPolicy` interface, one implementation per rule, and a coordinator that iterates over them or looks them up. In Spring, injecting `List<DiscountPolicy>` means a new rule is a new `@Component` and the coordinator never changes. How results combine (sum, best single discount, first rejection wins) is itself a policy and should be explicit.","The trade-off is indirection. An interface for every class, or a plugin framework for a rule that has never varied, is speculative generality. Introduce the extension point when the second or third variant appears, where the business actually varies. Consider alternatives too: rules business users change weekly may belong in data (a rule table or decision engine) rather than code, and for a closed set of types where you add operations more often than types, a sealed interface with an exhaustive `switch` is the better trade."],"keyPoints":["Stable abstractions allow adding behavior without repeatedly modifying central conditionals","strategy/policy objects can isolate variation, but over-abstraction should be avoided"],"examples":[{"title":"Discount policies injected as a list","lang":"java","code":"public interface DiscountPolicy {\n    boolean appliesTo(Order order);\n    BigDecimal discountFor(Order order);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass LoyaltyTierDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public boolean appliesTo(Order order) { return order.customer().tier() == Tier.GOLD; }\n    public BigDecimal discountFor(Order order) { return order.subtotal().multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.05\")); }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass BulkOrderDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public boolean appliesTo(Order order) { return order.lines().size() >= 20; }\n    public BigDecimal discountFor(Order order) { return new BigDecimal(\"25.00\"); }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PricingService {\n    private final List<DiscountPolicy> policies;   // Spring injects every DiscountPolicy bean\n\n    PricingService(List<DiscountPolicy> policies) { this.policies = policies; }\n\n    BigDecimal totalDiscount(Order order) {\n        BigDecimal sum = policies.stream()\n                .filter(policy -> policy.appliesTo(order))\n                .map(policy -> policy.discountFor(order))\n                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);\n        return sum.min(order.subtotal().multiply(new BigDecimal(\"0.30\")));   // explicit cap: 30%\n    }\n}","note":"A new campaign is a new `@Component`; `PricingService` and the existing policies are untouched. The combination rule (sum with a 30% cap) is explicit and tested once."},{"title":"Credit policies keyed by product","lang":"java","code":"public interface CreditPolicy {\n    String product();                                   // \"CARD\", \"LOAN\", \"BNPL\"\n    Decision evaluate(CreditApplication application);\n}\n\n@Service\nclass CreditDecisionService {\n    private final Map<String, CreditPolicy> byProduct;\n\n    CreditDecisionService(List<CreditPolicy> policies) {\n        this.byProduct = policies.stream()\n                .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(CreditPolicy::product, Function.identity()));\n    }\n\n    Decision decide(CreditApplication application) {\n        CreditPolicy policy = byProduct.get(application.product());\n        if (policy == null) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"No credit policy for \" + application.product());\n        }\n        return policy.evaluate(application);\n    }\n}","note":"`toUnmodifiableMap` throws on duplicate keys, so two beans claiming the same product fail at startup instead of in production traffic."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you implement multiple discount or credit policies without a giant if/else?","a":"Define a small interface such as `DiscountPolicy` with `appliesTo(Order)` and `discountFor(Order)`, implement one class per rule, and let Spring inject `List<DiscountPolicy>` into a `PricingService` that filters the applicable ones and combines them by an explicit rule (sum with a cap, or best single discount). For credit, key policies by product or market in a `Map<String, CreditPolicy>` built at startup, or chain them so the first rejection wins and each decision records which policy produced it. Keep parameters such as percentages and thresholds in configuration or data so tuning does not need new classes, and unit test each policy plus the combination rule."}],"extra":["**Ordering is part of the contract:** when policy order matters (first match wins), make it explicit with `@Order` or an `order()` method and test it; relying on bean registration order is fragile.","**Expression problem:** polymorphic OCP makes new types cheap and new operations expensive; sealed types plus pattern matching make the opposite trade, so choose by which axis changes more often.","**Shadow mode:** separate policy classes make it easy to run a new credit rule in parallel, logging its decision next to the live one, before switching traffic to it.","**Auditability:** in credit and payments, record which policy and version produced each decision; policy objects make that a natural field instead of a reconstruction from branches.","**Closed means verified:** the point is not that code never changes, but that tested, deployed behavior is not edited to add a rule, which shrinks review scope and regression risk."],"redFlags":["Creates an interface for every class","cannot explain trade-offs or real extension points"]},{"id":32,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Explain Liskov Substitution with a real failure example.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Liskov Substitution means code written against a base type must keep working with any subtype: a subtype may not demand more, promise less, break invariants, or throw exceptions callers were never told to expect.","answer":["LSP is about behavior, not signatures. The compiler checks that a subtype has the right methods; LSP asks whether it honors the base type's **contract**: preconditions (what callers must provide), postconditions (what the method guarantees), invariants (what always holds for the object) and the exceptions callers are prepared for. A subtype may accept more and guarantee more, never the reverse: no **stronger preconditions**, no **weaker postconditions**.","A real failure: the `PaymentGateway.refund(paymentId, amount)` contract allows partial refunds. A new voucher provider adapter only supports full refunds, so it throws when the amount is less than the captured amount. It compiles, passes its own tests, and the first partial refund on that provider fails in production inside a flow that never expected the exception. The subtype strengthened the precondition. Similar failures: a caching repository whose `save` is asynchronous, so `findById` right after `save` returns empty (weakened postcondition), or an adapter that returns null where the contract says never null.","Rectangle and Square only illustrates this if you name the contract: `setWidth` promises height stays unchanged, and a square cannot keep that promise. The fix is usually to model capabilities honestly, for example a separate interface for partial refunds, and to run one **contract test suite** against every implementation."],"keyPoints":["Subtypes must preserve base-type behavioral contracts, invariants and expectations","they must not strengthen preconditions or weaken postconditions"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: a gateway that strengthens the precondition","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentGateway {\n    /** Refunds any amount up to the captured amount. Never returns null. */\n    RefundResult refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\n// BAD: callers written against PaymentGateway now fail at runtime for this provider\nabstract class VoucherGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n\n    @Override\n    public RefundResult refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount) {\n        if (amount.compareTo(capturedAmount(paymentId)) != 0) {\n            throw new UnsupportedOperationException(\"voucher provider supports full refunds only\");\n        }\n        return fullRefund(paymentId);\n    }\n\n    abstract BigDecimal capturedAmount(String paymentId);\n\n    abstract RefundResult fullRefund(String paymentId);\n}","note":"Nothing in the type system warns you; only a contract test that runs a partial refund against every gateway implementation would catch it before production."},{"title":"GOOD: make the capability part of the contract","lang":"java","code":"// The base contract promises only what every provider can do\npublic interface PaymentGateway {\n    RefundResult refundInFull(String paymentId);\n}\n\n// Optional capability, discoverable before calling\npublic interface PartialRefunds {\n    RefundResult refundPartially(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\nclass RefundService {\n\n    RefundResult refund(PaymentGateway gateway, String paymentId,\n                        BigDecimal amount, BigDecimal captured) {\n        if (amount.compareTo(captured) == 0) {\n            return gateway.refundInFull(paymentId);\n        }\n        if (gateway instanceof PartialRefunds partial) {\n            return partial.refundPartially(paymentId, amount);\n        }\n        return RefundResult.storeCredit(paymentId, amount);   // explicit business fallback\n    }\n}","note":"Every `PaymentGateway` now honors its whole contract. Providers that can do more implement the extra interface, and the business decides the fallback instead of discovering it from a stack trace."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How can throwing UnsupportedOperationException violate LSP?","a":"If the base type's contract promises the operation, a subtype that throws `UnsupportedOperationException` has strengthened the precondition to \"never call this\", and every caller written against the base type now fails at runtime, where the compiler cannot help. It is usually a sign the base interface is too wide (an ISP problem) or the hierarchy is wrong. The JDK only avoids a formal violation by documenting collection mutators as \"optional operations\", which effectively weakens the `Collection` contract for every caller."},{"q":"What about read-only collections?","a":"`List.of`, `Collections.unmodifiableList` and `Stream.toList()` implement `List` but throw on `add`, `set` and `remove`. That is permitted by the documented optional-operation contract, so it is not formally an LSP break, but a method receiving a `List` cannot know whether it may mutate it, and the error appears at runtime. The practical rule: never mutate collections received as parameters, return new collections instead, and treat `List` in your APIs as read-only unless the contract explicitly says otherwise."}],"extra":["**Contract tests:** an abstract JUnit class holding the base-type expectations, extended once per implementation (including fakes used in other tests), turns LSP from a review opinion into a failing build.","**Exceptions are part of the contract:** a subtype that throws a new unchecked exception type the base never documented breaks callers' error handling just as surely as a wrong return value.","**equals and inheritance:** a subclass that adds a value field cannot preserve the `equals` contract (symmetry, transitivity) with its parent; `java.sql.Timestamp` and `java.util.Date` are the JDK's own example, and records avoid it by being final.","**What Java does enforce:** overrides may return a narrower type and declare fewer checked exceptions, which is LSP expressed in the type system; everything else must come from documentation and tests.","**History constraint:** a mutable subtype of a type documented as immutable violates LSP even without overriding anything, because callers rely on the value never changing."],"redFlags":["Only repeats 'child can replace parent' without behavior","uses Rectangle/Square but cannot explain the contract"]},{"id":33,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Interface Segregation: how do large interfaces hurt a codebase?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Clients should depend only on the operations they use; large interfaces create accidental coupling, bloated test fakes and UnsupportedOperationException stubs, so split interfaces by client role, not mechanically.","answer":["A large interface couples every client to every operation. A reporting job that needs `dailyStats` also compiles against `refund`, so it can call it, its tests must fake a 15-method type, and a signature change it never uses still forces a change and redeploy. Implementations suffer too: a read-only replica adapter or an in-memory fake must implement write methods it cannot support, which produces `UnsupportedOperationException` stubs, an LSP violation waiting to happen.","Interface Segregation says to shape interfaces from the **client's** point of view: role interfaces such as `PaymentCommands` for checkout and refunds, and `PaymentQueries` for status pages and support tools. One class can still implement several roles, so segregation does not force duplication; it limits what each client can see. It also clarifies permissions and boundaries: a query endpoint that only has `PaymentQueries` injected cannot refund anything.","The opposite mistake is mechanical splitting: one interface per method, grouped by nothing. That multiplies types without reducing coupling. Group operations used together by the same kind of client and changing together. The signals that a split is due are concrete: different clients using disjoint subsets, fakes full of unused stubs, or implementations that can only support half the methods."],"keyPoints":["Clients should depend only on capabilities they use","focused interfaces reduce accidental coupling, fake implementations and UnsupportedOperationException"],"examples":[{"title":"Refactoring a fat PaymentService into role interfaces","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: every client sees every operation\npublic interface PaymentService {\n    Payment authorize(AuthorizeCommand command);\n    Payment capture(String paymentId);\n    Refund refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n    Optional<Payment> findById(String paymentId);\n    Page<Payment> search(PaymentFilter filter, Pageable pageable);\n    PaymentStats dailyStats(LocalDate day);\n}\n\n// GOOD: interfaces shaped by the clients that use them\npublic interface PaymentCommands {\n    Payment authorize(AuthorizeCommand command);\n    Payment capture(String paymentId);\n    Refund refund(String paymentId, BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\npublic interface PaymentQueries {\n    Optional<PaymentView> findById(String paymentId);\n    Page<PaymentView> search(PaymentFilter filter, Pageable pageable);\n}\n\n@RestController\nclass PaymentStatusController {\n    private final PaymentQueries payments;   // cannot call refund(), and its test fake has two methods\n\n    PaymentStatusController(PaymentQueries payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n}","note":"The query side now returns a read model (`PaymentView`) and can be served by a read replica or projection without touching the command side. `dailyStats` moves to a reporting interface used only by the reporting job."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you refactor a PaymentService interface used by both query and command clients?","a":"First map which clients call which methods; that usually reveals a command group (authorize, capture, refund, used by checkout and back office) and a query group (find, search, used by status pages and support). Extract `PaymentCommands` and `PaymentQueries`, let the existing implementation implement both, and temporarily make `PaymentService` extend both so nothing breaks. Then switch each client to inject only the role it needs, delete the old interface once unused, and optionally give the query side its own implementation returning read DTOs, possibly from a read replica or projection."}],"extra":["**Spring Data supports it:** extend the marker interface `Repository<Order, Long>` and declare only `findById` and `save`, instead of inheriting the long method list of `JpaRepository`, including `deleteAll`.","**Read models:** a separate query interface can return DTOs or projections under `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`, which is hard while one interface returns managed entities to both sides.","**Migrating without breaking clients:** temporarily keep `interface PaymentService extends PaymentCommands, PaymentQueries`, mark it `@Deprecated`, and move clients over one at a time.","**Security surface:** narrower injected types reduce the blast radius of a bug or misuse, which matters in payment and credit systems where refund or approval rights are sensitive.","**One-method roles are fine when a client needs exactly that:** an `ExchangeRateProvider` or a `Supplier<Instant>` used as a clock is a legitimate ISP result; the problem is splitting with no client in mind."],"redFlags":["Splits interfaces mechanically into one-method interfaces","cannot connect interface design to clients"]},{"id":34,"category":"OOP & SOLID","question":"Dependency Inversion vs dependency injection: what is the difference?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Dependency Inversion is a design rule: high-level policy owns abstractions that low-level details implement; dependency injection is merely how objects receive collaborators, and a DI container does not create those boundaries for you.","answer":["**DIP** is about the direction of source-code dependencies. High-level modules (credit decision rules, order workflow) should not depend on low-level modules (HTTP clients, JPA, Kafka); both depend on abstractions, and the abstraction belongs to the policy side, expressed in its language. `CreditDecisionService` depends on a `CreditBureau` port returning a domain `CreditScore`; the HTTP adapter in the infrastructure package implements it. The source dependency points from infrastructure to domain, opposite to the call direction.","**DI** is a mechanism: an object receives its collaborators, ideally through the constructor, instead of creating or looking them up, and Spring's container automates the wiring. DI is how you usually realize DIP, but they are independent: you can inject a concrete `JdbcOrderDao`, or an interface returning `Page<OrderEntity>` that leaks JPA, and Spring wires it happily with no inversion at all. You can also satisfy DIP without any container by wiring objects by hand in a composition root.","So `@Autowired` everywhere proves nothing. What matters is where interfaces live and which types they expose: ports in the core module using domain types, adapters outside, and ideally no persistence or HTTP types in the domain. Enforce it with Gradle or Maven module boundaries or ArchUnit tests, because conventions alone erode."],"keyPoints":["DIP is a design principle where high-level policy depends on abstractions","DI is a mechanism for supplying dependencies","DI containers do not automatically create good boundaries"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: dependency injection without dependency inversion","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: wired by Spring, yet the policy depends directly on infrastructure details\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionServiceBad {\n\n    private final BureauRestClient bureauClient;                  // concrete HTTP client class\n    private final JpaRepository<ApplicationEntity, Long> repo;    // persistence types in the policy\n\n    public CreditDecisionServiceBad(BureauRestClient bureauClient,\n                                    JpaRepository<ApplicationEntity, Long> repo) {\n        this.bureauClient = bureauClient;\n        this.repo = repo;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(long applicationId) {\n        ApplicationEntity application = repo.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n        BureauScoreResponse response = bureauClient.getScore(application.getCustomerRef());  // vendor DTO\n        return response.getScore() >= 620 ? Decision.APPROVE : Decision.REFER;\n    }\n}","note":"Changing bureau vendor or persistence technology means editing the credit policy, and unit testing it needs HTTP and JPA fakes."},{"title":"GOOD: port owned by the domain, adapter in infrastructure","lang":"java","code":"// domain: the policy owns the port, expressed in domain types; no Spring, no HTTP\npublic interface CreditBureau {\n    CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId);\n}\n\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private final CreditBureau bureau;\n    public CreditDecisionService(CreditBureau bureau) { this.bureau = bureau; }\n\n    public Decision decide(CreditApplication application) {\n        CreditScore score = bureau.scoreFor(application.customerId());\n        return score.value() >= 620 ? Decision.APPROVE : Decision.REFER;\n    }\n}\n\n// infrastructure: the detail depends on the domain abstraction\n@Component\nclass HttpCreditBureauAdapter implements CreditBureau {\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n    HttpCreditBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.internal\").build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditScore scoreFor(CustomerId customerId) {\n        BureauScoreResponse body = restClient.get().uri(\"/v2/scores/{id}\", customerId.value())\n                .retrieve().body(BureauScoreResponse.class);\n        return new CreditScore(body.score());\n    }\n}\n\n// configuration: DI wires the plain domain service to the adapter\n@Configuration\nclass CreditConfig {\n    @Bean\n    CreditDecisionService creditDecisionService(CreditBureau bureau) { return new CreditDecisionService(bureau); }\n}","note":"The domain class has no annotations and can be unit tested with a lambda such as `id -> new CreditScore(700)`. Spring (DI) only connects the pieces; the inversion comes from where `CreditBureau` lives and what it returns."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where should a repository port live in hexagonal architecture?","a":"In the application or domain core, next to the use cases that need it, defined in domain terms: `OrderRepository.findById(OrderId)` returning a domain `Order`, not a JPA entity or a `Page` of entities. The implementation is a driven (secondary) adapter in the infrastructure module, for example a JPA or jOOQ class that maps between persistence entities and the domain model. The core then compiles and tests without a database, and changing persistence technology touches only the adapter."},{"q":"Can new inside a factory still satisfy DIP?","a":"Yes. DIP constrains which modules the high-level policy depends on, not whether `new` appears somewhere. A factory or composition root in the outer layer can call `new JdbcOrderRepository(dataSource)` and hand it to the domain as an `OrderRepository`, which is exactly what Spring does for you. What violates DIP is the policy class itself instantiating a concrete detail; if the domain needs to create objects at runtime, give it a factory interface it owns and implement that in the infrastructure layer."}],"extra":["**Ownership is the test:** ask which module ships the interface; if the domain must import the adapter's package to see it, the dependency was hidden behind an interface, not inverted.","**ArchUnit rule:** `noClasses().that().resideInAPackage(\"..domain..\").should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAnyPackage(\"org.springframework..\", \"jakarta.persistence..\")` fails the build when a detail leaks into the core.","**Pragmatic middle ground:** many Spring teams let the domain use a Spring Data interface directly; that trades purity for less mapping code and is reasonable when the persistence and domain models are the same and unlikely to diverge, as long as it is a conscious decision.","**Constructor injection:** dependencies are explicit and `final`, the class works without the container in unit tests, and a constructor that keeps growing is an honest SRP warning.","**Service locator is not DI:** calling `applicationContext.getBean(...)` in business code hides dependencies and makes the domain depend on the container, which is the opposite of the goal."],"redFlags":["Treats Spring injection as proof of DIP","puts infrastructure interfaces in the wrong layer without reasoning"]},{"id":35,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Strategy vs a large switch statement: when is Strategy justified?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use Strategy when the variation is real, growing and needs independent tests, dependencies or runtime selection; keep a switch (ideally an exhaustive Java 21 pattern switch) for a small, stable, closed set of branches.","answer":["A switch keeps every variant in one method. That is easy to read with three stable branches and painful once each branch grows its own rules, collaborators and tests. **Strategy** puts each algorithm behind one interface (`CreditPolicy.evaluate(app)`), so every variant is a separate class with its own dependencies, unit tests and owner, and the caller selects one at runtime by key, configuration or feature flag.","The justification is change pressure, not elegance. Strategy pays off when variants are added regularly (new segments, markets, providers), when variants need different dependencies (one policy calls a credit bureau, another reads internal limits), when you must switch at runtime or run an A/B test, or when different teams own different variants. For a stable two or three way branch with no dependencies it is overkill: a switch is more honest, and the extra indirection only hides the logic.","In Spring the strategies are beans, injected as `List<CreditPolicy>` and indexed by an explicit key each strategy declares. Build that index at startup and fail fast on duplicate or missing keys, so a misconfiguration breaks the deploy instead of silently routing a customer to the wrong policy. Avoid selection that depends on bean registration order or on the first strategy whose `supports()` returns true.","For a closed, compiler-checked set, Java 21 gives a middle ground: a sealed interface plus an exhaustive pattern switch has no `default`, and adding a variant becomes a compile error everywhere it is not handled."],"keyPoints":["Strategy encapsulates interchangeable algorithms, supports testing and runtime selection","use it when variation is real and growing, not for a trivial stable branch"],"examples":[{"title":"Credit policy per segment: Strategy with a fail-fast registry","lang":"java","code":"public enum Segment { RETAIL, SME, CORPORATE }\n\npublic record CreditApplication(String customerId, Segment segment, BigDecimal amount, int score) {}\npublic record CreditDecision(boolean approved, String policy, String reason) {}\n\npublic interface CreditPolicy {\n    Segment segment();\n    CreditDecision evaluate(CreditApplication app);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass SmeCreditPolicy implements CreditPolicy {\n    public Segment segment() { return Segment.SME; }\n    public CreditDecision evaluate(CreditApplication app) {\n        boolean ok = app.score() >= 620 && app.amount().compareTo(new BigDecimal(\"500000\")) <= 0;\n        return new CreditDecision(ok, \"SME_V3\", ok ? \"within SME limits\" : \"score or amount out of range\");\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class CreditPolicyRegistry {\n    private final Map<Segment, CreditPolicy> policies;\n\n    public CreditPolicyRegistry(List<CreditPolicy> all) {\n        this.policies = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(CreditPolicy::segment, p -> p,\n                (a, b) -> { throw new IllegalStateException(\"Duplicate policy for \" + a.segment()); },\n                () -> new EnumMap<>(Segment.class)));\n        EnumSet<Segment> missing = EnumSet.complementOf(EnumSet.copyOf(policies.keySet()));\n        if (!missing.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"No credit policy for \" + missing);\n    }\n\n    public CreditDecision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n        return policies.get(app.segment()).evaluate(app);\n    }\n}","note":"Selection is an explicit key lookup, not list order, and the constructor fails at startup on duplicates or gaps. `EnumSet.copyOf` throws on an empty collection, which is also a useful failure when no policies are registered."},{"title":"When a switch is the better tool: sealed types and an exhaustive switch","lang":"java","code":"public sealed interface FeeRule permits FlatFee, PercentageFee, TieredFee {}\npublic record FlatFee(BigDecimal amount) implements FeeRule {}\npublic record PercentageFee(BigDecimal rate) implements FeeRule {}\npublic record TieredFee(BigDecimal threshold, BigDecimal below, BigDecimal above) implements FeeRule {}\n\npublic final class Fees {\n    public static BigDecimal fee(FeeRule rule, BigDecimal amount) {\n        return switch (rule) {   // no default: a new FeeRule type is a compile error here\n            case FlatFee f -> f.amount();\n            case PercentageFee p -> amount.multiply(p.rate());\n            case TieredFee t -> amount.compareTo(t.threshold()) <= 0 ? t.below() : t.above();\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"Small, stable, dependency-free variants read better as data plus one exhaustive switch. Reach for Strategy when each branch needs its own collaborators or is added by different teams."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you select a credit policy by customer segment?","a":"Define a `CreditPolicy` interface with `segment()` and `evaluate()`, implement one bean per segment, and build an `EnumMap<Segment, CreditPolicy>` from the injected `List<CreditPolicy>`, throwing on duplicates and on segments with no policy. The service resolves the customer's segment and does a single map lookup. If selection depends on more than segment (country, product, experiment cohort), put that logic in a small resolver that computes the key, so policies stay unaware of how they were chosen. Store the chosen policy key and version with the decision for audit."},{"q":"How do you avoid hidden ordering?","a":"Do not rely on `List` injection order, `@Order` annotations scattered across classes, or a first-match `supports()` loop in which two strategies could both match. Select by an explicit, unique key and validate uniqueness and completeness at startup. When order genuinely matters, as in a rule chain, declare it in one place (a configuration list or a single `@Bean` method that assembles the chain) and pin it with a test that asserts the order."}],"extra":["**Expression problem:** Sealed types with pattern switches make adding operations cheap and adding variants expensive; Strategy is the opposite. Pick based on which axis changes more often in your domain.","**Enum strategy:** For small algorithms without dependencies, an enum with a per-constant method body (or a field holding a lambda) is a lightweight Strategy with exhaustiveness and fast `EnumMap` lookups.","**Map injection pitfall:** Spring can inject `Map<String, CreditPolicy>` keyed by bean name, but that ties routing to class or bean names that someone will rename; an explicit `segment()` key is safer and self-documenting.","**Not every family of classes is Strategy:** When several rules must all run in sequence (hard rules, then scoring, then affordability), you are in Chain of Responsibility territory; declare the order in one place instead of spreading `@Order` across classes.","**Observability and audit:** Record the selected strategy key and version on every decision (`policy=SME_V3`) in logs, metrics tags and the stored decision; in regulated credit flows you must be able to explain which rules ran."],"redFlags":["Adds patterns without need","cannot discuss discovery, ordering or dependency injection of strategies"]},{"id":36,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Factory Method vs Abstract Factory vs Builder.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Factory Method lets an implementation decide which single product to create, Abstract Factory creates a family of related products that must match, and Builder assembles one complex object step by step and validates it.","answer":["**Factory Method** defines a creation method whose override decides the concrete product: `createWriter()` in a CSV exporter returns a CSV writer, in a PDF exporter a PDF writer. The caller depends only on the product interface and polymorphism picks the class. In everyday Java the term is also used loosely for static factories such as `List.of()` or `Money.of(amount, currency)`, which add names, caching and the freedom to return a subtype.","**Abstract Factory** is one level up: an interface that creates several related objects that must be used together. A payment provider kit returning a matching API client, webhook signature verifier and error translator is an Abstract Factory; pairing one provider's verifier with another provider's client is exactly the bug it prevents. The cost is that adding a new product type to the family changes every concrete factory.","**Builder** solves a different problem: constructing one object with many parameters, several optional, and invariants across them. It replaces telescoping constructors, makes call sites readable, and should end in a `build()` that validates and returns an immutable object. A builder that produces a mutable JPA entity with no validation, or lets callers skip mandatory fields, just becomes a convenient way to create invalid state.","Rule of thumb: one product whose type varies, Factory Method; consistent families, Abstract Factory; one product with complex configuration, Builder. Spring DI replaces many hand-written factories, but the patterns still matter when the choice depends on runtime data."],"keyPoints":["Factory Method controls creation of one product through polymorphism","Abstract Factory creates related product families","Builder constructs complex objects step by step and handles optional parameters"],"examples":[{"title":"Builder with mandatory fields up front and validation in build()","lang":"java","code":"public final class PaymentRequest {\n    private final String orderId;\n    private final BigDecimal amount;\n    private final Currency currency;\n    private final String description;      // optional\n    private final Duration captureDelay;   // optional\n\n    private PaymentRequest(Builder b) {\n        this.orderId = b.orderId; this.amount = b.amount; this.currency = b.currency;\n        this.description = b.description; this.captureDelay = b.captureDelay;\n    }\n    public static Builder builder(String orderId, BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n        return new Builder(orderId, amount, currency);\n    }\n\n    public static final class Builder {\n        private final String orderId;\n        private final BigDecimal amount;\n        private final Currency currency;\n        private String description = \"\";\n        private Duration captureDelay = Duration.ZERO;\n\n        private Builder(String orderId, BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {\n            this.orderId = Objects.requireNonNull(orderId);\n            this.amount = Objects.requireNonNull(amount);\n            this.currency = Objects.requireNonNull(currency);\n        }\n        public Builder description(String d) { this.description = d; return this; }\n        public Builder captureDelay(Duration d) { this.captureDelay = d; return this; }\n        public PaymentRequest build() {\n            if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount must be positive\");\n            if (captureDelay.compareTo(Duration.ofDays(7)) > 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"capture delay too long\");\n            return new PaymentRequest(this);\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Mandatory fields are constructor arguments of the builder, so forgetting them does not compile; cross-field rules live in `build()`, and the product is immutable once built."},{"title":"Abstract Factory for a provider family, Factory Method for one product","lang":"java","code":"// Abstract Factory: every provider supplies a matching family of objects\npublic interface PaymentProviderKit {\n    PaymentClient client();\n    WebhookVerifier webhookVerifier();\n    ErrorTranslator errorTranslator();\n}\n\n// Factory Method: the subclass decides which writer the template method uses\npublic abstract class SettlementExporter {\n    public final byte[] export(SettlementReport report) {\n        ReportWriter writer = createWriter();\n        return writer.write(report);\n    }\n    protected abstract ReportWriter createWriter();\n}\n\npublic final class CsvSettlementExporter extends SettlementExporter {\n    @Override\n    protected ReportWriter createWriter() { return new CsvReportWriter(';'); }\n}","note":"The kit guarantees that a provider's client, verifier and translator are never mixed with another provider's; the exporter shows the classic polymorphic Factory Method inside a template method."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which would you use for different payment-provider clients?","a":"If each provider needs only a client, a Strategy behind a common `PaymentProvider` interface, instantiated by Spring and selected from a registry, is enough. If each provider comes with a family of objects that must match (API client, webhook verifier, error translator, idempotency key format), an Abstract Factory per provider, such as a `PaymentProviderKit`, guarantees consistency. Inside each client, Builder fits complex request objects with many optional fields such as 3DS data, metadata or capture delay."},{"q":"How does a record with named factories compare to Builder?","a":"A record with a compact constructor for validation and a few named static factories (`Refund.full(payment)`, `Refund.partial(payment, amount)`) is simpler, immutable by construction and reads well when there are few parameters or a handful of well-known combinations. It gets awkward with many optional fields, because you end up with many factories or positional nulls that are easy to swap by mistake. Builder wins with many optional parameters, and the two combine well: the builder can simply call the record's canonical constructor, which keeps validation in one place."}],"extra":["**Static factory vs GoF Factory Method:** Effective Java static factories (`Optional.of`, `Duration.ofSeconds`) are named constructors, while GoF Factory Method relies on overriding; interviewers sometimes probe whether you know the difference.","**Spring's factories:** `@Bean` methods and `FactoryBean<T>` are container-level factory methods, and `ObjectProvider<T>` gives lazy or optional creation without coupling business code to `ApplicationContext`.","**Lombok caveat:** `@Builder` generates no validation, so unset fields silently become null or zero; put checks in the constructor the builder calls, or write `build()` yourself, and avoid builders on JPA entities whose invariants belong in domain methods.","**Step builder:** When several fields are mandatory, a staged builder (each step returns a different interface) makes the required order and presence a compile-time guarantee, at the cost of more boilerplate.","**Immutability payoff:** A validated immutable product is safe to share across threads, cache and use as a map key, which is a concrete reason to prefer Builder plus final fields over setters."],"redFlags":["Defines all as 'object creation'","uses Builder for mutable entity lifecycle without validation"]},{"id":37,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Decorator vs Proxy: they look similar structurally, so how do their intents differ?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Both wrap the same interface, but a Decorator always delegates and adds behavior, while a Proxy stands in for the real object to control access to it: security, laziness, remoteness, caching or lifecycle.","answer":["Structurally they are identical: a class implements `ExchangeRateClient` and holds another `ExchangeRateClient`. The difference is intent. A **Decorator** adds responsibilities, is usually composed explicitly by the client or configuration, can be stacked (metrics around retry around the HTTP client), and always forwards the call, enriching it before or after. `BufferedInputStream` around a `FileInputStream` is the textbook case.","A **Proxy** controls access to a subject that callers treat as the real thing, and it may decide not to call the target at all. A protection proxy rejects unauthorized calls, a caching proxy answers from a cache, a virtual proxy delays creating an expensive object (Hibernate lazy associations), a remote proxy hides the network (a gRPC stub or a Feign client). The client usually does not know the proxy exists because a framework created it.","In Spring, `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable`, `@PreAuthorize` and `@Async` are implemented with AOP proxies (JDK dynamic proxies for interfaces, CGLIB subclasses otherwise). The practical consequence you should mention: a call from one method to another inside the same bean bypasses the proxy, so the annotation silently does nothing. And not every Spring proxy adds behavior: a scoped proxy only routes each call to the right target instance, which is why calling every Spring proxy a decorator is wrong."],"keyPoints":["Decorator adds behavior dynamically","Proxy controls access, location, lifecycle or caching","both wrap the same interface but solve different problems"],"examples":[{"title":"Decorators: explicit, stackable, always delegating","lang":"java","code":"public interface ExchangeRateClient {\n    BigDecimal rate(Currency from, Currency to);\n}\n\n// Decorator: adds retry for transient failures, always calls the delegate\npublic final class RetryingExchangeRateClient implements ExchangeRateClient {\n    private final ExchangeRateClient delegate;\n    private final RetryTemplate retry;\n\n    public RetryingExchangeRateClient(ExchangeRateClient delegate, RetryTemplate retry) {\n        this.delegate = delegate;\n        this.retry = retry;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public BigDecimal rate(Currency from, Currency to) {\n        return retry.execute(ctx -> delegate.rate(from, to));\n    }\n}\n\n@Configuration\nclass FxClientConfig {\n    @Bean\n    ExchangeRateClient exchangeRateClient(HttpExchangeRateClient http, RetryTemplate retry, MeterRegistry registry) {\n        Timer timer = Timer.builder(\"fx.rate.latency\").register(registry);\n        ExchangeRateClient retrying = new RetryingExchangeRateClient(http, retry);\n        // Decorator as a lambda: metrics(retry(http)), order is visible in one place\n        return (from, to) -> timer.record(() -> retrying.rate(from, to));\n    }\n}","note":"Order is a design decision: the timer here measures total latency including retries. Retrying is fine for a read-only rate lookup; a payment charge would need an idempotency key first."},{"title":"Proxies: protection and caching, by hand and via Spring","lang":"java","code":"// Protection proxy: may refuse to call the real subject at all\npublic final class AuthorizingAccountQueries implements AccountQueries {\n    private final AccountQueries target;\n    private final AccessPolicy access;\n\n    public AuthorizingAccountQueries(AccountQueries target, AccessPolicy access) {\n        this.target = target;\n        this.access = access;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public AccountStatement statement(AccountId id, YearMonth month) {\n        if (!access.canRead(CurrentUser.get(), id)) {\n            throw new AccessDeniedException(\"No access to account \" + id);\n        }\n        return target.statement(id, month);\n    }\n}\n\n// Same concerns declared through Spring's AOP proxies\n@Service\nclass StatementService implements AccountQueries {\n    @Override\n    @PreAuthorize(\"@accessPolicy.canRead(authentication, #id)\")\n    @Cacheable(cacheNames = \"statements\", key = \"#id + ':' + #month\")\n    public AccountStatement statement(AccountId id, YearMonth month) {\n        return ledger.buildStatement(id, month);   // skipped entirely on a cache hit\n    }\n}","note":"A cache hit never reaches the target, which is the defining proxy behavior. Authorization must be evaluated before the cache, otherwise a cached statement can be served to a caller who may not see it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is transaction interception a proxy or decorator?","a":"Mechanically it is a proxy: Spring creates a JDK or CGLIB proxy the client is unaware of, and `TransactionInterceptor` controls the execution context of the target by beginning, committing or rolling back a transaction around the call. By intent it manages resource lifecycle rather than adding business behavior, which fits the proxy definition, even though the around advice looks decorator-like. The self-invocation limitation is the practical proof: only calls that pass through the proxy get a transaction."},{"q":"Give examples of caching and authorization wrappers.","a":"Caching: `@Cacheable` on a service method, or a hand-written `CachingExchangeRateClient` that checks a Caffeine cache and calls the delegate only on a miss; both are caching proxies because a hit never reaches the target. Authorization: `@PreAuthorize(\"hasRole('ADMIN')\")` enforced by Spring Security's method interceptor, or a hand-written wrapper that throws `AccessDeniedException` before delegating; both are protection proxies. When combining them, authorization must run before the cache lookup, otherwise cached data is returned without an access check."}],"extra":["**Self-invocation:** `this.otherMethod()` inside a proxied bean skips `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable` and `@Async`; fix it by moving the method to another bean, and remember AspectJ weaving removes the limitation entirely.","**CGLIB limits:** Spring Boot proxies classes with CGLIB by default (`spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true`), which cannot intercept `final` or `private` methods, a frequent reason annotations silently do nothing.","**Wrapper order changes semantics:** Retry outside a circuit breaker counts failures differently than retry inside it, and a cache outside authorization leaks data; make the order explicit with advisor order (`@EnableCaching(order = ...)`), `@Order`, or manual composition.","**Runtime building blocks:** `java.lang.reflect.Proxy` with an `InvocationHandler` backs interface-based proxies, and Hibernate lazy entities are generated subclasses, which is why `getClass()` on a lazy reference may not return the entity class and why `equals` should not rely on it.","**Classic examples:** Decorators include `BufferedReader`, `Collections.synchronizedList` and `HttpServletRequestWrapper`; proxies include `@Lazy` injection points, request-scoped proxies, Feign clients and gRPC stubs."],"redFlags":["Cannot distinguish intent","calls every Spring proxy a decorator"]},{"id":38,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"What risks come with the Observer pattern in a production system?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Observer removes compile-time coupling but hides runtime coupling: unknown listeners, leaks, undefined ordering, recursive updates and failure propagation, and making it asynchronous adds lost, duplicated, reordered and not-yet-committed events.","answer":["Subscribe and notify look trivial, but the pattern moves coupling from code you can read to runtime wiring you cannot. The publisher no longer knows who reacts, so a change to `OrderPlaced` can break a listener three modules away, and following a business flow means hunting for every subscriber. Listeners registered on long-lived subjects and never removed leak memory, and lambdas that capture request or session objects keep those alive too.","In-process delivery is usually synchronous: listeners run on the publisher's thread, inside its transaction, one after another. That brings ordering uncertainty (Spring orders listeners only through `@Order`), latency coupling (a slow email listener slows checkout), failure propagation (one exception stops the remaining listeners and can roll back the publisher's transaction), and recursive updates, where a listener changes state that fires the same event again.","Going asynchronous fixes latency and isolation but brings distributed systems problems: events lost on crash, delivered twice on retry, processed out of order, or seen before the state change is committed. A production design therefore decides per listener: critical reactions go through a transactional outbox or a broker with at-least-once delivery and idempotent consumers, best effort reactions use an after-commit listener, and every failure path has metrics, retries and a dead letter destination."],"keyPoints":["Hidden coupling, listener leaks, ordering uncertainty, recursive updates and failure propagation","asynchronous event systems add delivery and consistency concerns"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: side effects of a domain event and the transaction boundary","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderPlaced(UUID orderId, String customerEmail) {}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n\n    OrderService(OrderRepository orders, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Order place(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n        Order order = orders.save(Order.from(cmd));\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.getId(), cmd.email()));\n        return order;\n    }\n}\n\n// BAD: runs in the publisher's thread and transaction; if the mail server throws,\n// the order rolls back, and if the order rolls back later, the email was already sent\n@Component\nclass EmailOnOrderPlaced {\n    @EventListener\n    void on(OrderPlaced e) { mailer.sendConfirmation(e.customerEmail(), e.orderId()); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: runs only after a successful commit, on another thread, isolated from checkout\n@Component\nclass EmailAfterCommit {\n    @Async\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void on(OrderPlaced e) { mailer.sendConfirmation(e.customerEmail(), e.orderId()); }\n}","note":"The GOOD version is still best effort: a crash right after commit loses the email. If the reaction must happen, persist it with the order (outbox or Spring Modulith's event publication registry) and process it with retries."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How is in-process Observer different from publishing to Kafka?","a":"In-process events live in memory, share the publisher's thread, transaction and lifecycle, and vanish if the JVM dies; there is no replay and no consumer outside the process. Kafka makes events durable, replayable and consumable by independent services at their own pace, with ordering only within a partition and at-least-once delivery in typical setups, so consumers must be idempotent. It also introduces the dual write problem: committing to PostgreSQL and publishing to Kafka are not atomic, which is why the transactional outbox (often relayed with Debezium) exists. You trade operational cost and eventual consistency for decoupling in time and deployment."},{"q":"What happens when one listener fails?","a":"With Spring's default synchronous multicaster, the exception propagates to the publisher: later listeners are not invoked, and if the publisher runs in `@Transactional`, the whole business operation rolls back because of a side concern. After-commit listeners cannot undo the committed transaction, so their exceptions are typically just logged unless you persisted the work for retry. Async listeners fail in isolation, which protects the publisher but is easy to miss, so they need metrics, alerting and a retry or dead letter strategy. Decide explicitly for each listener whether its failure should fail the business operation."}],"extra":["**Silent drops:** `@TransactionalEventListener` defaults to `AFTER_COMMIT` and ignores events published outside a transaction unless `fallbackExecution = true`, which surprises teams when a non-transactional code path starts publishing.","**Spring 6.1 rule:** A `@TransactionalEventListener` method that is also `@Transactional` must use `REQUIRES_NEW` or `NOT_SUPPORTED`, otherwise the context fails at startup, because the original transaction has already completed when the listener runs.","**In-process outbox:** Spring Modulith's event publication registry persists each publication for transactional listeners in the same transaction and marks it complete on success, so incomplete events can be republished after a restart.","**Error routing:** Spring's `SimpleApplicationEventMulticaster` accepts an `ErrorHandler`; without one, a synchronous listener exception propagates to the publisher, and with `@Async` it goes to the `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, which by default only logs.","**Recursion guard:** Publish facts (`OrderPlaced`), not commands disguised as events, keep handlers idempotent, and avoid listeners that mutate the aggregate that emitted the event; a cycle shows up as `StackOverflowError` when synchronous and as an event storm when asynchronous."],"redFlags":["Only describes subscribe/notify","ignores lifecycle, errors and ordering"]},{"id":39,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Why is Singleton often problematic even when a framework uses singleton-scoped beans?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Classic Singleton is global state reached through a static accessor, which hides dependencies and breaks test isolation; a Spring singleton bean is fine because it is injected explicitly, but it must be stateless or thread-safe.","answer":["The problem with the GoF Singleton is not having one instance; it is how callers get it. `TaxRates.getInstance()` is a global variable: any class can reach it without declaring the dependency, tests cannot substitute it, state leaks between tests that share a JVM, and initialization order and lifecycle (close, reload, reconfigure) are hard-coded. If it holds mutable state, every thread shares it too.","A container-managed singleton removes most of that. Spring creates one instance per application context and consumers receive it through constructor injection, so the dependency is visible in the signature, replaceable in tests with a stub or `@MockitoBean`, and its startup and shutdown are managed by the container. That is why singleton is the default scope and why it is the right choice for services, repositories and clients.","What the container does not give you is thread safety. A singleton bean is called concurrently by every request thread, so it must be stateless (only final references to thread-safe collaborators and configuration) or protect its state with immutable snapshots, concurrent data structures or proper synchronization. Request data belongs in method parameters and local variables, or in a request-scoped bean, never in a field of a singleton."],"keyPoints":["Global mutable state, hidden dependencies and test isolation are the problem","container-managed singleton lifetime is acceptable when dependencies are explicit and the bean is stateless/thread-safe"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: request state in a singleton bean","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: request-specific state stored in fields of a singleton bean\n@Service\npublic class StatefulCreditLimitService {\n    private Customer currentCustomer;        // shared by every request thread\n    private BigDecimal requestedAmount;\n\n    public Decision decide(Customer customer, BigDecimal amount) {\n        this.currentCustomer = customer;     // thread A writes\n        this.requestedAmount = amount;\n        return evaluate();                   // thread B may have overwritten both fields\n    }\n\n    private Decision evaluate() {\n        return requestedAmount.compareTo(currentCustomer.limit()) <= 0\n                ? Decision.approve(currentCustomer.id())\n                : Decision.reject(currentCustomer.id());\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: stateless singleton, request data flows through parameters and locals\n@Service\npublic class CreditLimitService {\n    private final LimitRepository limits;    // thread-safe collaborator, assigned once\n\n    public CreditLimitService(LimitRepository limits) {\n        this.limits = limits;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(Customer customer, BigDecimal amount) {\n        BigDecimal limit = limits.limitFor(customer.id());\n        return amount.compareTo(limit) <= 0 ? Decision.approve(customer.id()) : Decision.reject(customer.id());\n    }\n}","note":"The BAD version passes every single-threaded test and fails only under concurrent load, where customer A can receive a decision computed with customer B's data."},{"title":"Hidden dependency vs explicit dependency","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: static accessor hides the dependency and cannot be replaced in a test\npublic class InvoiceService {\n    public Invoice issue(Order order) {\n        BigDecimal vat = TaxRates.getInstance().rateFor(order.country());\n        return Invoice.of(order, vat);\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: same single instance at runtime, but declared and injectable\n@Service\npublic class InvoiceService {\n    private final TaxRates taxRates;\n\n    public InvoiceService(TaxRates taxRates) {\n        this.taxRates = taxRates;\n    }\n\n    public Invoice issue(Order order) {\n        return Invoice.of(order, taxRates.rateFor(order.country()));\n    }\n}","note":"Both versions have exactly one `TaxRates` at runtime; only the second lets a test pass a fixed rate table and makes the dependency visible in the constructor."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens when a singleton Spring bean stores request-specific mutable state?","a":"Every request thread shares that field, so concurrent requests overwrite each other: user A can get a result computed with user B's data, which is both a correctness bug and a data leak between customers. Because the writes are unsynchronized, there are also visibility and reordering effects, so behavior is nondeterministic, appears only under concurrent load and rarely in tests. The fix is to keep request data in parameters and local variables, or, if several components within one request truly need it, in a request-scoped bean."}],"extra":["**Per context, not per JVM:** Spring singleton means one per `ApplicationContext`; a test suite with several cached contexts has several instances, and in Kubernetes every pod has its own, so an in-memory counter, lock or rate limiter in a singleton is not cluster-wide.","**Scope mismatch:** Injecting a prototype or request-scoped bean into a singleton captures one instance forever; use `ObjectProvider<T>`, a `@Lookup` method, or a scoped proxy (`@RequestScope` creates one by default).","**True JVM-wide singleton:** Outside a container, an `enum` with one constant or the holder class idiom gives lazy, thread-safe initialization without double-checked locking; still expose it through an interface and inject it where you can.","**Legitimate shared state:** Caches, rate limiters and metrics are valid state in singletons; use `ConcurrentHashMap`, `LongAdder`, `AtomicReference` holding immutable snapshots, or a bounded Caffeine cache, and document the thread-safety contract.","**ThreadLocal is not the fix:** Moving the field into a `ThreadLocal` hides the design problem and leaks across pooled threads when not cleared; prefer parameters, or `ScopedValue` on recent JDKs (preview in 21, final in 25)."],"redFlags":["Says singleton is always bad or assumes singleton beans are automatically thread-safe"]},{"id":40,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Adapter vs Facade: when integrating a third-party API, which one do you need?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Adapter translates a vendor's interface, data and errors into your domain port so vendor types never leak; Facade simplifies a complex subsystem behind a few calls; real integrations often combine both.","answer":["An **Adapter** exists because two interfaces disagree. The domain defines a port in its own language, for example `CreditBureau.fetchReport(PersonalNumber)` returning a `CreditReport`, and the adapter implements it by calling the vendor API and translating everything: request shapes, field names, units and scales, enums, date formats, and vendor error codes into domain exceptions or result types. Vendor DTOs, SDK classes and HTTP status codes stop at the adapter package.","A **Facade** exists because a subsystem is complicated, not because it is incompatible. If the vendor needs a token exchange, a session, three calls and a polling loop to produce one report, a facade exposes `fetchReport()` and hides the choreography. It simplifies, but on its own it may still speak the vendor's language.","For a third-party integration you almost always need the adapter, because it is what protects the domain (the ports and adapters, or hexagonal, architecture). You add a facade when the vendor workflow is complex, and it usually sits inside or behind the adapter, so both are used together. Keep business rules out of the adapter but make its translation thorough, and put per-vendor timeouts, retries and circuit breakers at this boundary, since that is where you know which vendor failures are transient."],"keyPoints":["Adapter translates an incompatible interface into the domain port","Facade provides a simplified interface over a complex subsystem","both may be used together"],"examples":[{"title":"Adapter implementing a domain port and translating payloads and errors","lang":"java","code":"// Domain port: domain language only, no vendor types\npublic interface CreditBureau {\n    CreditReport fetchReport(PersonalNumber subject);\n}\n\n// Adapter: translates the vendor API, payload and errors into the port\n@Component\nclass AcmeBureauAdapter implements CreditBureau {\n    private final RestClient http;\n\n    AcmeBureauAdapter(RestClient.Builder builder, AcmeProperties props) {\n        this.http = builder.baseUrl(props.baseUrl()).build();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public CreditReport fetchReport(PersonalNumber subject) {\n        try {\n            AcmeReportDto dto = http.get()\n                    .uri(\"/v2/reports/{id}\", subject.value())\n                    .retrieve()\n                    .body(AcmeReportDto.class);\n            // Vendor risk index 0 to 1000 (high is bad); domain score 0 to 100 (high is good)\n            int score = 100 - dto.riskIndex() / 10;\n            boolean remarks = dto.remarks() != null && !dto.remarks().isEmpty();\n            return new CreditReport(score, remarks);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.NotFound e) {\n            throw new SubjectNotFoundException(subject);\n        } catch (HttpClientErrorException.TooManyRequests | HttpServerErrorException | ResourceAccessException e) {\n            throw new CreditBureauUnavailableException(\"Acme bureau unavailable\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Vendor DTO stays package-private inside the adapter package\nrecord AcmeReportDto(int riskIndex, List<String> remarks, String vendorStatus) {}","note":"The domain sees only `CreditReport`, `SubjectNotFoundException` and `CreditBureauUnavailableException`; the scale inversion and the status code meanings are written once, here, and tested."},{"title":"Testing the adapter against a fake HTTP server with vendor payloads","lang":"java","code":"class AcmeBureauAdapterTest {\n    private final RestClient.Builder builder = RestClient.builder();\n    private final MockRestServiceServer server = MockRestServiceServer.bindTo(builder).build();\n    private final AcmeBureauAdapter adapter = new AcmeBureauAdapter(builder, new AcmeProperties(\"https://acme.test\"));\n    private final PersonalNumber subject = new PersonalNumber(\"190001010000\");\n\n    @Test\n    void mapsVendorReportToDomainReport() {\n        server.expect(requestTo(\"https://acme.test/v2/reports/190001010000\"))\n              .andRespond(withSuccess(\"\"\"\n                      {\"riskIndex\": 200, \"remarks\": [\"LATE_PAYMENT\"], \"vendorStatus\": \"OK\"}\n                      \"\"\", MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));\n\n        CreditReport report = adapter.fetchReport(subject);\n\n        assertThat(report.score()).isEqualTo(80);\n        assertThat(report.hasPaymentRemarks()).isTrue();\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void translatesVendorOutageToDomainException() {\n        server.expect(requestTo(\"https://acme.test/v2/reports/190001010000\"))\n              .andRespond(withStatus(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE));\n\n        assertThatThrownBy(() -> adapter.fetchReport(subject))\n                .isInstanceOf(CreditBureauUnavailableException.class);\n    }\n}","note":"`MockRestServiceServer.bindTo(RestClient.Builder)` (Spring Framework 6.1 and later) exercises real HTTP mapping and JSON binding without a network; feed it recorded vendor responses, including error bodies."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where should vendor error codes be translated?","a":"In the adapter, at the boundary, and nowhere else. The adapter maps HTTP statuses and vendor codes (a not-found code, `429`, `503`, validation errors) into domain outcomes such as `SubjectNotFoundException`, a rejected result, or `CreditBureauUnavailableException`, and classifies them as retryable or not. The domain and API layers then deal with a small, stable set of domain errors. Unknown vendor codes map to a safe default and are logged with the raw code so you can extend the mapping."},{"q":"How would you test the adapter?","a":"Test it against a fake HTTP server rather than by mocking the vendor SDK: `MockRestServiceServer` for `RestClient`, or WireMock for any client, fed with recorded real vendor payloads, error bodies, slow responses and malformed JSON. Assert both the mapping into domain objects and the translation of every error class, plus a test that a slow response trips your timeout. Add a small smoke or contract test against the vendor sandbox in a separate pipeline stage, and test domain services against a fake implementation of the port so they never depend on vendor behavior."}],"extra":["**Anti-corruption layer:** In DDD terms, adapters plus translators around a vendor or legacy model form an anti-corruption layer, a useful name when justifying the extra mapping code to a team.","**Explicit semantic mapping:** MapStruct is fine for plain field copies, but semantic translations (score scale inversions, money in minor units, status meanings) deserve explicit, unit-tested code.","**Enforce the boundary:** An ArchUnit rule such as \"no class outside `..adapter.acme..` depends on `..acme..` types\" keeps vendor DTOs from creeping into the domain over time.","**Resilience at the edge:** Per-vendor timeouts, Resilience4j circuit breakers and bulkheads, and metrics tagged by vendor belong in the adapter, so the domain only sees available or unavailable semantics.","**Logging hygiene:** Log vendor request IDs and correlation IDs in the adapter, and mask personal data such as personal identity numbers before logging vendor payloads."],"redFlags":["Treats both as generic wrappers","lets vendor DTOs leak through domain code"]},{"id":41,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"Design a payment service supporting multiple providers. Which design pattern would you use and why? How does it satisfy SOLID principles?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Put each provider behind a `PaymentProvider` Strategy interface, let Spring DI create and register them, and route through a registry; new providers are new classes (OCP) and the core depends only on the abstraction (DIP).","answer":["The core is **Strategy**: a `PaymentProvider` interface (`id()`, `supports(method, currency)`, `authorize`, `capture`, `refund`) with one implementation per provider. `PaymentService` never knows which concrete provider it calls; a routing policy chooses by merchant, country, currency, method, cost or provider health, and a registry returns that strategy. Each implementation is also an adapter that translates the vendor API and errors into the domain's `PaymentResult`.","Instantiation is **Factory/DI**: Spring creates each provider bean with its own configuration and HTTP client, and injects `List<PaymentProvider>` into the service, which indexes them by id and fails fast on duplicates. When instances depend on runtime data, such as per-merchant credentials, a dedicated factory builds and caches them.","SOLID follows. **OCP**: a new provider is a new class plus configuration; the service and the existing providers are untouched. **DIP**: the high-level payment flow depends on the `PaymentProvider` abstraction, and vendor SDKs sit behind implementations of it. **SRP** separates routing, vendor translation and payment state; **LSP** requires every provider to honor the same contract (idempotency keys, amounts in minor units, identical failure semantics); **ISP** suggests optional capabilities like `RefundCapable` instead of throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.","Extensibility and testability are the payoff: the service is tested with a fake provider, each provider against a mocked HTTP server, routing rules as pure functions. In production add idempotency keys, per-provider timeouts and circuit breakers, and fail over to another provider only when the first call definitely did not charge."],"keyPoints":["Strategy Pattern for provider selection","Factory/DI for instantiation","OCP (add providers without modification)","DIP (depend on abstraction)","Easy extensibility","Testability"],"examples":[{"title":"Strategy interface, one provider, and a registry-based service","lang":"java","code":"public interface PaymentProvider {\n    String id();                                         // \"adyen\", \"stripe\", a value, not an enum\n    boolean supports(PaymentMethod method, Currency currency);\n    PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request);   // request carries an idempotency key\n}\n\n// Registered by AdyenConfig only when the provider is enabled\nclass AdyenPaymentProvider implements PaymentProvider {\n    private final AdyenClient client;\n    private final AdyenProperties props;\n\n    AdyenPaymentProvider(AdyenClient client, AdyenProperties props) { this.client = client; this.props = props; }\n\n    public String id() { return \"adyen\"; }\n    public boolean supports(PaymentMethod method, Currency currency) {\n        return props.currencies().contains(currency.getCurrencyCode());\n    }\n    public PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request) {\n        return AdyenMapper.toResult(client.authorize(AdyenMapper.toVendor(request, props.merchantAccount())));\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    private final Map<String, PaymentProvider> providers;\n    private final RoutingPolicy routing;\n\n    public PaymentService(List<PaymentProvider> all, RoutingPolicy routing) {\n        // toUnmodifiableMap throws on duplicate ids: fail at startup, not at checkout\n        this.providers = all.stream().collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap(PaymentProvider::id, p -> p));\n        this.routing = routing;\n    }\n\n    public PaymentResult authorize(AuthorizeRequest request) {\n        String providerId = routing.choose(request, providers.values());\n        return providers.get(providerId).authorize(request);\n    }\n}","note":"`PaymentService` compiles against the interface only; adding a provider never edits it. `RoutingPolicy` is a separate, pure component that can be unit tested with tables of cases."},{"title":"Provider-specific, validated configuration and conditional registration","lang":"java","code":"@Validated\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"payments.providers.adyen\")\npublic record AdyenProperties(\n        @NotBlank String baseUrl,\n        @NotBlank String merchantAccount,\n        @NotEmpty Set<String> currencies,\n        @NotNull Duration timeout,\n        @NotBlank String apiKey) {}              // from a Kubernetes secret or Vault, never from git\n\n@Configuration\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(AdyenProperties.class)\n@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = \"payments.providers.adyen\", name = \"enabled\", havingValue = \"true\")\nclass AdyenConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    AdyenClient adyenClient(RestClient.Builder builder, AdyenProperties props) {\n        var requestFactory = new SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory();\n        requestFactory.setConnectTimeout((int) props.timeout().toMillis());\n        requestFactory.setReadTimeout((int) props.timeout().toMillis());\n        RestClient http = builder.baseUrl(props.baseUrl())\n                .requestFactory(requestFactory)\n                .defaultHeader(\"X-API-Key\", props.apiKey())\n                .build();\n        return new AdyenClient(http);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    PaymentProvider adyenPaymentProvider(AdyenClient client, AdyenProperties props) {\n        return new AdyenPaymentProvider(client, props);\n    }\n}","note":"A missing merchant account fails startup through Bean Validation, and switching `payments.providers.adyen.enabled` adds or removes the provider without touching any other class. `AdyenClient` is your own thin wrapper, not a vendor SDK type."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you add a new provider without changing existing code?","a":"Implement `PaymentProvider` in a new package, add its `@ConfigurationProperties` record and a configuration class that registers the bean when `payments.providers.<id>.enabled=true`. The service discovers it through `List<PaymentProvider>` injection, and routing rules that live in configuration start sending it traffic, ideally one merchant or a small percentage first. `PaymentService` and the other providers are untouched, which is OCP in practice; this is also why the provider id should be a string or value object rather than an enum that would need editing."},{"q":"Would you use Spring DI?","a":"Yes: constructor injection of `List<PaymentProvider>` gives discovery for free, keeps dependencies explicit and makes the service trivial to test with fakes. Conditional beans (`@ConditionalOnProperty`, profiles) let each environment enable a different set of providers, each with its own HTTP client, metrics and circuit breaker. I would avoid field injection and calls to `ApplicationContext.getBean()` in business code, which turn DI back into a service locator."},{"q":"Where would Factory fit?","a":"The registry that maps ids to strategies is effectively a simple factory, and Spring `@Bean` methods are factory methods for provider instances. A dedicated factory is needed when instances depend on runtime data, for example per-merchant or per-tenant credentials and endpoints stored in a database: `PaymentProviderFactory.forMerchant(merchantId)` builds a client with those credentials and caches it. An Abstract Factory fits when each provider needs a matching family of objects, such as client, webhook verifier and error translator."},{"q":"How would you support provider-specific configuration?","a":"Give each provider its own typed, validated `@ConfigurationProperties` record under `payments.providers.<id>`, so a missing merchant account or a bad URL fails at startup instead of on the first payment. Secrets such as API keys come from Kubernetes secrets or Vault through environment variables, never from the repository, and resilience settings go into named instances (`resilience4j.circuitbreaker.instances.adyen`). Settings that change at runtime per merchant belong in a database table behind a cached lookup rather than in YAML."}],"extra":["**Idempotency is part of the contract:** Every provider call carries a stable idempotency key derived from the payment attempt, so a retry after a timeout cannot double charge; LSP means every provider must honor it the same way.","**Failover is not retry:** After a timeout you do not know whether the first provider charged, so query its status or wait for the webhook before routing the same payment elsewhere.","**Webhooks are the other half:** Each provider also needs a signature verifier and an event mapper for asynchronous notifications, which is where an Abstract Factory style provider kit starts to pay off.","**Money representation:** Use `BigDecimal` or minor units with an ISO currency at the port, and let each adapter convert to its vendor format; rounding and currency exponent differences (JPY has none) are classic provider bugs.","**Per-provider observability:** Tag latency, error rate and decline rate by provider and response code; routing on health or cost is only as good as these metrics."],"redFlags":["Hardcodes provider selection with if/else","Doesn't know Strategy","Mentions Singleton only","Cannot explain SOLID benefits"]},{"id":42,"category":"Design Patterns","question":"How would you manage expensive object creation? When would you choose Prototype over Object Pool, and why?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Prototype copies an already initialized object to avoid repeating expensive setup; Object Pool lends a bounded set of scarce, costly resources like connections or threads; pooling ordinary objects on a modern JVM usually hurts.","answer":["First decide what is actually expensive. If it is computing the object's state (parsing a large rules or template file, building a pricing grid), use **Prototype**: build one fully initialized instance and create new ones by copying it, then customize the copy. If the object is immutable you do not need copies at all; share the one instance. Prototype costs memory for the template and forces a careful deep versus shallow copy decision.","If the expensive part is acquiring a limited external resource (TCP, TLS and authentication for a database connection, an OS thread, a native buffer), use an **Object Pool**: keep a bounded set of live instances, lend them out and take them back. The pool doubles as a concurrency limit that protects the resource. Its costs are lifecycle work: validating stale connections, detecting leaks, resetting state between borrowers, and waiting when empty. A pool is not a cache: a cache shares values by key, while a pool lends exclusive use of interchangeable resources.","The trade-off is memory held versus creation cost saved. For ordinary Java objects pooling is usually an anti-pattern: TLAB allocation is a pointer bump, young GC cost is proportional to survivors rather than garbage, and escape analysis can remove allocations entirely. A pool keeps objects alive into the old generation, adds synchronization, and invites bugs when state is not reset. Pool only what is scarce: JDBC connections (HikariCP), threads (`ExecutorService`), HTTP connections, and sometimes large direct buffers (Netty's pooled allocator)."],"keyPoints":["Understands both patterns","Prototype clones complex objects to avoid expensive initialization","Object Pool reuses limited, expensive resources (e.g., DB connections, thread pools)","Explains trade-offs (memory vs creation cost)","Mentions that modern JVM often makes Object Pool an anti-pattern except for scarce resources"],"examples":[{"title":"Prototype: copy a parsed template instead of parsing again","lang":"java","code":"// FeeLine is an immutable record, so copying the list shallowly is a safe copy\npublic record FeeLine(String code, BigDecimal amount) {}\n\npublic final class LoanOfferTemplate {\n    private final Map<String, String> clauses;   // parsed from a large legal document\n    private final List<FeeLine> fees;\n\n    private LoanOfferTemplate(Map<String, String> clauses, List<FeeLine> fees) {\n        this.clauses = clauses;\n        this.fees = fees;\n    }\n\n    // Expensive: read and parse once at startup\n    public static LoanOfferTemplate load(Path source) throws IOException {\n        Map<String, String> clauses = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n        for (String line : Files.readAllLines(source)) {\n            int eq = line.indexOf('=');\n            if (eq > 0) clauses.put(line.substring(0, eq).trim(), line.substring(eq + 1).trim());\n        }\n        return new LoanOfferTemplate(clauses, new ArrayList<>());\n    }\n\n    // Prototype: a cheap, independent copy of the initialized state\n    public LoanOfferTemplate copy() {\n        return new LoanOfferTemplate(new LinkedHashMap<>(clauses), new ArrayList<>(fees));\n    }\n\n    public void putClause(String key, String text) { clauses.put(key, text); }\n    public void addFee(FeeLine fee) { fees.add(fee); }\n}","note":"Each customer offer starts from `template.copy()` and is customized without touching the shared template. Collections are copied; their elements are immutable, so a deeper copy is unnecessary."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: pool only what is scarce","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: pooling cheap, short-lived objects\nclass QuotePool {\n    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<MutableQuote> free = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();\n\n    MutableQuote borrow() {\n        MutableQuote q = free.poll();\n        return q != null ? q : new MutableQuote();\n    }\n\n    void release(MutableQuote q) {\n        q.reset();        // forget this once and one customer's data leaks into the next quote\n        free.offer(q);    // pooled objects survive GCs and get promoted to the old generation\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: just allocate; TLAB allocation is a pointer bump and young garbage is nearly free\nQuote quote(Order order) {\n    return new Quote(order.id(), pricing.total(order), order.currency());\n}\n\n// GOOD: pool what is scarce and slow to establish, bounded and with timeouts\nDataSource ordersDataSource() {\n    HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();\n    cfg.setJdbcUrl(\"jdbc:postgresql://orders-db:5432/orders\");\n    cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(20);         // sized to what PostgreSQL can serve, not to request threads\n    cfg.setConnectionTimeout(2_000);    // fail fast instead of waiting for the 30 s default\n    cfg.setMaxLifetime(1_500_000);      // below any server, proxy or firewall idle cutoff\n    return new HikariDataSource(cfg);\n}","note":"The pool is justified by the cost and scarcity of the resource behind the object, not by the object itself."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is Object Pool an anti-pattern?","a":"When the pooled object is cheap to create and holds no scarce external resource: DTOs, builders, small buffers, `StringBuilder`s, domain objects. Then the pool adds contention, keeps objects alive into the old generation (more GC work), and creates correctness risks when state is not reset or an object is used after release. It is also an anti-pattern when unbounded, because it then neither limits concurrency nor saves anything; it is a leak with extra steps."},{"q":"Why don't we pool ordinary Java objects?","a":"Because the JVM already makes allocating and reclaiming short-lived objects very cheap: allocation is a pointer bump in the thread's TLAB, and a young collection copies only live objects, so garbage that dies young costs almost nothing. Escape analysis can even remove allocations. Pooling fights that design by turning young garbage into long-lived objects that the old generation must track. The habit comes from early JVMs with slow allocators and collectors."},{"q":"Can you name examples of Object Pool in Spring or Java?","a":"JDBC connection pools (HikariCP is Spring Boot's default), thread pools (`ThreadPoolExecutor`, `ForkJoinPool`, Spring's `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor`), HTTP connection pools (Apache HttpClient's `PoolingHttpClientConnectionManager`, Reactor Netty's `ConnectionProvider`), Redis connection pools built on Apache Commons Pool 2 (Jedis, or Lettuce with pooling enabled), and Netty's `PooledByteBufAllocator` for buffers. Spring AOP also has `CommonsPool2TargetSource` for pooling bean instances, which is rarely a good idea today."},{"q":"How is HikariCP related to Object Pool?","a":"HikariCP is an Object Pool of JDBC connections: it keeps up to `maximumPoolSize` physical connections, lends one on `getConnection()`, and takes it back when your code calls `close()` on the proxy connection, which returns rather than closes it. It implements the pool responsibilities: waiting up to `connectionTimeout` when empty, retiring connections by `maxLifetime` and `idleTimeout`, validating them, and flagging leaks with `leakDetectionThreshold`. Its `ConcurrentBag` prefers handing a thread the connection it used last to reduce contention, and its Micrometer metrics expose pending threads and acquire time."}],"extra":["**Avoid `Cloneable`:** `Object.clone()` is shallow, bypasses constructors and final field initialization logic, and `Cloneable` declares no method; implement Prototype with a copy constructor or a `copy()` method instead.","**Spring prototype scope is different:** `@Scope(\"prototype\")` asks the container for a brand new bean on every lookup rather than cloning a template, and Spring does not call destroy callbacks on prototype beans.","**Do not pool virtual threads:** They are cheap and meant to be one per task; limit concurrency toward a scarce resource with a `Semaphore` or the connection pool itself.","**Pool metrics:** Active, idle and pending counts plus acquire time and timeouts (`hikaricp.connections.pending`, `hikaricp.connections.acquire`) show whether the pool or the resource behind it is the bottleneck.","**Escape analysis:** C2 can scalar replace objects that do not escape a method, so short-lived value objects in hot loops may never be heap allocated, an optimization a pool would defeat."],"redFlags":["Thinks Object Pool improves performance for all objects","Doesn't know Prototype","Confuses Object Pool with caching","Can't provide real-world examples"]},{"id":43,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"Explain heap, thread stacks, metaspace and direct memory.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"The heap holds objects, each thread has its own stack of frames, metaspace holds class metadata, and direct and other native memory live outside the heap, so a JVM's real footprint is well beyond `-Xmx`.","answer":["The **heap** holds every object and array and is the only area the garbage collector manages; it is sized by `-Xmx` or, in containers, `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage`. Each platform **thread** has its own **stack** (`-Xss`, 1 MB by default on 64-bit Linux) of frames with local variables, operand stacks and return addresses; references in those frames point into the heap and are GC roots. Deep recursion ends in `StackOverflowError`, too many threads in `OutOfMemoryError: unable to create native thread`.","**Metaspace** holds class metadata: class structures, method bytecode, constant pools, annotations. It is native memory, unbounded by default, and grows with loaded classes, so heavy proxy generation, scripting engines or classloader leaks on redeploy show up as `OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace`. **Direct memory** is native memory allocated through `ByteBuffer.allocateDirect`, used by NIO, Netty, database drivers and compression libraries so the OS can read and write without copying through the heap.","The JVM also uses native memory for the JIT code cache, GC data structures (card tables, remembered sets, mark bitmaps), symbols, and whatever JNI libraries allocate. The production consequence: a healthy heap graph says little about the process, because container limits apply to total resident memory. Leave headroom (heap at roughly 50 to 75 percent of the container limit, depending on thread count and native usage) and use Native Memory Tracking to see where the rest goes."],"keyPoints":["Heap stores objects","each thread has a stack with frames/local variables","metaspace stores class metadata","direct/native memory is outside heap and used by NIO and libraries"],"examples":[{"title":"Seeing all JVM memory areas with Native Memory Tracking","lang":"bash","code":"# Start with Native Memory Tracking (small overhead; enable when diagnosing)\njava -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary \\\n     -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 \\\n     -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=256m \\\n     -jar payments.jar &\nPID=$!\n\n# Committed memory per area: heap, class metadata, threads, code cache, GC, other\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory summary\n\n# Find growth: take a baseline, wait under load, then diff\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory baseline\nsleep 600\njcmd \"$PID\" VM.native_memory summary.diff\n\n# Compare with what the kernel (and the OOM killer) sees, in KB\nps -o rss= -p \"$PID\"\n\n# In Kubernetes a native OOM is a container kill, not a Java error\nkubectl get pod payments-7d9f -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'","note":"If RSS grows while heap and NMT totals stay flat, suspect memory NMT cannot see, such as allocations inside native libraries or malloc fragmentation."},{"title":"Memory areas, what sizes them and how each one fails","lang":"text","code":"Area            Holds                                  Sized by                           Typical failure\nHeap            objects and arrays                     -Xmx, -XX:MaxRAMPercentage         OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space\nThread stacks   frames, locals, operand stacks         -Xss per platform thread           StackOverflowError, unable to create native thread\nMetaspace       class metadata, bytecode               -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize (unbounded)   OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace\nCode cache      JIT compiled code                      -XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize          JIT stops compiling, CodeCache is full warning\nDirect memory   NIO direct ByteBuffers                 -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize            OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory\nOther native    GC structures, JNI and library malloc  not capped by the JVM              container OOMKilled, exit code 137","note":"Only the first row is \"the heap\"; the rest is why RSS is always larger than `-Xmx`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can a process be OOM-killed while heap looks healthy?","a":"Yes, and it is common in Kubernetes. The kernel OOM killer acts on the container's total memory (committed heap plus metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, GC structures, direct buffers and native library allocations), so a pod can be killed with exit code 137 and reason `OOMKilled` while heap usage sits at 40 percent. There is no Java `OutOfMemoryError` and no heap dump, because the JVM is killed from outside. Diagnose with Native Memory Tracking and RSS over time; fix by capping heap with headroom, bounding direct memory and thread counts, and chasing native leaks."},{"q":"What does -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize affect?","a":"It caps the total capacity of direct `ByteBuffer`s allocated through `ByteBuffer.allocateDirect`, including NIO's temporary direct buffers; when the cap is hit, the JVM first tries a GC to free unreachable buffers and then throws `OutOfMemoryError: Direct buffer memory`. When unset, the limit defaults to about the maximum heap size, which can nearly double your real exposure. It does not limit metaspace, thread stacks, memory-mapped files or JNI allocations, and Netty can allocate outside it and has its own `io.netty.maxDirectMemory` setting."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads:** An unmounted virtual thread's stack lives on the heap as stack chunk objects, so a million virtual threads cost heap rather than a million native stacks, and `-Xss` does not size them.","**Compressed class space:** With compressed class pointers, class structures live in a separate compressed class space inside metaspace, capped by `-XX:CompressedClassSpaceSize` (1 GB by default), which can run out before metaspace as a whole.","**Container defaults:** Without explicit flags the JVM typically sets max heap to 25 percent of the container memory limit, which wastes memory in small pods; most teams set `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` explicitly.","**glibc arenas:** Many threads allocating natively can fragment glibc malloc arenas and inflate RSS; a small `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX` or an alternative allocator such as jemalloc is a known mitigation.","**Code cache:** If the code cache fills, the JVM stops JIT compiling and throughput degrades quietly; a `CodeCache is full` warning in the logs is the only obvious symptom."],"redFlags":["Calls all memory 'heap'","assumes low heap usage means no memory issue"]},{"id":44,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"How can Java have a memory leak despite garbage collection?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"GC reclaims only unreachable objects, so anything still strongly referenced (unbounded caches, static maps, forgotten listeners, uncleared ThreadLocals, pinned classloaders) is a leak the collector is not allowed to fix.","answer":["Garbage collection answers one question: is this object reachable from a GC root (live thread stacks, static fields, JNI references, held monitors)? If yes, it stays. A Java memory leak is therefore a logical leak: objects the program no longer needs but still references. The collector works correctly; the reference graph is wrong.","The usual suspects are static or singleton maps used as caches with no size limit or expiry; listeners and callbacks registered on long-lived objects and never removed; `ThreadLocal` values on pooled threads that are never cleared; collections that only grow, such as an in-memory dedup set or a Hibernate persistence context in a long batch; lambdas and inner classes capturing a large outer object; and classloader leaks, where one reference from a JVM-wide place pins an old application classloader and all its classes in metaspace after each redeploy.","The symptom is a sawtooth whose floor keeps rising: occupancy after each old or full collection grows until GC runs back to back and you hit `OutOfMemoryError`. Raising `-Xmx` only delays the crash and makes each dump bigger. Prove retention instead: take a heap dump, or two some time apart, find the dominators and their path to GC roots, and fix the owning reference with bounds, expiry, explicit cleanup, or weak references where the semantics allow."],"keyPoints":["Objects remain strongly reachable through caches, static collections, listeners, ThreadLocals or classloaders","GC only collects unreachable objects"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: unbounded static cache vs bounded cache with expiry","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: static map as a cache, one entry per pair and day forever, never evicted\npublic class StaticRateCache {\n    private static final Map<String, RateSnapshot> CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    private final RateProvider provider;\n\n    public StaticRateCache(RateProvider provider) { this.provider = provider; }\n\n    public RateSnapshot get(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return CACHE.computeIfAbsent(pair + \":\" + day, k -> provider.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: bounded size, expiry and stats you can export as metrics\n@Component\npublic class RateCache {\n    private final RateProvider provider;\n    private final Cache<String, RateSnapshot> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n            .maximumSize(50_000)\n            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofHours(6))\n            .recordStats()\n            .build();\n\n    public RateCache(RateProvider provider) { this.provider = provider; }\n\n    public RateSnapshot get(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return cache.get(pair + \":\" + day, k -> provider.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}","note":"Every cache needs an answer to \"when does an entry leave?\"; if the answer is never, it is a leak with a delay."},{"title":"ThreadLocal on pooled request threads: always remove in finally","lang":"java","code":"public final class TenantContext {\n    private static final ThreadLocal<Tenant> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>();\n    public static void set(Tenant tenant) { CURRENT.set(tenant); }\n    public static Tenant get() { return CURRENT.get(); }\n    public static void clear() { CURRENT.remove(); }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass TenantFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)\n            throws ServletException, IOException {\n        TenantContext.set(Tenant.fromHeader(req.getHeader(\"X-Tenant-Id\")));\n        // BAD: calling chain.doFilter(req, res) without cleanup leaves the tenant on the pooled thread\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(req, res);\n        } finally {\n            TenantContext.clear();   // GOOD: removed even when the request throws\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Without `remove()`, the value stays on a Tomcat worker thread for the life of the application, retaining memory and possibly exposing one tenant's context to the next request."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What would you inspect in a heap dump?","a":"Open it in Eclipse MAT (or a similar tool) and start with the dominator tree sorted by retained size and the leak suspects report, to see which few objects keep most of the heap alive. For a suspect, follow the path to GC roots, excluding weak and soft references, to find the owning field: often a static map, a cache bean, a thread's `ThreadLocalMap` or a classloader. The class histogram shows what is numerous, and comparing two dumps taken some time apart shows what grows. Treat dumps as sensitive data because they contain customer PII, tokens and passwords."},{"q":"How can ThreadLocal leak in thread pools?","a":"Pool threads (Tomcat workers, executor threads) live as long as the application, and a `ThreadLocal` value set during one task stays in that thread's `ThreadLocalMap` until someone calls `remove()`. Without cleanup you retain memory and, worse, leak data: the next task on that thread can see the previous user or tenant context. If the value's class was loaded by an application classloader, the thread also pins that classloader after a redeploy. Set and remove in `try`/`finally` around the unit of work, or rely on a framework hook that does it."}],"extra":["**ThreadLocalMap internals:** Entries have weak keys (the `ThreadLocal`) but strong values, so a value can outlive its `ThreadLocal` until stale entries happen to be expunged; on a pooled thread that may be never, and a value whose class came from an application classloader pins that loader too.","**Hibernate batch leak:** A long `@Transactional` batch keeps every loaded or saved entity in the persistence context; `flush()` and `clear()` every N rows (aligned with `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size`) or use a `StatelessSession`.","**WeakHashMap trap:** `WeakHashMap` holds keys weakly but values strongly, so a value that references its own key is never collected; soft reference caches make GC behavior unpredictable, so prefer an explicit bounded cache.","**Leaks outside the heap:** Retention also shows up in metaspace (classloaders, generated classes), direct memory (unreleased Netty buffers, which `ResourceLeakDetector` can report) and threads (executors created per request and never shut down).","**Detection:** Alert on old generation occupancy after GC and on GC time percentage, not raw heap usage, and run with `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` and `-XX:HeapDumpPath` pointing at a volume that survives the pod restart."],"redFlags":["Says Java cannot leak memory","suggests increasing heap before investigating retention"]},{"id":45,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"What information do heap dumps, thread dumps and CPU profiles provide?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A heap dump shows what retains memory, a thread dump shows what every thread is doing or waiting for right now, and a CPU or allocation profile shows where time and allocations go.","answer":["A **heap dump** is a snapshot of every object on the heap with its references. It answers memory questions: leaks, unexpected retention, a bloated cache, duplicated strings. Tools such as Eclipse MAT compute the dominator tree and retained sizes, so you see which few objects keep most of the heap alive and the path from GC roots to them. It is expensive: it pauses the JVM, the file is roughly the size of the used heap, and it contains sensitive data.","A **thread dump** shows each thread's state (RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING), its stack, the monitors it holds and the lock it waits for, plus detected deadlocks. It is cheap and answers hangs, deadlocks, lock contention, pool exhaustion (200 threads parked in `HikariPool.getConnection`) and, through repetition, which code is hot. One dump is a single instant; take several a few seconds apart to tell a stuck thread from a busy one.","A **CPU profile** samples stacks over time and aggregates them, usually as a flame graph, to show which methods consume CPU. Allocation profiling shows who allocates the most bytes, which explains GC pressure, and lock or wall-clock modes show waiting. JFR (low overhead, production safe) and async-profiler are the standard tools. Match the artifact to the symptom: memory growth, heap dump; hang or latency, thread dumps; high CPU or GC churn, profiler."],"keyPoints":["Heap dump shows object retention and dominators","thread dump shows states, locks and stacks","CPU profile/sampling shows hot methods and allocation behavior"],"examples":[{"title":"Collecting each artifact with JDK tools and async-profiler","lang":"bash","code":"PID=$(pgrep -f orders-service.jar)\n\n# Heap dump of live objects (triggers a full GC); large and full of PII, handle with care\njcmd \"$PID\" GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-$(date +%s).hprof\n\n# Quick class histogram without writing a full dump\njcmd \"$PID\" GC.class_histogram | head -30\n\n# Thread dumps: several, a few seconds apart, including java.util.concurrent locks\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5; do\n  jcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print -l > /dumps/threads-$i.txt\n  sleep 5\ndone\n\n# Virtual threads are not listed by Thread.print; dump them to a file (JDK 21)\njcmd \"$PID\" Thread.dump_to_file -format=json /dumps/vthreads.json\n\n# CPU and allocation profile: 60 s JFR recording with the profile settings\njcmd \"$PID\" JFR.start name=incident settings=profile duration=60s filename=/dumps/incident.jfr\n\n# Or an async-profiler flame graph (modes: cpu, alloc, lock, wall)\nasprof -d 30 -e cpu -f /dumps/cpu-flame.html \"$PID\"","note":"Write dumps to a volume that outlives the pod, and remove the pod from load balancing before a heap dump on a large heap."},{"title":"Reading a thread dump: a lock owner doing slow work","lang":"text","code":"\"http-nio-8080-exec-17\" #58 daemon prio=5 os_prio=0 cpu=10234.55ms elapsed=3605.12s nid=0x2f4b waiting for monitor entry\n   java.lang.Thread.State: BLOCKED (on object monitor)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.refresh(RateCache.java:88)\n\t- waiting to lock <0x00000006c1a2b3c8> (a com.acme.pricing.RateCache)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.PricingService.quote(PricingService.java:41)\n\n\"http-nio-8080-exec-3\" #44 daemon prio=5 os_prio=0 cpu=98541.20ms elapsed=3605.40s nid=0x2f3d runnable\n   java.lang.Thread.State: RUNNABLE\n\tat java.util.regex.Pattern$Loop.match(Pattern.java:4852)\n\tat java.util.regex.Matcher.matches(Matcher.java:729)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.parseFeed(RateCache.java:131)\n\tat com.acme.pricing.RateCache.refresh(RateCache.java:95)\n\t- locked <0x00000006c1a2b3c8> (a com.acme.pricing.RateCache)","note":"Many threads BLOCKED on the same address plus one RUNNABLE owner with high `cpu=` points to a slow critical section (here regex parsing under a lock), not a deadlock."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How many thread dumps would you take for high CPU?","a":"At least three to five, spaced about 5 to 10 seconds apart, each paired with a per-thread CPU snapshot from `top -H -p <pid>` so you can match hot OS thread IDs to the `nid` values in the dumps. A thread that is RUNNABLE in the same frames across dumps and high in `top` is your hot path; a thread seen once may just be passing through. For intermittent spikes, script collection to trigger on a CPU threshold, and prefer a profiler over the same window because thousands of samples beat five snapshots."},{"q":"What is a dominator tree?","a":"Object A dominates object B if every path from the GC roots to B passes through A, so if A became unreachable, B would be collectable too. A's retained size is the memory that would be freed with it, and the dominator tree arranges objects so each node's children are what it exclusively keeps alive. Sorted by retained size, it points straight at the owner of a leak, for example one `ConcurrentHashMap` inside a cache bean retaining 3 GB, instead of at millions of `String` and `byte[]` instances that only look large in a histogram."}],"extra":["**Heap dump cost:** Dumping a 16 GB heap can pause the application for many seconds and fill a disk; take it from a pod drained of traffic, and store dumps encrypted with restricted access.","**Safepoint bias:** Thread dumps and old sampling profilers observe threads only at safepoints, which skews hot spots; JFR method sampling and async-profiler avoid that bias, so their flame graphs are more trustworthy.","**Allocation profiles often beat heap dumps:** `asprof -e alloc` or JFR's `jdk.ObjectAllocationSample` events explain GC pressure directly, because a high allocation rate is a flow problem that a snapshot of retained objects cannot show.","**Continuous profiling:** Running JFR or async-profiler continuously (for example through Pyroscope or a vendor agent) means the profile from the incident window already exists instead of needing a reproduction.","**Class histogram as a first step:** `jcmd <pid> GC.class_histogram` is fast and small; two histograms minutes apart often reveal a growing class before you commit to a full heap dump."],"redFlags":["Uses the wrong artifact for the symptom","takes only one thread dump for intermittent CPU issues"]},{"id":46,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"How would you investigate long GC pauses?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Start from GC logs, not flags: study the pause distribution and causes, allocation rate, live set, promotion and humongous allocations, correlate them with traffic, and fix allocation or retention before tuning the collector.","answer":["First establish facts. Enable unified GC logging (`-Xlog:gc*` to a rotating file) and look at the distribution of pauses, p99 and max rather than the average, because one 3 second full GC hidden among thousands of 20 ms young pauses is what users feel. For each long pause identify type and cause: young evacuation, mixed collection, remark, evacuation failure, humongous allocation, metadata threshold, or a full GC, which with G1 means concurrent collection lost the race.","Then look at the drivers. **Allocation rate** sets young collection frequency; **live set** (occupancy after a full or mixed cycle) sets marking and copying cost and tells you whether the heap is simply too small; **promotion rate** shows objects surviving long enough to reach old regions; **humongous objects** (at least half a G1 region, often large `byte[]` or `char[]` from JSON, files or exports) go straight to old regions and fragment the heap. Correlate with traffic, deploys, batch jobs and schedules, and rule out non-GC causes: slow time to safepoint, container CPU throttling of GC threads, and swapping.","Fix causes before tuning: stream large results instead of loading them, bound caches, avoid per-request copies of big payloads, fix leaks. Only then size the heap for the live set plus headroom, adjust `MaxGCPauseMillis`, or change collectors, for example generational ZGC on Java 21 for low latency on large heaps. Change one thing at a time and compare pause percentiles under the same load."],"keyPoints":["Inspect GC logs, allocation rate, live-set size, promotion, humongous objects and pause distribution","correlate with traffic","fix allocation/retention before random tuning"],"examples":[{"title":"Turning on the right logs and finding the long pauses","lang":"bash","code":"# Unified GC and safepoint logging, rotated, with wall clock and uptime\nJAVA_OPTS=\"-Xlog:gc*,safepoint:file=/logs/gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags:filecount=10,filesize=50m\"\n\n# The 20 longest pauses and their kinds\ngrep -E \"Pause (Young|Full|Remark|Cleanup)\" /logs/gc.log | awk '{print $NF, $0}' | sort -n | tail -20\n\n# Red flags with G1: full collections and humongous allocations\ngrep -c \"Pause Full\" /logs/gc.log\ngrep \"Humongous\" /logs/gc.log | tail -5\n\n# Live view: old generation occupancy and GC counts every second\njstat -gcutil \"$PID\" 1000\n\n# Allocation by stack trace tells you what to change in the code\njcmd \"$PID\" JFR.start settings=profile duration=5m filename=/logs/gc-investigation.jfr","note":"Analyzers such as GCeasy, GCViewer or JDK Mission Control turn the same log into pause histograms, allocation and promotion rates."},{"title":"Reading G1 pause lines (illustrative)","lang":"text","code":"[2026-09-20T10:15:02.118+0000] GC(4211) Pause Young (Normal) (G1 Evacuation Pause) 3890M->1204M(6144M) 38.412ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:07.903+0000] GC(4212) Pause Young (Concurrent Start) (G1 Humongous Allocation) 2510M->1650M(6144M) 61.020ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:31.440+0000] GC(4219) Pause Young (Normal) (G1 Evacuation Pause) 5980M->5870M(6144M) 412.771ms\n[2026-09-20T10:15:33.002+0000] GC(4220) Pause Full (G1 Compaction Pause) 6120M->4810M(6144M) 2874.330ms\n\n4211: normal young pause, heap drops to about 1.2 GB: healthy.\n4212: a humongous allocation started concurrent marking: look for large arrays or JSON buffers.\n4219: almost nothing freed (5980M to 5870M) and a long pause: the live set is near the heap size.\n4220: 2.9 s stop the world compaction, still 4.8 GB live afterwards: retention or undersized heap, not a flag problem.","note":"The before and after numbers are the key: memory that does not drop after a collection is live set, and no collector flag makes live data cheaper to keep."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is the difference between allocation rate and live set?","a":"Allocation rate is how fast the application creates new objects, in MB per second; it drives how often young collections run and is mostly short-lived garbage. Live set is how much memory is still reachable after a thorough collection; it determines old generation occupancy, marking and compaction work, and the minimum heap you need. A service allocating 2 GB/s with a 300 MB live set can be perfectly healthy, while a 5 GB live set in a 6 GB heap is in trouble at any allocation rate. Reduce allocation to cut GC frequency; reduce retention to cut GC cost and full GC risk."},{"q":"When can a larger heap increase pause risk?","a":"When pause time scales with the amount of memory that must be processed: a full GC compacts the whole heap, so a bigger heap means a longer worst case, and postponing collections lets a leak or a bloated old generation grow before a large collection. A larger young generation can also mean more survivors copied per pause when many objects live for a medium time. In containers, a heap too close to the memory limit leaves no room for native memory, leading to OOM kills or, on hosts with swap, very long pauses. Size the heap from the measured live set plus headroom, not as large as possible."}],"extra":["**G1 defaults:** The pause target `MaxGCPauseMillis` defaults to 200 ms, and G1 resizes the young generation to meet it; the humongous threshold is half a region, and region size is derived from heap size unless set with `-XX:G1HeapRegionSize`.","**Time to safepoint:** A pause can be long before GC even starts if a thread takes long to reach a safepoint; safepoint logging reports reaching time separately from the operation time.","**Container throttling:** GC worker threads share the CFS CPU quota with application threads, so a pause can stretch across throttled periods; check `nr_throttled` in the cgroup `cpu.stat` and keep GC thread counts consistent with the CPU limit.","**ZGC evolution:** Generational ZGC arrived in JDK 21 behind `-XX:+UseZGC -XX:+ZGenerational`, became the default ZGC mode in JDK 23 and the only mode in JDK 24, with sub-millisecond pauses in exchange for more CPU and memory headroom.","**Explicit GC:** Calls to `System.gc()` from libraries show up with that cause in the log and trigger full collections; `-XX:+ExplicitGCInvokesConcurrent` turns them into concurrent cycles with G1, or remove the call."],"redFlags":["Immediately changes collector or heap flags","looks only at average pause"]},{"id":47,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"CPU is 100% but memory is normal. What are your first diagnostic steps?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Confirm which process and threads burn CPU, map hot OS thread IDs to Java threads, take repeated thread dumps or a profile, then check loops, contention, serialization, regex, GC and traffic before restarting.","answer":["First confirm the symptom at the right level. Is it this JVM or a sidecar, user or system CPU, the whole node or a container at its CPU limit? Throttling shows as latency and throttled periods in the cgroup `cpu.stat`, not necessarily as 100 percent on the node. Then ask what changed: a deploy, a traffic spike, a new tenant or client, a retry storm from a failing dependency.","Next find the hot threads. `top -H -p <pid>` lists threads by CPU; convert the TID to hex and find the matching `nid` in a thread dump. Take three to five dumps a few seconds apart and, where possible, record 30 to 60 seconds with async-profiler or JFR for a flame graph. If the hot threads are GC workers, the root cause is memory pressure, not code. Capture this evidence before restarting, because a restart clears both the symptom and the clues; if you must mitigate, drain the pod first.","Typical findings: tight or infinite loops (retry without backoff, spin waits, a corrupted non thread-safe `HashMap`), catastrophic regex backtracking on user input, heavy JSON serialization or logging of large payloads, lock contention with spinning, crypto or compression on a hot path, and simply more traffic or a costly new request pattern. Mitigate first (scale out, rate limit, disable a feature flag, roll back), then fix the code with the profile as proof."],"keyPoints":["Confirm process/container CPU, identify hot threads, collect repeated thread dumps or JFR/profile, check loops, lock contention, serialization, regex and traffic changes"],"examples":[{"title":"From 100 percent CPU to the guilty Java thread","lang":"bash","code":"# 1. Which process, and is the container being throttled? (cgroup v2)\ntop -b -n 1 | head -15\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat            # nr_throttled, throttled_usec\n\n# 2. Hot threads inside the JVM\nPID=$(pgrep -f checkout-service.jar)\ntop -H -b -n 1 -p \"$PID\" | head -20    # note the hottest TIDs, for example 12107\n\n# 3. Map the Linux TID to the Java thread: nid is the TID in hex\nprintf 'nid=0x%x\\n' 12107              # prints nid=0x2f4b\njcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print > /tmp/td-1.txt\ngrep -A 20 'nid=0x2f4b' /tmp/td-1.txt\n\n# 4. Repeat to see what stays hot\nfor i in 2 3 4 5; do sleep 5; jcmd \"$PID\" Thread.print > /tmp/td-$i.txt; done\n\n# 5. Profile instead of guessing: 60 s CPU flame graph\nasprof -d 60 -e cpu -f /tmp/cpu.html \"$PID\"\n\n# 6. Is it GC? Back-to-back collections and a full old generation\njstat -gcutil \"$PID\" 1000 10","note":"Steps 2 and 3 connect the OS view to the Java view; step 5 gives statistical evidence that survives a restart."},{"title":"A classic CPU burner: catastrophic regex backtracking","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: nested quantifiers backtrack exponentially on input that almost matches\nprivate static final Pattern REFERENCE = Pattern.compile(\"^([A-Za-z0-9]+\\\\s?)*$\");\n\nboolean isValidReference(String input) {\n    return REFERENCE.matcher(input).matches();   // 40 letters then \"!\" can pin a core for a long time\n}\n\n// GOOD: unambiguous pattern and a length limit before matching\nprivate static final Pattern REFERENCE_SAFE = Pattern.compile(\"^[A-Za-z0-9]+(?: [A-Za-z0-9]+)*$\");\nprivate static final int MAX_REFERENCE_LENGTH = 140;\n\nboolean isValidReferenceSafe(String input) {\n    return input.length() <= MAX_REFERENCE_LENGTH\n            && REFERENCE_SAFE.matcher(input).matches();\n}","note":"In the BAD pattern a run of letters can be split between iterations in exponentially many ways; in the GOOD one each repetition must start with a space, so there is only one way to match."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you map a Linux thread ID to a Java thread dump?","a":"Get the decimal TIDs of the hottest threads with `top -H -p <pid>` or `ps -L -o tid,pcpu -p <pid>`, convert each to hex with `printf '%x\\n' 12107`, and search for `nid=0x2f4b` in a thread dump taken at about the same moment. The matching entry gives the Java thread name, state and stack. Repeat across several dumps to confirm it stays hot. async-profiler and JFR skip the manual step because they report Java thread names and stacks directly."},{"q":"What if CPU is caused by GC?","a":"Then `top -H` shows GC worker threads at the top, and the GC log or `jstat -gcutil` shows back-to-back collections with old generation occupancy staying high after each one. It is a memory problem presenting as CPU: a leak, a live set too big for the heap, or an allocation rate the collector cannot keep up with. Take a heap dump and an allocation profile, fix retention or allocation, and only then revisit heap size or collector choice; a restart just resets the clock."}],"extra":["**Thread names in top:** Recent JDKs set native thread names on Linux, so `top -H` often shows truncated names such as `GC Thread#0` or `C2 CompilerThre` directly, which speeds up triage.","**JIT can be the hot thread:** Right after a deploy, C2 compiler threads can dominate CPU for a while; that is warm-up, not a bug, and it fades as compilation completes.","**Container CPU sizing:** `availableProcessors()` follows the container CPU limit and sizes GC threads, the common `ForkJoinPool` and many library pools, so a pod limited to 1 CPU on a 64 core node behaves very differently from your laptop.","**HashMap under concurrency:** Before Java 8, concurrent resizing could create a cycle and an infinite loop; Java 8 changed resizing, but unsynchronized concurrent writes still corrupt the map, so use `ConcurrentHashMap`.","**Keep the pod for diagnosis:** In Kubernetes, remove the pod from the Service by changing a label or failing readiness, so traffic moves away but the process stays alive for dumps and profiling."],"redFlags":["Restarts immediately","focuses on heap","cannot connect OS thread IDs to Java threads"]},{"id":48,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"Why do JIT warm-up and benchmarking methodology matter?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"The JVM starts interpreted and optimizes hot code speculatively over time, so naive timing loops measure warm-up, dead-code elimination or constant folding instead of your code; use JMH with warm-up, forks and realistic data.","answer":["HotSpot runs code in the interpreter first, profiles it, compiles hot methods with C1 and then C2 using that profile (tiered compilation), inlines aggressively and optimizes speculatively, deoptimizing when an assumption breaks, for example when a new subclass is loaded or a call site becomes megamorphic. Performance therefore changes over the first seconds or minutes and depends on the history of the run. A loop timed with `System.currentTimeMillis()` mixes interpreter time, compilation, on-stack replacement and GC.","The optimizer can also delete the work you meant to measure. If a result is never used, **dead-code elimination** removes the computation; if the inputs are constants, **constant folding** computes the answer at compile time; loop unrolling and hoisting can make a single operation disappear from the loop. The result is an impressively small number that means nothing.","**JMH** exists to control this: warm-up iterations until steady state, measurement iterations, several forks (fresh JVMs) to expose run-to-run variance, returned values or `Blackhole` to defeat dead-code elimination, non-final `@State` fields to prevent constant folding, and profilers like `-prof gc` for allocation. Even then use realistic data sizes and distributions, and confirm conclusions with production-like load tests, since a microbenchmark answers only a narrow question. In production, the same warm-up explains slow first requests after every deploy."],"keyPoints":["JIT compiles and optimizes hot code over time","dead-code elimination and constant folding distort naive tests","use JMH, warm-up, forks and realistic workloads"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: a naive benchmark that measures the wrong thing","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one JVM, one loop, wall clock time, constant input, result discarded\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    FeeCalculator calculator = new FeeCalculator();\n    Order order = new Order(\"ORD-1\", new BigDecimal(\"1250.00\"), \"SEK\");   // constant input\n\n    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();\n    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {\n        calculator.calculate(order);   // result unused: the JIT may remove the work\n    }\n    System.out.println(\"took \" + (System.currentTimeMillis() - start) + \" ms\");\n}","note":"This mixes interpreted and compiled execution, may time an empty loop, and a single run cannot show variance."},{"title":"GOOD: the same question asked with JMH","lang":"java","code":"@BenchmarkMode(Mode.AverageTime)\n@OutputTimeUnit(TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS)\n@Warmup(iterations = 5, time = 1)\n@Measurement(iterations = 10, time = 1)\n@Fork(3)\n@State(Scope.Benchmark)\npublic class FeeCalculatorBenchmark {\n\n    private FeeCalculator calculator;\n    private List<Order> orders;   // non-final state: nothing to constant fold\n    private int next;\n\n    @Setup(Level.Trial)\n    public void setUp() {\n        calculator = new FeeCalculator();\n        orders = SampleOrders.realisticMix(10_000, 42L);   // amounts, currencies, discounts like production\n    }\n\n    @Benchmark\n    public BigDecimal calculateFee() {\n        Order order = orders.get(next);\n        next = (next + 1) % orders.size();\n        return calculator.calculate(order);   // returned value is consumed by JMH: no dead-code elimination\n    }\n}","note":"Run with `java -jar target/benchmarks.jar FeeCalculator -prof gc` to see allocation per operation next to the time, and compare implementations only when the difference exceeds the reported error."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does JMH use forks?","a":"Each fork runs the benchmark in a fresh JVM, so JIT profiles, inlining decisions, loaded class hierarchy and heap layout from one benchmark cannot pollute another; for example, a call site that saw three implementations becomes megamorphic and slows every later benchmark in the same JVM. Forks also expose run-to-run variance, because the JIT can make different decisions in different runs, so results across forks give a realistic error margin. `@Fork(0)` disables forking and is only acceptable while debugging the benchmark itself."},{"q":"How can dead-code elimination invalidate a benchmark?","a":"If the benchmark computes a value and discards it, the JIT can prove the computation has no side effects and remove it, so you time an empty loop and conclude the operation takes a fraction of a nanosecond. JMH prevents this when you return the result from the `@Benchmark` method or pass intermediate values to `Blackhole.consume()`. Pair that with inputs held in non-final `@State` fields, so the JIT cannot constant fold the whole calculation either."}],"extra":["**Tiered levels:** Code moves from level 0 (interpreter) to level 3 (C1 with profiling) to level 4 (C2); `-XX:+PrintCompilation` shows these transitions and deoptimizations marked `made not entrant` while a benchmark runs.","**Faster warm-up in production:** Send warm-up traffic before marking a pod ready, and look at AppCDS, CRaC and the Project Leyden AOT cache (class loading and linking in JDK 24, method profiles in JDK 25) to shorten time to peak performance.","**Coordinated omission:** Load generators that wait for a slow response before sending the next request hide latency outliers; use a constant arrival rate (wrk2, Gatling open model, k6 arrival-rate executors) and report percentiles.","**Pick the right mode:** `Mode.Throughput`, `Mode.AverageTime` and `Mode.SampleTime` answer different questions; `SampleTime` reports percentiles, which matter more than averages for latency-sensitive code.","**Trust the error bars:** JMH prints a confidence interval per result; a difference smaller than the error is noise, not an improvement."],"redFlags":["Benchmarks with currentTimeMillis in a single loop","trusts one run"]},{"id":49,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"How do connection-pool size and application concurrency interact?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Size the pool from database capacity and connection hold time (Little's law), not request threads; too small queues requests, too large overloads PostgreSQL, so watch acquire time, pending threads and DB saturation.","answer":["A connection pool is a concurrency limit on the database. The number of connections you need is throughput times the time each connection is held (Little's law): 1,000 transactions per second at 10 ms each keep about 10 connections busy on average. Hold time is the whole transaction, not only the SQL, so an HTTP call inside `@Transactional`, open session in view, or slow result processing multiplies the pool you need.","Too small a pool makes requests wait in `getConnection()`: latency rises, request threads pile up, and after `connectionTimeout` requests fail. Too large a pool adds no capacity, because PostgreSQL has a fixed number of cores and disks and each connection is a backend process with its own memory. Past the database's sweet spot, more concurrent queries mean more context switching, lock and buffer contention, and higher latency for everyone. Remember the multiplier: 30 pods with 20 connections each is 600 connections against a `max_connections` that defaults to 100.","So size from the database backwards: decide how many concurrent active queries it handles well, often a small multiple of its cores, split that across instances, and let the application queue briefly in the pool with a short timeout instead of queueing inside the database. Verify with metrics: acquire time, pending threads, active versus max on the application side, and CPU, I/O, locks and `pg_stat_activity` on the database side. Use PgBouncer in transaction mode when many instances must share a limited number of server connections."],"keyPoints":["Pool must reflect DB capacity and query latency","too small causes waits, too large overloads DB and increases contention","monitor acquire time, usage and DB saturation"],"examples":[{"title":"Sizing a HikariCP pool with arithmetic, not guesswork","lang":"yaml","code":"# Sizing sketch for one service\n#   peak 600 transactions/s per pod, p95 connection hold time 15 ms\n#   busy connections = 600 * 0.015 = 9, plus headroom for bursts: 16\n#   8 pods * 16 = 128 connections, below PostgreSQL max_connections=200 with room for admin and migrations\nspring:\n  datasource:\n    hikari:\n      maximum-pool-size: 16\n      minimum-idle: 16                  # fixed size pool avoids churn under load\n      connection-timeout: 2000          # ms; fail fast instead of the 30 s default\n      max-lifetime: 1500000             # ms; below any proxy or firewall idle cutoff\n      leak-detection-threshold: 20000   # ms; logs a stack trace for connections held too long\n  jpa:\n    open-in-view: false                 # do not hold a connection for the whole web request\nmanagement:\n  metrics:\n    distribution:\n      percentiles:\n        hikaricp.connections.acquire: [0.5, 0.95, 0.99]","note":"Recalculate when pod count changes: autoscaling from 8 to 20 pods silently multiplies database connections by 2.5."},{"title":"BAD vs GOOD: transaction duration drives pool demand","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: a DB connection stays checked out while waiting for a remote service\n@Transactional\npublic void approveLoan(UUID applicationId) {\n    LoanApplication app = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n    FraudVerdict verdict = fraudClient.check(app);   // 300 ms HTTP call holding a connection\n    app.apply(verdict);\n}\n\n// GOOD: remote I/O outside the transaction, a short transaction for the write\npublic void approveLoan(UUID applicationId) {\n    LoanApplication snapshot = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n    FraudVerdict verdict = fraudClient.check(snapshot);            // no connection held here\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status -> {\n        LoanApplication app = applications.findById(applicationId).orElseThrow();\n        app.apply(verdict);   // @Version on the entity detects concurrent changes\n    });\n}","note":"At 50 approvals per second, the BAD version needs about 15 connections just for waiting on HTTP; the GOOD version needs one or two. `tx` is a `TransactionTemplate`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens with 200 request threads and 20 DB connections?","a":"At most 20 requests do database work at a time; the other threads block in `HikariPool.getConnection()` for up to `connectionTimeout` (30 seconds by default) and then fail with `SQLTransientConnectionException`. If transactions are short, say 5 ms, 20 connections serve about 4,000 transactions per second and the waits are negligible, so this can be a perfectly sound setup. If transactions are long or a query slows down, all 200 threads end up waiting, Tomcat stops accepting work, health checks may time out, and the failure cascades to callers. Keep transactions short, use a short acquire timeout, and shed load early."},{"q":"Which metric shows pool exhaustion?","a":"`hikaricp.connections.pending` (threads waiting for a connection) rising above zero while `hikaricp.connections.active` sits at `hikaricp.connections.max` is the direct signal. `hikaricp.connections.acquire` (watch its p99) shows the latency impact, and `hikaricp.connections.timeout` counts requests that gave up. Add `hikaricp.connections.usage`, how long connections are held, to tell whether the cause is more traffic or transactions that became slower."}],"extra":["**HikariCP's sizing advice:** Its pool sizing guide starts from roughly `(core_count * 2) + effective_spindle_count` on the database server and argues that a small pool with a queue in front beats a large one.","**Virtual threads move the limit:** With virtual threads, request concurrency is no longer capped by a thread pool, so the connection pool becomes the real limit; add a bulkhead or semaphore and a short acquire timeout so thousands of virtual threads do not queue for 30 seconds.","**Separate pools for separate workloads:** Give batch jobs and reports their own small pool or a read replica, so a long export cannot starve checkout; this is the bulkhead pattern applied to connections.","**Idle in transaction:** Sessions in state `idle in transaction` in `pg_stat_activity` reveal code holding transactions open while doing other work; `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` is a useful safety net.","**Pool deadlock:** A request that needs a second connection while holding one (for example a `REQUIRES_NEW` inner transaction) can deadlock the pool under load when every thread holds one and waits for another; avoid the pattern or size for it explicitly."],"redFlags":["Sets pool equal to request threads or arbitrarily high","ignores transaction duration"]},{"id":50,"category":"JVM & Performance","question":"CPU-bound vs I/O-bound bottlenecks: how do symptoms and remedies differ?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"CPU-bound systems show hot runnable threads and saturated cores, fixed by doing less work or adding compute; I/O-bound systems show waiting threads and downstream latency, fixed with timeouts, batching, caching and bounded concurrency.","answer":["**CPU-bound**: CPU is near its limit (or the container is throttled), thread dumps show many threads RUNNABLE in application frames, a CPU profile has clear hot spots, and throughput plateaus while latency climbs with load. Adding threads beyond the core count only adds context switches and cache misses. The remedies are to do less work (better algorithms and data structures, less serialization, logging and allocation, caching computed results) and then to scale out or up. Bounded pools sized near the core count suit CPU work.","**I/O-bound**: CPU is moderate or low while latency is high; threads wait on sockets, JDBC calls, `HikariPool.getConnection()` or HTTP clients; a wall-clock profile shows time in I/O; and your latency tracks a dependency's latency or saturation. Here more concurrency can help, and virtual threads make waiting cheap, but only up to what the dependency can absorb; beyond that you just move the queue downstream and slow everyone down.","I/O remedies target the dependency relationship: timeouts and deadlines on every call, connection pooling and reuse, batching and fewer round trips (N+1 queries are the classic), caching, bulkheads and circuit breakers, and fixing the dependency itself, such as a missing index or an undersized downstream service. Latency is rarely purely an application issue, so use distributed tracing to see where the time actually goes before optimizing code."],"keyPoints":["CPU-bound shows runnable hot threads and high CPU","optimize algorithms/code or scale compute. I/O-bound shows waiting, latency and downstream saturation","use concurrency carefully, timeouts, batching and dependency fixes"],"examples":[{"title":"Different concurrency tools for CPU work and for I/O fan-out","lang":"java","code":"// CPU-bound: a bounded pool near the core count; more threads only add context switches\nprivate final ExecutorService scoringPool =\n        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());\n\n// I/O-bound: virtual threads make waiting cheap, a semaphore protects the dependency\nprivate final Semaphore bureauPermits = new Semaphore(50);   // what the credit bureau can take\n\npublic List<CreditReport> fetchReports(List<Applicant> applicants) throws InterruptedException {\n    try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n        List<Future<CreditReport>> futures = applicants.stream()\n                .map(a -> executor.submit(() -> fetchLimited(a)))\n                .toList();\n        List<CreditReport> reports = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Future<CreditReport> f : futures) {\n            try {\n                reports.add(f.get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS));\n            } catch (TimeoutException e) {\n                f.cancel(true);                          // interrupt the stuck call, do not hang\n                reports.add(CreditReport.unavailable());\n            } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n                reports.add(CreditReport.unavailable());\n            }\n        }\n        return reports;\n    }\n}\n\nprivate CreditReport fetchLimited(Applicant a) throws InterruptedException {\n    bureauPermits.acquire();\n    try {\n        return bureauClient.fetch(a.personalNumber());   // client has its own connect and read timeouts\n    } finally {\n        bureauPermits.release();\n    }\n}","note":"The I/O path gets cheap concurrency, a cap matched to the downstream capacity, and timeouts that degrade instead of blocking; the CPU path gets no more threads than cores."},{"title":"Telling the two apart from signals you already have","lang":"text","code":"Signal                    CPU-bound                              I/O-bound\nProcess CPU               near 100% of cores or of the limit     low to moderate\nThread dumps              RUNNABLE in app code, same frames      WAITING or TIMED_WAITING, socket reads, getConnection\nCPU profile               clear hot methods                      little to see\nWall-clock profile        same as CPU profile                    time in socket read, JDBC, pool acquire\nLatency vs load           rises once cores saturate              tracks downstream latency and pool waits\nPool metrics              normal                                 pending above 0, acquire time up\nAdding threads            makes it worse                         helps until the dependency saturates\nMain fix                  less work, better code, more cores     timeouts, batching, caching, fix the dependency","note":"Use the CPU profile and the wall-clock profile together: a method that is hot in wall-clock but absent in CPU mode is waiting, not computing."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you distinguish lock contention from external I/O wait?","a":"Look at what threads wait on across repeated thread dumps: lock contention shows BLOCKED threads `waiting to lock` the same monitor, or WAITING threads parked in `AbstractQueuedSynchronizer` for a `ReentrantLock`, with one owner thread holding it; I/O wait shows threads in socket reads, JDBC driver frames or `HikariPool.getConnection()`. JFR quantifies it with `jdk.JavaMonitorEnter` and `jdk.ThreadPark` events versus `jdk.SocketRead`, and async-profiler's `lock` mode versus `wall` mode separates them in flame graphs. Contention also grows with internal concurrency even when dependencies are fast, while I/O wait correlates with downstream latency in traces and dependency metrics."}],"extra":["**Socket reads look RUNNABLE:** A platform thread blocked in a socket read usually shows as RUNNABLE in a native frame (for example `sun.nio.ch.Net.poll` or `socketRead0`), so counting states misleads; read the top frames.","**Throttling masquerades as waiting:** A container hitting its CPU quota can show moderate average CPU but long stalls; check `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` before blaming a dependency.","**Virtual thread pinning:** On Java 21, blocking inside `synchronized` pins the carrier thread, which can starve I/O-bound virtual threads; JDK 24 (JEP 491) removed most of that pinning, and JFR's `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event reveals it on 21.","**USE method:** For each resource check utilization, saturation and errors (CPU run queue, pool pending count, disk queue); saturation metrics show where the queue actually is.","**Queueing reality:** Near full utilization, queueing makes latency climb steeply, so keep critical resources, CPU or connections, well below 100 percent on latency-sensitive paths."],"redFlags":["Adds threads to CPU-bound work","treats latency as purely an application issue"]},{"id":51,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Constructor injection vs field injection: why is constructor injection preferred?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, `final` and required at construction, so beans are never half-built, are testable with plain `new`, and oversized constructors expose design problems early.","answer":["Constructor injection declares every collaborator as a constructor parameter assigned to a `final` field. The object cannot be created, by Spring or by a test calling `new`, without its required dependencies, so it never exists half-initialized and its collaborators cannot be swapped later. Since Spring 4.3 a class with a single constructor needs no `@Autowired`, and a missing bean fails the context at startup with a clear message instead of an NPE on the first request.","Field injection hides dependencies: they are private fields set by reflection after construction, invisible in the class's API. The fields cannot be `final`, unit tests need a Spring context or reflection tricks such as `@InjectMocks` or `ReflectionTestUtils`, and the class accumulates collaborators silently because one more annotated field costs nothing visually. Outside the container the object is simply broken.","The trade-offs are small. Constructors get longer (Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor` or just accepting the lines solves that), and circular dependencies fail fast at startup, which is a design signal rather than a nuisance. Setter injection is still fine for genuinely optional or reconfigurable collaborators, and `ObjectProvider<T>` covers optional or lazy lookup. The production payoff is fail-fast wiring, immutable thread-safe singletons, and plain JUnit tests that run in milliseconds."],"keyPoints":["Dependencies are explicit, immutable and testable","invalid objects cannot be created without required dependencies","field injection hides dependencies"],"examples":[{"title":"Field injection vs constructor injection","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: hidden, mutable, untestable without Spring\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    @Autowired private PaymentGateway gateway;\n    @Autowired private LedgerRepository ledger;\n    @Autowired private FraudClient fraud;      // grew silently over time\n}\n\n// GOOD: explicit and final; no @Autowired needed for a single constructor\n@Service\npublic class PaymentService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n    private final LedgerRepository ledger;\n\n    public PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway, LedgerRepository ledger) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n        this.ledger = ledger;\n    }\n\n    public PaymentResult pay(PaymentCommand cmd) {\n        PaymentResult result = gateway.charge(cmd.amount(), cmd.cardToken());\n        ledger.record(cmd.accountId(), result);\n        return result;\n    }\n}","note":"The good version documents its needs in the constructor signature, cannot be created without them, and keeps its collaborators immutable for the lifetime of the singleton."},{"title":"Unit test without a Spring context","lang":"java","code":"class PaymentServiceTest {\n    private static final UUID ACCOUNT_ID = UUID.randomUUID();\n\n    @Test\n    void recordsApprovedChargeInLedger() {\n        var gateway = mock(PaymentGateway.class);\n        var ledger = mock(LedgerRepository.class);\n        var approved = PaymentResult.approved(\"tx-42\");\n        when(gateway.charge(any(), any())).thenReturn(approved);\n\n        var service = new PaymentService(gateway, ledger);   // plain constructor call\n        service.pay(new PaymentCommand(ACCOUNT_ID, new BigDecimal(\"99.00\"), \"tok_visa\"));\n\n        verify(ledger).record(ACCOUNT_ID, approved);\n    }\n}","note":"No `@SpringBootTest`, no reflection: the compiler forces the test to supply every dependency, and adding a ninth one breaks every test loudly."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does a constructor with eight dependencies tell you?","a":"It is a Single Responsibility smell: the class probably orchestrates several use cases or mixes business logic with cross-cutting side effects such as notifications, audit and metrics. Constructor injection did its job by making that visible; field injection would have hidden it. Look for clusters of dependencies that are always used together and extract them into a cohesive collaborator, split the class per use case, or move side effects behind domain events. Do not hide the problem with a parameter object that simply bundles the same eight things."}],"extra":["**Circular references:** Spring Boot 2.6+ sets `spring.main.allow-circular-references=false` by default; with constructor injection a cycle fails at startup, which usually points to a missing abstraction or a side effect that should be an event.","**Multiple constructors:** if a class has several constructors, annotate the one Spring should use with `@Autowired`; otherwise Spring falls back to a no-arg constructor and fails if there is none.","**Safe publication:** `final` fields get initialization-safety guarantees from the Java Memory Model, a real bonus for singletons read concurrently by hundreds of request threads.","**Configuration classes:** in `@Configuration` classes prefer parameters on `@Bean` methods over `@Autowired` fields; each bean then declares exactly what it needs, and it works with `proxyBeanMethods = false`.","**Optional dependencies:** instead of `@Autowired(required = false)` on a field, inject `ObjectProvider<T>` and call `getIfAvailable()`, which keeps the dependency visible and supports lazy resolution."],"redFlags":["Prefers field injection because it is shorter","classes have many hidden dependencies"]},{"id":52,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Explain Spring bean scopes and the thread-safety implications of singleton beans.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Singleton (the default) means one instance per application context shared by all request threads, so it must be stateless or explicitly thread-safe; per-request data belongs in parameters, locals or a request-scoped bean.","answer":["Spring's scopes are `singleton` (default: one instance per bean definition per `ApplicationContext`), `prototype` (a new instance per injection or lookup, with no destroy callbacks), and the web scopes `request`, `session`, `application` and `websocket`. Singleton is not the GoF singleton: it is per context, so two contexts in one JVM, every pod in a cluster and every test context each have their own instance. Never use a singleton field for cluster-wide state or coordination.","A singleton is not automatically thread-safe. Spring creates it once, every Tomcat or virtual thread calls it concurrently, and no synchronization is added. The rule is that singleton services are stateless: fields hold only `final` injected collaborators and immutable configuration, while request data travels as method parameters and local variables on each thread's stack. Genuinely shared mutable state (a cache, a counter, a rate limiter) must use thread-safe structures such as `ConcurrentHashMap`, `LongAdder`, Caffeine or Micrometer meters, never plain fields.","Scope mismatches are the other trap. Injecting a prototype or request-scoped bean into a singleton captures one instance at startup unless you go through a scoped proxy (`@RequestScope` proxies by default), `ObjectProvider<T>` or `@Lookup`. Request scope is handy for per-request context such as tenant or correlation id, but explicit parameters are usually clearer and easier to test."],"keyPoints":["Singleton is one instance per application context, not automatically thread-safe","singleton services should usually be stateless"],"examples":[{"title":"Mutable request state in a singleton","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one instance serves every thread; requests overwrite each other's fields\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private Applicant applicant;                 // shared mutable state\n    private BigDecimal requestedAmount;\n\n    public Decision decide(Applicant a, BigDecimal amount) {\n        this.applicant = a;\n        this.requestedAmount = amount;\n        return evaluate();                       // may see another thread's applicant\n    }\n\n    private Decision evaluate() {\n        return applicant.score() >= 620 ? Decision.approve(requestedAmount) : Decision.decline();\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: stateless; request data lives on the caller's stack\n@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n    private final ScoringModel model;            // immutable collaborator\n\n    public CreditDecisionService(ScoringModel model) { this.model = model; }\n\n    public Decision decide(Applicant applicant, BigDecimal amount) {\n        int score = model.score(applicant);\n        return score >= 620 ? Decision.approve(amount) : Decision.decline();\n    }\n}","note":"The bad version passes every single-threaded unit test and fails only under concurrent load, by approving or declining the wrong applicant."},{"title":"Using narrower scopes from a singleton","lang":"java","code":"@Component\n@RequestScope                                    // proxied by default, safe to inject\npublic class RequestContext {\n    private String tenantId;\n    public String tenantId() { return tenantId; }\n    public void tenantId(String id) { this.tenantId = id; }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class StatementService {\n    private final RequestContext ctx;                     // really a scoped proxy\n    private final ObjectProvider<PdfRenderer> renderers;  // PdfRenderer is @Scope(\"prototype\")\n\n    public StatementService(RequestContext ctx, ObjectProvider<PdfRenderer> renderers) {\n        this.ctx = ctx;\n        this.renderers = renderers;\n    }\n\n    public byte[] render(Statement statement) {\n        PdfRenderer renderer = renderers.getObject();     // fresh instance per call\n        return renderer.render(statement, ctx.tenantId());\n    }\n}","note":"Injecting `PdfRenderer` directly would give the singleton one renderer forever; the provider and the scoped proxy resolve the right instance on each call."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if a singleton bean contains mutable request state?","a":"Concurrent requests overwrite each other's field values, so a thread computes with another user's data: approving the wrong applicant, sending one customer's statement to another, or mixing tenant ids. It is intermittent and load-dependent, passes single-threaded tests, and usually appears first in production under traffic as a silent data leak rather than a crash. Fix it by moving the state into parameters or locals, into an immutable value object passed through the call chain, or into a request-scoped bean if it truly is per-request context."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads:** with `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` (Boot 3.2+) many more requests hit the same singletons at once, so latent races surface faster; the stateless rule matters more, not less.","**Eager creation:** singletons are instantiated at startup by default, so wiring errors fail the deployment; `@Lazy` or `spring.main.lazy-initialization=true` trade that safety for faster startup.","**Prototype lifecycle:** Spring does not call `@PreDestroy` on prototype beans; whoever obtains the instance owns its cleanup.","**ThreadLocal leaks:** stashing request data in a `ThreadLocal` inside a singleton leaks between requests on pooled threads unless it is cleared in `finally`; Java 21 previews `ScopedValue` for bounded, immutable per-call context.","**Enforce it:** treat any non-final instance field in a `@Service`, `@Component` or `@Controller` as a review finding; an ArchUnit rule can check it in CI."],"redFlags":["Believes singleton means one instance across all JVMs","stores request data in fields"]},{"id":53,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How do Spring proxies enable @Transactional and @Async, and what is the self-invocation problem?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Spring wraps the bean in a proxy whose interceptors start transactions or hand work to an executor, but only calls entering through the proxy are advised, so `this.method()` silently skips `@Transactional` and `@Async`.","answer":["Annotations do not change bytecode. At startup a `BeanPostProcessor` (the auto-proxy creator) sees `@Transactional` or `@Async` and replaces the bean with a proxy, and that proxy is what gets injected everywhere. An external call hits the proxy, runs an interceptor chain, then delegates to the target. `TransactionInterceptor` obtains a transaction from the `PlatformTransactionManager`, binds the connection to the current thread, invokes the method, then commits or rolls back. `AsyncExecutionInterceptor` submits the invocation to a `TaskExecutor` and returns immediately with `void` or a `CompletableFuture`.","Self-invocation: once inside the target, `this.otherMethod()` is a plain Java call on the raw object, not on the proxy, so no advice runs. A `REQUIRES_NEW` audit method called internally just joins the outer transaction, and an internal `@Async` call runs synchronously on the caller's thread. There is no error, only wrong behavior.","Proxy type matters. JDK dynamic proxies implement the bean's interfaces, so only interface methods are intercepted and injecting the concrete class fails. CGLIB proxies subclass the target (Spring Boot's default), so `final` classes cannot be proxied and `final` or `private` methods are silently not intercepted. Since Spring 6.0, `protected` and package-private methods can be transactional with class-based proxies, while interface proxies still need public interface methods. The proxy is also not in play during `@PostConstruct`, so do not rely on transactions there."],"keyPoints":["A proxy intercepts external calls","calls through this bypass the proxy","visibility, final methods and proxy type matter"],"examples":[{"title":"Self-invocation bug and the structural fix","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: internal call bypasses the proxy; the audit joins (and dies with) the outer transaction\n@Service\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final AuditRepository audit;\n    // constructor omitted\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void placeOrder(Order order) {\n        orders.save(order);\n        this.writeAudit(order.getId(), \"PLACED\");   // plain call on the target\n    }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\n    public void writeAudit(UUID orderId, String event) {\n        audit.save(new AuditEntry(orderId, event));\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: the advised method lives in another bean, so the call crosses a proxy\n@Service\npublic class AuditService {\n    private final AuditRepository audit;\n    public AuditService(AuditRepository audit) { this.audit = audit; }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)\n    public void write(UUID orderId, String event) {\n        audit.save(new AuditEntry(orderId, event));\n    }\n}\n// OrderService injects AuditService and calls auditService.write(...): new transaction","note":"Both versions compile and look identical in review; only the second actually runs the audit in its own transaction."},{"title":"Proving what was proxied","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass ProxyCheck implements ApplicationRunner {\n    private final OrderService orderService;\n\n    ProxyCheck(OrderService orderService) { this.orderService = orderService; }\n\n    @Override\n    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {\n        System.out.println(orderService.getClass());               // OrderService$$SpringCGLIB$$0\n        System.out.println(AopUtils.isAopProxy(orderService));     // true\n        System.out.println(AopUtils.isCglibProxy(orderService));   // true\n    }\n}\n// application.properties:\n// logging.level.org.springframework.transaction.interceptor=TRACE\n// logs \"Getting transaction for [...]\" only for calls that went through the proxy","note":"When a transaction seems to be ignored, TRACE logging on the transaction interceptor shows immediately whether the call was intercepted at all."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you fix self-invocation? JDK proxy vs CGLIB?","a":"The cleanest fix is structural: move the advised method into its own bean and inject it, so the call crosses a proxy boundary. Alternatives are a programmatic `TransactionTemplate` for the inner boundary, self-injection through `@Lazy` or `ObjectProvider<OrderService>` (works but is a smell), `AopContext.currentProxy()` with `exposeProxy = true` (couples code to Spring AOP), or AspectJ weaving. JDK proxies implement the target's interfaces and intercept only interface methods, so you must inject by interface; CGLIB generates a runtime subclass, lets you inject the concrete class and intercepts any non-final, non-private method, but cannot proxy final classes. Spring Boot uses CGLIB by default (`spring.aop.proxy-target-class=true`)."}],"extra":["**Advice ordering:** when a method carries `@Transactional` plus a retry or custom aspect, the order decides nesting; retry must wrap the transaction so every attempt gets a fresh transaction and persistence context.","**Async context loss:** `@Async` runs on another thread, so the caller's transaction, `SecurityContext` and MDC do not follow; use a `TaskDecorator` for MDC and security context and give the async method its own `@Transactional`.","**Async exceptions:** exceptions from `void` `@Async` methods never reach the caller; they go to the `AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler`, so return a `CompletableFuture` when the caller needs the outcome.","**AspectJ mode:** `@EnableTransactionManagement(mode = AdviceMode.ASPECTJ)` with compile-time or load-time weaving advises internal calls and private methods, at the cost of build or agent complexity.","**Spring 6 naming:** CGLIB proxy classes are now named `...$$SpringCGLIB$$0`, which is a quick way to spot a proxy in a stack trace or debugger."],"redFlags":["Thinks annotations change bytecode directly","cannot explain internal calls"]},{"id":54,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"What transaction boundaries would you choose for a service method?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Put the transaction on the application service method that implements one use case, keep it short and database-only, and move remote calls and message publishing outside it using separate transactions or an outbox.","answer":["The natural boundary is the application service method that implements one use case, such as `approveLoan` or `capturePayment`: everything that must be atomic commits together, and nothing else is inside. Not the controller, which would hold the transaction through validation, serialization and web concerns, and not only the repository, where each call commits separately and you lose atomicity across steps. Ideally one transaction changes one aggregate; consistency across aggregates goes through events.","Keep transactions short. A transaction pins a pooled connection (HikariCP's default pool is only 10), holds row locks until commit, and in PostgreSQL keeps an old snapshot alive that blocks vacuum cleanup. Load, decide and write inside; do expensive computation, file parsing and waiting outside.","Never make remote calls inside a database transaction. An HTTP call to a payment provider or credit bureau can take seconds or time out while the connection and locks are held; under load the pool drains and unrelated endpoints fail. It also cannot be rolled back: if the provider charges the card and your commit then fails, the two systems disagree. Split the flow: transaction one records intent (`PENDING` plus an idempotency key), the remote call runs without a transaction, transaction two records the result, and a reconciliation job repairs crashes in between.","Use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for query use cases, set `timeout` on risky ones, and route messages through an outbox rather than sending them from inside the transaction."],"keyPoints":["Use the application-service use-case boundary","keep transactions short","avoid remote calls inside"],"examples":[{"title":"Remote call inside vs outside the transaction","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: connection and row locks held while waiting on the payment provider\n@Transactional\npublic void capture(UUID orderId) {\n    Order order = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n    order.markPaymentPending();\n    PaymentResult result = paymentClient.charge(order.total(), order.idempotencyKey()); // 50 ms to 30 s\n    order.applyPayment(result);\n}\n\n// GOOD: two short transactions (TransactionTemplate tx) around the remote call\npublic void capture(UUID orderId) {\n    ChargeRequest request = tx.execute(status -> {\n        Order order = orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow();\n        order.markPaymentPending();\n        return new ChargeRequest(order.total(), order.idempotencyKey());\n    });\n\n    PaymentResult result = paymentClient.charge(request.amount(), request.idempotencyKey());\n\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status ->\n        orders.findById(orderId).orElseThrow().applyPayment(result));\n}","note":"The idempotency key makes a retried charge safe, and the `PENDING` state lets a reconciliation job find orders whose second transaction never ran."},{"title":"PostgreSQL guardrails and finding long transactions","lang":"sql","code":"-- Safety nets for the application role\nALTER ROLE payments_app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s';\nALTER ROLE payments_app SET statement_timeout = '5s';\n\n-- Longest running transactions right now\nSELECT pid, usename, state,\n       now() - xact_start AS tx_age,\n       left(query, 80)    AS last_query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY tx_age DESC\nLIMIT 10;","note":"An `idle in transaction` session with a large `tx_age` is the classic signature of application code doing remote work or waiting while a transaction is open."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why are long transactions dangerous?","a":"They pin a pooled connection for their whole duration, so a few slow ones exhaust the pool and unrelated requests start timing out. They hold row locks longer, which increases lock waits, deadlocks and optimistic lock conflicts. In PostgreSQL an open transaction holds back the xmin horizon, so VACUUM cannot remove dead tuples, causing table and index bloat, and a huge persistence context grows memory and dirty-checking cost. The longer the window, the more work is lost and replayed on rollback."},{"q":"Where should event publication happen?","a":"Record the event inside the same transaction as the state change, typically as a row in an outbox table, and let a relay (a poller or Debezium CDC) publish it to Kafka after commit. Publishing to the broker inside the transaction can emit events for changes that later roll back, and publishing after commit in the same thread loses the event if the process dies in between. For in-process reactions that may be lost, `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)` is fine; Spring Modulith's event publication registry adds persistence to that model."}],"extra":["**readOnly:** `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` makes Hibernate skip flushing and dirty checking and passes a read-only hint to the driver; it is also the usual hook for routing queries to a replica through an `AbstractRoutingDataSource`.","**Open Session in View:** Spring Boot enables `spring.jpa.open-in-view` by default and logs a warning; it keeps the persistence context open for the whole request, so disable it to make the service method the real boundary.","**Timeouts as guardrails:** `@Transactional(timeout = 5)` plus PostgreSQL `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` and `statement_timeout` stop one bad code path from pinning connections forever.","**Isolation is part of the boundary:** PostgreSQL defaults to READ COMMITTED; if you choose REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE the whole use case must be retryable, because commits can fail with SQLSTATE `40001`.","**Retry wraps the transaction:** retries for optimistic lock or serialization failures belong outside the transactional method so each attempt starts with a fresh transaction and persistence context."],"redFlags":["Annotates controllers or holds DB transactions during HTTP calls"]},{"id":55,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"Explain @Transactional propagation REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW and NESTED.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"`REQUIRED` joins the current transaction or starts one, `REQUIRES_NEW` suspends it and runs an independent physical transaction on a second connection, and `NESTED` stays in the same transaction using a savepoint for partial rollback.","answer":["`REQUIRED` is the default: if a transaction is active the method joins it, otherwise it starts one. Joined methods share one physical transaction and one connection, so an unchecked exception escaping an inner method marks the whole transaction rollback-only, even if the outer method catches it; the outer commit then fails with `UnexpectedRollbackException`. Isolation, timeout and read-only settings on a joining method are ignored.","`REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the outer transaction and starts an independent physical transaction on a second pooled connection. It commits or rolls back on its own: its commit survives an outer rollback, and its failure does not doom the outer transaction if caught. The costs are real: two connections per thread (pool deadlock when many threads each wait for their second connection), no visibility of the outer transaction's uncommitted rows, and a self-deadlock if it tries to lock a row the suspended outer transaction already holds.","`NESTED` is one physical transaction with a JDBC savepoint: an inner failure rolls back to the savepoint and the outer can continue, but an outer rollback undoes everything, inner work included. It needs savepoint support: it works with `DataSourceTransactionManager` and `JdbcTransactionManager`, while `JpaTransactionManager` disallows it by default because rolling back a savepoint does not roll back entity state in the persistence context.","Use `REQUIRED` almost always, `REQUIRES_NEW` for work that must outlive a failure (audit of attempts, idempotency markers), and `NESTED` for best-effort sub-steps in JDBC code."],"keyPoints":["REQUIRED joins/starts","REQUIRES_NEW suspends outer","NESTED uses savepoints when supported","semantics affect rollback and connections"],"examples":[{"title":"Audit of attempts with REQUIRES_NEW","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class LoanApprovalService {\n    private final LoanRepository loans;\n    private final AuditLog auditLog;              // separate bean, so propagation applies\n\n    public LoanApprovalService(LoanRepository loans, AuditLog auditLog) {\n        this.loans = loans;\n        this.auditLog = auditLog;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional                                // REQUIRED\n    public void approve(UUID loanId, String officer) {\n        auditLog.record(loanId, officer, \"APPROVAL_ATTEMPTED\");   // committed immediately\n        Loan loan = loans.findById(loanId).orElseThrow();\n        loan.approve(officer);                    // may throw LimitExceededException\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class AuditLog {\n    private final AuditRepository repo;\n    public AuditLog(AuditRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }\n\n    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)   // own connection, own commit\n    public void record(UUID loanId, String actor, String event) {\n        repo.save(new AuditEntry(loanId, actor, event, Instant.now()));\n    }\n}","note":"The audit row survives if `approve` rolls back, which is why it records an attempt rather than an approval; while `record` runs, the thread holds two connections."},{"title":"Propagation cheat sheet","lang":"text","code":"Propagation   | Outer tx exists          | Connections | Inner failure            | Later outer rollback\nREQUIRED      | join it                  | 1           | whole tx rollback-only   | undoes inner work\nREQUIRES_NEW  | suspend, start new tx    | 2           | only inner rolls back    | inner already committed\nNESTED        | savepoint in same tx     | 1           | roll back to savepoint   | undoes inner work\n(no outer tx) | all three simply start a new physical transaction","note":"The last column is the one interviewers probe: only `REQUIRES_NEW` produces work that outlives an outer rollback."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if an audit transaction commits but the business transaction fails?","a":"That is the intended behavior of `REQUIRES_NEW`, so the audit row must describe an attempt, not a fact: record `APPROVAL_ATTEMPTED` with a correlation id, then record the outcome separately, for example from an `AFTER_COMMIT` or `AFTER_ROLLBACK` `@TransactionalEventListener`. If the audit must only contain committed business facts, it belongs in the same transaction (or the outbox), not in `REQUIRES_NEW`. The failure mode to avoid is an audit trail that says `APPROVED` for a loan whose approval rolled back, which is a compliance problem, not just a bug."}],"extra":["**Pool deadlock math:** with a pool of 10 and 10 concurrent requests that each hold an outer connection and wait for a `REQUIRES_NEW` connection, everyone blocks until HikariCP's `connectionTimeout` (30 s by default); size the pool for the worst-case connections per request.","**Other propagations:** `MANDATORY` fails without an active transaction (good for domain methods that must never run alone), `NOT_SUPPORTED` suspends around slow non-database work, and `SUPPORTS` and `NEVER` complete the set.","**Proxy caveat:** propagation is applied by the proxy, so a `REQUIRES_NEW` method called through `this` silently joins the outer transaction.","**Strict participation:** setting `validateExistingTransaction` to `true` on the transaction manager makes a joining method with an incompatible isolation level or read-only flag fail instead of being silently ignored."],"redFlags":["Uses REQUIRES_NEW for every rollback","assumes NESTED is a new physical transaction"]},{"id":56,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"When does Spring roll back a transaction by default?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"By default Spring rolls back only when a `RuntimeException` or `Error` escapes the transactional method; checked exceptions commit unless you add rollback rules, and exceptions caught inside never trigger rollback.","answer":["The rule comes from the EJB tradition: unchecked exceptions (`RuntimeException` and subclasses, plus `Error`) signal an unexpected failure and roll back, while checked exceptions are treated as business outcomes and the transaction commits. So a method that throws a checked `TransferRejectedException` after it already debited an account commits that partial debit. Fix it with `rollbackFor`, by making the exception unchecked, or on Spring 6.2+ globally with `@EnableTransactionManagement(rollbackOn = RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS)`. `noRollbackFor` does the opposite, and when several rules match, the most specific one wins.","The interceptor can only react to exceptions that leave the proxied method. If you catch and log inside the transactional method, the proxy sees a normal return and commits whatever was written before the failure. If you must handle the exception locally, rethrow it (wrapped if needed) or call `TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly()` explicitly.","The subtle production case is a joined transaction: an inner `REQUIRED` method throws a runtime exception, the proxy marks the shared transaction rollback-only, and the outer method catches it and carries on. The outer commit then rolls back anyway and throws `UnexpectedRollbackException`. Exception handling and transaction boundaries are one design decision, not two."],"keyPoints":["By default on unchecked RuntimeException and Error","checked exceptions need rules","caught exceptions do not trigger rollback"],"examples":[{"title":"Checked and swallowed exceptions commit partial work","lang":"java","code":"public class TransferRejectedException extends Exception {\n    public TransferRejectedException(String reason) { super(reason); }\n}\n\n// BAD: both failure paths COMMIT the debit\n@Transactional\npublic void transfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) throws TransferRejectedException {\n    accounts.debit(from, amount);\n    if (accounts.isBlocked(to)) {\n        throw new TransferRejectedException(\"target blocked\");  // checked: commit\n    }\n    try {\n        accounts.credit(to, amount);\n    } catch (DataAccessException e) {\n        log.error(\"credit failed\", e);                          // swallowed: commit\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: explicit rule, nothing swallowed\n@Transactional(rollbackFor = TransferRejectedException.class)\npublic void transfer(UUID from, UUID to, BigDecimal amount) throws TransferRejectedException {\n    if (accounts.isBlocked(to)) {\n        throw new TransferRejectedException(\"target blocked\");\n    }\n    accounts.debit(from, amount);\n    accounts.credit(to, amount);            // runtime exceptions propagate and roll back\n}","note":"Money moved out of one account and never arrived in the other: the classic result of assuming every exception rolls back."},{"title":"Global rule and local handling with rollback","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableTransactionManagement(rollbackOn = RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS)   // Spring 6.2+\nclass TransactionConfig {\n}\n\n@Service\nclass ReversalService {\n    private final LedgerService ledger;\n    ReversalService(LedgerService ledger) { this.ledger = ledger; }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public ReversalResult reverse(ReversalCommand cmd) {\n        try {\n            return ledger.reverse(cmd);\n        } catch (LedgerPeriodClosedException e) {\n            // return a business result, but make sure nothing written so far commits\n            TransactionAspectSupport.currentTransactionStatus().setRollbackOnly();\n            return ReversalResult.rejected(e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"`setRollbackOnly()` lets you return a normal response while still discarding the transaction's writes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is UnexpectedRollbackException?","a":"It is thrown when the outermost transaction tries to commit but the transaction was already marked rollback-only, typically because an inner `@Transactional` method (propagation `REQUIRED`) threw a runtime exception that the outer method caught and swallowed. Spring rolls back and throws `UnexpectedRollbackException` so the caller is not misled into believing the commit succeeded. Fix the design rather than the symptom: let the exception propagate, or, if the inner failure is genuinely tolerable, give that step its own boundary (`REQUIRES_NEW`, or `NESTED` with JDBC) or make the inner method non-transactional."}],"extra":["**Persistence exceptions:** once Hibernate throws (constraint violation, optimistic lock failure), the JPA transaction is marked rollback-only and the `EntityManager` should be considered unusable; you cannot catch it and keep working in the same persistence context.","**Exception translation:** `@Repository` beans get JDBC and Hibernate exceptions translated to Spring's unchecked `DataAccessException` hierarchy, so they roll back by default; `DataIntegrityViolationException` is the one you typically map to HTTP 409.","**Kotlin:** Kotlin has no checked exceptions, so a Java-checked exception thrown from Kotlin code commits silently; Spring's own documentation recommends `RollbackOn.ALL_EXCEPTIONS` for Kotlin applications.","**Reacting to rollbacks:** `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_ROLLBACK)` lets you record metrics or trigger compensation without catching exceptions inside the transaction.","**Programmatic transactions:** with `TransactionTemplate`, a runtime exception thrown from the callback rolls back, and `status.setRollbackOnly()` rolls back while still returning a value."],"redFlags":["Assumes every exception rolls back","catches and logs inside the transaction"]},{"id":57,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"@Component vs @Bean: when is each appropriate?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Use `@Component` stereotypes for classes you own and want discovered by scanning; use `@Bean` factory methods when you need explicit construction: third-party types, several configured instances of one type, or conditional setup.","answer":["`@Component` (and `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller`, `@Configuration`) marks your own class for classpath scanning. Spring finds it under the `@ComponentScan` base packages (in Boot, the package of the `@SpringBootApplication` class and below), picks the constructor and wires its parameters. It is the right default for application services, adapters and controllers: one class, one bean, trivial construction.","`@Bean` is a factory method in a `@Configuration` class where you write the construction yourself. Use it for types you cannot annotate (`ObjectMapper`, `RestClient`, `DataSource`, `KafkaTemplate`, a vendor SDK client), for several instances of the same type with different settings distinguished by name and `@Qualifier`, for conditional or environment-specific wiring (`@Profile`, `@ConditionalOnProperty`), and whenever construction needs builders, properties or lifecycle methods.","The trade-off is implicit versus explicit. Scanning is convenient but should stay bounded to your application root: scanning broad packages drags in library or test classes, slows startup and produces surprising beans. `@Bean` is explicit and reviewable but more code. Shared libraries should not ask consumers to scan their packages; they should ship auto-configuration whose `@Bean` methods are guarded by conditions."],"keyPoints":["@Component is discovered for owned classes","@Bean gives explicit construction for third-party types, conditional setup or multiple instances"],"examples":[{"title":"Two differently configured HTTP clients","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(ClientsProperties.class)\nclass HttpClientsConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    RestClient creditBureauClient(RestClient.Builder builder, ClientsProperties props) {\n        return builder                                   // prototype: a fresh builder per method\n            .baseUrl(props.creditBureau().baseUrl())\n            .requestFactory(timeouts(Duration.ofMillis(500), Duration.ofSeconds(3)))\n            .defaultHeader(\"X-Api-Key\", props.creditBureau().apiKey())\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    RestClient paymentGatewayClient(RestClient.Builder builder, ClientsProperties props) {\n        return builder\n            .baseUrl(props.paymentGateway().baseUrl())\n            .requestFactory(timeouts(Duration.ofMillis(300), Duration.ofSeconds(10)))\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    private static ClientHttpRequestFactory timeouts(Duration connect, Duration read) {\n        var factory = new SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory();\n        factory.setConnectTimeout(connect);\n        factory.setReadTimeout(read);\n        return factory;\n    }\n}","note":"`RestClient` is a third-party type needed twice with different settings, the textbook case for `@Bean`; the bean names come from the method names."},{"title":"Owned class as @Component consuming qualified beans","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class CreditBureauAdapter {\n    private final RestClient client;\n\n    public CreditBureauAdapter(@Qualifier(\"creditBureauClient\") RestClient client) {\n        this.client = client;\n    }\n\n    public CreditReport fetch(String nationalId) {\n        return client.get()\n            .uri(\"/reports/{id}\", nationalId)\n            .retrieve()\n            .body(CreditReport.class);\n    }\n}","note":"Your own adapter is simply scanned; the `@Qualifier` picks one of the two `RestClient` beans, and without it startup fails with a no-unique-bean error."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you register two differently configured HTTP clients?","a":"Define two `@Bean` methods in a `@Configuration` class, each built from Spring Boot's auto-configured `RestClient.Builder`, which is prototype-scoped so every method gets a fresh builder that already has Boot's customizers (message converters, observability) applied. Give each its own base URL, timeouts and authentication from typed `@ConfigurationProperties`, and inject them by bean name with `@Qualifier` or a custom qualifier annotation, optionally marking a default with `@Primary`. Keep connection pools, timeouts and retry policies separate per client, so a slow credit bureau cannot eat the timeout budget or connections of the payment gateway."}],"extra":["**Full vs lite mode:** `@Configuration` classes are CGLIB-proxied so calling one `@Bean` method from another returns the singleton; `proxyBeanMethods = false` (what Boot's own auto-configurations use) skips that proxy for faster startup, so inject beans as method parameters instead of calling methods.","**Destroy inference:** a `@Bean` whose type has a public `close()` or `shutdown()` method gets it called on context shutdown automatically; set `destroyMethod = \"\"` for objects you do not own.","**Declared return type:** type matching and conditions such as `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` look at the declared return type before instantiation, so return the most specific type rather than a vague interface.","**No silent overrides:** Spring Boot disables bean definition overriding by default, so two beans with the same name fail startup instead of one silently replacing the other.","**Static post-processor beans:** declare `BeanFactoryPostProcessor` beans as `static` `@Bean` methods so they do not force early instantiation of their configuration class."],"redFlags":["Treats them as identical","uses uncontrolled component scanning"]},{"id":58,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How does Spring Boot auto-configuration work?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Boot loads a list of `@AutoConfiguration` classes after your own configuration and applies each one only if its conditions (classpath, existing beans, properties) match, so defining your own bean makes the default back off.","answer":["`@SpringBootApplication` includes `@EnableAutoConfiguration`, which imports `AutoConfigurationImportSelector`. It reads candidate class names from `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` in every jar on the classpath (Boot 3 no longer uses `spring.factories` for this), removes exclusions and registers the rest as ordinary configuration classes. Because it is a deferred import selector, auto-configuration is processed after your own `@Configuration` classes and scanned components.","Each auto-configuration is guarded by conditions. `@ConditionalOnClass` activates it only if a library is on the classpath (Hikari, Jackson, Kafka client), `@ConditionalOnProperty` checks a property, `@ConditionalOnWebApplication` checks the application type, and `@ConditionalOnBean` or `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` inspect the bean registry. Settings come from `@ConfigurationProperties` classes such as `DataSourceProperties` bound to `spring.datasource.*`, and ordering between auto-configurations uses `@AutoConfiguration(before = ..., after = ...)`.","Back-off is the key design idea: most auto-configured beans are `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, so declaring your own `DataSource` or `ObjectMapper` replaces the default with no extra ceremony. Prefer customizer beans such as `Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer` or `RestClientCustomizer` when you only want to tweak the default, and exclude an auto-configuration explicitly with `spring.autoconfigure.exclude` when you want it gone.","It is not magic, it is inspectable: run with `--debug` to print the condition evaluation report, or query `/actuator/conditions`, to see which configurations matched and why the others did not."],"keyPoints":["Conditional configuration activates based on classpath, beans and properties","user beans can make auto-config back off"],"examples":[{"title":"A company starter's auto-configuration","lang":"java","code":"@AutoConfiguration(after = JacksonAutoConfiguration.class)\n@ConditionalOnClass(AuditClient.class)\n@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = \"acme.audit\", name = \"enabled\", havingValue = \"true\", matchIfMissing = true)\n@EnableConfigurationProperties(AuditProperties.class)\npublic class AuditAutoConfiguration {\n\n    @Bean\n    @ConditionalOnMissingBean          // backs off if the application defines its own AuditClient\n    AuditClient auditClient(AuditProperties props, ObjectMapper objectMapper) {\n        return new HttpAuditClient(props.endpoint(), props.timeout(), objectMapper);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    @ConditionalOnMissingBean\n    AuditEventPublisher auditEventPublisher(AuditClient client) {\n        return new AuditEventPublisher(client);\n    }\n}","note":"This is exactly the pattern Boot's own modules use: guard on the classpath, allow a property switch, and let user beans win through `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`."},{"title":"Registering and debugging auto-configuration","lang":"bash","code":"# Registration file inside the starter jar (one class name per line)\ncat src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports\ncom.acme.audit.autoconfigure.AuditAutoConfiguration\n\n# Why did an auto-configuration apply or not?\njava -jar credit-service.jar --debug | grep -A3 AuditAutoConfiguration\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/conditions | jq '.contexts[].negativeMatches | keys'\n\n# Switch one off explicitly\njava -jar credit-service.jar \\\n  --spring.autoconfigure.exclude=com.acme.audit.autoconfigure.AuditAutoConfiguration","note":"The conditions report lists positive and negative matches with the exact condition that decided each one, which settles most 'why is this bean here' questions."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What does @ConditionalOnMissingBean do?","a":"`@ConditionalOnMissingBean` registers the annotated bean only if no bean of the given type (by default the `@Bean` method's return type), name or annotation has already been defined when the condition is evaluated. It is the mechanism that makes auto-configuration back off: declare your own `ObjectMapper` and Boot's default is never created. Because it only sees bean definitions registered so far, it is order-sensitive; it is reliable in auto-configuration classes, which are processed after user configuration, and the Boot documentation recommends using it only there."}],"extra":["**Registration file:** since Boot 2.7 auto-configurations are listed in `AutoConfiguration.imports`, and Boot 3.0 dropped support for registering them through `spring.factories`; an old starter that was never migrated silently does nothing on Boot 3.","**Cheap filtering:** an annotation processor writes `META-INF/spring-autoconfigure-metadata.properties`, letting Boot discard candidates whose `@ConditionalOnClass` fails without loading their classes, which keeps startup fast with hundreds of candidates.","**Testing starters:** `ApplicationContextRunner` with `AutoConfigurations.of(...)` and `withPropertyValues(...)` tests each condition branch in milliseconds without starting a server.","**Native images:** with Spring AOT and GraalVM, conditions are evaluated at build time, so classpath and profile-driven conditions are frozen into the binary.","**Replacement pitfalls:** replacing an auto-configured `ObjectMapper` also drops everything Boot did to it (registered modules, `spring.jackson.*` properties), which is why customizers are usually the better lever."],"redFlags":["Calls it magic","cannot explain conditional annotations"]},{"id":59,"category":"Spring & Spring Boot","question":"How should configuration and secrets be managed across environments?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Bind configuration into validated, typed `@ConfigurationProperties` that fail fast at startup, supply environment differences from outside the artifact, and keep secrets in a secret manager or Kubernetes Secret, never in Git.","answer":["Build one artifact and promote it unchanged through environments; everything that differs comes from outside it: environment variables, mounted files, config imports or a config server. Spring Boot's property source order (command-line arguments, environment variables with relaxed binding such as `SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL`, profile-specific files, then `application.yaml`) makes this natural. Use profiles for environment wiring, not for business logic.","Make configuration typed and validated. Group related settings into one `@ConfigurationProperties` record per concern, annotate it with `@Validated` and Bean Validation constraints, and let the context refuse to start when a required URL or credential is missing. No silent defaults for anything environment-specific: a default production URL or an empty fallback password turns a deployment mistake into a data incident. Scattered `@Value` strings are untyped, unvalidated and hard to discover.","Secrets never go into Git, Dockerfiles, Helm values or `application-prod.yaml`. Keep them in a secret manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault) or Kubernetes Secrets, ideally synced by the External Secrets Operator or a CSI driver, and mount them as files read through `spring.config.import=configtree:`. Files beat environment variables, which leak into child processes, crash dumps and debug output. A Kubernetes Secret is only base64 encoded, so enable etcd encryption and restrict RBAC on secrets. Add secret scanning (gitleaks, push protection) in CI; a leaked secret must be rotated, not just deleted from history."],"keyPoints":["Use externalized typed configuration, startup validation and a secret manager/Kubernetes Secret","never commit credentials"],"examples":[{"title":"Typed, validated properties that fail fast","lang":"java","code":"@Validated\n@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"credit.bureau\")\npublic record CreditBureauProperties(\n        @NotNull URI baseUrl,\n        @NotBlank String apiKey,\n        @NotNull Duration connectTimeout,\n        @NotNull Duration readTimeout,\n        @DefaultValue(\"3\") @Min(1) @Max(10) int maxAttempts) {\n\n    @AssertTrue(message = \"readTimeout must be greater than connectTimeout\")\n    public boolean isTimeoutOrderValid() {\n        return readTimeout == null || connectTimeout == null\n            || readTimeout.compareTo(connectTimeout) > 0;\n    }\n}\n\n@SpringBootApplication\n@ConfigurationPropertiesScan\npublic class CreditServiceApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(CreditServiceApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n// Missing api-key: APPLICATION FAILED TO START, Property: credit.bureau.apiKey, Reason: must not be blank","note":"Durations parse from values like `500ms` or `2s`, and a broken deployment is rejected before it takes traffic instead of failing on the first credit check."},{"title":"Committed config vs mounted secret","lang":"yaml","code":"# File 1: application.yaml (committed): structure and non-secret settings only\nspring:\n  config:\n    import: \"optional:configtree:/etc/secrets/\"\ncredit:\n  bureau:\n    base-url: ${CREDIT_BUREAU_URL}     # no default: startup fails if unset\n    connect-timeout: 500ms\n    read-timeout: 2s\n    # credit.bureau.api-key arrives as the file /etc/secrets/credit.bureau.api-key\n---\n# File 2: pod spec excerpt; the Secret is synced from Vault by External Secrets Operator\ncontainers:\n  - name: credit-service\n    env:\n      - name: CREDIT_BUREAU_URL\n        value: https://api.bureau.example.com\n    volumeMounts:\n      - name: app-secrets\n        mountPath: /etc/secrets\n        readOnly: true\nvolumes:\n  - name: app-secrets\n    secret:\n      secretName: credit-service-secrets","note":"With `configtree:` each file name becomes a property name, so the Secret key `credit.bureau.api-key` binds straight into the record without appearing in any committed file."}],"followUps":[{"q":"@ConfigurationProperties vs @Value?","a":"`@ConfigurationProperties` binds a whole prefix into a typed object or record: relaxed binding, conversion to `Duration`, `DataSize`, lists and maps, nested groups, Bean Validation at startup with `@Validated`, and IDE metadata from the configuration processor. Each concern gets one discoverable, testable class. `@Value(\"${key}\")` injects a single value and supports SpEL, but scatters string keys across the codebase without grouping or validation. Use `@Value` for a rare one-off and `@ConfigurationProperties` for everything else."},{"q":"How would you rotate a secret?","a":"Design for overlap: old and new credentials must be valid at the same time. Create the new credential at the provider (a second database user or API key), write it to the secret manager, let it sync to the Kubernetes Secret, then roll the pods (a checksum annotation or a tool like Reloader triggers a rolling restart) and watch health and error rates. Only then revoke the old credential. Better still, use Vault dynamic database credentials or the cloud provider's managed rotation so rotation is routine and automated rather than an incident."}],"extra":["**Actuator masking:** since Boot 3.0 `/actuator/env` and `/actuator/configprops` mask all values by default; keep it that way and never expose those endpoints publicly.","**Configuration metadata:** adding `spring-boot-configuration-processor` generates metadata so IDEs autocomplete and document your custom properties and flag unknown keys.","**Rotation and mounts:** Kubernetes eventually refreshes mounted Secret files, but not `subPath` mounts or environment variables; either way Spring binds configuration at startup, so rotation needs a restart or an explicit refresh mechanism.","**Dynamic credentials:** Vault's database secrets engine issues short-lived, per-instance database users with leases, which removes long-lived shared passwords entirely.","**Cross-field validation:** an `@AssertTrue` method on the properties record (as in the example) catches inconsistent combinations such as a read timeout shorter than the connect timeout at startup."],"redFlags":["Uses scattered @Value strings","commits secrets","relies on silent defaults"]},{"id":60,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Explain transient, managed, detached and removed entity states.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Transient objects are unknown to JPA, managed ones are tracked and dirty-checked, detached ones are no longer tracked, removed ones await deletion; `merge` copies detached state onto a managed instance and returns it.","answer":["A **transient** entity is a plain `new` object with no persistence context; `persist()` makes it managed and schedules an INSERT. A **managed** entity lives in the persistence context, which is both an identity map (one instance per id per context) and a unit of work: Hibernate keeps a snapshot of its loaded state and at flush compares the current state with it, issuing UPDATEs automatically. Inside a transaction, changing a managed entity needs no `save()` call.","A **detached** entity was managed but its context ended: the transaction finished and the context closed, `clear()` or `detach()` was called, or the object went to a client and came back. It keeps its id, but changes are not tracked, and touching an uninitialized lazy association throws `LazyInitializationException`. A **removed** entity is still managed but scheduled for DELETE at flush.","`merge(detached)` finds or loads the managed instance with the same id (a SELECT if it is not in the context yet), copies the detached state onto it and returns that managed instance; the argument stays detached. Transient input produces a new managed copy and an INSERT. Spring Data's `save()` calls `persist` when `isNew()` is true and `merge` otherwise, which matters for entities with application-assigned ids: they look not new, so every save costs an extra SELECT. Implementing `Persistable` or adding a wrapper-typed `@Version` field (null means new) fixes that."],"keyPoints":["Managed entities are tracked and dirty-checked","detached are not","merge copies state into a managed instance"],"examples":[{"title":"State transitions in one service","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CustomerService {\n    private final EntityManager em;\n\n    public CustomerService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public UUID register(String email) {\n        Customer c = new Customer(email);           // transient\n        em.persist(c);                              // managed: INSERT at flush\n        return c.getId();\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void changeEmail(UUID id, String email) {\n        Customer c = em.find(Customer.class, id);   // managed\n        c.changeEmail(email);                       // no save(): UPDATE at commit\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public Customer verify(Customer fromClient) {   // detached: has id, not tracked\n        Customer managed = em.merge(fromClient);    // SELECT, copy state, return managed copy\n        fromClient.markVerified();                  // BAD: ignored, still detached\n        managed.markVerified();                     // GOOD: tracked and flushed\n        return managed;                             // managed != fromClient\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void delete(UUID id) {\n        em.remove(em.find(Customer.class, id));     // removed: DELETE at flush\n    }\n}","note":"The key line is the BAD one: changes to the object you passed into `merge` are silently lost, because only the returned instance is managed."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What object does merge return?","a":"It returns the managed instance from the persistence context, which is a different object from the argument unless the argument was already managed. The argument remains detached or transient, so later changes to it are ignored; always continue with the return value, as in `order = em.merge(order)`. If there is no row for the id, or no id at all, merge creates a new managed instance that will be inserted. Spring Data's `save()` has the same contract, so use its return value too."},{"q":"When does flush occur?","a":"With Hibernate's default `FlushMode.AUTO`, flush happens at transaction commit, before a JPQL/HQL or Criteria query whose tables overlap with pending changes, before native SQL queries run through the JPA `EntityManager`, and whenever you call `flush()`. Persisting an entity with an IDENTITY id also executes its INSERT immediately because the id is needed right away. `FlushModeType.COMMIT` defers flushing to commit, and Spring's `readOnly = true` switches Hibernate to manual flushing so nothing is written."}],"extra":["**Identity guarantee:** within one persistence context two `find` calls for the same id return the same Java instance, which gives application-level repeatable reads for entities.","**getReference:** `em.getReference(Customer.class, id)` returns an uninitialized proxy without a SELECT, ideal for setting a foreign key association when you only have the id.","**clear() drops pending work:** `clear()` detaches everything including unflushed changes, so always `flush()` before `clear()` in batch code.","**Hibernate 6 API:** the legacy `Session.save`, `update` and `saveOrUpdate` methods are deprecated in Hibernate 6; use `persist` and `merge`, which follow the JPA state model.","**Web updates:** instead of merging a client-built entity (which can null out fields the client never sent), carry the id and version in a DTO, load the managed entity, check the version and apply the change through domain methods."],"redFlags":["Thinks save is required for every managed change","assumes merge returns the same object"]},{"id":61,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Lazy vs eager loading: why is changing everything to EAGER not a solution?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"EAGER is a static, global decision that loads associations for every use case, causing over-fetching, large joins and hidden N+1; keep associations LAZY and pick a fetch plan per use case inside the transaction.","answer":["`FetchType.EAGER` is fixed in the mapping, so it applies to every query and every use case that touches the entity, whether or not it needs the association. A list screen that shows 50 order numbers ends up loading customers, addresses and lines. Eager collections produce wide joins and cartesian products (two eager `List` collections even fail with `MultipleBagFetchException`), and JPQL queries do not join eager associations automatically, so Hibernate loads them with extra SELECTs afterwards: EAGER often creates N+1 rather than preventing it. You also cannot switch it back to lazy for one query.","JPA defaults make this worse: `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne` are EAGER by default, while collections are LAZY. The production-safe rule is to declare every association `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` explicitly and decide what to load per use case.","LAZY is not free either: it requires deliberate transaction and fetch design. Data must be loaded inside the transaction, and each use case declares its fetch plan with `JOIN FETCH`, an `@EntityGraph`, a DTO projection, or batch fetching as a safety net. `LazyInitializationException` is a useful signal that a use case has no fetch plan, not a reason to switch to EAGER, rely on Open Session in View, or enable `hibernate.enable_lazy_load_no_trans`, which opens a temporary session and connection for every lazy load."],"keyPoints":["EAGER can over-fetch and create large joins","LAZY needs deliberate transaction and fetch plans"],"examples":[{"title":"Lazy mapping with per-use-case fetch plans","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LoanApplication {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    // BAD would be plain @ManyToOne: EAGER by default, loaded by every query\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"application\")          // LAZY by default\n    private Set<Document> documents = new HashSet<>();\n\n    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)\n    private ApplicationStatus status;\n}\n\npublic interface LoanApplicationRepository extends JpaRepository<LoanApplication, Long> {\n\n    // Review screen: customer and documents in one statement\n    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"customer\", \"documents\"})\n    Optional<LoanApplication> findWithDetailsById(Long id);\n\n    // Underwriting batch: only the customer\n    @Query(\"select a from LoanApplication a join fetch a.customer where a.status = :status\")\n    List<LoanApplication> findWithCustomerByStatus(@Param(\"status\") ApplicationStatus status);\n}","note":"The mapping says nothing about what to load; each repository method states the plan its use case needs, so a list screen never pays for the review screen's graph."},{"title":"Safety net configuration","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  jpa:\n    open-in-view: false              # lazy loading only inside service transactions\n    properties:\n      hibernate:\n        default_batch_fetch_size: 32 # remaining lazy loads use IN lists, not one query per row\n        # never: enable_lazy_load_no_trans: true","note":"Disabling OSIV surfaces missing fetch plans as exceptions in tests, and batch fetching limits the damage of any plan you missed."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Entity graph vs fetch join?","a":"Both load associations in the same SQL statement. `JOIN FETCH` is written in JPQL, so it gives full control (inner or left join, combined with filters and ordering) but ties the fetch plan to a query string. An entity graph is declarative, uses left outer joins, can be attached to derived query methods or `findById` through `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = ...)`, and lets you reuse one query with different fetch plans. Both share the same limits: fetching a collection breaks SQL pagination, and fetching two collections at once multiplies rows."},{"q":"What are OSIV trade-offs?","a":"Open Session in View keeps the persistence context open for the whole web request, so lazy associations load in controllers and during JSON serialization; convenient, and it hides `LazyInitializationException`. The cost is that those loads run outside the service transaction, one statement at a time, creating invisible N+1 in the web layer; the database connection can stay checked out until the response is written, reducing pool throughput; and persistence concerns leak into the view. Spring Boot enables it by default (`spring.jpa.open-in-view=true`) and logs a warning; production APIs should turn it off and return DTOs built inside the transaction."}],"extra":["**Batch fetching:** `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size` initializes many proxies or collections of the same association per query using an IN list, turning N+1 into roughly N divided by the batch size plus one.","**Inverse one-to-one:** a `@OneToOne(mappedBy = ...)` side cannot be truly lazy without bytecode enhancement, because Hibernate must query to decide between null and a proxy; prefer `@MapsId` or mapping it only from the foreign key side.","**fetchgraph vs loadgraph:** a fetch graph treats attributes outside the graph as lazy while a load graph keeps their mapped fetch type; Spring Data's `@EntityGraph` defaults to the FETCH type.","**Measure the plan:** assert statement counts in integration tests (Hibernate statistics or datasource-proxy) so a fetch plan regression fails the build rather than production latency.","**Skip entities for reads:** for read-only screens a DTO projection sidesteps the lazy vs eager question entirely and selects only the columns shown."],"redFlags":["Fixes LazyInitializationException by making all relations eager"]},{"id":62,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"What is the N+1 query problem and how do you fix it?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"N+1 is one parent query plus one query per parent when a lazy association is touched; fix it per use case with fetch joins, entity graphs, DTO projections or batch fetching, and measure query counts.","answer":["You run one query for the parents, say 50 orders, then iterate and touch a lazy association such as `order.getCustomer().getName()` or `order.getLines()`. Each access initializes a proxy or collection with its own SELECT, so the page costs 51 round trips or more. Each query is fast alone, so the problem hides in development with 5 rows and explodes in production with 500, adding latency, connection pool pressure and database CPU. EAGER mappings cause it too, because JPQL loads eager associations with secondary selects.","Fixes, chosen per use case: `JOIN FETCH` or an `@EntityGraph` loads the association in the same statement; a DTO projection (`select new ...` or a Spring Data interface or record projection) selects only the needed columns and skips entities altogether, the best option for read-only APIs; batch fetching (`@BatchSize` or `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size`) initializes many parents' associations with one `IN` query, a good global safety net; and `@Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)` loads the collections of all parents from the previous query in one statement.","Measure, do not guess. Log SQL in development, enable Hibernate statistics, and add integration tests that assert the number of statements per use case. In production, the signature is a burst of identical statements in APM traces or a huge `calls` count relative to rows in `pg_stat_statements`."],"keyPoints":["One parent query triggers one query per child","fix with fetch joins, entity graphs, projections or batch fetching"],"examples":[{"title":"N+1 loop vs single DTO query","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderView(UUID id, String customerName, Long lineCount) {}\n\n// BAD: 1 query for orders, then 1 per order for lines and 1 per distinct customer\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic List<OrderView> recentOrders() {\n    return orders.findTop50ByOrderByCreatedAtDesc().stream()\n        .map(o -> new OrderView(o.getId(), o.getCustomer().getName(), (long) o.getLines().size()))\n        .toList();\n}\n\n// GOOD: one statement, only the columns the screen needs\npublic interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, UUID> {\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        select new com.acme.orders.api.OrderView(o.id, c.name, count(l))\n        from Order o join o.customer c left join o.lines l\n        group by o.id, c.name, o.createdAt\n        order by o.createdAt desc\n        \"\"\")\n    List<OrderView> findRecentViews(Pageable page);   // PageRequest.of(0, 50)\n}","note":"The projection pages parents safely because it returns one row per order, and it never creates entities, snapshots or proxies."},{"title":"Guarding the query count in a test","lang":"java","code":"@SpringBootTest(properties = \"spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true\")\nclass OrderQueryCountTest {\n\n    @Autowired OrderService orderService;\n    @Autowired EntityManagerFactory emf;\n\n    @Test\n    void recentOrdersUsesASingleStatement() {\n        Statistics stats = emf.unwrap(SessionFactory.class).getStatistics();\n        stats.clear();\n\n        orderService.recentOrders();\n\n        assertThat(stats.getPrepareStatementCount()).isEqualTo(1);\n    }\n}","note":"Seed the test data with several parents and children; with one parent an N+1 is invisible because N is 1."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can a fetch join break pagination?","a":"Fetch joining a collection returns one row per child, so an SQL `LIMIT/OFFSET` would cut through a parent's children and return incomplete collections. Hibernate therefore does not apply the limit in SQL: it loads the whole result set and paginates in memory, logging a warning ('firstResult/maxResults specified with collection fetch; applying in memory'), which is a memory and latency bomb on big tables. Set `hibernate.query.fail_on_pagination_over_collection_fetch=true` to fail fast instead. The fix is two queries: page the parent ids first, then load those parents with `join fetch ... where o.id in :ids`, or use batch fetching for the collection."}],"extra":["**Hibernate 6 deduplication:** join fetching a collection no longer needs `distinct` in HQL; Hibernate 6 deduplicates the root entities automatically.","**Cartesian products:** fetching two collections in one query multiplies rows (orders x lines x payments); fetch one collection per query and let batch fetching or a second query load the other.","**Hidden triggers:** `toString`, `equals`/`hashCode` and Jackson serialization that touch lazy associations cause N+1 just like explicit loops, so keep them off associations.","**Bind logging:** in Hibernate 6 `org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG` logs statements and `org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE` logs bind values; never leave bind logging on in production because it writes customer data to logs."],"redFlags":["Uses EAGER as a universal fix","does not measure query count"]},{"id":63,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Optimistic vs pessimistic locking in JPA.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Optimistic locking holds no locks and detects conflicts at write time via a `@Version` check, so it needs retries; pessimistic locking takes row locks up front, risking waits, deadlocks and lower throughput.","answer":["Optimistic locking adds a `@Version` column. Every UPDATE becomes `update ... set version = ? where id = ? and version = ?`; if another transaction committed first, zero rows match and Hibernate throws `OptimisticLockException` (Spring translates it to `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`). No locks are held between read and write, so it scales when conflicts are rare and even works across requests: return the version as an ETag and require `If-Match` on updates.","The price is conflict handling. Retry the whole use case in a new transaction (re-read, re-apply, re-validate) a bounded number of times, or return 409 Conflict when the collision is a real human edit conflict. Use `OPTIMISTIC_FORCE_INCREMENT` to bump an aggregate root's version when only a child changed.","Pessimistic locking (`LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE`, a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` style lock in PostgreSQL) locks rows at read time until commit. Conflicting transactions wait instead of failing, which is right for hot rows with frequent conflicts, but waiting transactions hold connections, throughput drops, and deadlocks appear when two transactions lock rows in different orders. Always pair it with a lock timeout or `NOWAIT`, lock in a consistent order, and keep the transaction short.","Default to optimistic; reach for pessimistic when contention is high or retries are expensive, and consider an atomic conditional UPDATE, which often beats both for balances and counters."],"keyPoints":["Optimistic uses a version and conflict detection","pessimistic acquires DB locks and risks waits/deadlocks"],"examples":[{"title":"Optimistic locking with bounded retries","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class CreditLimit {\n    @Id private UUID accountId;\n    private BigDecimal available;\n    @Version private long version;               // checked and bumped on every UPDATE\n\n    public void reserve(BigDecimal amount) {\n        if (available.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new LimitExceededException(accountId);\n        available = available.subtract(amount);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class LimitService {\n    private final TransactionTemplate tx;\n    private final CreditLimitRepository limits;\n\n    public LimitService(TransactionTemplate tx, CreditLimitRepository limits) {\n        this.tx = tx;\n        this.limits = limits;\n    }\n\n    public void reserve(UUID accountId, BigDecimal amount) {\n        for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {\n            try {                                // each attempt: new transaction, fresh read\n                tx.executeWithoutResult(s -> limits.findById(accountId).orElseThrow().reserve(amount));\n                return;\n            } catch (OptimisticLockingFailureException e) {\n                if (attempt == 3) throw e;       // surface as 409 after bounded retries\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The retry loop sits outside the transaction, so every attempt re-reads the current balance and re-validates the business rule instead of replaying stale data."},{"title":"Pessimistic lock vs atomic conditional update","lang":"java","code":"public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, UUID> {\n\n    // Pessimistic: row lock until commit; timeout \"0\" means NOWAIT (fail instead of queueing)\n    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)\n    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = \"jakarta.persistence.lock.timeout\", value = \"0\"))\n    @Query(\"select a from Account a where a.id = :id\")\n    Optional<Account> findByIdForUpdate(@Param(\"id\") UUID id);\n\n    // Often better: one atomic statement, no read, no lock wait window\n    @Modifying\n    @Query(\"update Account a set a.balance = a.balance - :amount where a.id = :id and a.balance >= :amount\")\n    int debitIfSufficient(@Param(\"id\") UUID id, @Param(\"amount\") BigDecimal amount);\n}","note":"A return value of 0 from `debitIfSufficient` means insufficient funds; note that bulk JPQL updates bypass `@Version` and the persistence context."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is SELECT FOR UPDATE justified?","a":"It is justified when conflicts on the same rows are frequent, so optimistic retries would thrash (a hot account balance, a flash-sale inventory row, seat or slot reservations), when retrying is expensive or has side effects, and for work queues where `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets workers claim rows safely. Keep the critical section tiny, lock rows in a deterministic order (for example sorted by id) to avoid deadlocks, set a lock timeout or `NOWAIT`, and never hold the lock across a remote call. If the invariant fits a single conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= :amount`, prefer that over locking at all."}],"extra":["**SKIP LOCKED queues:** `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (lock timeout hint `-2` in Hibernate) lets several workers claim different rows from a job or outbox table without blocking each other.","**Deadlock detection:** PostgreSQL checks for deadlocks after `deadlock_timeout` (1 s by default) and aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`, which your code should treat as retryable.","**Bulk updates and versions:** JPQL bulk `update` statements do not increment `@Version` unless you set it yourself (HQL also offers `update versioned`), so mixing bulk updates with optimistic entities needs care.","**Version type:** prefer a numeric `@Version` over a timestamp; timestamps can collide within clock resolution and depend on clock sync across nodes.","**Compare the right version:** across HTTP requests the check only protects you if you compare the version the client saw (from `If-Match` or the DTO); loading fresh and copying fields without that check reintroduces lost updates."],"redFlags":["Uses pessimistic locking by default","forgets optimistic retries"]},{"id":64,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Explain cascade options and orphanRemoval.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Cascade propagates entity manager operations such as persist, merge and remove from a parent to its associated entities; `orphanRemoval` also deletes a child when it is disconnected from its owning parent's association.","answer":["Cascading is about lifecycle ownership. `CascadeType.PERSIST`, `MERGE`, `REMOVE`, `REFRESH` and `DETACH` (or `ALL`) make an operation on the parent apply to associated entities, so persisting an `Order` also persists its new `OrderLine`s. It is not a database `ON DELETE CASCADE`: Hibernate performs it on loaded entities. Use it only from an aggregate root to children that live and die with it.","`orphanRemoval = true` goes further: when a child is removed from the parent's collection, or a one-to-one reference is set to null, Hibernate deletes the child row at flush even though the parent still exists. That is exactly right for composition (order lines, documents of a loan application) and wrong for anything shared or reassignable.","Misused cascades delete data. `CascadeType.ALL` or `REMOVE` on a `@ManyToOne` means deleting one order deletes its customer; on a `@ManyToMany` it deletes shared tags or roles still used elsewhere. Cascade REMOVE also loads every child and deletes them one by one, which is slow for large collections, and JPQL bulk deletes bypass cascades entirely.","A sound default: `@OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)` on true compositions, no cascade on `@ManyToOne` or `@ManyToMany`, and helper methods that keep both sides of the association in sync."],"keyPoints":["Cascade propagates lifecycle operations","orphanRemoval deletes children removed from an owned association"],"examples":[{"title":"Aggregate root owning its lines","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\n@Table(name = \"orders\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)              // GOOD: no cascade toward a shared entity\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"order\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n    private List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();\n\n    public void addLine(Product product, int quantity) {\n        lines.add(new OrderLine(this, product, quantity));   // sets the owning side\n    }\n\n    public void removeLine(OrderLine line) {\n        lines.remove(line);                         // orphanRemoval: DELETE at flush\n        line.detachFromOrder();                     // keep both sides consistent\n    }\n\n    public void replaceLines(List<OrderLine> newLines) {\n        lines.clear();                              // GOOD: mutate the managed collection\n        newLines.forEach(l -> { l.attachTo(this); lines.add(l); });\n        // BAD: this.lines = newLines;  fails at flush with orphanRemoval\n    }\n}","note":"`OrderLine` owns the foreign key through its `@ManyToOne order` field; `Order` owns the lifecycle through cascade and orphanRemoval."},{"title":"Cascades that delete the wrong data","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LoanApplication {\n    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;\n\n    // BAD: removing an application also removes the shared customer\n    @ManyToOne(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)\n    private Customer customer;\n\n    // BAD: removing an application deletes Tag rows other applications still use\n    @ManyToMany(cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE)\n    private Set<Tag> tags = new HashSet<>();\n\n    // GOOD: owned children, removed with the application or when detached from it\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"application\", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)\n    private List<Document> documents = new ArrayList<>();\n}","note":"Ask of each cascade: if the parent disappears, should this row disappear too? Only the documents pass that test."}],"followUps":[{"q":"REMOVE vs orphanRemoval?","a":"`CascadeType.REMOVE` deletes children only when the parent itself is removed with `em.remove(parent)`. `orphanRemoval = true` does that too, and additionally deletes a child that is disconnected from a parent that still exists, for example `order.getLines().remove(line)` or setting a one-to-one reference to null. Use orphanRemoval only when the child cannot exist without that specific parent; the JPA specification says orphaned entities must not be reassigned to another relationship, so it is wrong for children that move between parents."},{"q":"Which side owns the relationship?","a":"The owning side is the one that maps the foreign key: in a bidirectional one-to-many it is the child's `@ManyToOne` with `@JoinColumn`, while the `@OneToMany(mappedBy = ...)` side is the inverse. For `@OneToOne` it is the side with the foreign key column, and for `@ManyToMany` the side without `mappedBy` controls the join table. Hibernate writes the foreign key only from the owning side, so adding a line to `order.getLines()` without setting `line.order` persists no link; keep both sides in sync with helper methods. Ownership and cascade are independent: you usually cascade from the inverse parent side."}],"extra":["**Bulk operations skip everything:** JPQL or Criteria bulk `delete` and `update` bypass cascades, orphanRemoval, lifecycle callbacks and the persistence context, so children must be handled explicitly or by the database.","**Large child sets:** `@OnDelete(action = OnDeleteAction.CASCADE)` on the child's `@ManyToOne` together with `ON DELETE CASCADE` in the schema lets PostgreSQL delete children in one statement instead of loading and deleting them one by one.","**Replace vs mutate:** with orphanRemoval, assigning a new collection instance instead of mutating the managed one fails with 'A collection with cascade=\"all-delete-orphan\" was no longer referenced by the owning entity instance'.","**Unidirectional @OneToMany:** without `mappedBy`, Hibernate uses a join table, or with `@JoinColumn` inserts children and then issues extra UPDATEs to set the foreign key; the bidirectional mapping with the `@ManyToOne` owning side is the efficient one.","**MERGE cascade on client graphs:** cascading `merge` from a client-built graph can overwrite children or, with orphanRemoval, delete the ones the client simply omitted from the payload."],"redFlags":["Uses CascadeType.ALL everywhere","cannot explain accidental deletes"]},{"id":65,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"What is dirty checking, and why can it create unexpected updates?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Hibernate snapshots each managed entity on load and, at flush, issues UPDATEs for any difference from the snapshot, so any mutation of a managed entity inside a transaction gets written.","answer":["When an entity is loaded into the persistence context, Hibernate stores a copy of its loaded state. At flush (commit, before overlapping queries, or an explicit `flush()`), it compares every managed entity's current property values with its snapshot and schedules an UPDATE for each dirty entity, by default setting all columns. No `save()` is needed; calling `repository.save()` on a managed entity changes nothing. The cost grows with the number of managed entities and properties, and snapshots roughly double the memory of a large context.","Surprise updates come from treating entities as ordinary mutable objects inside a transaction. A service masks a phone number for a response, a mapper calls a setter, validation fills in a default, or a temporary calculation writes into a field, and all of it is persisted at commit. Broad boundaries amplify this: class-level `@Transactional` on a read-heavy service, or Open Session in View keeping entities managed through the web layer. Mutable types cause phantom updates too: a `Date` mutated in place, a JSON attribute, or an `AttributeConverter` that does not round-trip identically looks dirty on every flush, bumping `@Version` and producing optimistic lock failures nobody can explain.","Mitigations: use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for reads (no snapshots, no flush), return DTO projections instead of entities, keep transactions narrow, expose intention-revealing domain methods instead of setters, mark reference data `@Immutable`, and watch for UPDATE statements on GET endpoints."],"keyPoints":["Hibernate tracks managed snapshots and writes changes at flush","broad transactions and mutable entities cause surprise writes"],"examples":[{"title":"Presentation logic writing to the database","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: formatting mutates a managed entity; the commit issues an UPDATE on a GET\n@Transactional\npublic CustomerResponse getCustomer(UUID id) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    c.setPhone(PhoneFormat.mask(c.getPhone()));   // masked number now stored in the DB\n    c.setName(c.getName().trim());                 // silent data change\n    return CustomerResponse.from(c);\n}\n\n// GOOD: read-only transaction, formatting applied to the DTO only\n@Transactional(readOnly = true)\npublic CustomerResponse getCustomer(UUID id) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n    return new CustomerResponse(c.getId(), c.getName().trim(), PhoneFormat.mask(c.getPhone()));\n}","note":"No `save()` appears anywhere in the bad version, yet it permanently overwrites the customer's phone number with the masked value."},{"title":"What the SQL log shows for the bad version","lang":"sql","code":"-- GET /customers/42 with logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG\nselect c1_0.id,c1_0.name,c1_0.phone,c1_0.version from customer c1_0 where c1_0.id=?\nupdate customer set name=?,phone=?,version=? where id=? and version=?","note":"An UPDATE in the log of a read endpoint is the fastest way to spot unintended dirty checking in production code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When does auto-flush happen?","a":"With the default `FlushMode.AUTO`, Hibernate flushes at commit, on explicit `flush()`, and before executing a query that could otherwise miss pending changes: for JPQL/HQL and Criteria it flushes when the query's tables overlap with dirty entities, and through the JPA `EntityManager` a native SQL query flushes the whole context because Hibernate cannot tell which tables it reads. The surprise is that a harmless-looking `findBy...` halfway through a method sends pending UPDATEs, so constraint violations or optimistic lock failures surface on that line rather than at commit. `FlushModeType.COMMIT` or Spring's `readOnly = true` (manual flush) change this behavior."}],"extra":["**readOnly internals:** for `readOnly = true` transactions Spring's `HibernateJpaDialect` sets `FlushMode.MANUAL` and makes the session default to read-only, so entities are loaded without snapshots and never flushed.","**@DynamicUpdate:** makes Hibernate write only the changed columns, useful for wide tables, at the cost of generating SQL per update instead of reusing one cached statement.","**Bytecode enhancement:** Hibernate's enhancement plugin with dirty tracking lets entities record their own changes, avoiding the full snapshot comparison in very large persistence contexts.","**Selective read-only:** `session.setReadOnly(entity, true)` or the query hint `org.hibernate.readOnly` loads specific entities without snapshots inside an otherwise writable transaction.","**Symptoms to watch:** `@PreUpdate` callbacks and `@LastModifiedDate` fire for these surprise updates too, so `updated_at` changing on rows nobody edited is a strong hint."],"redFlags":["Thinks only repository.save triggers UPDATE"]},{"id":66,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"Why use DTO projections instead of returning entities from APIs?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"DTO projections fetch only the columns a use case needs, avoid lazy-loading and serialization traps, keep persistence internals private, and give clients a stable contract that does not change every time the schema does.","answer":["A DTO projection selects exactly what the endpoint returns: a JPQL constructor expression, a Spring Data interface or record projection, or plain SQL through `JdbcClient`. No entities are loaded, so there are no snapshots, no dirty checking, no accidental UPDATEs, fewer columns over the wire, and often index-only scans. For read-heavy APIs it is the single biggest ORM performance win.","Returning entities couples the API to the persistence model. Jackson walks the object graph: uninitialized lazy proxies either throw `LazyInitializationException` or, with Open Session in View, fire N+1 queries during serialization; bidirectional associations recurse infinitely; and Hibernate proxy internals leak into the JSON. Internal fields such as password hashes, risk scores, `version` or soft-delete flags are exposed unless someone remembers `@JsonIgnore`. On the input side, binding a request body to an entity is a mass-assignment hole: a client can set `creditLimit` or `status`.","DTOs make the API a deliberate contract. You can rename columns, split tables or change associations without breaking clients, version the API independently, and review exactly what leaves the service. The cost is mapping code, which records and MapStruct keep small. For writes, accept request DTOs, map them to commands, and let entity methods enforce invariants."],"keyPoints":["Fetch only required data, avoid lazy serialization and persistence leakage, and stabilize API contracts"],"examples":[{"title":"Entity as API vs query straight into a record","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the entity is the API contract; lazy graph, internals and version leak into JSON\n@GetMapping(\"/customers/{id}\")\npublic Customer get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n    return customers.findById(id).orElseThrow();\n}\n\n// GOOD: an API record filled directly by the query\npublic record CustomerSummary(UUID id, String name, String segment, Long openApplications) {}\n\npublic interface CustomerRepository extends JpaRepository<Customer, UUID> {\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        select new com.acme.credit.api.CustomerSummary(c.id, c.name, c.segment, count(a))\n        from Customer c left join c.applications a on a.status = :status\n        where c.id = :id\n        group by c.id, c.name, c.segment\n        \"\"\")\n    Optional<CustomerSummary> findSummary(@Param(\"id\") UUID id,\n                                          @Param(\"status\") ApplicationStatus status);\n}\n\n@GetMapping(\"/customers/{id}\")\npublic CustomerSummary get(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n    return customers.findSummary(id, ApplicationStatus.OPEN).orElseThrow(CustomerNotFoundException::new);\n}","note":"The good endpoint issues one narrow query, cannot trigger lazy loading during serialization, and exposes only the four fields it declares."},{"title":"Write side: request DTO instead of entity binding","lang":"java","code":"public record UpdateContactRequest(@NotBlank @Email String email, @NotBlank String phone) {}\npublic record ContactResponse(UUID id, String email, String phone) {}\n\n@PutMapping(\"/customers/{id}/contact\")\npublic ContactResponse updateContact(@PathVariable UUID id,\n                                     @Valid @RequestBody UpdateContactRequest req) {\n    return customerService.updateContact(id, req.email(), req.phone());\n}\n\n// in CustomerService\n@Transactional\npublic ContactResponse updateContact(UUID id, String email, String phone) {\n    Customer c = customers.findById(id).orElseThrow(CustomerNotFoundException::new);\n    c.changeContact(email, phone);          // entity enforces invariants; creditLimit is unreachable\n    return new ContactResponse(c.getId(), c.getEmail(), c.getPhone());   // mapped inside the tx\n}","note":"The request type physically cannot carry fields the client must not change, which closes the mass-assignment hole by construction."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Where should mapping occur?","a":"At the application service boundary: the service runs the use case inside the transaction and returns DTOs or read models, so everything the response needs is loaded while the persistence context is open and entities never leave the service layer. For reads, the best mapping is none at all: query straight into the DTO. Controllers may map application DTOs to HTTP-specific representations when they differ, but should never receive entities, and the domain model should not depend on API types. Use record factory methods or MapStruct, and cover mappings with tests."}],"extra":["**Closed vs open projections:** Spring Data closed interface projections select only the declared columns, but open projections using `@Value` SpEL load the full entity and lose the benefit.","**Index-only scans:** a narrow DTO query can be served entirely from a covering index in PostgreSQL (`CREATE INDEX ... INCLUDE (...)`), which an entity load selecting every column never can.","**Error contracts too:** return `ProblemDetail` (RFC 9457) bodies instead of exception or entity dumps, so error payloads are as stable as success payloads.","**Contract tests:** with explicit DTOs, JSON snapshot or consumer-driven contract tests (Spring Cloud Contract, Pact) catch accidental API changes that an entity refactor would otherwise ship silently.","**Security review:** DTOs turn field exposure into a visible code change: a new sensitive column added to an entity never reaches clients unless someone adds it to a DTO."],"redFlags":["Returns managed entities and exposes internal graphs"]},{"id":67,"category":"JPA & Hibernate","question":"How do batching and identifier generation affect bulk inserts?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Enable JDBC batching with ordered inserts so many rows share one round trip, use pooled SEQUENCE or application-generated ids because IDENTITY forces immediate inserts, and flush and clear periodically to bound memory.","answer":["Without batching, persisting 100,000 rows means 100,000 network round trips. Setting `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` (for example 50) makes Hibernate group identical INSERT or UPDATE statements into JDBC batches sent in one round trip each. Add `hibernate.order_inserts=true` and `hibernate.order_updates=true` so parent and child statements are grouped per table instead of alternating and breaking batches, and on PostgreSQL set `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` on the JDBC URL so the driver rewrites a batch into multi-row INSERTs.","The identifier strategy decides whether batching happens at all. With `IDENTITY`, the id exists only after the row is inserted, but Hibernate needs it at `persist()` time, so it executes each INSERT immediately and silently disables insert batching for that entity. `SEQUENCE` with a pooled optimizer (`allocationSize` 50, matching the sequence's `INCREMENT BY`) fetches a block of ids per database call, so inserts can be deferred and batched. Application-generated UUIDs also work; prefer time-ordered UUIDv7 over random v4 for B-tree locality.","Memory is the other limit. Every persisted entity stays managed, with its snapshot, until the transaction ends, so a naive loop over a million rows grows the heap and makes every flush slower. Call `flush()` and `clear()` every batch-size entities and split huge imports into chunked transactions. Spring Data `saveAll()` does neither, and with assigned ids it calls `merge`, adding a SELECT per row. For very large loads, `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate` or PostgreSQL `COPY` beat the ORM."],"keyPoints":["JDBC batching reduces round trips","IDENTITY may limit batching","flush/clear periodically to control memory"],"examples":[{"title":"Sequence ids and flush/clear in chunks","lang":"java","code":"@Entity\npublic class LedgerEntry {\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = \"ledger_entry_seq\")\n    @SequenceGenerator(name = \"ledger_entry_seq\", sequenceName = \"ledger_entry_seq\", allocationSize = 50)\n    private Long id;            // BAD alternative: GenerationType.IDENTITY disables insert batching\n    private UUID accountId;\n    private BigDecimal amount;\n    // constructors and factory omitted\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class LedgerImportService {\n    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 50;       // same as hibernate.jdbc.batch_size\n    private final EntityManager em;\n\n    public LedgerImportService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }\n\n    @Transactional                                  // caller passes chunks of a few thousand rows\n    public void importChunk(List<LedgerRow> rows) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < rows.size(); i++) {\n            em.persist(LedgerEntry.from(rows.get(i)));\n            if ((i + 1) % BATCH_SIZE == 0) {\n                em.flush();                         // send one JDBC batch\n                em.clear();                         // drop managed entities and snapshots\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The database sequence must be created with `INCREMENT BY 50` to match `allocationSize`, otherwise ids collide or Hibernate reports a mismatch."},{"title":"Batching configuration for PostgreSQL","lang":"properties","code":"spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/ledger?reWriteBatchedInserts=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_updates=true\n# Verify: session metrics report how many JDBC batches were executed\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true","note":"Enable statistics in a test or staging run to confirm batches are actually executed, then turn it off again in production."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can IDENTITY disable batching?","a":"With IDENTITY the database assigns the primary key during the INSERT itself. Hibernate keys every managed entity by its id in the persistence context, so on `persist()` it must execute the INSERT immediately and read back the generated key instead of queuing the statement until flush, and statements that run one by one cannot be grouped into a batch. Hibernate therefore silently disables insert batching for IDENTITY entities, while their updates and deletes can still batch. Switch to a pooled SEQUENCE or application-generated UUIDs, or bypass the ORM with `JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate` or `COPY` for bulk loads."}],"extra":["**Hibernate 6 sequence defaults:** Hibernate 6 uses one sequence per entity named after it (suffix `_seq`) instead of a shared `hibernate_sequence`, and the JPA default `allocationSize` is 50, so schema migrations must create sequences with a matching increment.","**Logs can mislead:** `org.hibernate.SQL` prints every statement even when they are sent as one batch, so verify batching with Hibernate statistics or a datasource-proxy listener rather than by counting log lines.","**Versioned entities:** batched UPDATEs of `@Version` entities are supported (`hibernate.jdbc.batch_versioned_data` is enabled by default), so optimistic locking does not rule out batching.","**Chunked transactions:** commit every few thousand rows (Spring Batch chunk-oriented steps do exactly this) so a late failure does not roll back a million-row import and a long transaction does not hold back vacuum.","**COPY for large loads:** PostgreSQL `COPY ... FROM STDIN` through PgJDBC's `CopyManager` is usually far faster than batched INSERTs for millions of rows, at the cost of bypassing entity logic entirely."],"redFlags":["Loops save() and assumes one batch","retains huge persistence contexts"]},{"id":68,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How does an index improve reads, and what are its costs?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"An index is a separate sorted structure (a B-tree by default in PostgreSQL) that lets the planner reach matching rows without scanning the whole table, paid for with storage, slower writes and ongoing maintenance.","answer":["A B-tree index keeps key values sorted with pointers (TIDs) to heap tuples, so a lookup costs a few page reads plus one heap fetch per match instead of reading every page. Because it is ordered, the same index can also serve `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` without a sort, support merge joins, enforce uniqueness and, when it holds every referenced column, answer the query with an index-only scan.","The costs are real. Each index takes disk and buffer cache, and every `INSERT`, `DELETE` and non-HOT `UPDATE` must also modify every index on the table: write amplification, extra WAL and extra replication traffic. Indexes bloat, need vacuum and sometimes `REINDEX CONCURRENTLY`, and adding one to a live table requires `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` to avoid blocking writes.","Whether an index helps depends on selectivity and query shape. Reading 30% of a table through random heap access is slower than a sequential scan, so a plain index on a low-cardinality `status` column is usually ignored; a partial index on the rare value is what helps. The predicate must also match the index: a function on the column, a leading wildcard `LIKE`, an implicit cast or a filter on a non-leading composite column defeats it.","In production I add indexes from evidence (`pg_stat_statements`, `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`), prefer one composite index that serves several queries over many single-column ones, and drop indexes that `pg_stat_user_indexes` shows are never scanned."],"keyPoints":["Reduces scanned rows","costs storage, write amplification and maintenance","selectivity and query shape matter"],"examples":[{"title":"Index from a real query, not one per column","lang":"sql","code":"-- Hot query: a customer's most recent orders\nSELECT id, status, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n\n-- BAD: single-column indexes on everything\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders (status);        -- 4 distinct values, planner ignores it\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_total  ON orders (total_amount);  -- no query filters on it, pure write cost\n\n-- GOOD: one composite index matching filter and sort, built without blocking writes\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer_created\n    ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC);\n\n-- GOOD: partial index for a skewed predicate (only a few rows are PENDING)\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_pending_created\n    ON orders (created_at)\n    WHERE status = 'PENDING';\n\n-- Case-insensitive lookups need an expression index\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_customers_email_lower\n    ON customers (lower(email));","note":"The composite index serves both the filter and the `ORDER BY ... LIMIT`, so the query stops after 20 index entries. The partial index stays tiny because it only contains the rows the job actually looks for."},{"title":"Find indexes that cost writes but never serve reads","lang":"sql","code":"SELECT s.relname                                   AS table_name,\n       s.indexrelname                              AS index_name,\n       s.idx_scan,\n       pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid)) AS index_size\nFROM pg_stat_user_indexes s\nJOIN pg_index i ON i.indexrelid = s.indexrelid\nWHERE s.idx_scan = 0\n  AND NOT i.indisunique        -- unique indexes enforce constraints even if never scanned\n  AND NOT i.indisprimary\nORDER BY pg_relation_size(s.indexrelid) DESC;","note":"Statistics are per server and reset on `pg_stat_reset()`, so check replicas and the time window before dropping anything. Drop with `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why may an index not be used?","a":"The planner chose a cheaper alternative: the predicate is not selective enough, the table is small, or statistics are stale after a bulk load (run `ANALYZE`). Or the query shape prevents use: a function or expression on the column (`lower(email)` without an expression index), an implicit type cast, a leading wildcard `LIKE '%abc'`, `OR` across different columns, or a filter that skips the leading column of a composite index. Prepared statements can also switch to a generic plan that ignores the actual parameter value, and a `LIKE 'abc%'` prefix search needs `text_pattern_ops` unless the column uses the C collation."},{"q":"What is a covering index?","a":"A covering index contains every column a query reads, so PostgreSQL can answer it with an index-only scan without touching the table. Since PostgreSQL 11 you add non-key payload columns with `INCLUDE`, for example `CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id, created_at) INCLUDE (status, total_amount)`, which keeps the search key small while avoiding heap fetches. The trade-off is a larger index and more write cost, and the benefit depends on the visibility map being current, so it works best on tables that vacuum keeps up with."}],"extra":["**HOT updates:** PostgreSQL skips index maintenance when an `UPDATE` changes no indexed column and the new tuple fits on the same page; indexing a frequently changed column such as `updated_at` disables that and multiplies write cost. A `fillfactor` below 100 leaves room for HOT updates.","**Index-only scans need the visibility map:** even with a covering index, PostgreSQL visits the heap for pages not marked all-visible, which shows up as high `Heap Fetches` in `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on poorly vacuumed tables.","**Other index types:** GIN for `jsonb`, arrays and full-text search; BRIN for huge append-only tables whose physical order follows a column such as `created_at` (tiny but coarse); GiST for ranges, geometry and exclusion constraints.","**Concurrent builds can fail halfway:** `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` cannot run inside a transaction block, and if it fails it leaves an `INVALID` index that still costs writes; find it via `pg_index.indisvalid` and drop it before retrying.","**Foreign keys are not indexed automatically:** PostgreSQL indexes the referenced primary key but not the referencing column, so deleting a parent row or joining on `order_lines.order_id` scans the child table unless you add that index yourself."],"redFlags":["Adds indexes to every column","says indexes always help"]},{"id":69,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How does column order affect a composite index?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A B-tree on (a, b, c) is sorted by a, then b, then c, so it only seeks on a leftmost prefix; put equality columns first, then the range or sort column, based on real queries.","answer":["A composite B-tree is sorted lexicographically, like a phone book ordered by last name and then first name. It can seek efficiently for `a = ?`, `a = ? AND b = ?`, and `a = ? AND b = ? AND c > ?`, because each prefix narrows the scan to one contiguous range. `WHERE b = ?` alone cannot seek, because matching `b` values are scattered across every `a` group.","Equality columns usually go before range and sort columns. Once the scan hits a range condition (`created_at >= ?`), the columns after it no longer narrow the range; they are only checked row by row inside it. So for `merchant_id = ? AND status = ? AND created_at >= ? ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50`, the right index is `(merchant_id, status, created_at DESC)`: the two equalities pin a single range, entries come out already sorted, and the scan stops after 50 rows.","\"Most selective column first\" is a myth when all predicates are equalities; the seek is equally tight in any order. What matters more is reuse: pick the order whose prefixes serve the most real queries, since `(merchant_id, status, created_at)` also covers `merchant_id = ?` and makes a separate index on `merchant_id` redundant.","So I design from the workload: take the top queries from `pg_stat_statements`, group them by access pattern, and verify with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`. A wrong order shows up as an extra `Sort` node or far more buffers read than rows returned."],"keyPoints":["Leftmost-prefix principle","equality columns usually precede range/sort columns","design from real queries"],"examples":[{"title":"Equality first, then range and sort","lang":"sql","code":"-- Query: recent pending orders of one merchant, newest first\nSELECT id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND status = 'PENDING'\n  AND created_at >= $2\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- BAD: range column first; merchant_id and status are checked across the whole date range\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_created_merchant_status\n    ON orders (created_at, merchant_id, status);\n\n-- GOOD: equalities pin one contiguous range that is already in ORDER BY order\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_merchant_status_created\n    ON orders (merchant_id, status, created_at DESC);\n\n-- The GOOD index also serves, through its leftmost prefix:\n--   WHERE merchant_id = $1\n--   WHERE merchant_id = $1 AND status = $2\n-- It cannot seek for:\n--   WHERE status = 'PENDING'      (leading column missing)\n--   WHERE created_at >= $2        (leading columns missing)","note":"With the BAD index, `EXPLAIN` may still list all three predicates under `Index Cond`, yet the scan walks every entry since `$2` for all merchants. Compare `Buffers` between the two plans, not just the index name."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can WHERE b = ? use index (a, b, c) effectively?","a":"Usually not. `b` is sorted only within each value of `a`, so the planner can at best do a full index scan (acceptable only if the index is much smaller than the table, or covering for an index-only scan) or it chooses a sequential scan. PostgreSQL 18 skip scan can make it reasonably efficient when `a` has only a handful of distinct values, by seeking once per `a` value. If `WHERE b = ?` is a real, frequent query, add an index that leads with `b`, or add the known `a` value to the query."}],"extra":["**Skip scan:** PostgreSQL 18 added B-tree skip scan, which can use `(a, b)` for `WHERE b = ?` when `a` has few distinct values by probing each `a` group; it is a fallback, not a reason to ignore column order.","**Sort direction matters:** `ORDER BY a ASC, b DESC` needs an index declared `(a ASC, b DESC)` or its exact reverse; a plain `(a, b)` forces an explicit sort for mixed directions.","**Index Cond is not proof of efficiency:** conditions on later columns can appear as `Index Cond` even when the scan range is set only by the leading range column, so judge by rows and buffers touched.","**Redundant indexes:** `(a)` is redundant next to `(a, b)` unless it enforces uniqueness or is much smaller for a very hot query; each redundant index is pure write amplification.","**Same rule elsewhere:** MongoDB documents it as ESR (Equality, Sort, Range) for compound indexes, and MySQL InnoDB follows the same leftmost-prefix behavior, so the reasoning transfers across engines."],"redFlags":["Believes order never matters"]},{"id":70,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How do you read an EXPLAIN execution plan?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`, read the tree from the inside out, and compare estimated with actual rows at every node; large mismatches reveal the misestimates that make the planner choose bad scans, joins and sorts.","answer":["`EXPLAIN` shows the planner's chosen tree with estimates: `cost=startup..total`, `rows` and `width`. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the statement and adds `actual time`, actual `rows` and `loops`, and `BUFFERS` adds shared hits versus reads, which separates cache work from disk I/O. Child nodes feed their parents, so read it inside out, and remember that actual time and rows are per loop: multiply by `loops`.","At each node I check the scan type (Seq, Index, Index Only with `Heap Fetches`, Bitmap with `Recheck`), the join algorithm (Nested Loop for small outer inputs with an indexed inner side, Hash Join for large unsorted sets, Merge Join for sorted inputs), sorts that spill (`Sort Method: external merge`), high `Rows Removed by Filter`, and where the time and buffers really go.","The most important signal is estimated versus actual rows. The planner costs every alternative from cardinality estimates, so if it expects 12 rows and gets 480,000 it picks a nested loop that runs an index lookup 480,000 times. Typical causes are stale statistics after a bulk load, correlated columns the planner treats as independent, skew, expressions without statistics, and generic plans for prepared statements. Fixes are `ANALYZE`, a higher per-column statistics target, `CREATE STATISTICS` for correlated columns, expression indexes, or rewriting the query.","Plans depend on data volume and cache state, so capture real ones from production with `auto_explain` instead of trusting a laptop. Seeing an index in the plan proves nothing on its own."],"keyPoints":["Inspect actual vs estimated rows, scans, joins, sorts, loops and timing","cardinality errors can cause bad plans"],"examples":[{"title":"A cardinality misestimate causing a slow nested loop","lang":"text","code":"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT o.id, c.email\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'PENDING' AND o.region = 'EU';\n\nNested Loop  (cost=0.87..152.40 rows=12 width=48) (actual time=0.041..4210.337 rows=480112 loops=1)\n  Buffers: shared hit=1920455 read=38211\n  ->  Index Scan using idx_orders_status on orders o  (cost=0.43..48.10 rows=12 width=16) (actual time=0.020..610.114 rows=480112 loops=1)\n        Index Cond: (status = 'PENDING'::text)\n        Filter: (region = 'EU'::text)\n        Rows Removed by Filter: 91230\n  ->  Index Scan using customers_pkey on customers c  (cost=0.43..8.45 rows=1 width=40) (actual time=0.006..0.006 rows=1 loops=480112)\n        Index Cond: (id = o.customer_id)\nPlanning Time: 0.412 ms\nExecution Time: 4298.905 ms","note":"Estimated 12 rows, actual 480,112: the inner index scan runs 480,112 times (`loops`). With correct estimates the planner would hash join the two tables instead."},{"title":"Fixing the estimate and explaining writes safely","lang":"sql","code":"-- Refresh statistics after a bulk load\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- Teach the planner that status and region are correlated (PostgreSQL 10+, mcv since 12)\nCREATE STATISTICS orders_status_region (dependencies, mcv)\n    ON status, region FROM orders;\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- Finer histogram for a skewed column\nALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN status SET STATISTICS 1000;\nANALYZE orders;\n\n-- EXPLAIN ANALYZE really executes the statement: wrap writes in a rolled back transaction\nBEGIN;\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nUPDATE orders SET status = 'EXPIRED'\nWHERE status = 'PENDING' AND created_at < '2026-01-01';\nROLLBACK;","note":"Extended statistics fix estimates for predicates on correlated columns without changing the query. The `BEGIN ... ROLLBACK` wrapper is essential because `ANALYZE` runs the `UPDATE` for real."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is a sequential scan correct?","a":"A sequential scan is the right choice when the query needs a large fraction of the table (often beyond a few percent of rows, depending on physical correlation), when the table is small enough to fit in a few pages, or for reporting and aggregate queries over most rows. Sequential I/O is cheap and can use parallel workers, while random heap fetches for a third of the table are not. It is only a problem when the plan expected many rows but few actually match, or when a large table is scanned on a hot OLTP path. On SSDs, lowering `random_page_cost` toward 1.1 lets the planner value index scans more realistically."}],"extra":["**auto_explain in production:** set `auto_explain.log_min_duration` to log plans of slow statements, and use `auto_explain.log_analyze` with `auto_explain.sample_rate` because per-node timing adds overhead.","**Generic plans:** pgJDBC switches to a server-side prepared statement after `prepareThreshold` executions (default 5), and PostgreSQL may then pick a generic plan that ignores the actual parameter value; `plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan` helps queries with heavily skewed parameters.","**Spilling sorts and hashes:** `Sort Method: external merge  Disk: ...` or a hash with multiple batches means `work_mem` was too small; raise it per session or query, since it applies per operation per backend and a global increase can exhaust memory.","**Parallel and JIT noise:** compare `Workers Planned` with `Workers Launched`, and check `JIT` timing at the bottom; on short OLTP queries JIT compilation can cost more than the query, which is why many teams run with `jit = off`.","**Startup cost matters for LIMIT:** the first number in `cost=startup..total` is the cost before the first row; plans for `LIMIT` queries favor low startup cost, which is why an ordered index scan can beat a cheaper-looking sort."],"redFlags":["Only checks whether an index appears"]},{"id":71,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Explain dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantoms and lost updates.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Dirty reads see uncommitted data, non-repeatable reads see a row change between two reads, phantoms see the matching set change, and lost updates silently overwrite a concurrent write; isolation levels and locking prevent different subsets.","answer":["Concrete scenarios: a **dirty read** sees another transaction's uncommitted debit that later rolls back. A **non-repeatable read** reads a balance of 100, another transaction commits 50, and a second read in the same transaction returns 50. A **phantom** counts a customer's open orders as 3 for a credit check while a concurrent insert makes it 4. A **lost update** has two transactions both read 100, each subtract its own amount in application code and write back, and the second commit erases the first.","Mapping to PostgreSQL: MVCC never allows dirty reads, so Read Uncommitted behaves as Read Committed. Read Committed, the default, takes a fresh snapshot per statement, so non-repeatable reads, phantoms and lost updates are all possible. Repeatable Read is snapshot isolation: no non-repeatable reads or phantoms, and a concurrent update of the same row fails with SQLSTATE `40001`, but write skew across different rows remains. Serializable (SSI) also detects write skew. Other engines differ; MySQL InnoDB Repeatable Read, for example, does not detect lost updates on plain reads.","Lost updates are the one teams hit most, and they need more than a level name: an atomic conditional `UPDATE`, a pessimistic `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, optimistic versioning with a `version` column, or a higher isolation level with a retry loop. I pick atomic SQL when the change fits one statement, optimistic locking for low contention or long user think time, and row locks for hot rows with short transactions."],"keyPoints":["Concurrency anomalies differ by isolation and locking","lost updates often need versioning, atomic SQL or locks"],"examples":[{"title":"Lost update on a balance, and two fixes","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: read-modify-write at Read Committed; two concurrent debits both read 100\n@Transactional\npublic void debit(long accountId, BigDecimal amount) {\n    Account acc = accountRepository.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();\n    acc.setBalance(acc.getBalance().subtract(amount)); // last commit wins, one debit vanishes\n}\n\n// GOOD (1): atomic conditional update; the row lock serializes writers\npublic interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, Long> {\n    @Modifying\n    @Query(\"\"\"\n        update Account a set a.balance = a.balance + :delta\n        where a.id = :id and a.balance + :delta >= 0\n        \"\"\")\n    int applyDelta(@Param(\"id\") long id, @Param(\"delta\") BigDecimal delta); // 0 rows: insufficient funds\n}\n\n// GOOD (2): optimistic locking; Hibernate adds \"where version = ?\" to the UPDATE\n@Entity\npublic class Account {\n    @Id private Long id;\n    private BigDecimal balance;\n    @Version private long version; // conflict throws OptimisticLockException, retry or return 409\n}","note":"Option 1 needs no read at all and is the simplest correct fix. Option 2 keeps the read-modify-write style but turns a silent overwrite into a detectable conflict."},{"title":"Anomalies by isolation level in PostgreSQL","lang":"text","code":"Anomaly                  Read Committed   Repeatable Read (PG)       Serializable (PG)\nDirty read               prevented        prevented                  prevented\nNon-repeatable read      possible         prevented                  prevented\nPhantom read             possible         prevented (snapshot)       prevented\nLost update (R-M-W)      possible         detected, SQLSTATE 40001   detected, SQLSTATE 40001\nWrite skew               possible         possible                   detected, SQLSTATE 40001","note":"The SQL standard allows phantoms at Repeatable Read, but PostgreSQL's snapshot prevents them. Every 40001 means the application must retry the whole transaction."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which anomaly affects concurrent balance updates?","a":"The lost update: two transactions read the same balance, compute the new value in application code and write it back, so the later commit overwrites the earlier debit. At PostgreSQL's default Read Committed nothing prevents it. If the invariant spans rows (for example the sum of sub-account balances must stay non-negative) you also face write skew. Fix it with an atomic conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance + :delta >= 0`, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, a `@Version` column, or Serializable with retries; ledgers often go further and append immutable entries, deriving or atomically updating the balance."}],"extra":["**Write skew:** two on-call doctors each check that someone else is on call and both go off call; they update different rows, so no row conflict occurs. Only Serializable, or explicitly locking the checked rows with `FOR UPDATE`, prevents it.","**Retries are mandatory above Read Committed:** at Repeatable Read or Serializable, catch SQLSTATE `40001` (and `40P01` for deadlocks) on the root `SQLException` and rerun the entire transaction with bounded attempts; Spring surfaces these as `ConcurrencyFailureException` subtypes.","**Serializable read-only optimization:** declaring a transaction `SERIALIZABLE READ ONLY DEFERRABLE` lets PostgreSQL wait for a safe snapshot, after which the read can never fail with a serialization error, which suits long consistent reports.","**JPA versioning scope:** `@Version` only protects writes that go through the entity; bulk JPQL or native `UPDATE` statements bypass it unless you increment the version column yourself."],"redFlags":["Memorizes isolation names but cannot map scenarios"]},{"id":72,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How do database deadlocks happen and how should applications respond?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A deadlock is a lock-wait cycle between transactions; the database aborts one victim, so prevent cycles with consistent lock order and short transactions, and retry the victim idempotently with a bound.","answer":["Transaction 1 locks account A and then needs B, while transaction 2 locks B and then needs A; neither can proceed. PostgreSQL runs deadlock detection after a lock wait exceeds `deadlock_timeout` (1 second by default), aborts one transaction with SQLSTATE `40P01`, and lets the other continue. Typical sources are transfers in opposite directions, batch updates touching the same rows in different orders, and `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` without a deterministic order.","Prevention matters more than handling. Acquire locks in one global order (sort the ids, lock the lower id first, or lock all rows up front with `ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE`), keep transactions short with no remote calls inside, touch as few rows as possible, and set `lock_timeout` so waits are bounded.","A deadlock is not corruption: the victim is rolled back completely and the database stays consistent. The application should retry the whole transaction, not the failed statement, with a small attempt limit, exponential backoff and jitter, and only if the unit of work is idempotent. The retry must sit outside the `@Transactional` boundary so each attempt gets a fresh transaction and re-reads state.","Operationally, track `pg_stat_database.deadlocks` and enable `log_lock_waits`. A steady deadlock rate is a design bug in lock ordering, not something to hide behind retries."],"keyPoints":["Conflicting lock order creates a cycle","keep transactions short, lock consistently and retry a selected victim safely"],"examples":[{"title":"The cycle, and the ordered-locking fix","lang":"sql","code":"-- Session 1: transfer from account 1 to account 2\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;   -- holds row 1\n-- Session 2: transfer from account 2 to account 1\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50 WHERE id = 2;    -- holds row 2\n-- Session 1\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;   -- waits for session 2\n-- Session 2\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 50 WHERE id = 1;    -- cycle\n-- ERROR: deadlock detected (SQLSTATE 40P01); session 2 is rolled back\n\n-- GOOD: lock every involved row in a deterministic order before changing anything\nBEGIN;\nSELECT id, balance\nFROM accounts\nWHERE id IN (1, 2)\nORDER BY id\nFOR UPDATE;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;","note":"PostgreSQL applies `ORDER BY` before the locking clause, so rows are locked in id order and two opposite transfers queue instead of forming a cycle."},{"title":"Bounded retry outside the transaction, idempotent unit of work","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class TransferFacade {\n    private final TransferService transferService;\n\n    public TransferFacade(TransferService transferService) {\n        this.transferService = transferService;\n    }\n\n    // Each attempt calls a @Transactional method, so it runs in a brand new transaction.\n    // CannotAcquireLockException (deadlock loser, lock timeout) extends PessimisticLockingFailureException.\n    @Retryable(retryFor = PessimisticLockingFailureException.class,\n               maxAttempts = 3,\n               backoff = @Backoff(delay = 50, multiplier = 2, random = true))\n    public TransferResult transfer(TransferCommand cmd) {\n        return transferService.execute(cmd);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass TransferService {\n    // execute() is @Transactional: it inserts into transfers (transfer_id is the primary key),\n    // then locks both accounts ordered by id and applies the debit and credit.\n    // A replayed transfer_id returns the stored result instead of moving money twice.\n}","note":"Spring Retry (`@EnableRetry`) retries only transient lock failures, three times with jittered exponential backoff. Putting `@Retryable` and `@Transactional` on different beans guarantees a fresh transaction per attempt."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why must the retry be idempotent?","a":"The database rolls back only its own work, but an attempt may already have caused effects outside it: a Kafka event published, an email sent, a payment provider called. The caller may also retry after a timeout when the first attempt actually committed, so the same request arrives twice. An idempotency key backed by a unique constraint, plus an outbox for side effects, guarantees that rerunning the unit of work produces the same final state instead of a double debit. Each retry must also re-read current state rather than reuse entities loaded in the failed attempt."}],"extra":["**deadlock_timeout is not a transaction timeout:** it is how long a lock wait lasts before PostgreSQL checks for a cycle, so every deadlock costs at least that much latency; with `log_lock_waits = on` any wait longer than it is logged with the blocking process.","**Bound waits explicitly:** `lock_timeout` fails a blocked statement with SQLSTATE `55P03` and `statement_timeout` caps total runtime, so a blocked request frees its pooled connection instead of pinning it.","**Batch updates:** `UPDATE ... WHERE id = ANY(?)` locks rows in whatever order the plan visits them; sort ids in the application and lock through an ordered `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, or process fixed ordered chunks.","**Server log has the story:** PostgreSQL logs both process ids, the lock types and the statements involved in the deadlock, which is usually enough to identify the conflicting code paths.","**Hot rows are a different problem:** a single contended counter row causes lock queueing rather than deadlocks; the fix there is redesign (append-only ledger rows, sharded counters), not ordering."],"redFlags":["Retries forever or assumes deadlock means corruption"]},{"id":73,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Offset pagination vs keyset pagination.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"OFFSET reads and discards every skipped row and shifts when data changes; keyset pagination seeks from the last seen unique sort key through an index, so every page costs the same and stays stable.","answer":["`ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50 OFFSET 200000` makes the database produce 200,050 rows and throw 200,000 away, so page cost grows linearly with depth and deep pages become the slowest queries in the system. It is also inconsistent: an insert or delete between two requests shifts every later page, so users see duplicates or miss rows. Its advantages are simplicity and jumping to an arbitrary page number.","Keyset (seek, cursor) pagination remembers the sort key of the last row returned and asks for rows after it: `WHERE (created_at, id) < ($2, $3) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 50`. With an index on `(merchant_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)` each page is an index seek plus 50 entries, whatever the depth, and concurrent inserts do not shift pages you have not read yet.","It requires a total, stable order: a unique tiebreaker (the primary key) in both `ORDER BY` and the cursor, and sort columns that do not change while a client is paging. The cursor should be opaque to clients, and each supported sort order needs its own index. You lose random page access and cheap page counts.","I use offset only for small admin lists with a capped maximum offset, and keyset for public APIs, infinite scroll, exports and batch jobs. Spring Data 3.1+ supports keyset scrolling natively with `ScrollPosition` and `Window`."],"keyPoints":["Offset is simple but slow/inconsistent at depth","keyset uses stable ordering and a cursor for efficient sequential navigation"],"examples":[{"title":"Deep OFFSET versus keyset seek","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: scans and discards 200000 rows; pages shift when orders are inserted\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50 OFFSET 200000;\n\n-- Index for the keyset query; id makes the order total and unique\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_merchant_created_id\n    ON orders (merchant_id, created_at DESC, id DESC);\n\n-- GOOD: first page\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- GOOD: next page; $2 and $3 are created_at and id of the last row already returned\nSELECT id, customer_id, total_amount, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND (created_at, id) < ($2, $3)\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 50;","note":"The row-value comparison `(created_at, id) < ($2, $3)` becomes a single index range condition because both columns sort in the same direction, so page 4,000 costs the same as page 1."},{"title":"Keyset scrolling with Spring Data 3.1+","lang":"java","code":"public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {\n    Window<Order> findFirst50ByMerchantIdOrderByCreatedAtDescIdDesc(long merchantId, ScrollPosition position);\n}\n\n@Service\npublic class OrderExportService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n\n    public OrderExportService(OrderRepository orders) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n    }\n\n    // Walks every order of a merchant page by page without OFFSET\n    public void export(long merchantId, Consumer<Order> sink) {\n        WindowIterator<Order> it = WindowIterator\n                .of(position -> orders.findFirst50ByMerchantIdOrderByCreatedAtDescIdDesc(merchantId, position))\n                .startingAt(ScrollPosition.keyset());\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            sink.accept(it.next());\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Spring Data derives the `(created_at, id)` seek predicate from the sort. For a REST API, serialize the keys of `window.positionAt(last)` into an opaque cursor and rebuild it with `ScrollPosition.forward(keys)`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you handle ties in created_at?","a":"Add a unique tiebreaker, normally the primary key, to both the `ORDER BY` and the cursor, compare the pair as a tuple `(created_at, id) < ($2, $3)`, and index `(created_at DESC, id DESC)`. Without it, rows sharing a timestamp at a page boundary are skipped (with `<`) or repeated (with `<=`). Ties are more common than expected, because rows inserted in one transaction share the same `now()`. Also keep the cursor at full precision: PostgreSQL `timestamptz` stores microseconds, so do not truncate the value to milliseconds when you put it in the cursor."}],"extra":["**Mixed sort directions:** a row-value comparison only maps to one index range when all columns sort the same way; for `created_at DESC, id ASC` you need `created_at < $2 OR (created_at = $2 AND id > $3)`, which is less index friendly, so keep directions aligned.","**Counting is expensive too:** `COUNT(*)` for \"page X of Y\" is a full scan in PostgreSQL because MVCC keeps no stored row count; fetch `LIMIT n + 1` to know if there is a next page, or show an estimate from `pg_class.reltuples`.","**Cursor hygiene:** encode the sort values as opaque base64, bind the cursor to the filter and sort it was issued for, and sign it (HMAC) if clients must not craft or tamper with cursors.","**Batch jobs and backfills:** walking a big table with `WHERE id > :last ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000` keeps every batch fast and lets the job resume after a crash, while offset batching gets slower with each batch.","**Backward navigation:** a \"previous page\" is the same query with the comparison and sort flipped, then the result reversed in the application."],"redFlags":["Uses large OFFSET","cursor is not unique or stable"]},{"id":74,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Normalization vs denormalization: how do you decide?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Normalize by default so each fact lives in one place and constraints protect integrity; denormalize only for a measured read problem, with one declared source of truth and an explicit synchronization and reconciliation plan.","answer":["Normalization stores each fact once: an update touches one row, and foreign keys, unique and check constraints keep the data valid, so update anomalies cannot happen. Systems of record such as orders, payments and accounts start normalized, and with the right indexes PostgreSQL joins are cheap at OLTP scale; \"joins are slow\" usually means a missing index.","Denormalization is an optimization for a proven read problem: a hot endpoint whose `EXPLAIN` shows expensive joins or aggregates, such as an account balance summed from millions of ledger entries, or a list page needing counts and names from four tables. The forms range from a stored aggregate column, to a copied field, a materialized view, a CQRS read model, or a search index.","It is never free. Every copy needs a declared source of truth and a synchronization plan: update it in the same transaction (strongly consistent, but more write work and possible hot-row contention), refresh it periodically (bounded staleness), or propagate it asynchronously through an outbox or CDC (eventual consistency, rebuildable). Add a reconciliation job that detects drift, because copies do drift.","Distinguish a snapshot from a copy: an order's unit price and shipping address are facts about the order at purchase time, not redundancy, and must not follow later changes. I decide field by field based on read to write ratio, staleness tolerance and who owns the truth."],"keyPoints":["Normalize for integrity","denormalize intentionally for measured reads with a source of truth and synchronization plan"],"examples":[{"title":"Stored balance with a sync plan and drift detection","lang":"sql","code":"-- Source of truth: append-only ledger\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entries (\n    id          bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    account_id  bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts (id),\n    amount      numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- Denormalized read field on the account row\nALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN balance numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;\n\n-- Sync plan: both writes in one transaction, so they cannot diverge\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO ledger_entries (account_id, amount) VALUES (42, -250.00);\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 250.00 WHERE id = 42;\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- Reconciliation: accounts whose stored balance differs from the ledger\nSELECT a.id, a.balance, COALESCE(SUM(l.amount), 0) AS ledger_sum\nFROM accounts a\nLEFT JOIN ledger_entries l ON l.account_id = a.id\nGROUP BY a.id, a.balance\nHAVING a.balance <> COALESCE(SUM(l.amount), 0);","note":"The ledger stays the truth and the balance is a cache of it; the reconciliation query is the safety net that proves they still agree."},{"title":"Materialized view for a reporting read path","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW merchant_daily_sales AS\nSELECT merchant_id,\n       date_trunc('day', created_at) AS sales_day,\n       count(*)                      AS order_count,\n       sum(total_amount)             AS revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'PAID'\nGROUP BY merchant_id, date_trunc('day', created_at);\n\n-- A unique index is required for a concurrent refresh\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON merchant_daily_sales (merchant_id, sales_day);\n\n-- Scheduled every few minutes; readers are not blocked during the refresh\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY merchant_daily_sales;","note":"Staleness is bounded by the refresh interval and the view can always be rebuilt from `orders`, which makes it a low-risk form of denormalization for dashboards."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Would you copy customer name into an order?","a":"It depends on what the name means on the order. If the order must show the name as it was at purchase time (invoice, shipping label, legal record), store it on the order as a snapshot: it is a separate fact and must not change when the customer later renames. If you only want the current name on an order list, keep it normalized and join on `customer_id`, which is cheap with an index, or keep it in a read model updated from a `CustomerRenamed` event. Keep in mind that each copy of personal data also extends the scope of GDPR erasure requests."}],"extra":["**Generated columns:** for values derived from the same row, `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED` columns let PostgreSQL maintain the copy, with no application sync code to get wrong.","**Triggers versus application code:** triggers keep a copy consistent no matter who writes (other services, manual SQL), but they are hidden logic that is harder to test and reason about; application updates are explicit but must share the transaction.","**Hot-row contention:** a denormalized counter such as `merchants.order_count` updated by every insert serializes all writers on one row lock; aggregate periodically or spread the counter over several rows.","**jsonb is not an escape hatch:** a `jsonb` column with a GIN index suits genuinely variable attributes, but moving relational facts into JSON loses constraints and type checks.","**Erasure cost:** every copy of personal data multiplies GDPR erasure and retention work, so personal fields are a strong argument for keeping a single source."],"redFlags":["Treats denormalization as free"]},{"id":75,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"How do you enforce idempotent creation at database level?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Give every create request an idempotency or natural business key, back it with a unique constraint, and insert atomically with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`, so concurrent duplicates collapse into one row instead of racing a check-then-insert.","answer":["Retries are normal: client timeouts, at-least-once message delivery, double clicks. The naive `if (!exists) insert` races, because two requests can both see no row and both insert. Only the database can arbitrate that cheaply: a unique constraint on the key makes a second row impossible no matter how many application instances run.","The key is either a natural business key (`merchant_id, merchant_order_ref`, a provider's payment id, a message id) or a client-supplied `Idempotency-Key`, scoped per client or tenant. Insert atomically with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (...) DO NOTHING RETURNING id`: a returned row means you created it, no row means it already exists, so load it and return the original result. Catching the unique violation (SQLSTATE `23505`, `DataIntegrityViolationException` in Spring) also works, but in PostgreSQL an error aborts the surrounding transaction, so you need a savepoint or a fresh transaction.","For APIs, store the key together with a hash of the request and the response you returned, written in the same local transaction as the business row so both commit or neither does. Replays then return the identical response, a concurrent in-flight duplicate can get `409`, and old keys are purged after the documented retry window.","Side effects (events, emails) belong in an outbox table written in that same transaction, so they happen exactly once per created row."],"keyPoints":["Use an idempotency/business key with a unique constraint and atomic insert/upsert","check-then-insert alone races"],"examples":[{"title":"Unique key plus one atomic insert","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE payments (\n    id               uuid PRIMARY KEY,\n    merchant_id      bigint         NOT NULL,\n    idempotency_key  text           NOT NULL,\n    request_hash     text           NOT NULL,\n    amount           numeric(19, 4) NOT NULL,\n    currency         char(3)        NOT NULL,\n    status           text           NOT NULL,\n    created_at       timestamptz    NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    CONSTRAINT uq_payments_idempotency UNIQUE (merchant_id, idempotency_key)\n);\n\n-- BAD: check-then-insert; two concurrent requests both see no row and both insert\nSELECT id FROM payments WHERE merchant_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2;\n\n-- GOOD: one statement; the unique index arbitrates the race\nINSERT INTO payments (id, merchant_id, idempotency_key, request_hash, amount, currency, status)\nVALUES ($3, $1, $2, $4, $5, $6, 'PENDING')\nON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING\nRETURNING id;\n-- No row returned: duplicate. Load the existing row and compare request_hash.","note":"If the first insert has not committed yet, the second one waits on the unique index and then does nothing (or inserts, if the first rolled back), so concurrency is resolved inside the database."},{"title":"Service method: create, replay or reject","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic PaymentResult create(long merchantId, String idempotencyKey, CreatePayment cmd) {\n    String hash = RequestHashes.sha256(cmd);              // hash of the canonical request body\n    UUID newId = UUID.randomUUID();\n    Optional<UUID> inserted = jdbc.sql(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO payments (id, merchant_id, idempotency_key, request_hash, amount, currency, status)\n            VALUES (:id, :merchantId, :key, :hash, :amount, :currency, 'PENDING')\n            ON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING\n            RETURNING id\n            \"\"\")\n        .param(\"id\", newId).param(\"merchantId\", merchantId).param(\"key\", idempotencyKey)\n        .param(\"hash\", hash).param(\"amount\", cmd.amount()).param(\"currency\", cmd.currency())\n        .query((rs, rowNum) -> rs.getObject(\"id\", UUID.class))\n        .optional();\n\n    if (inserted.isPresent()) {\n        outbox.append(new PaymentCreated(newId, merchantId, cmd.amount())); // same transaction\n        return PaymentResult.created(newId);\n    }\n    Payment existing = payments.findByMerchantIdAndIdempotencyKey(merchantId, idempotencyKey);\n    if (!existing.requestHash().equals(hash)) {\n        throw new IdempotencyKeyReusedException(idempotencyKey);           // mapped to 422\n    }\n    return PaymentResult.replayed(existing.id());\n}","note":"`JdbcClient` (Spring 6.1+) keeps the atomic upsert explicit. The three outcomes are created, identical replay, and key reuse with a different payload, which is rejected."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if the same key arrives with a different payload?","a":"Treat it as a client error, never as a replay: store a hash of the canonical request (normalized JSON of the relevant fields) with the key, compare it on every duplicate, and reject a mismatch with `422 Unprocessable Content`, which is what the IETF `Idempotency-Key` header draft recommends. Silently returning the first result would hide a client bug, and applying the new payload would break the idempotency guarantee. Same key with the same payload returns the stored original response, and a duplicate that arrives while the first is still processing gets `409 Conflict`."}],"extra":["**JPA save() with assigned ids:** `repository.save()` on an entity with a pre-set id calls `merge`, which does a `SELECT` then an `INSERT`, a hidden check-then-insert; implement `Persistable#isNew` or use an explicit insert so the constraint handles duplicates cleanly.","**Repeatable Read caveat:** at Repeatable Read, `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` against a row committed after your snapshot raises a serialization failure, and a follow-up `SELECT` would not see that row anyway; the create-or-load pattern is simplest at Read Committed.","**Key retention:** keep keys at least as long as clients may retry (payment APIs commonly honor keys for about 24 hours) and purge them with a cleanup job or daily partitions that you drop.","**Consumers too:** for Kafka or queue consumers, the same pattern is an inbox table with primary key `(consumer_name, message_id)` inserted in the transaction that applies the effect.","**Upsert is not idempotent create:** `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` implements create-or-update; a retried request can overwrite newer state, so use it only when last-write-wins is the intended semantics."],"redFlags":["Relies only on an application existence check"]},{"id":76,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"Why are long-running transactions dangerous?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A long transaction holds row locks, an old MVCC snapshot and a pooled connection for its entire duration, so it blocks writers and DDL, stops vacuum from removing dead rows, raises deadlock risk and starves the pool.","answer":["**Locks:** row locks from `UPDATE` or `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` are held until commit, so other writers queue behind them. Even a plain `SELECT` holds a table-level lock, so a migration's `ALTER TABLE` waits for the long transaction, and every later query on that table then queues behind the waiting DDL: a small migration becomes an outage.","**Snapshots:** PostgreSQL keeps old row versions as long as any snapshot might need them. A transaction open for an hour holds back the xmin horizon, so vacuum cannot remove dead tuples in any table of the database; tables and indexes bloat and queries slow down. Sessions left `idle in transaction` by application bugs are the classic cause.","**Connections:** the transaction pins a pooled connection. A `@Transactional` method that calls a payment API for two seconds holds a HikariCP connection that whole time, so a pool of 10 supports five such requests per second before requests start waiting and timing out. Longer lock hold times also raise deadlock probability, and a failure late in a long transaction throws away a lot of work.","Keep transactions to local database work: no remote calls, no user think time, large jobs chunked with a commit per chunk. Back that with `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`, `statement_timeout`, `lock_timeout` on migrations, and alerts on transaction age."],"keyPoints":["They hold locks, snapshots and connections, increasing contention, bloat and deadlock risk"],"examples":[{"title":"Remote call inside versus outside the transaction","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the connection, row locks and snapshot are held during a slow HTTP call\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrder(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n    Order order = orderRepository.save(Order.pending(cmd));\n    inventoryRepository.reserve(cmd.items());                                // row locks taken\n    PaymentResponse res = paymentClient.charge(cmd.paymentToken(), order.total()); // 2 s remote call\n    order.markPaid(res.reference());\n}\n\n// GOOD: two short local transactions around the remote call (tx is a TransactionTemplate)\npublic void placeOrder(PlaceOrder cmd) {\n    Order order = tx.execute(status -> {\n        Order o = orderRepository.save(Order.pending(cmd));\n        inventoryRepository.reserve(cmd.items());\n        return o;\n    });                                                                      // committed, locks released\n    PaymentResponse res = paymentClient.charge(cmd.paymentToken(), order.total());\n    tx.executeWithoutResult(status ->\n            orderRepository.markPaid(order.getId(), res.reference()));       // conditional on PENDING\n}","note":"The GOOD version holds a connection only for milliseconds. It now needs a state machine for failures between the steps, which the follow-up covers."},{"title":"Find long transactions and set guard rails","lang":"sql","code":"-- Oldest open transactions and what they last ran\nSELECT pid, usename, application_name, state,\n       now() - xact_start AS xact_age,\n       left(query, 80)    AS last_query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY xact_start\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- Guard rails for the application role\nALTER ROLE order_service SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '30s';\nALTER ROLE order_service SET statement_timeout = '5s';\n\n-- Migrations: fail fast instead of queueing every query behind the DDL\nSET lock_timeout = '3s';\nALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN sales_channel text;","note":"`state = 'idle in transaction'` with a large `xact_age` is the smoking gun. A migration tool should retry the `lock_timeout` failure a few times rather than wait indefinitely."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you split the workflow?","a":"Split it into short local transactions around the remote calls. First, persist the intent (order `PENDING`, stock reservation with an expiry, outbox record) and commit. Then call the payment provider outside any transaction, with a timeout and an idempotency key so a retry after an unknown outcome cannot double charge. Finally record the outcome with a conditional update (`WHERE status = 'PENDING'`). Failures between steps are handled by the state machine: retries, compensation such as releasing the reservation, and a reconciler job for orders stuck in `PENDING`. Across services this becomes a saga driven by outbox events instead of one distributed transaction."}],"extra":["**Open Session in View:** Spring Boot enables `spring.jpa.open-in-view` by default and logs a warning about it; it keeps the persistence context open for the whole web request, so lazy loading can run queries outside your transactional boundary. Set it to `false` in services.","**Other horizon holders:** abandoned replication slots, forgotten prepared transactions (`pg_prepared_xacts`) and replicas with `hot_standby_feedback = on` running long queries hold back vacuum exactly like a long transaction; check `age(backend_xmin)` and slot `xmin` values.","**Pool-side detection:** HikariCP's `leakDetectionThreshold` logs a stack trace when a connection is held longer than the threshold, which points straight at the offending code path.","**Chunked batch work:** process large updates in keyset-ordered chunks of a few thousand rows with a commit per chunk, so locks and snapshots stay short and a failure resumes from the last chunk."],"redFlags":["Keeps transactions open across remote calls"]},{"id":77,"category":"SQL & Database","question":"What consistency problems do read replicas introduce?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Replicas apply the primary's changes asynchronously, so reads can be stale: users may not see their own writes and data can appear to go backwards across replicas; route critical reads to the primary or enforce read-your-writes.","answer":["PostgreSQL streaming replication ships WAL to replicas, asynchronously by default. Replay lag is usually milliseconds, but it grows to seconds or minutes under heavy writes, bulk jobs, network trouble, or when long queries on the replica delay replay. Even synchronous replication with `synchronous_commit = on` only waits for the WAL to be flushed on the standby, not replayed; only `remote_apply` makes the commit visible there before the client is acknowledged.","The resulting anomalies are **read-after-write** failures (create an order, get redirected, the GET hits a lagging replica and returns 404), **non-monotonic reads** (successive requests hit replicas with different lag, so a row appears and then disappears), and **stale decisions** (checking a balance or an idempotency key on a replica and then acting on it).","Critical reads go to the primary: anything inside a writing transaction, balances before a debit, uniqueness and idempotency checks. For user-facing flows, add session guarantees: pin a user to the primary for a short window after a write, or record the commit LSN and use a replica only once it has replayed that LSN. Pinning a session to one replica gives monotonic reads.","Operationally, monitor replay lag, remove replicas above a threshold from the read pool, and decide per endpoint whether staleness is acceptable: reports and catalogs yes, money and permissions no."],"keyPoints":["Replication lag causes stale/read-after-write failures","critical reads may need primary routing or session guarantees"],"examples":[{"title":"Routing read-only transactions, with a read-your-writes fence","lang":"java","code":"public class ReadWriteRoutingDataSource extends AbstractRoutingDataSource {\n    @Override\n    protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {\n        boolean readOnly = TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly();\n        boolean recentWrite = WriteFence.userWroteWithin(Duration.ofSeconds(5)); // own helper, per session\n        return (readOnly && !recentWrite) ? \"replica\" : \"primary\";\n    }\n}\n\n@Configuration\nclass DataSourceConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Primary\n    DataSource dataSource(@Qualifier(\"primaryDs\") DataSource primary,\n                          @Qualifier(\"replicaDs\") DataSource replica) {\n        var routing = new ReadWriteRoutingDataSource();\n        routing.setTargetDataSources(Map.<Object, Object>of(\"primary\", primary, \"replica\", replica));\n        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);\n        routing.afterPropertiesSet();\n        // Lazy proxy: the physical connection is chosen after @Transactional(readOnly) is known\n        return new LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy(routing);\n    }\n}","note":"Without `LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy`, the connection is fetched before the read-only flag is set and every query goes to the primary. The write fence keeps a user on the primary right after they changed something."},{"title":"LSN-based read-your-writes and lag monitoring","lang":"sql","code":"-- On the primary, right after the write commits: remember the WAL position in the session\nSELECT pg_current_wal_lsn() AS commit_lsn;           -- e.g. 3/5A0C2F10\n\n-- On the replica, before serving that user's next read\nSELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= '3/5A0C2F10'::pg_lsn AS caught_up;\n-- caught_up = false: read from the primary, or wait briefly and check again\n\n-- Lag per replica, measured on the primary\nSELECT application_name, client_addr, write_lag, flush_lag, replay_lag\nFROM pg_stat_replication;\n\n-- On a replica: time since the last replayed transaction\nSELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS replay_delay;","note":"The LSN check is precise, unlike a fixed time window. `replay_delay` also grows when the primary is simply idle, so alert on LSN distance or `replay_lag` instead."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does a user see a just-created order?","a":"The simplest way is to return the created order in the `201 Created` response body so the client never needs to re-read it immediately. For the next pages, either route that user's reads to the primary for a short window after a write (a session flag or cookie with a timestamp comfortably above normal lag), or store the commit LSN in the session and read from a replica only once `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()` has passed it, otherwise fall back to the primary. The UI can also insert the new order into the list optimistically. The LSN approach is exact, while the time window is a heuristic that fails during lag spikes."}],"extra":["**Built-in routing:** since Spring Framework 6.1.2, `LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy#setReadOnlyDataSource` sends `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` work to a replica without a custom `AbstractRoutingDataSource`.","**Replica query conflicts:** a long query on a hot standby can be cancelled with \"conflict with recovery\" or delay replay, depending on `max_standby_streaming_delay`; `hot_standby_feedback = on` avoids cancellations but causes bloat on the primary.","**Failover loses data too:** promoting an asynchronous replica can drop the last acknowledged transactions, so the replica set has a nonzero recovery point objective unless you use synchronous replication.","**Caches inherit lag:** repopulating a cache from a lagging replica right after an invalidation re-caches stale data for a full TTL, so cache refills after writes should read from the primary."],"redFlags":["Assumes replicas are immediately consistent"]},{"id":78,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"When would you choose MongoDB over a relational database? What trade-offs would you accept?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Choose MongoDB when data is naturally aggregate-shaped, read and written as whole documents, has an evolving schema and needs horizontal write scaling; in return accept weaker joins, reporting and cross-document constraints.","answer":["MongoDB fits when the **document model** matches the domain: a product with category-specific attributes, a user profile with nested preferences, an order with its lines. One document is one aggregate, so the hot path is one read and one atomic write. A **flexible schema** lets heterogeneous or fast-evolving attributes live together without a migration per field (you still enforce shape with `$jsonSchema` validators), and **sharding** scales writes and data volume horizontally, with replica sets for automatic failover. That combination supports **high write throughput** for ingestion, catalogs and content.","The trade-offs I accept: data is **denormalized**, so duplicated fields need a sync strategy; **joins are limited** (`$lookup` exists but is slower and there are no foreign keys); multi-document transactions exist but cost more and should be the exception; ad hoc **reporting** and analytics across entities are harder and usually move to a warehouse; and the shard key is a near-permanent decision.","A relational database is the better fit when relationships are many-to-many and queried in many directions, when integrity spans entities (ledgers, inventory, bookings), and when ad hoc SQL matters. PostgreSQL `jsonb` also covers \"some flexible attributes\", so flexibility alone does not justify MongoDB.","So I **choose by use case**: known aggregate-oriented access patterns and scale needs point to MongoDB, while cross-entity invariants point to SQL. MongoDB is not faster by default; performance comes from modeling and indexes, and many systems use both."],"keyPoints":["Flexible schema","Document model","High write throughput","Horizontal scaling","Denormalization","Limited joins and reporting","Choose based on the use case"],"examples":[{"title":"A document that plays to MongoDB's strengths","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"_id\": \"sku-48213\",\n  \"name\": \"Trail Running Shoe X2\",\n  \"category\": \"footwear\",\n  \"price\": { \"amount\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"129.00\" }, \"currency\": \"EUR\" },\n  \"attributes\": {\n    \"dropMm\": 6,\n    \"waterproof\": true,\n    \"surface\": [\"trail\", \"gravel\"]\n  },\n  \"variants\": [\n    { \"sku\": \"sku-48213-41-blk\", \"size\": 41, \"color\": \"black\" },\n    { \"sku\": \"sku-48213-42-blu\", \"size\": 42, \"color\": \"blue\" }\n  ],\n  \"categoryIds\": [\"running\", \"outdoor\"],\n  \"schemaVersion\": 3,\n  \"updatedAt\": { \"$date\": \"2026-09-20T10:15:00Z\" }\n}","note":"A laptop in the same collection would carry completely different `attributes`; the page needs the whole product in one read, and there are no cross-product invariants to enforce."},{"title":"Deciding by use case","lang":"text","code":"Use case                          Better fit         Main reason\nProduct catalog, CMS content      MongoDB            heterogeneous attributes, whole-document reads\nUser profiles and preferences     MongoDB            one aggregate per user, key lookups\nEvent or IoT ingestion at scale   MongoDB            sharded writes, time series collections\nPayments ledger, accounting       PostgreSQL         multi-row invariants, constraints, audit SQL\nInventory with reservations       PostgreSQL         concurrent decrements across entities\nAd hoc reporting, analytics       SQL or warehouse   joins and aggregations across entities","note":"Mixed systems are common: PostgreSQL for the transactional core, MongoDB for catalog, content or read models fed from events."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Would you use MongoDB for a payment system? Why?","a":"For the core ledger and payment state machine, no: I would use a relational database. Money movement needs multi-row atomicity (debit and credit together), declarative constraints (unique idempotency keys, foreign keys, check constraints), strong consistency and reconciliation and audit queries that SQL handles well. MongoDB can do it with majority write concern and multi-document transactions, but you fight the model and lose constraints. It fits well around the core, for example raw provider webhook payloads, merchant configuration or dashboard read models."},{"q":"What about an inventory system?","a":"It depends on the operations. If stock is per SKU and each reservation touches one document, MongoDB works with an atomic conditional update such as `updateOne({ sku, available: { $gte: qty } }, { $inc: { available: -qty } })`, which cannot oversell. If an order must reserve many SKUs atomically, stock spans warehouses with transfers, and you need reporting across locations, those are multi-entity invariants and a relational database with row locks or conditional updates is simpler and safer."}],"extra":["**Transactions exist but are not free:** multi-document ACID transactions arrived in 4.0 (replica sets) and 4.2 (sharded clusters), with a default 60 second lifetime limit, and cross-shard transactions pay two-phase commit costs; needing them everywhere signals the wrong model or the wrong database.","**Write and read concern:** durability is tunable per operation; since MongoDB 5.0 the default write concern is `majority`, and `readConcern: \"majority\"` avoids reading data that a failover could roll back, while `w: 1` is faster but can lose acknowledged writes.","**Shard key pitfalls:** a monotonically increasing key such as an ObjectId or timestamp sends every insert to one shard; prefer a high-cardinality, evenly distributed key aligned with the main query, since queries without it become scatter-gather across all shards.","**Schema still exists:** \"schemaless\" means the schema lives in the application, so use `$jsonSchema` validation and a `schemaVersion` field to evolve documents safely."],"redFlags":["Says MongoDB is always faster or always better","Cannot explain trade-offs","Doesn't know when SQL is a better fit"]},{"id":79,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"Embed vs Reference in MongoDB: when should you use each?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Embed data that is owned by the parent, read with it and bounded in size; reference data that is shared, large, unbounded or updated independently, and decide from the real query and update patterns.","answer":["**Embed** what is always read together and belongs to one parent: order lines inside the order, a customer's few addresses, a small status history. You get one read, an atomic single-document update and good locality, with no `$lookup`. The cost is duplication when the embedded data is really shared, and document growth.","**Reference** what is shared by many parents (a product used by thousands of orders), large (long descriptions, media metadata), unbounded (all orders of a customer, reviews of a popular product, event histories), or read and updated independently (a price that changes daily should not require rewriting historical orders). The cost is extra queries or `$lookup` and no referential integrity enforced by the database.","**Size and query patterns decide.** A BSON document is capped at 16 MB, and performance degrades long before that: large documents waste bandwidth and cache, and every update rewrites the whole document. Unbounded arrays are the classic anti-pattern. A useful guide: one-to-few, embed; one-to-many, an array of ids in the parent; one-to-very-many, the child stores the parent id. The hybrid **extended reference** embeds the id plus the few fields you display.","I list the hot operations with their frequency and atomicity needs, then check how often the embedded data changes. Neither embedding nor referencing is a default."],"keyPoints":["Embed data that is always read together","Reference data that is shared, large or independent","Consider document size and query patterns"],"examples":[{"title":"Order with embedded lines and extended references","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"_id\": { \"$oid\": \"66f1c2a9e4b0a1b2c3d4e5f6\" },\n  \"orderNumber\": \"ORD-2026-000481\",\n  \"customer\": {\n    \"customerId\": { \"$oid\": \"66a0f1d2e4b0a1b2c3d4e001\" },\n    \"name\": \"Sara Lind\",\n    \"email\": \"sara.lind@example.com\"\n  },\n  \"status\": \"PAID\",\n  \"lines\": [\n    { \"productId\": \"sku-48213\", \"name\": \"Trail Running Shoe X2\",\n      \"unitPrice\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"129.00\" }, \"qty\": 1 },\n    { \"productId\": \"sku-10077\", \"name\": \"Merino Socks\",\n      \"unitPrice\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"15.00\" }, \"qty\": 3 }\n  ],\n  \"shippingAddress\": { \"street\": \"Storgatan 12\", \"city\": \"Stockholm\", \"postalCode\": \"111 51\", \"country\": \"SE\" },\n  \"total\": { \"$numberDecimal\": \"174.00\" },\n  \"createdAt\": { \"$date\": \"2026-09-20T10:15:00Z\" }\n}","note":"Lines are embedded (owned and bounded); product and customer are referenced by id with a snapshot of the fields the order must keep as they were at purchase time."},{"title":"Embedded versus referenced in Spring Data MongoDB","lang":"java","code":"// Embedded value objects: owned, bounded, always read with the order\nrecord OrderLine(String productId, String name, BigDecimal unitPrice, int qty) {}\nrecord CustomerSnapshot(String customerId, String name, String email) {}\n\n@Document(\"orders\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private CustomerSnapshot customer;   // extended reference: id plus fields shown on the order\n    private List<OrderLine> lines;       // bounded by checkout rules, for example max 100 lines\n    private String status;\n    @Field(targetType = FieldType.DECIMAL128) private BigDecimal total;\n}\n\n// Referenced: unbounded and read independently, so the child points to the parent\n@Document(\"reviews\")\n@CompoundIndex(def = \"{ 'productId': 1, 'createdAt': -1 }\")\nclass Review {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private String productId;            // never an ever-growing array inside Product\n    private int rating;\n    private String text;\n    private Instant createdAt;\n}","note":"Reviews of a popular product would grow without limit, so they live in their own collection with an index on `productId`; the product keeps only a computed rating summary."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you model Orders and Products?","a":"Orders embed their lines, because lines are owned by the order, bounded, always read with it and must change atomically with its status. Each line references the product by `productId` but also copies a snapshot of name, SKU, unit price and tax rate, because the order must keep what the customer bought at that price even after the catalog changes. Products stay in their own collection, since they are shared by many orders, fairly large and updated independently, and a product never embeds its orders because that list is unbounded."},{"q":"When would you embed customer information?","a":"Embed a customer snapshot on the order when the order needs the data as it was at order time, such as name, email and billing or shipping address for invoices, fulfillment and legal records, and when it is shown on every order view; keep `customerId` alongside it to navigate back. Do not embed mutable data that must be current (loyalty tier, marketing consent) unless you accept staleness and have an update mechanism such as a change stream consumer. Remember that each embedded copy of personal data widens the scope of GDPR erasure."}],"extra":["**Updates rewrite the document:** changing one array element still ends up persisted as a new version of the whole document, so large, frequently updated documents cost write throughput and cache even far below 16 MB.","**Bucket pattern:** for histories and time series, group children into bounded bucket documents (per device per hour, or per 100 events) to get embedding benefits without unbounded growth; time series collections automate this.","**Outlier pattern:** embed the first N reviews for typical products and move overflow into a separate collection flagged with `hasOverflow: true`, so a few viral products do not dictate the model for all.","**$lookup needs an index:** `$lookup` runs a lookup per input document against the foreign field, so it needs an index there; it works with sharded collections since MongoDB 5.1, but frequent `$lookup` on a hot path suggests you should embed.","**Atomicity boundary:** only single-document writes are atomic without a transaction, so data that must change together (order status and totals) belongs in the same document."],"redFlags":["Always chooses Embed or always chooses Reference","Ignores query patterns","Doesn't know document size limitations"]},{"id":80,"category":"NoSQL / MongoDB","question":"How does data modeling in MongoDB differ from relational databases?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Relational modeling normalizes entities first and lets queries join them later; MongoDB modeling starts from the application's access patterns and shapes documents around aggregates, trading controlled duplication for single-document reads and atomic writes.","answer":["In a relational design you model entities and relationships to third normal form, largely independent of the queries, and rely on joins, constraints and the optimizer to serve whatever queries come later. MongoDB modeling is **query-first**: list the operations, their frequency, latency targets and consistency needs, then **design around those access patterns** so each hot operation touches one document or one index range, with **fewer joins** because `$lookup` is comparatively expensive.","**Document-oriented design** means the document is the aggregate and the consistency boundary: what changes together lives together, because single-document writes are atomic. Things with an independent lifecycle become separate documents linked by id. **Denormalization** is deliberate: copy read-mostly fields (product name into an order line, author name into a post) and decide how each copy is maintained, for example a change stream consumer, a background job or accepting staleness.","The rest of the toolkit differs too: the schema lives in the application plus `$jsonSchema` validators, documents carry a `schemaVersion` and migrate lazily, compound indexes follow the ESR rule, arrays get multikey indexes, and the shard key is part of the model from day one.","The common failure is porting a relational schema one table per collection and joining in the application, which gives you the weaknesses of both. The opposite failure is one giant document that grows forever."],"keyPoints":["Design around access patterns","Denormalization","Fewer joins","Document-oriented Design","Query-first Modeling"],"examples":[{"title":"Query-first worksheet for an order service","lang":"text","code":"Operation                           Frequency  Latency    Shape it implies\nGet order by id with lines          high       < 20 ms    one document, lines embedded\nList a customer's recent orders     high       < 50 ms    index { customerId: 1, createdAt: -1 }, projection\nUpdate order status                 medium     < 20 ms    atomic update of one document\nShow product name and price on it   high       n/a        copy name and price into each line (snapshot)\nRename a product                    rare       async      do not propagate to past orders\nDaily sales per product             low        minutes    aggregation pipeline or warehouse export","note":"The collections, embedded fields and indexes are read off this table. A relational design would start from the entity diagram instead."},{"title":"Model and repository derived from the access patterns","lang":"java","code":"@Document(\"orders\")\n@CompoundIndex(name = \"customer_recent\", def = \"{ 'customerId': 1, 'createdAt': -1 }\")\npublic class Order {\n    @Id private String id;\n    private String customerId;\n    private int schemaVersion = 2;            // readers handle v1 and v2, migration is lazy\n    private String status;\n    private List<OrderLine> lines;            // embedded: part of the aggregate\n    private BigDecimal total;                 // computed pattern: stored, not summed on every read\n    private Instant createdAt;\n}\n\npublic interface OrderRepository extends MongoRepository<Order, String> {\n    // Served by the compound index; the projection keeps list pages small\n    @Query(value = \"{ 'customerId': ?0 }\",\n           fields = \"{ 'status': 1, 'total': 1, 'createdAt': 1 }\",\n           sort = \"{ 'createdAt': -1 }\")\n    List<Order> findRecentSummaries(String customerId, Pageable page);\n}\n\n// Status change is one atomic single-document update, no transaction required\nmongoTemplate.updateFirst(\n        Query.query(Criteria.where(\"_id\").is(orderId).and(\"status\").is(\"PENDING\")),\n        new Update().set(\"status\", \"PAID\").set(\"paidAt\", Instant.now()),\n        Order.class);","note":"Every hot operation maps to one document or one index range. The conditional filter on `status` makes the transition safe under concurrency without locks."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why is normalization often not the best approach in MongoDB?","a":"Normalizing spreads one aggregate over several collections, so every read needs `$lookup` or application-side joins, which are slower and less optimized than SQL joins, and MongoDB has no foreign keys to protect the relationships anyway. Writes that must change several of those documents together then need multi-document transactions, while a denormalized aggregate gets atomic single-document writes for free. You trade some controlled duplication for one-read access and simple atomicity. Referencing is still right for data that is shared and changes often, or that grows without bound."},{"q":"How would you model a product catalog?","a":"One `products` document per product holds the common fields (sku, name, brand, price, `categoryIds`) plus category-specific attributes. For faceted filtering over arbitrary attributes, use the attribute pattern `attributes: [{ k: \"color\", v: \"black\" }]` with a compound index on `attributes.k` and `attributes.v`. Variants are embedded when bounded (sizes and colors); categories live in their own collection with an ancestors array for breadcrumbs, and the category name is copied onto the product if it is displayed. Reviews go in a separate collection with a computed rating summary on the product, fast-changing stock usually belongs to an inventory service, and full-text search goes to Atlas Search or a dedicated search engine."}],"extra":["**ESR rule:** MongoDB's guideline for compound indexes is Equality fields first, then Sort fields, then Range fields, the same reasoning as composite index order in SQL.","**Schema versioning pattern:** store a `schemaVersion`, make readers accept old and new shapes, and upgrade documents on write or with a background job instead of a big-bang migration.","**Many-to-many:** model it as an array of ids on the side you query from (for example `categoryIds` on products with a multikey index), and only introduce a separate linking collection when the relationship has its own attributes or is unbounded.","**Validation levels:** a `$jsonSchema` validator with `validationLevel: \"moderate\"` checks inserts and updates to documents that already conform, so legacy documents can coexist while new writes are enforced."],"redFlags":["Models MongoDB exactly like a relational database","Always normalizes data","Doesn't understand aggregate boundaries"]},{"id":81,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Explain Cache-Aside and its consistency trade-offs.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Cache-aside reads the cache, falls back to the database on a miss and populates the cache; writes update the database and then delete the key, which is simple and resilient but always leaves stale windows.","answer":["Read path: `GET` the key; on a hit return it; on a miss load from the database, `SET` it with a TTL and return. Write path: update the database, which stays the source of truth, then invalidate the key. I prefer `DEL` over writing the new value into the cache: two concurrent writers can commit to the database in one order and update the cache in the other, leaving the older value cached, while a delete is idempotent and order-insensitive. The cache only holds data that is actually requested, and if Redis is down the application can still read the database.","The database and the cache are never updated atomically, so stale windows remain. Between the commit and the `DEL`, readers see the old value. If the `DEL` fails (network error, crash after commit), the stale entry lives until its TTL, which is why every entry needs a TTL as the upper bound on staleness. A slow reader can also write an old value back after the invalidation. And invalidation must happen after commit: deleting inside the transaction lets a reader repopulate from the pre-commit state.","Mitigations scale with how much staleness hurts: always set a TTL, delete after commit, add a delayed second delete, store a version and refuse to overwrite with an older one, or drive invalidation from CDC (Debezium reading the WAL) so every committed change, from any writer, evicts reliably. For data where staleness is unacceptable, such as balances or limits, read the database for the decision."],"keyPoints":["Application reads cache then DB and populates cache","writes update DB and invalidate/update cache","stale windows remain"],"examples":[{"title":"Cache-aside with after-commit invalidation","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class ProductCatalog {\n    private static final Duration TTL = Duration.ofMinutes(10);\n    private final RedisTemplate<String, ProductView> redis;\n    private final ProductRepository products;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n    // constructor omitted\n\n    public ProductView get(String sku) {\n        String key = \"product:v1:\" + sku;\n        ProductView cached = redis.opsForValue().get(key);\n        if (cached != null) return cached;                       // hit\n        ProductView fresh = products.findView(sku).orElseThrow();\n        redis.opsForValue().set(key, fresh, TTL);                // TTL bounds staleness\n        return fresh;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void changePrice(String sku, BigDecimal price) {\n        products.updatePrice(sku, price);                        // database is the source of truth\n        events.publishEvent(new ProductChanged(sku));\n    }\n\n    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)\n    void onProductChanged(ProductChanged e) {\n        redis.delete(\"product:v1:\" + e.sku());                   // delete, do not overwrite\n    }\n}","note":"The eviction runs only after the transaction commits, so no reader can repopulate the key from pre-commit data. If the delete fails, the TTL still caps how long the stale value survives."},{"title":"The race that re-caches stale data","lang":"text","code":"t1  Reader A: GET product:v1:42             miss\nt2  Reader A: SELECT price                  returns 100 (old)\nt3  Reader A: long GC pause\nt4  Writer B: UPDATE price = 120; COMMIT\nt5  Writer B: DEL product:v1:42             nothing to delete yet\nt6  Reader A: SET product:v1:42 = 100       stale value cached until the TTL expires","note":"Invalidation happened correctly, yet the cache ends up stale because the reader's write lands after the delete."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What race can repopulate stale data after invalidation?","a":"A reader misses, loads the old value from the database (or from a lagging replica), and is then delayed by a GC pause or slow network. Meanwhile a writer commits the new value and deletes the key, which is still empty. The reader finally `SET`s the old value, and it stays cached until the TTL expires. Mitigations are a short enough TTL, a delayed second delete a few hundred milliseconds after the write, versioned values where a Lua script refuses to overwrite a newer version, or lease tokens (as in Facebook's memcache design) so the invalidation cancels the reader's right to populate."}],"extra":["**Negative caching:** cache \"not found\" results with a short TTL (or check a Bloom filter first) so repeated lookups of nonexistent ids do not all fall through to the database.","**Versioned key prefixes:** putting a version in the key (`product:v1:`) lets a deploy that changes the cached DTO shape switch to `v2` instead of failing to deserialize old entries or flushing the whole cache.","**Spring Cache and transactions:** `@CacheEvict` runs when the method returns, which is still inside the transaction; `RedisCacheManager.builder(factory).transactionAware()` defers puts and evictions until after commit.","**Local single-flight:** `@Cacheable(sync = true)` makes concurrent misses for the same key on one instance wait for a single load, which helps against stampedes but is not a distributed lock."],"redFlags":["Treats cache as source of truth","has no invalidation strategy"]},{"id":82,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"What is cache stampede and how do you prevent it?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A stampede happens when a hot key expires or the cache is cold and many requests miss at once and hammer the database; prevent it with TTL jitter, single-flight or per-key locks, stale-while-revalidate and prewarming.","answer":["A hot key serving 5,000 requests per second expires, and in the same millisecond thousands of requests miss and run the same expensive query. The database saturates, latency climbs, requests time out and clients retry, so the recomputation that would fix the problem never completes. The same happens when many keys were written together with the same TTL, after a cache restart or flush, or when a deploy changes the key format.","The tools, usually combined: **TTL jitter** (for example plus or minus 10%) spreads expirations of keys written together. **Single-flight** collapses concurrent misses for one key into one load per instance (Caffeine `LoadingCache`, `@Cacheable(sync = true)`). A **per-key distributed lock** (`SET lock:key token NX PX 5000`) lets one instance recompute across the cluster. **Stale-while-revalidate** stores a soft expiry inside the value with a longer physical TTL, serves the stale value and refreshes once in the background. **Prewarming** loads known hot keys before traffic arrives.","Locks need failure handling: always an expiry, so a crashed holder does not block the key forever; waiters with a short deadline that then serve stale data or fail fast; and a bounded fallback when Redis itself is unavailable. A global lock or unbounded waiting just moves the outage.","Also protect the source directly with a concurrency limit on loaders, and alert when the miss rate spikes in step with expirations."],"keyPoints":["Many requests miss together and overload the source","use TTL jitter, single-flight, locks, stale-while-revalidate or prewarming"],"examples":[{"title":"Soft TTL, per-key lock and serve-stale","lang":"java","code":"public ProductView get(String sku) {\n    String key = \"product:\" + sku;\n    CachedValue<ProductView> cv = cache.get(key);                  // value plus softExpiresAt\n    if (cv != null && cv.softExpiresAt().isAfter(Instant.now())) {\n        return cv.value();                                         // fresh hit\n    }\n    String lockKey = \"lock:\" + key;\n    String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n    Boolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()\n            .setIfAbsent(lockKey, token, Duration.ofSeconds(5));  // SET NX PX, always with expiry\n    if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {\n        try {\n            ProductView fresh = products.findView(sku).orElseThrow();\n            Duration soft = Duration.ofSeconds(240 + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(60)); // jitter\n            cache.put(key, fresh, soft, soft.plusMinutes(10));     // physical TTL outlives soft TTL\n            return fresh;\n        } finally {\n            releaseIfOwner(lockKey, token);                        // compare-and-delete in Lua\n        }\n    }\n    if (cv != null) return cv.value();                             // another node refreshes: serve stale\n    return loadWithConcurrencyLimit(sku);                          // cold miss: semaphore-bounded DB load\n}","note":"Only the lock winner hits the database; everyone else gets the stale value immediately. The cold-miss path is still bounded, so a lost lock or a Redis failure cannot become a stampede."},{"title":"In-process single-flight and refresh-ahead with Caffeine","lang":"java","code":"LoadingCache<String, ProductView> local = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n        .maximumSize(50_000)\n        .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))\n        .refreshAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(4))   // old value served while one async reload runs\n        .build(sku -> products.findView(sku).orElse(null));\n\n// Concurrent get() calls for the same missing key share a single load\nProductView view = local.get(\"sku-48213\");","note":"`refreshAfterWrite` is stale-while-revalidate for a local cache, and the loading cache gives single-flight per key per JVM for free."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is stale-while-revalidate?","a":"Stale-while-revalidate means serving a cached value after its freshness period (a soft TTL) while exactly one background refresh fetches a new value. Callers get a fast, slightly stale response and the database sees one query per key per refresh period instead of a burst. You implement it with a logical expiry stored in the value, a longer physical TTL in Redis and a per-key flag or lock so only one refresh runs, or with Caffeine `refreshAfterWrite` for local caches. The name comes from the HTTP directive `Cache-Control: max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=30` (RFC 5861), and the hard TTL still caps how stale data can get."}],"extra":["**Probabilistic early expiration:** with XFetch, each reader recomputes early with a probability that rises as expiry approaches and scales with recompute cost, so usually one request refreshes before expiry and no lock is needed.","**Hot keys also overload a shard:** one very hot key lands on a single Redis node; a short-TTL local near-cache in front of Redis, or copies of the key under several suffixes, spreads that load.","**Cold starts are self-inflicted:** `FLUSHALL` during an incident or a changed key prefix in a deploy creates a fleet-wide cold cache, so roll out new key versions gradually and prewarm the top keys from access logs.","**Signature in metrics:** a stampede looks like a sawtooth of miss rate and database QPS aligned with the TTL; track misses per key prefix, loader concurrency and loader latency."],"redFlags":["Only increases TTL","uses a global lock without failure handling"]},{"id":83,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"How do you choose TTL and eviction policy?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"TTL is a per-data-type freshness decision (staleness tolerance, change rate, traffic), eviction is what Redis does when memory is full; choose the policy from the workload and watch hit ratio, memory and eviction rate.","answer":["Expiration and eviction are different mechanisms. A TTL (`SET ... EX`, `EXPIRE`) removes a key when its time is up, lazily on access plus an active background sampling cycle, and it expresses freshness. Eviction only starts when `maxmemory` is reached, and `maxmemory-policy` chooses victims regardless of how fresh they are; it expresses capacity.","The TTL follows the freshness tolerance and traffic of each data type: feature flags or FX rates seconds, a product catalog with explicit invalidation 30 minutes or more, sessions a sliding TTL equal to the idle timeout. Shorter TTLs mean fresher data but more misses and database load; longer TTLs raise the hit ratio but also staleness and memory use. With reliable invalidation the TTL is only a safety net and can be long; rarely read keys should get short TTLs so they do not occupy memory. Add jitter either way.","Eviction depends on the workload. `allkeys-lru` suits a general cache with recency-driven access, `allkeys-lfu` a skewed, stable popularity distribution, and `volatile-*` policies evict only keys with a TTL when cache entries share an instance with data that must stay. `noeviction`, the default, rejects writes when memory is full, which is right for locks, queues and primary data. Separate instances for cache and durable data are cleaner than mixing them.","Then monitor hit ratio (`keyspace_hits` against misses, and per key prefix in the application), used memory against `maxmemory`, the rate of `evicted_keys` and `expired_keys`, and fragmentation."],"keyPoints":["TTL follows freshness tolerance and traffic","eviction depends on workload","monitor hit ratio, memory and evictions"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-cache TTLs in Spring Boot","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass CacheTtlConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    RedisCacheManagerBuilderCustomizer cacheTtls() {\n        return builder -> builder\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"featureFlags\",          // must react quickly to changes\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofSeconds(30)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"fxRates\",               // provider updates every minute\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(1)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"productViews\",          // invalidated on change, TTL is a safety net\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(30)))\n            .withCacheConfiguration(\"merchantProfiles\",      // rarely changes, moderate traffic\n                RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig().entryTtl(Duration.ofHours(6)));\n    }\n}","note":"One TTL for everything is a red flag; each cache name documents its own freshness contract next to the reason for it."},{"title":"Configure eviction and read the signals","lang":"bash","code":"# Cache-only instance: cap memory and evict by access frequency\nredis-cli CONFIG SET maxmemory 6gb\nredis-cli CONFIG SET maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu\n\n# Hit ratio inputs and churn counters\nredis-cli INFO stats | grep -E 'keyspace_hits|keyspace_misses|evicted_keys|expired_keys'\n\n# Memory pressure and fragmentation\nredis-cli INFO memory | grep -E 'used_memory_human|maxmemory_human|maxmemory_policy|mem_fragmentation_ratio'\n\n# Hot keys (needs an LFU policy) and the biggest keys per type\nredis-cli --hotkeys\nredis-cli --bigkeys\n\n# Remaining TTL and LFU access counter for one key\nredis-cli TTL product:v1:sku-48213\nredis-cli OBJECT FREQ product:v1:sku-48213","note":"Persist the settings in `redis.conf` or your managed service's parameter group; `CONFIG SET` changes are lost on restart unless followed by `CONFIG REWRITE`."}],"followUps":[{"q":"LFU vs LRU?","a":"LRU evicts the least recently used keys, which suits recency-driven workloads such as sessions, but a one-off scan (a batch job reading many keys once) pushes out the real hot set. LFU evicts the least frequently used keys, with a counter that decays over time, so consistently popular keys such as top-selling products survive scans. Its weakness is fast-shifting popularity: new keys start with a low count (Redis gives them a small initial value so they are not evicted immediately). Choose LFU for skewed, stable popularity and LRU when recent access predicts the next access."},{"q":"What does rising eviction rate mean?","a":"Redis has hit `maxmemory` and is removing keys before their TTL to make room, meaning the working set no longer fits. Expect the hit ratio to fall and database load and latency to rise. Typical causes are traffic growth, new key types, larger values, keys written without TTLs, or fragmentation. Check memory per key prefix (`--bigkeys`, `MEMORY USAGE`), verify TTLs, trim or compress values, shorten TTLs for cold data, or add memory or shards. If the instance also holds non-cache data, every eviction is data loss and should page someone."}],"extra":["**The default is noeviction:** a cache deployed without a policy starts failing writes with OOM errors when full; a Redis that holds locks, queues or sessions must never use an `allkeys-*` policy.","**Approximate algorithms:** Redis LRU and LFU sample `maxmemory-samples` keys (default 5) instead of tracking a global order, and LFU uses a small logarithmic counter that decays over time (`lfu-log-factor`, `lfu-decay-time`).","**volatile-* trap:** with `volatile-lru`, keys without a TTL are never evicted, so if most keys lack one, Redis behaves like `noeviction` and returns OOM errors.","**SET clears the TTL:** overwriting a key with `SET` removes its expiry unless you pass `EX` again or `KEEPTTL` (Redis 6.0+), a common source of keys that silently live forever.","**Sliding versus absolute TTL:** sliding expiry (`GETEX key EX 1800`, Redis 6.2+) fits sessions, but on shared cached data it means a hot key is never refreshed; cached data should use absolute TTLs."],"redFlags":["Uses one TTL for all data","confuses expiration with eviction"]},{"id":84,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"What can go wrong with Redis-based distributed locks?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A Redis lock is a lease, not a guarantee: expiry during a pause, partitions and failover can produce two owners, so use owner tokens with compare-and-delete, and fencing tokens checked by the resource when correctness matters.","answer":["The minimum correct pattern is `SET lock:settlement 9b1d4e7a NX PX 30000`, where the value is a random per-owner token: one atomic command that acquires only if absent and always sets an expiry. `SETNX` followed by `EXPIRE` is two commands, and a crash between them leaves a lock that never expires. Release must be compare-and-delete in a Lua script (delete only if the value is still my token); a plain `DEL` can remove a lock another client acquired after mine expired.","Even done right, a lease can have **multiple owners**. The holder can pause (GC, VM freeze, a slow database call) past the lease; the lock expires, another client acquires it, and the first one resumes and writes, still believing it owns the lock. Watchdog renewal shrinks the window but a paused or partitioned process cannot renew or notice. Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can promote a replica that never saw the lock. Redlock (majority of independent masters) addresses failover but still relies on timing assumptions.","Where correctness matters, add **fencing**: every acquisition returns a monotonically increasing token, every write to the protected resource carries it, and the resource rejects tokens older than the highest it has seen. A stale owner then fails harmlessly, but the resource has to take part in the check.","So I use Redis locks for **efficiency** (one node runs a job, avoid duplicate recomputation), where an occasional double run is tolerable. For money, stock or uniqueness, the database enforces correctness with constraints, row locks or conditional updates."],"keyPoints":["Lease expiry, pauses and partitions can create multiple owners","use owner tokens, compare-and-delete and fencing where correctness matters"],"examples":[{"title":"SETNX without expiry versus token plus compare-and-delete","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: two commands (a crash between them leaves the lock forever) and an unconditional delete\nredis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(\"lock:settlement\", \"1\");\nredis.expire(\"lock:settlement\", Duration.ofSeconds(30));\nrunSettlement();\nredis.delete(\"lock:settlement\");                      // may delete a lock another node now holds\n\n// GOOD: atomic SET NX PX with an owner token, release only if we still own it\nprivate static final RedisScript<Long> RELEASE = RedisScript.of(\"\"\"\n        if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n          return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1])\n        end\n        return 0\n        \"\"\", Long.class);\n\nString token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\nBoolean acquired = redis.opsForValue()\n        .setIfAbsent(\"lock:settlement\", token, Duration.ofSeconds(30));\nif (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {\n    try {\n        runSettlement();                              // must finish well within the lease\n    } finally {\n        redis.execute(RELEASE, List.of(\"lock:settlement\"), token);\n    }\n}","note":"The GOOD version fixes the two classic bugs, a lock without expiry and deleting someone else's lock, but it still cannot stop a paused holder from acting after its lease expired."},{"title":"Fencing enforced by the resource","lang":"sql","code":"-- Each lease comes with a monotonically increasing fencing token,\n-- ideally from a linearizable source such as a database sequence.\nCREATE SEQUENCE settlement_fencing_seq;\n\nCREATE TABLE settlement_state (\n    merchant_id    bigint PRIMARY KEY,\n    last_token     bigint      NOT NULL,\n    settled_until  timestamptz NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- Every write carries the holder's token; an older token is rejected\nUPDATE settlement_state\nSET settled_until = $2,\n    last_token    = $3\nWHERE merchant_id = $1\n  AND last_token < $3;\n-- 0 rows updated: this holder's lease is stale, abort without side effects","note":"Client A with token 33 pauses, client B gets 34 and writes; when A resumes, its write with 33 updates zero rows. Safety no longer depends on timing."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is a fencing token?","a":"A fencing token is a number that increases with every successful lock acquisition. The holder sends it with every write to the protected resource, and the resource remembers the highest token it has accepted and rejects any write carrying a lower one, typically with a conditional update such as `WHERE last_token < :token`. If holder A pauses, its lease expires, B acquires token 34 and writes, then A's late write with 33 is refused. It turns a timing-based lease into safe mutual exclusion for effects, but only if the storage layer checks the token."},{"q":"When should the DB enforce correctness?","a":"Whenever a violation would corrupt business state: moving money, decrementing stock, one active subscription per customer, state transitions that must happen once. The database can enforce these in the same transaction as the write, with unique constraints, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, optimistic `@Version` columns, conditional updates such as `WHERE status = 'PENDING'`, or `pg_advisory_xact_lock`. A Redis lock in front can still reduce contention and duplicate work, but the database is the final guard, so a lock failure produces a rejected write instead of a double effect."}],"extra":["**Redisson watchdog:** an `RLock` acquired without an explicit lease time is renewed periodically while the JVM is alive (default watchdog timeout 30 seconds); passing a `leaseTime` disables the watchdog, and neither mode protects against a paused or partitioned holder.","**Fencing source must be consistent:** a Redis `INCR` counter can go backwards after an asynchronous failover, so take fencing tokens from something linearizable such as a PostgreSQL sequence, an etcd revision or a ZooKeeper zxid.","**Redlock debate:** Martin Kleppmann argued Redlock depends on bounded clock drift and pauses and provides no fencing; Salvatore Sanfilippo disputed parts of that. The practical takeaway is the same: fine for efficiency, not a substitute for fencing.","**Scheduled jobs:** ShedLock (`@SchedulerLock(name = \"settlement\", lockAtMostFor = \"PT10M\")`) with a JDBC or Redis provider is the usual Spring answer; `lockAtMostFor` must exceed the worst-case run time and the job should still be idempotent.","**Checking ownership is still racy:** reading the lock value before a write is a time-of-check to time-of-use race; only the resource-side check makes a stale holder harmless."],"redFlags":["Uses SETNX without expiry","deletes another owner's lock"]},{"id":85,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"Redis is unavailable. Which features fail and which degrade?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"It depends on what Redis does for each feature: optional caches degrade to the database behind a concurrency limit, while sessions, rate limits, locks and Redis-held primary data each need an explicit fail-open or fail-closed decision.","answer":["Start with an inventory of Redis roles. A **cache** holds derived, rebuildable data, so the feature degrades: bypass to the database, but with protection. **Sessions** hold user state, so users are logged out or cannot log in unless sessions can fall back to another store. **Rate limiters** fail open (allow traffic, perhaps with a local limiter) when availability matters more, and fail closed for abuse and cost protection such as login attempts or OTP sends. **Locks** that only prevent duplicate work can skip a run; locks guarding correctness must fail closed unless the database enforces it anyway. **Primary data** in Redis (queues, carts, leaderboards) is simply unavailable.","The deciding question per feature is the cost of a wrong answer against the cost of no answer. A payment idempotency check stored only in Redis must fail closed (or move to the database); a recommendations widget should just disappear.","Degradation also needs mechanics: short client timeouts so every request does not wait for the default, a circuit breaker so a dead Redis is not retried per request, a concurrency limit on the database fallback, a small local cache for hot keys, and feature flags to shed optional work. Keep optional dependencies out of liveness and readiness probes.","I write the per-feature decision into the runbook and test it by killing Redis in staging, including the recovery, since Redis returns empty and everything misses at once."],"keyPoints":["Depends on whether Redis is cache, session, lock or primary data","optional caches may bypass with DB protection, critical correctness may fail closed"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-feature outage behavior","lang":"text","code":"Redis role                      On outage                               Why\nProduct and price cache         degrade: DB behind a concurrency limit  cache is derived, DB is the truth\nHTTP sessions (Spring Session)  fail: users must log in again           no other copy of session state\nLogin and OTP rate limiter      fail closed                             abuse, fraud and SMS cost risk\nPartner API quota limiter       fail open with a local token bucket     availability matters more\nScheduler lock (ShedLock)       skip this run, or JDBC lock provider    duplicate batch run is costly\nPayment idempotency keys        fail closed, or keep them in the DB     a double charge is unacceptable\nLeaderboard (primary data)      feature unavailable, 503                nothing to fall back to","note":"Each row is a product decision as much as a technical one, and it belongs in the runbook before the incident, not during it."},{"title":"Cache bypass that cannot stampede the database","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class PriceService {\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final PriceRepository prices;\n    private final CircuitBreaker redisBreaker;                   // Resilience4j\n    private final Semaphore dbPermits = new Semaphore(20);       // sized from DB capacity per instance\n    // constructor and cachePut (same breaker, errors swallowed) omitted\n\n    public Price get(String sku) {\n        String key = \"price:\" + sku;\n        Optional<String> cached = cacheGet(key);\n        if (cached.isPresent()) return Price.parse(cached.get());\n        if (!dbPermits.tryAcquire()) {                           // shed load instead of queueing\n            throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE, \"price lookup shed\");\n        }\n        try {\n            Price p = prices.find(sku);\n            cachePut(key, p.serialize());                        // best effort, failures only counted\n            return p;\n        } finally {\n            dbPermits.release();\n        }\n    }\n\n    private Optional<String> cacheGet(String key) {\n        try {\n            return Optional.ofNullable(redisBreaker.executeSupplier(() -> redis.opsForValue().get(key)));\n        } catch (Exception e) {                                  // Redis down or breaker open\n            return Optional.empty();\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The breaker makes a dead Redis cost microseconds instead of a timeout per request, and the semaphore caps concurrent database loads whether the miss comes from an outage or a cold key."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you prevent a DB stampede?","a":"Bound the load the fallback can create: a per-instance semaphore or bulkhead sized from database capacity divided by the number of instances, with fast rejection (`503` plus `Retry-After`) instead of queueing. Collapse duplicate work with in-process single-flight per key (Caffeine `LoadingCache` or a map of in-flight futures) and absorb hot keys with a short-TTL local cache. Shed optional features and low-priority traffic, and use a circuit breaker so a dead Redis is not retried per request. When Redis returns, prewarm hot keys and add TTL jitter so the refill does not trigger a second stampede; HikariCP's `maximumPoolSize` with a short `connectionTimeout` is the last line of defense."}],"extra":["**Timeouts first:** client defaults are usually far too long for a cache; set `spring.data.redis.timeout` and `spring.data.redis.connect-timeout` to a few hundred milliseconds at most, or every request waits the full timeout before the fallback runs.","**Health probes:** the aggregate `/actuator/health` includes the Redis indicator, so a platform probing it marks every pod down during a cache outage; point Kubernetes at `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`, and add Redis to readiness only if the service truly cannot work without it.","**Local near-cache:** a small Caffeine cache with a short TTL in front of Redis absorbs hot keys during an outage and saves round trips normally, at the price of per-instance staleness.","**Degraded rate limiting:** a local token bucket at roughly the global limit divided by the instance count keeps approximate protection while Redis is gone.","**Recovery is a second incident:** Redis comes back empty, so keep the database limiter in place, prewarm the top keys and use TTL jitter until the hit ratio recovers."],"redFlags":["Says everything must always stop or always bypass"]},{"id":86,"category":"Redis & Caching","question":"RDB vs AOF and replication: what durability should you expect?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"RDB snapshots can lose everything since the last snapshot, AOF with `appendfsync everysec` can lose about a second, and asynchronous replication means a failover can still drop writes that clients saw acknowledged.","answer":["**RDB** writes point-in-time snapshots by forking the process (`BGSAVE`) on a schedule such as `save 3600 1 300 100 60 10000`. Files are compact and restarts fast, which makes RDB good for backups, but a crash loses every write since the last snapshot, potentially minutes. On large datasets the fork itself can stall the server, and copy-on-write can nearly double memory under heavy writes.","**AOF** appends every write command to a log, and `appendfsync` sets the durability: `always` fsyncs on every write (most durable, slowest), `everysec` (the default) can lose about one second on a crash or power loss, and `no` leaves flushing to the OS. The log is compacted by rewrites, and since Redis 7 it is a multi-part AOF with an RDB base plus incremental files. The common setup is AOF `everysec` plus periodic RDB snapshots for backups.","Persistence is per node, and **replication is asynchronous**: the primary acknowledges the client before replicas receive the write. If it fails right then, Sentinel or Cluster promotes a replica without that write, and an acknowledged write is gone. `WAIT` narrows the window by blocking until N replicas acknowledge, `WAITAOF` (7.2+) until the write is fsynced, and `min-replicas-to-write` stops an isolated primary from accepting writes, but none of this turns Redis into a consensus system.","So expect \"durable enough for caches, sessions, rate limits and rebuildable data\", and never treat replicas as backups or Redis as a ledger."],"keyPoints":["RDB may lose recent writes","AOF improves durability with fsync cost","async replication/failover may still lose acknowledged data"],"examples":[{"title":"Persistence and replication safety settings","lang":"properties","code":"# RDB: snapshot after 3600 s if 1 change, 300 s if 100 changes, 60 s if 10000 changes\nsave 3600 1 300 100 60 10000\n\n# AOF with fsync once per second: a crash loses about the last second\nappendonly yes\nappendfsync everysec\naof-use-rdb-preamble yes\nauto-aof-rewrite-percentage 100\nauto-aof-rewrite-min-size 64mb\n\n# Stop accepting writes when fewer than 1 replica is connected with lag of 10 s or less\nmin-replicas-to-write 1\nmin-replicas-max-lag 10","note":"These are `redis.conf` directives. `min-replicas-to-write` limits how long an isolated primary keeps accepting writes that will be lost, at the cost of rejecting writes when replicas are down."},{"title":"WAIT and WAITAOF on the same connection","lang":"bash","code":"# WAIT applies to writes made on the same connection, so send both in one redis-cli session\nredis-cli <<'EOF'\nSET payment:intent:9f2c AUTHORIZED\nWAIT 1 100\nEOF\n# WAIT returns how many replicas acknowledged within 100 ms (0 means only the primary has it)\n\n# Redis 7.2+: wait until the write is fsynced to the local AOF and to 1 replica's AOF\nredis-cli <<'EOF'\nSET payment:intent:9f2c CAPTURED\nWAITAOF 1 1 100\nEOF\n\n# Persistence and replication health\nredis-cli INFO persistence | grep -E 'rdb_last_bgsave_status|aof_enabled|aof_last_write_status'\nredis-cli INFO replication | grep -E 'role|connected_slaves|master_repl_offset'","note":"A timeout does not roll the write back: the value stays on the primary even if `WAIT` returns 0, so the caller only learns that durability is uncertain."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is Redis inappropriate as system of record?","a":"When losing acknowledged writes would be unacceptable or undetectable: payments, ledgers, orders, inventory counts, idempotency keys that prevent double charges, and audit records. It is also a poor fit when data must be retained for regulatory periods, needs ad hoc querying and constraints, or is larger than RAM can economically hold. Use Redis as a derived layer that can be rebuilt from the real system of record (caches, sessions, rate limits, leaderboards recomputed from the database). If some feature must keep primary data in Redis, design for loss with reconciliation against a durable source and idempotent replays."}],"extra":["**Replicas are not backups:** `FLUSHALL`, a bad deploy deleting keys, or a buggy script replicate to every replica within milliseconds; keep off-box RDB snapshots with retention and test restores.","**Cluster split brain:** during a partition, a primary on the minority side keeps accepting writes until `cluster-node-timeout` expires, and those writes are discarded when it rejoins as a replica.","**Persistence off plus auto-restart:** if a primary without persistence restarts automatically, it comes back empty and its replicas resync to the empty dataset, wiping every copy; the Redis docs explicitly warn against that combination.","**Fork headroom:** leave memory headroom for copy-on-write during `BGSAVE` and AOF rewrites, and disable Transparent Huge Pages, which Redis recommends because they make copy-on-write far more expensive.","**Managed variants differ:** some managed Redis-compatible services add a durable multi-zone transaction log, so read the provider's actual guarantees instead of assuming open-source behavior."],"redFlags":["Assumes replicas are backups or Redis is automatically a durable ledger"]},{"id":87,"category":"REST API Design","question":"PUT vs PATCH: what are the semantic differences?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"PUT replaces the whole representation and is idempotent by definition; PATCH applies a partial change whose idempotency depends on the patch format, so you must define exactly how omitted and null fields behave.","answer":["`PUT` means \"store this representation at this URI\": the body is the complete new state, and any field the client omits is reset to its default or removed. RFC 9110 defines PUT as idempotent, so sending the same PUT twice leaves the resource in the same state, which makes it safe to retry after a timeout. PUT can also create the resource when the client controls the identifier.","`PATCH` (RFC 5789) sends a set of changes, and its semantics come from the patch media type. It is not guaranteed idempotent, but it is not always non-idempotent either: a JSON Merge Patch setting `status` to `SUSPENDED` is idempotent, while a JSON Patch `add` to the end of an array or an \"increment balance\" operation is not. The key design decision is omitted versus null: with Merge Patch an absent field means \"leave unchanged\" and an explicit `null` means \"clear it\", so your request model must be able to tell the two apart.","Use PUT when clients own the full document (settings, configuration) and PATCH for sparse updates of large resources such as a customer profile. Never use PUT as an undocumented partial update: a client that sends only `email` silently wipes `phone`. Pair both with optimistic concurrency (`ETag` plus `If-Match`) so concurrent writers get `412 Precondition Failed` instead of lost updates."],"keyPoints":["PUT replaces representation and is idempotent","PATCH applies partial changes and needs clear omitted/null semantics"],"examples":[{"title":"Same intent, PUT versus JSON Merge Patch","lang":"http","code":"PUT /customers/c-1042 HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/json\nIf-Match: \"v7\"\n\n{ \"name\": \"Sara Lind\", \"email\": \"sara@example.com\", \"phone\": null, \"segment\": \"RETAIL\" }\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nETag: \"v8\"\n\nPATCH /customers/c-1042 HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/merge-patch+json\nIf-Match: \"v8\"\n\n{ \"email\": \"sara.lind@example.com\", \"phone\": null }\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nETag: \"v9\"","note":"The PUT body must be complete; the merge patch touches only `email` and clears `phone`, leaving `name` and `segment` untouched. `If-Match` turns a stale concurrent write into `412` instead of a lost update."},{"title":"Distinguishing absent from null in a Spring merge patch endpoint","lang":"java","code":"@PatchMapping(path = \"/customers/{id}\", consumes = \"application/merge-patch+json\")\npublic ResponseEntity<CustomerDto> patch(@PathVariable UUID id,\n                                         @RequestHeader(\"If-Match\") String ifMatch,\n                                         @RequestBody JsonNode patch) {\n    Customer customer = repository.findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);\n    if (!ifMatch.equals(customer.etag())) {\n        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.PRECONDITION_FAILED).build();\n    }\n    // BAD: binding to a DTO with String fields makes \"absent\" and \"null\" identical\n    // GOOD: has() means the client sent the field, isNull() means clear it\n    if (patch.has(\"email\")) {\n        JsonNode email = patch.get(\"email\");\n        customer.setEmail(email.isNull() ? null : email.asText());\n    }\n    if (patch.has(\"phone\")) {\n        JsonNode phone = patch.get(\"phone\");\n        customer.setPhone(phone.isNull() ? null : phone.asText());\n    }\n    validator.validateMerged(customer);          // validate the result, not the partial body\n    Customer saved = repository.save(customer);\n    return ResponseEntity.ok().eTag(saved.etag()).body(CustomerDto.from(saved));\n}","note":"Binding a patch straight to a plain DTO loses the difference between \"not sent\" and \"set to null\". Reading a `JsonNode` (or using a wrapper such as `JsonNullable`) keeps it, and validation runs on the merged entity."}],"followUps":[{"q":"JSON Merge Patch vs JSON Patch?","a":"JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396, `application/merge-patch+json`) is a partial document shaped like the resource: present fields replace, `null` removes, absent fields stay untouched. It is readable and naturally idempotent, but it cannot edit individual array elements or set a value to literal null. JSON Patch (RFC 6902, `application/json-patch+json`) is an ordered list of operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`) addressed by JSON Pointer and applied atomically, so it handles arrays precisely and `test` gives an inline precondition check. It is more verbose and some operations, such as `add` to `/items/-`, are not idempotent. Default to Merge Patch for typical DTOs and choose JSON Patch when clients must edit collections precisely."}],"extra":["**Merge Patch limits:** RFC 7396 replaces arrays wholesale and cannot store a literal `null` value distinct from removal, so editing one line item in a large array needs JSON Patch or a dedicated sub-resource.","**Validation on partial input:** constraints like `@NotNull` that apply on create cannot be applied to a sparse patch body; apply the patch to the loaded state, then validate the merged result.","**Retry-safe PATCH:** a conditional PATCH with `If-Match` makes even a non-idempotent patch safe to retry, because a replay after success fails with `412` instead of applying twice.","**Discoverability:** advertise supported formats with the `Accept-Patch` header and reject unsupported patch documents with `415 Unsupported Media Type`, as RFC 5789 recommends.","**Client gotcha:** `HttpURLConnection` rejects the `PATCH` method, so a `RestTemplate` on the default `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory` fails; use a request factory backed by the JDK `HttpClient` or Apache HttpClient."],"redFlags":["Says PATCH is always non-idempotent","uses PUT as undocumented partial update"]},{"id":88,"category":"REST API Design","question":"How do you design an idempotent payment or order-creation API?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"The client sends an `Idempotency-Key`; the server claims it atomically under a unique constraint, stores a request fingerprint and the final response, replays that response on retries and rejects concurrent or mismatched reuse.","answer":["The client generates one key per logical operation (a UUID in the `Idempotency-Key` header) and reuses it on every retry of that operation. The server scopes the key by client or merchant and claims it atomically, for example with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` against a unique constraint in the same PostgreSQL database as the payment. Keys held only in memory are lost on restart and invisible to other replicas, and a check-then-insert race lets two instances both charge the card.","Next to the key, store a fingerprint of the request (SHA-256 over method, path and canonicalized body), a status such as `IN_PROGRESS` or `COMPLETED`, and, once finished, the response status and body. A retry with a completed key replays the stored response, including deterministic failures like a declined card. A retry whose fingerprint differs is rejected, because the client reused a key for a different request.","Concurrency is the subtle part: if a duplicate arrives while the first request is still in progress, return `409 Conflict` with `Retry-After` instead of executing again. Commit the business write and the key outcome in one local transaction where possible; when an external PSP is involved, pass a derived idempotency key downstream too, because your rollback cannot undo their charge. Expire keys after a documented window, often 24 hours, and run a recovery job that resolves keys stuck in progress by querying the PSP."],"keyPoints":["Client idempotency key, atomic uniqueness, request fingerprint, stored outcome and safe concurrent retry behavior"],"examples":[{"title":"Idempotency key table and atomic claim in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (\n    merchant_id    uuid        NOT NULL,\n    idem_key       text        NOT NULL,\n    request_hash   bytea       NOT NULL,\n    status         text        NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('IN_PROGRESS', 'COMPLETED')),\n    response_code  int,\n    response_body  jsonb,\n    payment_id     uuid,\n    created_at     timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (merchant_id, idem_key)\n);\n\n-- Claim: exactly one concurrent caller gets a row back\nINSERT INTO idempotency_keys (merchant_id, idem_key, request_hash, status)\nVALUES ($1, $2, $3, 'IN_PROGRESS')\nON CONFLICT (merchant_id, idem_key) DO NOTHING\nRETURNING idem_key;\n\n-- Everyone else inspects the existing record\nSELECT request_hash, status, response_code, response_body\nFROM idempotency_keys\nWHERE merchant_id = $1 AND idem_key = $2;","note":"The primary key does the deduplication, not application code, so it holds across replicas and restarts. Scoping by `merchant_id` stops one client's key from colliding with another's."},{"title":"Service flow: replay, mismatch and in-flight handling","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic ApiResponse createPayment(UUID merchantId, String key, CreatePaymentRequest req) {\n    byte[] hash = Fingerprint.sha256(req);\n    if (!keys.tryClaim(merchantId, key, hash)) {           // INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING\n        IdempotencyRecord existing = keys.find(merchantId, key);\n        if (!Arrays.equals(existing.requestHash(), hash)) {\n            return ApiResponse.problem(422, \"IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED\");\n        }\n        if (existing.status() == KeyStatus.IN_PROGRESS) {\n            return ApiResponse.problem(409, \"REQUEST_IN_PROGRESS\").retryAfterSeconds(1);\n        }\n        return ApiResponse.replay(existing.responseCode(), existing.responseBody());\n    }\n    Payment payment = payments.save(Payment.pending(merchantId, req));\n    ApiResponse response = ApiResponse.created(PaymentDto.from(payment));\n    keys.complete(merchantId, key, response.status(), response.bodyJson(), payment.id());\n    return response;                                        // key, payment and outcome commit together\n}","note":"Because claim and payment share one transaction here, a concurrent duplicate blocks on the unique index until the first commits and then sees `COMPLETED`. When a slow PSP call sits in the middle, commit the claim in its own short transaction first so duplicates see `IN_PROGRESS` and get `409` quickly."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if the same key has a different payload?","a":"Reject it without executing anything: compare the stored fingerprint with the new one and return `422 Unprocessable Content` (the IETF draft's choice; some APIs use `409` or `400`) with a stable code like `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`. Never silently replay the original response, because the client would believe a different request succeeded, and never execute the new payload under the old key. Compute the fingerprint over a canonical form (sorted keys, normalized numbers) and exclude volatile fields such as client timestamps, or legitimate retries will be flagged as mismatches."}],"extra":["**IETF draft:** `draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header` standardizes the `Idempotency-Key` header and suggests `400` for a missing required key, `409` for a request still in flight and `422` for a key reused with a different payload.","**What to store as final:** cache completed outcomes including deterministic `4xx` results, but do not freeze a transient `5xx` or timeout as the answer; mark the key retryable so the next attempt can actually succeed.","**Downstream keys:** derive the PSP idempotency key from your own (for example `merchantId:key:auth`) so a crash between the PSP call and your commit is recovered by safely repeating the PSP call instead of double charging.","**Natural idempotency as a backstop:** add a business uniqueness constraint such as unique `(merchant_id, order_reference)`, which protects you even when a client forgets the header.","**Operations:** purge expired keys with a batched job or time-partitioned table, and alert on keys stuck in `IN_PROGRESS` beyond a threshold, since they indicate crashed requests with unknown outcomes."],"redFlags":["Stores keys only in memory","no conflict behavior"]},{"id":89,"category":"REST API Design","question":"What should a consistent API error contract contain?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Return one documented error shape, ideally RFC 9457 Problem Details, with the correct HTTP status, a stable machine code, a safe message, a trace ID, field-level violations and explicit retry guidance.","answer":["Clients should branch on a stable machine code (`INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`, `ACCOUNT_FROZEN`), never on message text, so the code set is part of the contract and evolves like any other field. The human message must be safe: no stack traces, SQL, class names or internal hostnames, which leak implementation details and help attackers. Include a trace or correlation ID that matches your logs and distributed traces so support can find the failing request in seconds.","For validation failures, return a list of violations with the field path, a code and a message, so a UI can highlight every bad field in one round trip. Add retryability information: the status itself (`429`, `503`), a `Retry-After` header where relevant and optionally an explicit `retryable` flag, because otherwise clients guess and either retry permanent errors or give up on transient ones.","The standard shape is RFC 9457 Problem Details (`application/problem+json`, obsoleting RFC 7807) with `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance`, plus extension members for your code, trace ID and violations. Spring Boot 3 supports it natively with `ProblemDetail` and `ErrorResponseException`. Never return HTTP 200 with an error flag in the body: it breaks caching, retries, monitoring, alerting and every client library that relies on status codes."],"keyPoints":["Stable machine code, safe message, trace ID, field violations and retryability metadata"],"examples":[{"title":"A validation error in Problem Details format","lang":"http","code":"HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Content\nContent-Type: application/problem+json\n\n{\n  \"type\": \"https://api.example.com/problems/validation-failed\",\n  \"title\": \"Request validation failed\",\n  \"status\": 422,\n  \"detail\": \"2 fields are invalid\",\n  \"instance\": \"/payments\",\n  \"code\": \"VALIDATION_FAILED\",\n  \"traceId\": \"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\",\n  \"retryable\": false,\n  \"violations\": [\n    { \"field\": \"amount.value\", \"code\": \"MUST_BE_POSITIVE\", \"message\": \"must be greater than 0\" },\n    { \"field\": \"currency\", \"code\": \"UNSUPPORTED_CURRENCY\", \"message\": \"expected SEK, EUR or USD\" }\n  ]\n}","note":"Standard members give generic tooling something to work with; `code`, `traceId`, `retryable` and `violations` are extension members your clients actually branch on."},{"title":"Central exception handler producing safe ProblemDetail responses","lang":"java","code":"@RestControllerAdvice\nclass ApiExceptionHandler {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)\n    ProblemDetail insufficientFunds(InsufficientFundsException ex) {\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY,\n                \"Account balance is too low for this payment\");\n        pd.setType(URI.create(\"https://api.example.com/problems/insufficient-funds\"));\n        pd.setProperty(\"code\", \"INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"retryable\", false);\n        pd.setProperty(\"traceId\", MDC.get(\"traceId\"));\n        return pd;\n    }\n\n    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)\n    ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception ex) {\n        log.error(\"Unhandled error\", ex);                  // GOOD: stack trace goes to logs only\n        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,\n                \"An unexpected error occurred\");           // BAD would be: ex.getMessage()\n        pd.setProperty(\"code\", \"INTERNAL_ERROR\");\n        pd.setProperty(\"traceId\", MDC.get(\"traceId\"));\n        return pd;\n    }\n}","note":"With Micrometer Tracing in Spring Boot 3 the current `traceId` is in the MDC, so the value in the body matches your logs. The catch-all handler never echoes exception messages, which often contain SQL or internal identifiers."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do clients distinguish retryable errors?","a":"Primarily by status code: `408`, `429`, `502`, `503` and `504` are usually transient, while most other `4xx` codes are permanent and should not be retried without changing the request. `Retry-After` on `429` or `503` tells the client how long to wait, and an explicit `retryable` flag or a specific error code resolves ambiguous cases, such as a `409` meaning \"your idempotent request is still in progress\". The client must also know the operation is safe to repeat: a `500` on a non-idempotent `POST` is only safely retryable when the request carries an idempotency key."}],"extra":["**Framework errors too:** set `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` or extend `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` so errors like `MethodArgumentNotValidException` and `HttpMessageNotReadableException` use the same shape as your domain errors.","**Code catalog:** keep error codes in a documented enum or catalog in your OpenAPI spec; adding a code is additive, but renaming or removing one breaks clients that switch on it.","**Do not leak existence:** for resources the caller must not know about, such as another merchant's payment, return `404` rather than `403` so IDs cannot be enumerated.","**Localization:** keep `code` language-neutral and let clients localize, or honor `Accept-Language` for `title` and `detail`; never translate the machine code.","**Trace propagation:** use W3C `traceparent` end to end so the trace ID in the error body matches what the gateway, every service and your log platform already index."],"redFlags":["Returns stack traces or HTTP 200 for failures"]},{"id":90,"category":"REST API Design","question":"How do you choose status codes for validation, conflict and async work?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use 400 or 422 consistently for validation, 409 for state or concurrency conflicts, 202 plus a status URL for async work, 401 for missing or bad credentials and 403 for authenticated but forbidden.","answer":["Validation failures are client errors, never `500`: a `500` pages on-call, burns error budgets and tells clients to retry something that will never succeed. A common convention is `400` for malformed input (unparseable JSON, wrong types) and `422 Unprocessable Content` for well-formed requests that break semantic rules; some teams use `400` for both. Either is fine if it is consistent and documented, with detail carried by the error code.","`409 Conflict` means the request is valid but clashes with current state: a duplicate business key, an illegal transition such as refunding a cancelled order, or a concurrent modification. When the client sent `If-Match`, a stale version is better expressed as `412 Precondition Failed`, and `428 Precondition Required` can force clients to send one.","`202 Accepted` means the work was queued, not completed, and is no promise of success. Return a status resource the client can poll or subscribe to; use `201 Created` with `Location` only when the resource really exists at response time.","`401 Unauthorized` actually means unauthenticated: no token, an expired token or a bad signature, and it must carry `WWW-Authenticate`. `403 Forbidden` means the identity is known but lacks permission. Mixing them breaks clients, because a `401` should trigger a token refresh or login and a `403` should not."],"keyPoints":["Use 400/422 consistently for validation, 409 for conflicts, 202 for accepted async work, and distinguish 401 from 403"],"examples":[{"title":"Async credit application: 202 with a status resource","lang":"http","code":"POST /credit-applications HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/json\nIdempotency-Key: 7c9e6679-7425-40de-944b-e07fc1f90ae7\n\n{ \"customerId\": \"c-1042\", \"amount\": 50000, \"currency\": \"SEK\" }\n\nHTTP/1.1 202 Accepted\nLocation: /credit-applications/ca-88121/status\nRetry-After: 2\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{ \"applicationId\": \"ca-88121\", \"status\": \"PENDING\",\n  \"statusUrl\": \"/credit-applications/ca-88121/status\" }\n\nGET /credit-applications/ca-88121/status HTTP/1.1\n\nHTTP/1.1 303 See Other\nLocation: /credit-applications/ca-88121","note":"While scoring runs, the status endpoint returns `200` with `PENDING`; once decided it redirects with `303` to the final resource (returning `200` with a result link is an equally valid style)."},{"title":"Scenario to status code cheat sheet","lang":"text","code":"Scenario                                        Status\nBody is not valid JSON, wrong field type        400 Bad Request\namount <= 0, unsupported currency               422 (or 400, pick one and stay consistent)\nMissing or expired bearer token                 401 + WWW-Authenticate\nValid token, lacks scope payments:write         403 Forbidden\nCancel requested for an already SHIPPED order   409 Conflict\nIf-Match ETag is stale                          412 Precondition Failed\nSame Idempotency-Key still in progress          409 Conflict + Retry-After\nClient exceeded its rate limit                  429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After\nAccepted for async scoring                      202 Accepted + status URL\nCreated synchronously                           201 Created + Location\nService overloaded or in maintenance            503 Service Unavailable + Retry-After","note":"Consistency matters more than the exact choice between 400 and 422; document the table and have every service follow it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What should a 202 response include?","a":"It should give the client everything needed to track the outcome: a status resource URL (in `Location` and in the body), an operation or application ID, the current state such as `PENDING`, and a `Retry-After` hint for polling frequency. The status endpoint returns state and timestamps, and on completion either links to the created resource (or answers `303 See Other`) or returns Problem Details describing the failure. If you support webhooks or push notifications, state which events will be sent. Accept an idempotency key on the original request so a resubmission does not enqueue a duplicate job."}],"extra":["**422 is core HTTP now:** RFC 9110 moved 422 from WebDAV into the core spec as \"Unprocessable Content\", removing the old objection that it was WebDAV-only.","**Spring Security mapping:** the `AuthenticationEntryPoint` produces the `401` (the resource server's `BearerTokenAuthenticationEntryPoint` adds `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer`) and the `AccessDeniedHandler` produces the `403`; customize both so they emit your Problem Details body.","**404 to hide existence:** for another tenant's resource, returning `404` instead of `403` prevents ID enumeration; make that a documented policy, not an accident.","**429 versus 503:** `429` says the caller exceeded its quota, `503` says the server lacks capacity; clients, dashboards and alerts should treat them differently.","**Not 204 for async:** `204 No Content` means done with nothing to return; if work is still running, `202` is the honest code."],"redFlags":["Uses 500 for domain validation","confuses 401 and 403"]},{"id":91,"category":"REST API Design","question":"When should an API introduce a new version?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"Evolve additively without a version bump while old clients keep working; introduce a new version only for breaking contract or semantic changes, and ship it with deprecation headers, a migration guide and per-client usage tracking.","answer":["Most changes should not need a new version. New optional request fields, new response fields, new endpoints and new optional query parameters are backward compatible, provided clients follow the tolerant reader rule and ignore unknown fields. Versioning every field addition multiplies the code paths you must test, secure and run for years.","A new version is justified when a change breaks existing clients: removing or renaming a field, changing a type or format (amount from integer minor units to decimal string), making an optional field required, tightening validation, changing error codes or changing pagination style. Semantic changes are the most dangerous because the schema stays identical: if `balance` silently switches from booked to available, or `status=ACTIVE` starts including suspended accounts, clients compute wrong numbers with no error at all. Those need a new field or a new version, never an in-place change.","When you do version, pick one scheme and apply it everywhere. A URI prefix like `/v2` is the easiest to route, cache and see in logs; media type or header versioning is purer but harder to debug. Run both versions in parallel over one domain model with version-specific mapping at the edge, announce retirement with `Deprecation` and `Sunset` headers, publish a migration guide, measure traffic per version and per client, and contact the remaining callers before removal."],"keyPoints":["Prefer additive backward-compatible changes","version breaking contract or semantic changes","provide deprecation and migration"],"examples":[{"title":"Signaling deprecation on the old version","lang":"http","code":"GET /v1/accounts/acc-123 HTTP/1.1\nAccept: application/json\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\nDeprecation: @1767225600\nSunset: Wed, 31 Mar 2027 23:59:59 GMT\nLink: <https://developer.example.com/migrate/accounts-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"; type=\"text/html\"\n\n{ \"id\": \"acc-123\", \"balance\": 125000, \"currency\": \"SEK\" }","note":"`Deprecation` (RFC 9745, a Unix timestamp here meaning 1 Jan 2026) says since when the endpoint is deprecated, `Sunset` (RFC 8594) says when it stops working, and the `Link` points to the migration guide."},{"title":"Which changes need a new version","lang":"text","code":"Change                                                   Breaking?  Action\nAdd optional response field \"riskScore\"                  No         ship in v1\nAdd optional query parameter includeClosed=true          No         ship in v1\nAdd endpoint GET /accounts/{id}/limits                   No         ship in v1\nAdd value DORMANT to response enum AccountStatus         Depends    only if documented as open enum\nMake request field \"purpose\" required                    Yes        v2, or default it server side\nRename \"balance\" to \"availableBalance\"                   Yes        add new field, deprecate old\nAmount changes from minor units to decimal string        Yes        v2\n\"balance\" changes from booked to available (same type)   Yes        new field; never reuse the name\nTighten validation: max length 100 becomes 50            Yes        v2 or grandfather old clients","note":"Expand and contract (add the new field, migrate consumers, remove the old one later) resolves many breaking changes without a whole new API version."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Is changing an enum breaking?","a":"Removing or renaming a value is always breaking. Adding a value to a request enum is safe, but adding one to a response enum breaks any client that deserializes into a closed Java `enum`, because Jackson fails on the unknown constant by default, and clients with exhaustive `switch` logic may mishandle it. Document response enums as open, so clients must handle unknown values, for example with `@JsonEnumDefaultValue` plus `READ_UNKNOWN_ENUM_VALUES_USING_DEFAULT_VALUE` mapping to an `UNKNOWN` constant. Announce new values before you start emitting them."}],"extra":["**Tolerant reader in Java:** Spring Boot's auto-configured `ObjectMapper` disables `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`, but a plain `new ObjectMapper()` does not, which is a classic way internal clients break on a purely additive change.","**Catch breaks in CI:** run consumer-driven contract tests (Pact or Spring Cloud Contract) and an OpenAPI diff tool such as `oasdiff` in the pipeline so breaking changes fail the build instead of production.","**Events are APIs too:** Kafka message schemas need the same discipline; a schema registry with backward or full compatibility mode blocks producers from publishing breaking Avro or Protobuf changes.","**Internal versus public:** with a handful of known internal consumers you can coordinate a migration in weeks; public APIs need deprecation windows of many months and must assume some clients never migrate."],"redFlags":["Versions every field addition or silently changes meaning"]},{"id":92,"category":"REST API Design","question":"How do you design filtering, sorting and pagination safely?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Allowlist filter fields, operators and sort keys, cap page size, add a unique tiebreaker to every ordering, and paginate with opaque keyset cursors instead of deep offsets or a raw query language.","answer":["Treat query parameters as untrusted input mapped onto an explicit allowlist: filter by `status`, `createdFrom`, `createdTo`, sort by `createdAt` or `amount`, each mapped to a known column with a supporting index. Rejecting anything else with `400` prevents SQL injection through `ORDER BY` (column names cannot be bound as parameters), stops clients from filtering on unindexed or sensitive columns, and keeps your schema private so you can refactor it. Exposing raw SQL, JPQL or an unrestricted query language turns every client into a potential full table scan.","Enforce a default and a maximum page size (for example 50 and 200) and clamp or reject larger values; an unbounded `limit=100000` is a denial of service against your own database and heap. Ordering must be total and stable: sort by the requested key plus a unique tiebreaker such as `id`, otherwise rows with equal timestamps shuffle between pages, producing duplicates and gaps.","Prefer keyset pagination with an opaque cursor encoding the last row's sort values and the filter it belongs to. `WHERE (created_at, id) < (?, ?)` with a matching index costs the same on page 1 and page 10,000, while `OFFSET` reads and discards every skipped row and drifts when data changes. Fetch one extra row to compute `hasNext`, and avoid exact totals on large tables."],"keyPoints":["Whitelist fields/operators, enforce limits, use stable ordering and opaque cursors","avoid exposing raw query languages"],"examples":[{"title":"Offset versus keyset pagination in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- BAD: reads and discards 200000 rows; ties on created_at shuffle between pages\nSELECT id, amount, status, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\nORDER BY created_at DESC\nOFFSET 200000 LIMIT 50;\n\n-- GOOD: keyset pagination with a unique tiebreaker, served by one index\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_created_id ON orders (customer_id, created_at, id);\n\nSELECT id, amount, status, created_at\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = $1\n  AND (created_at, id) < ($2, $3)        -- last row of previous page, decoded from cursor\nORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 51;                                 -- 51st row only tells us hasNext","note":"The row comparison `(created_at, id) < ($2, $3)` matches the index order, so PostgreSQL scans the index backward from the cursor position regardless of page depth."},{"title":"Allowlisted sort keys and clamped page size","lang":"java","code":"public record OrderQuery(OrderStatus status, SortKey sort, int limit, Cursor after) {\n\n    private static final int DEFAULT_LIMIT = 50;\n    private static final int MAX_LIMIT = 200;\n\n    public enum SortKey {\n        CREATED_AT(\"createdAt\", \"created_at\"), AMOUNT(\"amount\", \"amount\");\n\n        final String apiName;\n        final String column;                      // hard coded, never taken from the request\n\n        SortKey(String apiName, String column) { this.apiName = apiName; this.column = column; }\n\n        static SortKey parse(String value) {\n            return Arrays.stream(values()).filter(k -> k.apiName.equals(value)).findFirst()\n                    .orElseThrow(() -> new BadRequestException(\"UNSUPPORTED_SORT\", value));\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static OrderQuery from(String status, String sort, Integer limit, String cursor,\n                                  CursorCodec codec) {\n        SortKey key = sort == null ? SortKey.CREATED_AT : SortKey.parse(sort);\n        int size = limit == null ? DEFAULT_LIMIT : Math.clamp(limit, 1, MAX_LIMIT);\n        OrderStatus st = status == null ? null : OrderStatus.parse(status);\n        Cursor after = cursor == null ? null : codec.decodeAndVerify(cursor, key, st); // HMAC plus filter binding\n        return new OrderQuery(st, key, size, after);\n    }\n}","note":"`Math.clamp` is new in Java 21. The cursor is verified against the sort and filter it was issued for, so a client cannot tamper with it or replay it with different filters."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you prevent deep pagination?","a":"Make depth structurally cheap by using keyset cursors, so cost no longer grows with the page number, and do not offer arbitrary page jumps on large collections. If you must keep offsets, cap them (Elasticsearch, for example, limits `from + size` to 10,000 by default via `index.max_result_window`) and steer bulk consumers to an export job, a streaming endpoint or a CDC feed. Rate limit per client and alert on cursor chains that walk entire tables, which usually indicate a scraper or an integration that should use a bulk API."}],"extra":["**Spring Data defaults:** `spring.data.web.pageable.max-page-size` defaults to 2000, far too high for most public APIs, and a `Pageable` sort parameter accepts any mapped property path, including unindexed or sensitive ones, unless you validate it.","**Cursor hygiene:** sign cursors with an HMAC, bind them to the filter and sort, and version the format so you can change the encoding later without breaking clients holding old cursors.","**Counting is expensive:** under MVCC, `COUNT(*)` on a large filtered PostgreSQL table scans rows; return `hasNext`, or an approximate total from planner statistics when a UI truly needs one.","**Complexity budget:** if filters can be combined, cap the number of conditions and the size of `IN` lists, and set a `statement_timeout` for the API's database role so a pathological query dies instead of saturating the database."],"redFlags":["Accepts arbitrary fields or page size","unstable ordering"]},{"id":93,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"When are microservices a bad choice?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Microservices are a bad fit when teams are few, boundaries are still unclear, scale is modest or automation and observability are weak; a modular monolith delivers most benefits without distributed failure modes.","answer":["Microservices are primarily an organizational scaling tool: they let many teams deploy independently. With one or two teams you pay for network calls, versioned contracts, distributed tracing, per-service pipelines and on-call rotations without the autonomy payoff. They are also a poor choice while boundaries are unclear, as in a new product still discovering its domain: a wrong boundary inside a monolith is a refactoring, across services it is a coordinated migration of APIs, data and deployments.","Every in-process call turned into a network call gains latency, partial failure, timeouts, retries and the need for idempotency, and every shared transaction becomes a saga with eventual consistency. Without mature CI/CD, centralized logging, tracing and alerting you end up with a distributed monolith: services that must deploy together and fail together. Low scale is another signal: a horizontally scaled monolith behind a load balancer handles far more traffic than most teams expect, and independent scaling only pays off when components have very different resource profiles.","The default I argue for is a modular monolith: one deployable with enforced module boundaries (Spring Modulith or ArchUnit rules), module-owned tables and in-process events. Refactoring stays cheap, and when a real signal appears you extract a module whose boundary is already proven."],"keyPoints":["Small teams, unclear boundaries, low scale and weak automation favor a modular monolith","microservices add distributed failure and operational cost"],"examples":[{"title":"Modular monolith with verified boundaries (Spring Modulith)","lang":"java","code":"// One deployable: com.acme.shop.orders, com.acme.shop.payments, com.acme.shop.inventory\n@SpringBootApplication\npublic class ShopApplication {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SpringApplication.run(ShopApplication.class, args);\n    }\n}\n\nclass ModularityTests {\n    @Test\n    void modulesRespectBoundaries() {\n        // Fails the build on cycles or when orders touches payments internals\n        ApplicationModules.of(ShopApplication.class).verify();\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final OrderRepository orders;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n\n    OrderService(OrderRepository orders, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.orders = orders;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void place(Order order) {\n        orders.save(order);\n        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.id(), order.total())); // no direct call into payments\n    }\n}","note":"Modules talk through events and published interfaces, exactly as services would, but a boundary mistake is still a refactoring inside one repository and one deployment."},{"title":"Decision signals","lang":"text","code":"Signal                               Modular monolith        Microservices\nTeams working on the system          1 to 3                  many, blocking each other\nDomain boundaries                    still moving            stable, proven in code\nScale profile                        uniform, moderate       parts differ by an order of magnitude\nDeployment automation, tracing       partial                 mature, self-service platform\nConsistency needs                    local ACID is valuable  eventual consistency acceptable\nRelease conflicts                    rare                    frequent release train blocking","note":"If most answers fall in the left column, microservices add cost and failure modes without solving a problem you actually have."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What signals justify extracting a service?","a":"Organizational signals come first: several teams blocked on one release train, or one team's changes repeatedly breaking another's area. Technical signals include a component with a very different scaling or resource profile (a CPU-heavy scoring engine beside a CRUD API), a stricter availability or compliance requirement (isolating card data shrinks PCI scope), a different release cadence, or a technology the monolith cannot host. Extract only when the module boundary is already clean and stable inside the monolith, and measure the result afterward: lead time, deployment frequency and incident rate should improve."}],"extra":["**Conway's Law:** service boundaries end up mirroring team communication; splitting a system owned by one team into ten services adds coordination overhead with no organizational benefit.","**Distributed monolith smell:** if features routinely require synchronized releases of several services, or services share database tables, you carry the costs of both architectures and the benefits of neither.","**Multiplied chores:** every service needs its own pipeline, dashboards, alerts, JDK and Spring Boot upgrades, CVE patching, secrets and runbooks; across dozens of services that is a platform team's worth of work.","**Public reversal:** the Amazon Prime Video monitoring team described moving a distributed serverless pipeline back into a single process and reported a large cost reduction, a good reminder that granularity depends on the workload.","**Extraction readiness:** a module with its own tables, communication only via events or a published interface and no cyclic dependencies can be extracted quickly; enforcing that inside the monolith is cheap insurance."],"redFlags":["Says microservices are always more scalable or modern"]},{"id":94,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"How do you define good service boundaries?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Draw boundaries around business capabilities or bounded contexts that own their data and invariants, change together, belong to one team, and can serve most requests without synchronous calls to other services.","answer":["Start from the domain, not the technology: capabilities such as Payments, Credit Decisioning, Order Fulfillment or Customer Onboarding, refined with Domain-Driven Design bounded contexts in which a term like \"account\" has one consistent meaning. Splitting by technical layer (a UI service, a logic service, a data-access service) or by table (a CustomerService wrapping the customer table) guarantees every feature touches every service and couples them through chatty CRUD calls.","A good boundary encloses cohesive data and invariants: the data needed to enforce \"the credit limit may not be exceeded\" lives in one service and one local transaction. It matches change patterns, so things that change together for the same business reason stay together, which you can verify from version control history. And it has exactly one owning team that can design, deploy and operate it without asking permission.","Finally, a boundary should minimize synchronous chatter. If one request needs three serial calls to other services, you split a cohesive unit, and your availability becomes the product of theirs. Prefer boundaries where collaboration is asynchronous (events like `OrderPlaced`) and where a service keeps a local copy of the reference data it needs. Event Storming is a practical way to find the seams, because domain events and aggregates cluster naturally into contexts."],"keyPoints":["Align with business capability/bounded context, cohesive data and change patterns, clear ownership and minimal synchronous chatter"],"examples":[{"title":"Layer or table split versus capability split","lang":"text","code":"BAD: split by technical layer or by table\n  order-api        controllers only\n  order-logic      rules; calls order-data and customer-data on every request\n  order-data       CRUD over the orders table\n  customer-data    CRUD over the customers table\n  result: every feature touches 4 services; one schema change breaks 3 deployments\n\nGOOD: split by business capability, each owning data and invariants\n  ordering         owns orders, order_lines; enforces \"cannot ship an unpaid order\"\n                   publishes OrderPlaced, OrderCancelled\n  payments         owns payments, refunds; integrates the PSP; publishes PaymentCaptured\n  credit-decision  owns applications, scores; consumes CustomerRiskProfileChanged\n                   and keeps a local projection of the risk data it needs\n  result: a typical change touches one service and one team","note":"The good split follows reasons for change and ownership of invariants, not the shape of the database."},{"title":"Chatty runtime coupling versus a local projection","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: the boundary cut a cohesive unit; every decision needs 3 blocking calls\npublic Decision decideChatty(CreditApplication app) {\n    Customer customer = customerClient.get(app.customerId());      // network hop 1\n    List<Loan> loans = loanClient.activeLoans(app.customerId());   // network hop 2\n    Income income = incomeClient.latest(app.customerId());         // network hop 3\n    return policy.evaluate(app, customer, loans, income);\n}\n\n// GOOD: credit-decision owns a read model fed by events from other contexts\n@KafkaListener(topics = \"customer-risk-events\", groupId = \"credit-decision\")\nvoid onRiskProfileChanged(CustomerRiskProfileChanged event) {\n    riskProfiles.upsertIfNewer(event.customerId(), event.riskClass(), event.version());\n}\n\npublic Decision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n    RiskProfile profile = riskProfiles.find(app.customerId())\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProfileNotYetAvailableException(app.customerId()));\n    return policy.evaluate(app, profile);                          // one local query\n}","note":"The good version survives the customer service being down and removes three network hops from the latency path, at the cost of eventual consistency on risk data."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What metrics reveal a bad boundary?","a":"High co-change: a large share of features or commits requiring coordinated changes and releases across several services. Chatty runtime coupling in traces: high fan-out and call depth per user request, many synchronous calls per request, and latency or error budgets dominated by downstream calls. Data smells: services reading each other's tables, large datasets duplicated just to avoid calls, or frequent sagas and compensations for what is logically one transaction. Organizational signals such as many cross-team pull requests or tickets waiting on another team confirm the diagnosis."}],"extra":["**Aggregates are the minimum unit:** never split one aggregate across services, because its invariants need a single transactional boundary; a bounded context usually contains several aggregates.","**Cognitive load:** Team Topologies sizes a service so one stream-aligned team can fully understand and operate it; a boundary that needs two teams to change it is in the wrong place.","**Context mapping:** make relationships explicit (customer and supplier, conformist, anticorruption layer); an anticorruption layer in front of a legacy core system keeps its model from leaking into your new boundary.","**Start coarse:** splitting a coarse service later is easier than merging fine-grained ones, because a merge means reconciling two data models, two deployments and two ownership lines."],"redFlags":["Splits by technical layer or database table"]},{"id":95,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Synchronous REST vs asynchronous messaging: how do you choose?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use synchronous REST or gRPC when the caller needs the answer to proceed; use asynchronous messaging for decoupled reactions, load buffering and long-running workflows, deciding by latency, consistency, ordering and failure requirements.","answer":["Synchronous calls fit queries and commands where the caller cannot continue without the result: authorizing a card, fetching a price, validating an address. They are simple to reason about and debug and give immediate errors, but they couple availability and latency: if Payments is down or slow, Checkout is too, and a chain of calls multiplies tail latency.","Asynchronous messaging (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS) fits when the producer does not need the consumer's answer now: sending notifications, updating read models, starting fulfillment, or absorbing spikes by letting consumers work at their own pace. It enables long-running workflows and sagas, but you accept eventual consistency, duplicate delivery, limited ordering (Kafka orders only within a partition, so key by `orderId`), dead letter handling and harder debugging.","Async does not remove coupling, it changes its form. Temporal coupling goes away, but consumers still depend on the event's schema and meaning, and a producer changing `OrderPlaced` can silently break five teams. The deciding questions: does the user wait for this result, can the business tolerate seconds of staleness, what happens if the other side is down for an hour, and does ordering matter? Most real systems mix both, for example a synchronous API that accepts a command and returns `202`, backed by events internally."],"keyPoints":["Sync for immediate response and simple coupling","async for decoupling, buffering and workflows","consider latency, consistency, ordering and failure"],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing per interaction","lang":"text","code":"Use case                                    Style   Why\nAuthorize card at checkout                  sync    user waits; result decides next step\nShow current account balance                sync    read that must be current\nSend order confirmation email               async   caller does not need the result; retry later\nUpdate search index or reporting model      async   eventual consistency is acceptable\nCredit decision calling a bureau (30 s)     async   long running: 202 plus status or notification\nReserve stock, charge, ship (saga)          async   multi-step workflow with compensation\n50k payouts at month end                    async   queue absorbs the burst, consumers scale out","note":"The style is chosen per interaction, not per system; one service typically exposes sync APIs and also publishes and consumes events."},{"title":"Sync call on the critical path, async event for everything else","lang":"java","code":"@Transactional\npublic OrderId checkout(Cart cart) {\n    // sync: we cannot confirm the order without the authorization result\n    AuthorizationResult auth = paymentsClient.post()\n            .uri(\"/authorizations\")\n            .body(new AuthorizeRequest(cart.total(), cart.cardToken(), cart.idempotencyKey()))\n            .retrieve()\n            .body(AuthorizationResult.class);\n    if (!auth.approved()) {\n        throw new PaymentDeclinedException(auth.declineCode());\n    }\n    Order order = orders.save(Order.placed(cart, auth.authorizationId()));\n\n    // async: fulfillment, email and analytics react on their own schedule.\n    // Keyed by order id so all events of one order stay ordered in one partition.\n    // Stored in the outbox table in this same transaction, published by a relay.\n    outbox.append(\"order-events\", order.id().toString(),\n            new OrderPlaced(order.id(), cart.customerId(), cart.total()));\n    return order.id();\n}","note":"Only the authorization is on the synchronous path; downstream reactions are decoupled, so an email outage cannot fail a checkout."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How does a user learn the final result of an async command?","a":"The command endpoint returns `202 Accepted` with an operation ID and a status URL. The client then polls that URL (guided by `Retry-After`), subscribes to a push channel such as WebSocket or Server-Sent Events, or, for server-to-server integrations, receives a webhook. Behind it, a status read model is updated by consuming the workflow's outcome events (`CreditApproved`, `CreditRejected`). Human users often also get an email or push notification, and the UI shows a pending state instead of blocking; failures should be reported in the same Problem Details format as synchronous errors."}],"extra":["**Availability math:** a request that synchronously depends on five services at 99.9% each is at best about 99.5% available; moving work off the critical path removes it from that product.","**Request and reply over a broker:** possible with reply topics and correlation IDs, but it usually combines sync-style waiting with async complexity; reserve it for long-running or bursty work.","**Backpressure signals:** sync calls push back instantly through timeouts and `429`; with messaging, consumer lag or queue depth is the primary health metric, because a growing backlog is invisible to producers.","**Ordering versus parallelism:** Kafka keys give per-key ordering, but a hot key serializes onto one consumer; competing consumers on a RabbitMQ queue lose ordering unless you use the single active consumer feature.","**Event contracts:** treat event schemas as public APIs with a schema registry and compatibility checks, and include an event ID and version so consumers can deduplicate and evolve."],"redFlags":["Uses async for everything or assumes it removes coupling"]},{"id":96,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Explain Saga and compensation.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A saga replaces a distributed transaction with local transactions coordinated by an orchestrator or by events; failures are undone by compensating business actions, which can fail and be retried, not by a rollback.","answer":["With database per service, a process like \"place order\" becomes a sequence of local transactions: create the order as `PENDING`, reserve stock, authorize payment, confirm the order. If payment fails, the saga runs compensations for completed steps in reverse: release the stock, cancel the order. You get eventual atomicity but no isolation, since other transactions can see intermediate states, so you design for them with explicit states and semantic locks. It is not a distributed ACID transaction.","Coordination has two styles. In orchestration, a central orchestrator (a persisted state machine in the order service, or a workflow engine such as Temporal or Camunda) sends commands and tracks progress, which makes the flow visible and easy to change. In choreography, each service reacts to events and emits its own; it is loosely coupled but hard to follow beyond three or four steps.","Compensation is a business action, not a rollback. You cannot un-send an email or un-capture money; you issue a cancellation notice or a refund, which may carry fees and side effects. Compensations can fail too, so they must be idempotent, retried with backoff, and escalated to a manual queue when retries run out. Place irreversible steps (capture, shipping) last, after all compensatable steps succeed, and persist saga state so a crashed orchestrator resumes where it stopped."],"keyPoints":["A saga coordinates local transactions by orchestration or choreography","compensation is a business action, not a DB rollback, and may fail/retry"],"examples":[{"title":"Order saga steps and their compensations","lang":"text","code":"Step  Service     Local transaction              Compensation\n1     ordering    create order PENDING           cancel order (CANCELLED + notify)\n2     inventory   reserve 2 x SKU-881            release reservation\n3     payments    authorize 499 SEK              void the authorization\n4     payments    capture 499 SEK   (pivot)      refund: new transaction, may incur a fee\n5     shipping    create shipment   (retriable)  none: retried until it succeeds","note":"Steps before the pivot are compensatable, the pivot is the point of no return, and steps after it must be retriable because they can no longer be undone cheaply."},{"title":"Orchestrator reacting to replies and failed compensations","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass PlaceOrderSaga {\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentAuthorizationFailed event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        saga.startCompensation(event.reason());\n        // Compensate completed steps in reverse order; every command is idempotent\n        outbox.send(new ReleaseStock(saga.orderId(), saga.stepKey(\"release-stock\")));\n        outbox.send(new CancelOrder(saga.orderId(), \"PAYMENT_DECLINED\"));\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentAuthorized event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        saga.markPaymentAuthorized(event.authorizationId());\n        // Pivot: capture only after every compensatable step has succeeded\n        outbox.send(new CapturePayment(saga.orderId(), saga.stepKey(\"capture\")));\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(CompensationFailed event) {\n        SagaState saga = sagas.load(event.orderId());\n        if (saga.incrementCompensationAttempts() > 5) {\n            saga.markRequiresManualIntervention();          // alert operations, never drop silently\n        } else {\n            outbox.sendDelayed(event.command(), Duration.ofMinutes(1));\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Saga state changes and outgoing commands commit together through the outbox, and step-specific keys make each command safe to redeliver."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Orchestration vs choreography?","a":"Orchestration uses a central coordinator that issues commands and holds saga state, so the flow is explicit, testable and easy to monitor, at the cost of one component knowing every participant. Choreography lets services react to each other's events without a coordinator, which suits simple two or three step flows, but the process becomes implicit, cyclic event dependencies creep in, and answering \"where is order 123 stuck?\" means correlating events across services. A common rule: choreography for simple, stable reactions such as sending an email on `OrderPlaced`, orchestration for business-critical multi-step workflows with compensations."},{"q":"How do you compensate a payment?","a":"It depends on how far the payment got. An authorization that was not captured is voided, which releases the hold on the card without moving money, while a captured payment needs a refund: a new financial transaction with its own ID, possible fees and settlement delay. Both calls carry an idempotency key derived from the saga and step so a retry cannot refund twice. If the original call timed out, query the PSP first, because compensating a payment that never happened, or missing one that did, corrupts the ledger. Record the compensation in your own ledger and reconcile against PSP settlement reports."}],"extra":["**Step classification:** Chris Richardson's model of compensatable, pivot and retriable transactions gives a practical ordering rule that minimizes irreversible compensation.","**Isolation countermeasures:** because sagas lack isolation, use semantic locks (`PENDING` states), commutative updates, rereading values before acting and version checks to handle dirty reads and lost updates between concurrent sagas.","**Reliable messaging:** each step must update its state and emit its message atomically, which is why sagas are almost always paired with the Transactional Outbox and idempotent consumers.","**Timeouts are outcomes:** a participant that never replies must time out; an unanswered payment call is \"unknown\", so query its status before deciding whether to compensate.","**Workflow engines:** Temporal, Camunda or AWS Step Functions persist state, timers and retries for you; a hand-rolled orchestrator needs the same durability, step timeouts and observability."],"redFlags":["Calls saga a distributed ACID transaction"]},{"id":97,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"What dual-write problem does Transactional Outbox solve?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A database write plus a broker publish can fail independently; the outbox stores the event in the same local transaction as the state change, and a relay publishes it later, at least once.","answer":["The dual-write problem: a service saves an order, then calls `kafkaTemplate.send(...)`. If the process crashes between the two, the broker is down, or the transaction rolls back after the send, you get an order without an event or an event without an order. Sending inside `@Transactional` does not help because the broker is not part of the database transaction, and XA two-phase commit is rarely available or worth its cost.","With a Transactional Outbox the service inserts the business row and an `outbox` row describing the event in one local ACID transaction, so both exist or neither does. A separate relay publishes outbox rows: either a poller selecting unpublished rows (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets instances share the work) and marking them sent, or change data capture such as Debezium reading the PostgreSQL WAL, which avoids polling load and follows commit order.","The guarantee is at-least-once, never exactly-once. The relay can publish and then crash before marking the row, so the event goes out again. Consumers must therefore be idempotent: record processed event IDs in their own database in the same transaction as their side effects, or make updates naturally idempotent with version checks. Publish with the aggregate ID as the message key to keep per-aggregate ordering, and operate the table: purge published rows and alert on the age of the oldest unpublished one."],"keyPoints":["Business state and outbox event are written in one DB transaction","poller/CDC publishes later","consumers must handle duplicates"],"examples":[{"title":"Dual write versus outbox","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: two systems, no atomicity\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrderUnsafe(Order order) {\n    orders.save(order);\n    kafka.send(\"order-events\", order.id().toString(), new OrderPlaced(order.id()));\n    // send may succeed and the commit fail afterward, or the process dies before send\n}\n\n// GOOD: the event is part of the same local transaction as the state change\n@Transactional\npublic void placeOrder(Order order) {\n    orders.save(order);\n    outbox.save(new OutboxEvent(\n            UUID.randomUUID(),                  // event id, consumers deduplicate on it\n            \"Order\",\n            order.id().toString(),              // becomes the Kafka key: per-order ordering\n            \"OrderPlaced\",\n            payloadOf(new OrderPlaced(order.id())),\n            Instant.now()));\n}","note":"The good version never talks to Kafka inside the business transaction; publishing is a separate, retryable concern handled by the relay."},{"title":"Outbox table and polling relay in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE outbox (\n    id             uuid        PRIMARY KEY,\n    aggregate_type text        NOT NULL,\n    aggregate_id   text        NOT NULL,\n    event_type     text        NOT NULL,\n    payload        jsonb       NOT NULL,\n    created_at     timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    published_at   timestamptz\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_outbox_pending ON outbox (created_at) WHERE published_at IS NULL;\n\n-- Relay loop: claim a batch without blocking other relay instances\nBEGIN;\nSELECT id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload\nFROM outbox\nWHERE published_at IS NULL\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 100\nFOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;\n-- publish each row to Kafka keyed by aggregate_id and wait for acks, then:\nUPDATE outbox SET published_at = now() WHERE id = ANY($1);\nCOMMIT;","note":"`SKIP LOCKED` trades strict ordering for throughput: with several relays, two events of one aggregate can be published out of order, so use a single active relay or CDC when per-aggregate order matters."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if the publisher crashes after send but before marking sent?","a":"The row is still unpublished, so after a restart, or on another relay instance, it is published again and consumers receive a duplicate. That is expected: the outbox guarantees at-least-once delivery, so every event carries a stable ID (the outbox row `id`) and consumers deduplicate on it, typically by inserting it into a processed-events table with a unique constraint in the same transaction as their side effect. CDC behaves the same way: after a crash the connector resumes from its last committed offset and may re-emit some events."}],"extra":["**Debezium outbox router:** Debezium's outbox event router routes outbox rows to topics by aggregate type and uses the aggregate ID as the key; with CDC you can even delete the row in the same transaction, because the WAL still carries the insert.","**Spring Modulith:** its event publication registry persists application events in the business transaction, can republish incomplete ones after a restart and can externalize them to Kafka, an outbox you do not have to hand-write.","**Idempotent producer is not end to end:** Kafka's `enable.idempotence=true` removes duplicates from producer retries within a session, not duplicates from a relay republishing a row after a crash, so consumer deduplication is still required.","**The inbox:** the consumer-side mirror stores incoming event IDs in a table with a unique constraint, inserted in the same transaction as the business update, so a redelivered event becomes a no-op."],"redFlags":["Writes DB then broker directly and calls it reliable","claims exactly-once"]},{"id":98,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"How should retries, timeouts and circuit breakers work together?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Every attempt gets a timeout, only transient failures of idempotent calls are retried with capped exponential backoff and jitter inside a total deadline, and a circuit breaker stops calls to a clearly failing dependency.","answer":["Timeouts come first: without one, a slow dependency holds your threads and connections until you run out. Set connect and read timeouts per dependency from its latency profile (p99 plus margin), not library defaults, which are often infinite. The timeout bounds a single attempt.","Retry only transient failures (connection errors, `503`, `429` honoring `Retry-After`, timeouts) and only operations that are safe to repeat: reads, or writes carrying an idempotency key. Never retry validation errors or business rejections. Use exponential backoff with jitter so thousands of clients do not retry in lockstep, and cap attempts at two or three.","A circuit breaker tracks failure and slow-call rates; above a threshold it opens and fails fast for a wait period, then half-opens to let a few trial calls through. That protects the struggling dependency, frees your resources and gives you a place for a fallback, such as a cached value or a `PENDING` status.","They share one budget: the caller has a total deadline, and attempts times timeout plus backoff must fit inside it, with the remaining time propagated downstream. Retry in exactly one layer, because gateway, service and client retries multiply. In Resilience4j's default aspect order, retry wraps the circuit breaker, so every attempt is recorded by the breaker and an open breaker stops further attempts."],"keyPoints":["Timeout bounds attempts","retry only transient/idempotent work with backoff/jitter","circuit breaker stops repeated calls","control total deadline"],"examples":[{"title":"Resilience4j retry and circuit breaker for a bank API","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass BankApiResilience {\n\n    @Bean\n    Retry bankApiRetry() {\n        return Retry.of(\"bankApi\", RetryConfig.custom()\n                .maxAttempts(3)                                    // total, including the first\n                .intervalFunction(IntervalFunction.ofExponentialRandomBackoff(\n                        Duration.ofMillis(100), 2.0, 0.5))         // 100 ms, 200 ms, +/-50% jitter\n                .retryOnException(BankApiResilience::isTransient)\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    CircuitBreaker bankApiBreaker() {\n        return CircuitBreaker.of(\"bankApi\", CircuitBreakerConfig.custom()\n                .slidingWindowSize(50)\n                .minimumNumberOfCalls(20)\n                .failureRateThreshold(50)\n                .slowCallDurationThreshold(Duration.ofMillis(400))\n                .slowCallRateThreshold(60)\n                .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(30))\n                .ignoreExceptions(PaymentDeclinedException.class)   // business result, not a fault\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    static boolean isTransient(Throwable t) {\n        return t instanceof ResourceAccessException                        // I/O error or read timeout\n            || t instanceof HttpServerErrorException.ServiceUnavailable    // 503\n            || t instanceof HttpServerErrorException.GatewayTimeout;       // 504\n    }\n}","note":"Compose them as `Retry.decorateSupplier(retry, CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(breaker, call))` with a 500 ms read timeout on the client. `CallNotPermittedException` from an open breaker is not transient, so the retry stops at once instead of hammering a dependency already declared unhealthy."},{"title":"Fitting attempts into a deadline, and how stacking explodes","lang":"text","code":"User request deadline at the gateway:     2000 ms\nPer-attempt read timeout (bank API):       500 ms\nAttempts:                                  3\nBackoff with 50% jitter:                   100 ms and 200 ms, at most 150 + 300 ms\nWorst case: 3 x 500 + 450                = 1950 ms   fits the budget\n\nBAD: gateway retries 3x, service retries 3x, HTTP client retries 3x\n     during an outage every user click becomes up to 27 bank API calls","note":"Derive per-attempt timeouts and retry counts from the end-to-end deadline, not independently per layer."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do retry storms happen?","a":"A dependency slows down or fails briefly and every client retries at once, often immediately and at several layers, so the struggling service receives two to ten times its normal load exactly when it has the least capacity, which prevents recovery. Stacked retries multiply: three attempts at each of three layers is 27 calls per original request. Retries without jitter arrive in synchronized waves, and long timeouts keep original requests alive while retries pile on top. Prevent it with a single retry layer, capped attempts with jittered backoff, retry budgets, circuit breakers and server-side load shedding that returns `429` or `503` with `Retry-After`."}],"extra":["**Defaults that bite:** the JDK `HttpClient` has no request timeout unless you set one, `HttpURLConnection` defaults to infinite timeouts, and PgJDBC's `socketTimeout` defaults to 0, meaning no timeout.","**Retry budgets:** cap retries as a fraction of total traffic rather than only per call, as gRPC retry throttling and Envoy retry budgets do, so a broad outage cannot multiply load.","**Deadline propagation:** gRPC propagates deadlines natively; over HTTP, pass the remaining budget in a header so downstream services skip work whose caller has already given up.","**Slow is worse than down:** configure slow-call thresholds, not only error rates, because a dependency answering in ten seconds exhausts resources faster than one failing instantly.","**Respect server hints:** honor `Retry-After` on `429` and `503`, and pair breakers with bulkheads so a half-open probe cannot flood a recovering service."],"redFlags":["Retries every error immediately or stacks retries at many layers"]},{"id":99,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"What problem does the Bulkhead pattern solve?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"A bulkhead gives each dependency or workload its own bounded pool of threads, connections or concurrent calls, so one slow dependency can exhaust only its own compartment while the rest of the service stays available.","answer":["Without isolation, all outbound calls share one resource: the servlet request threads, one HTTP connection pool or one executor. When a single dependency such as a bank API starts answering in 30 seconds, requests to it pile up and hold every thread, and endpoints that never touch the bank (order history, health checks) stop responding too. One slow dependency turns partial degradation into a full outage.","A bulkhead partitions those resources: a separate concurrency limit, thread pool or connection pool per dependency or per workload class. When the bank compartment is full, new calls to it are rejected immediately (return `503` or a `PENDING` status) while everything else keeps its capacity. Variants include a semaphore bulkhead (a cap on concurrent calls on the caller's thread), a thread pool bulkhead with a bounded queue, separate database pools for batch and online traffic, and separate deployments or node pools for critical flows.","It is not a circuit breaker: a breaker decides whether to call based on recent failures, while a bulkhead limits how much capacity calls may occupy, even when they all succeed slowly. They complement each other, with timeouts bounding how long a slot stays taken. Size compartments with Little's Law: concurrency equals throughput times latency, so 50 requests per second at 200 ms needs about 10 slots."],"keyPoints":["Isolates threads, connections or concurrency so one dependency cannot consume all resources and destroy partial availability"],"examples":[{"title":"Separate Resilience4j bulkheads per dependency","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass BulkheadConfiguration {\n\n    @Bean\n    Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead() {\n        return Bulkhead.of(\"bankApi\", BulkheadConfig.custom()\n                .maxConcurrentCalls(10)                // about 50 rps x 200 ms\n                .maxWaitDuration(Duration.ZERO)         // reject at once, never park request threads\n                .build());\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    Bulkhead fraudApiBulkhead() {                      // separate compartment, separate limit\n        return Bulkhead.of(\"fraudApi\", BulkheadConfig.custom().maxConcurrentCalls(20).build());\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass PayoutService {\n    private final Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead;\n    private final BankClient bank;\n\n    PayoutService(Bulkhead bankApiBulkhead, BankClient bank) {\n        this.bankApiBulkhead = bankApiBulkhead;\n        this.bank = bank;\n    }\n\n    PayoutStatus submit(Payout payout) {\n        try {\n            return bankApiBulkhead.executeSupplier(() -> bank.transfer(payout));\n        } catch (BulkheadFullException e) {\n            return PayoutStatus.queuedForRetry(payout.id());   // degrade, do not block\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"A slow bank can occupy at most 10 concurrent calls; fraud checks and every other endpoint keep their own capacity, and callers get a fast degraded answer instead of a hung request."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you isolate a slow bank API?","a":"Put all its calls behind a dedicated concurrency limit (a Resilience4j bulkhead or a semaphore) sized from expected throughput and latency, and give its HTTP client a dedicated connection pool with tight connect and read timeouts, so it can never consume shared request threads or connections. Add a circuit breaker with a slow-call threshold so sustained slowness stops calls entirely. Where the business allows, decouple it: accept the payout, return `202` with a `PENDING` status and process bank calls from a queue with their own consumers, so bank latency only delays payout completion. Monitor that compartment's saturation separately so incidents are attributed to the bank immediately."}],"extra":["**Virtual threads shift the scarce resource:** with Java 21 virtual threads (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` in Spring Boot 3.2 and later), request threads are cheap, so thread pool bulkheads matter less and explicit concurrency limits matter more; unbounded virtual threads can flood a dependency.","**Connection pools are bulkheads:** give each downstream HTTP client its own pool with a per-route maximum, and consider a separate HikariCP pool for reporting or batch jobs so a long query cannot starve checkout of connections.","**Process-level isolation:** separate Kubernetes deployments for critical and non-critical traffic, with resource requests and limits, also isolate memory leaks, GC pauses and noisy neighbors.","**Tenant bulkheads:** per-tenant concurrency limits stop one customer's batch integration from starving everyone else, the same pattern applied to callers instead of dependencies.","**Watch saturation:** monitor available permits and rejection counts per bulkhead; rejections under normal load mean an undersized compartment, rejections only during incidents mean it is doing its job."],"redFlags":["Confuses bulkhead with circuit breaker","shares one pool for all dependencies"]},{"id":100,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"Database per service: why, and what problems does it create?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Each service owns its data and nobody else reads or writes it directly, which enables independent change and deployment, but joins, cross-service consistency and reporting move from SQL into APIs, events, sagas and read models.","answer":["Database per service means a service's data is private: other services access it only through its API or the events it publishes. Physically it can be a separate PostgreSQL cluster, a separate database or at minimum a separate schema with its own credentials; the ownership rule is what matters. It gives autonomy (a team can change schema, indexes or even database technology without coordinating), encapsulation (invariants are enforced in one place) and fault isolation, since another team's runaway query cannot lock the payments tables.","The costs are real. Cross-service joins disappear: \"orders with customer name and payment status\" needs API composition or a local read model. Multi-service ACID transactions disappear, so changes spanning services need sagas and the outbox, with eventual consistency the business must accept. Reporting can no longer be one SQL query over everything, and duplicated data needs a clear owner of the authoritative copy.","So share data deliberately: synchronous APIs for current values that must not be cached, domain events so others can maintain their own projections (shipping keeps the customer address it needs), and read models or a data platform for cross-domain queries. Unrestricted cross-service table access, even read-only, is the red line: it turns your schema into a public API nobody can change and recreates the shared database with extra network hops."],"keyPoints":["Supports autonomy and encapsulation","cross-service joins, consistency and reporting become harder","share through APIs/events/read models"],"examples":[{"title":"Enforcing ownership in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- Cheap starting point: one cluster, one schema and one role per service\nCREATE ROLE orders_svc LOGIN;\nCREATE ROLE payments_svc LOGIN;\nCREATE SCHEMA orders AUTHORIZATION orders_svc;\nCREATE SCHEMA payments AUTHORIZATION payments_svc;\nREVOKE ALL ON SCHEMA orders, payments FROM PUBLIC;\n\n-- BAD: payments reads orders tables; the orders schema becomes a frozen public API\n-- GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA orders TO payments_svc;\n-- GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA orders TO payments_svc;\n\n-- GOOD: payments keeps its own projection, fed by OrderPlaced events\nCREATE TABLE payments.order_snapshot (\n    order_id     uuid    PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_id  uuid    NOT NULL,\n    total_minor  bigint  NOT NULL,\n    currency     char(3) NOT NULL,\n    version      bigint  NOT NULL        -- lets consumers ignore stale or duplicate events\n);","note":"Ownership is enforced by the database itself, not by convention; moving a schema to its own cluster later does not change any other service."},{"title":"Idempotent projection update from events","lang":"java","code":"@KafkaListener(topics = \"order-events\", groupId = \"payments\")\npublic void on(OrderPlaced event) {\n    jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n        INSERT INTO payments.order_snapshot (order_id, customer_id, total_minor, currency, version)\n        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\n        ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE\n           SET customer_id = EXCLUDED.customer_id,\n               total_minor = EXCLUDED.total_minor,\n               currency    = EXCLUDED.currency,\n               version     = EXCLUDED.version\n         WHERE payments.order_snapshot.version < EXCLUDED.version\n        \"\"\", event.orderId(), event.customerId(), event.totalMinor(), event.currency(), event.version());\n}","note":"The version guard makes the update idempotent and safe against out-of-order delivery, so duplicates from an at-least-once outbox are harmless."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would analytics query across domains?","a":"Not by pointing BI tools at every service's database. Stream changes out, either domain events or CDC such as Debezium on each PostgreSQL WAL, into an analytics platform (a warehouse or lakehouse such as BigQuery, Snowflake, or Iceberg tables queried with Trino), where cross-domain joins are cheap and do not load production. Treat each export as a published data product with an owned, versioned schema rather than a raw table dump, so services can still refactor internally. For cross-domain views the product itself needs at runtime, build a dedicated read model that consumes events from the involved domains."}],"extra":["**Schemas before clusters:** separate schemas and roles on a shared PostgreSQL instance give ownership at low cost; move a service to its own cluster when it needs independent scaling, maintenance windows or a different engine.","**Migrating off a shared database:** first route all access to a table through its owning module, then replicate to the new store via CDC, switch writes, and finally revoke the old grants.","**Erasure fans out:** duplicated personal data means a GDPR erasure request must reach every projection; define an erasure event and track completion per service.","**Make staleness visible:** store the source event version or timestamp in projections so support tooling and reconciliation jobs can detect lagging copies."],"redFlags":["Allows unrestricted cross-service table access"]},{"id":101,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"What belongs in an API Gateway, and what should stay out?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"A gateway owns cross-cutting edge concerns such as routing, TLS, authentication, rate limits and observability; domain rules and business orchestration stay in the services, or the gateway becomes a new monolith.","answer":["An API gateway is the single entry point for external traffic and the right place for concerns identical across services: TLS termination, routing and path rewriting, authentication (validating JWT signature, issuer, audience and expiry, or introspecting opaque tokens), coarse route-level authorization such as required scopes, rate limiting and quotas per client, request size limits, CORS, and consistent access logs, metrics and trace context. Centralizing these removes duplicated code and gives one place to enforce edge security policy.","Business logic stays out: pricing, credit limits, order state checks, and transformations that encode domain knowledge. Once the gateway knows domain rules, every feature needs a gateway change, one team becomes everyone's bottleneck, and each gateway deployment risks every API at once. That is the ESB mistake repeated as a gateway monolith.","The gray zone is client-specific aggregation. If a mobile screen needs data from three services, build a Backend for Frontend owned by the client team, a separate service with its own release cycle, instead of scripting it into the shared gateway. Keep the gateway stateless and highly available since it sits on every request's path, and never make it the only security layer: services still authorize every request themselves."],"keyPoints":["Routing, auth enforcement, rate limits, TLS and observability may belong","domain business logic should not create a gateway monolith"],"examples":[{"title":"Spring Cloud Gateway: edge concerns only","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  cloud:\n    gateway:\n      routes:\n        - id: payments\n          uri: http://payments.payments.svc.cluster.local:8080\n          predicates:\n            - Path=/api/payments/**\n          filters:\n            - StripPrefix=1\n            - name: RequestRateLimiter\n              args:\n                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 50\n                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 100\n                key-resolver: \"#{@clientIdKeyResolver}\"\n            - name: RequestSize\n              args:\n                maxSize: 1MB\n        - id: orders\n          uri: http://orders.orders.svc.cluster.local:8080\n          predicates:\n            - Path=/api/orders/**\n          filters:\n            - StripPrefix=1\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      resourceserver:\n        jwt:\n          issuer-uri: https://auth.example.com/realms/shop\n# Stays OUT of the gateway: credit limit checks, discounts, currency conversion","note":"Routing, rate limiting, size limits and JWT validation are declarative config; nothing here knows what an order or a credit limit is. From Spring Cloud 2025.0 the prefix becomes `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux`, with the old one supported through the properties migrator."},{"title":"The service still performs object-level authorization","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\nclass PaymentController {\n\n    private final PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    PaymentController(PaymentRepository payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n\n    @GetMapping(\"/payments/{id}\")\n    @PreAuthorize(\"hasAuthority('SCOPE_payments:read')\")\n    PaymentDto get(@PathVariable UUID id, @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt) {\n        Payment payment = payments.findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);\n        // Only this service knows who owns payment {id}; the gateway cannot check this\n        if (!payment.merchantId().toString().equals(jwt.getClaimAsString(\"merchant_id\"))) {\n            throw new NotFoundException();       // 404 so other merchants' ids are not revealed\n        }\n        return PaymentDto.from(payment);\n    }\n}","note":"The gateway proved who the caller is; the service decides what that caller may see, which is where broken object-level authorization bugs are prevented."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Gateway authentication vs service authorization?","a":"The gateway authenticates: it validates the token (signature, issuer, audience, expiry), rejects anonymous or malformed requests with `401`, and can enforce coarse route-level rules such as requiring scope `payments:write`. Services authorize: only the owning service knows whether this merchant may read payment 123 or refund more than was captured, so object-level and business authorization must live there, and Broken Object Level Authorization is the top risk in the OWASP API Security Top 10. Services should also verify identity themselves, by validating the forwarded JWT or using mTLS, instead of trusting any request that reaches them, in line with zero trust."}],"extra":["**Backend for Frontend:** one BFF per client type (web, mobile, partner API) handles aggregation and response shaping and is owned by the team that owns that client, keeping the edge gateway generic.","**Token exchange at the edge:** gateways often exchange an external access token for a short-lived internal token (OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, RFC 8693), so internal services never handle raw third-party tokens.","**Gateway versus service mesh:** the gateway handles north-south traffic into the cluster, while a mesh such as Istio or Linkerd handles east-west mTLS, retries and traffic splitting; blurring the two makes it unclear where a policy lives.","**Blast radius:** one bad gateway change can take every API down, so keep routes as reviewed config in Git and roll them out with canaries and automated smoke tests.","**No blind retries:** gateway-level retries on non-idempotent routes can double-submit payments and stack with service retries; restrict them to safe methods."],"redFlags":["Moves all business logic to gateway"]},{"id":102,"category":"Microservice Architecture","question":"CQRS and Event Sourcing: when are they justified?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"CQRS splits write and read models; Event Sourcing makes an append-only event log the source of truth; both add real complexity and pay off only for divergent read shapes, audit or temporal queries.","answer":["CQRS separates the model that handles commands and enforces invariants from one or more models optimized for queries. At its simplest it is two sets of classes over one database; at its fullest, writes go to a normalized PostgreSQL schema while events update denormalized read models in Elasticsearch or dedicated tables, eventually consistent. It is justified when read and write shapes diverge strongly, such as a rich aggregate versus dashboards spanning several contexts, or when read and write loads must scale independently.","Event Sourcing stores state as an append-only sequence of domain events (`AccountOpened`, `FundsDeposited`, `FundsWithdrawn`), and current state is derived by replaying them, usually with snapshots for long streams. You gain a complete audit trail, answers to \"what did we know at time T\", and new projections built by replaying history. The operational price is high: projections and rebuild tooling, fast and safe replay, event schema versioning and upcasting forever since events are immutable, GDPR erasure in an immutable log, and a team comfortable with eventual consistency.","Both require a real problem. For ordinary CRUD such as settings or a product catalog they add moving parts, read lag and debugging pain with no payoff. Good fits are ledgers, payments, trading and credit decisions where audit and temporal queries are core requirements, and even then usually for that bounded context only, with a clear operational plan."],"keyPoints":["CQRS separates read/write models","Event Sourcing stores events as truth and needs projections, replay and versioning","both require a real problem"],"examples":[{"title":"Event-sourced account aggregate in Java 21","lang":"java","code":"sealed interface AccountEvent permits AccountOpened, FundsDeposited, FundsWithdrawn {}\nrecord AccountOpened(UUID accountId, String currency) implements AccountEvent {}\nrecord FundsDeposited(UUID accountId, long amountMinor) implements AccountEvent {}\nrecord FundsWithdrawn(UUID accountId, long amountMinor) implements AccountEvent {}\n\nfinal class Account {\n    private long balanceMinor;\n    private long version;\n\n    static Account replay(List<AccountEvent> history) {\n        Account account = new Account();\n        history.forEach(account::apply);\n        return account;\n    }\n\n    List<AccountEvent> withdraw(UUID id, long amountMinor) {\n        if (amountMinor > balanceMinor) {                       // invariant on the write model\n            throw new InsufficientFundsException(id, amountMinor, balanceMinor);\n        }\n        return List.of(new FundsWithdrawn(id, amountMinor));    // decide, do not mutate\n    }\n\n    private void apply(AccountEvent event) {\n        switch (event) {                                        // exhaustive over the sealed type\n            case AccountOpened e  -> balanceMinor = 0;\n            case FundsDeposited e -> balanceMinor += e.amountMinor();\n            case FundsWithdrawn e -> balanceMinor -= e.amountMinor();\n        }\n        version++;\n    }\n\n    long version() { return version; }\n}","note":"Commands produce events and state changes only by applying events, so replay rebuilds exactly the same state; the sealed interface makes a forgotten event type a compile error."},{"title":"Event store with optimistic concurrency and a read model","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE account_events (\n    account_id   uuid        NOT NULL,\n    version      bigint      NOT NULL,\n    event_type   text        NOT NULL,\n    schema_ver   int         NOT NULL,        -- enables upcasting of old event shapes\n    payload      jsonb       NOT NULL,\n    recorded_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (account_id, version)          -- two writers of the same version: one fails\n);\n\n-- Loaded at version 7, append version 8; a unique violation means a concurrent write\nINSERT INTO account_events (account_id, version, event_type, schema_ver, payload)\nVALUES ($1, 8, 'FundsWithdrawn', 1, '{\"amountMinor\": 25000}');\n\n-- Query side, rebuilt at any time by replaying events from version 1\nCREATE TABLE account_balance_view (\n    account_id    uuid   PRIMARY KEY,\n    balance_minor bigint NOT NULL,\n    last_version  bigint NOT NULL\n);","note":"The primary key gives optimistic concurrency for free, and the read model is disposable because it can always be rebuilt from the log."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Can CQRS exist without Event Sourcing?","a":"Yes, and that is the most common and most useful form. The write side can be an ordinary JPA or JDBC model on PostgreSQL, while read models are SQL views, materialized views, denormalized tables updated in the same transaction, or projections fed through an outbox or CDC stream into Elasticsearch. Event Sourcing practically implies CQRS, because querying an event log directly is impractical, but the reverse does not hold."}],"extra":["**CQRS light:** a separate query path using SQL or Spring Data interface projections on the same database, with commands going through the domain model, captures much of the benefit without any eventual consistency.","**Read your own writes:** with asynchronous projections, return the new version from the command and let the UI poll until the read model reaches it, or read that user's own data from the write side.","**Never rewrite history:** do not change the meaning of a stored event; add new event types or versions and upcast old payloads on read, which is why frameworks like Axon ship upcaster support.","**Replay time is an SLO:** snapshot long streams and measure full projection rebuild time, because a rebuild that takes days turns a projection bug into a multi-day incident.","**Domain events are not integration events:** fine-grained internal events change with the model; publish separate, stable integration events to other services rather than exposing the event store."],"redFlags":["Uses them for ordinary CRUD without operational plan"]},{"id":103,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"Explain CAP theorem precisely.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"CAP says that during a network partition a replicated system must either refuse some requests to stay linearizable (CP) or keep answering with possibly stale or divergent data (AP); without partitions you get both.","answer":["Precisely: Gilbert and Lynch proved that no system can provide **linearizability** (every read sees the latest completed write, as if there were one copy), **availability** (every request to a non-failing node gets a non-error response) and **partition tolerance** (correct behavior despite arbitrary message loss between nodes) at the same time. Partitions are not optional: switches fail, cross-AZ links drop, and a long GC pause looks exactly like a partition to peers. So partition tolerance is a given, and the real decision is what each operation does **during** a partition.","A **CP** choice makes the minority side reject or block requests: etcd, ZooKeeper and Raft-based stores only serve writes with a quorum, and PostgreSQL with synchronous replication blocks commits when no synchronous standby answers. An **AP** choice keeps both sides accepting writes and reconciles later with last-write-wins, version vectors or CRDTs (Cassandra at consistency level `ONE`, DynamoDB eventually consistent reads). The choice can be made per operation, not per product.","Two common confusions. CAP consistency is not ACID consistency: ACID C means a transaction preserves invariants such as constraints, while CAP C is a recency and ordering guarantee on single objects. And a partition is not a node crash: a crashed node owes nobody an answer, whereas in a partition both sides are alive and reachable by clients but cannot talk to each other, which is what creates split brain.","In production you pick per use case: debits and uniqueness go CP, catalog browsing and dashboards go AP."],"keyPoints":["During a network partition choose between consistent responses and availability","partition tolerance is unavoidable","CAP consistency is not ACID"],"examples":[{"title":"Partition between two regions: CP vs AP behavior","lang":"text","code":"Setup: 3 replicas, eu-north (2 nodes) and eu-west (1 node). Link between regions drops.\n\n                    CP store (quorum writes)           AP store (write to any replica)\neu-north (majority) reads and writes succeed           reads and writes succeed\neu-west (minority)  writes fail or time out (503)      writes succeed locally\n                    reads fail or are served stale     reads may return stale data\n                    only if explicitly allowed\nAfter healing       nothing to merge                   conflicting versions must be merged\n                                                       (LWW loses updates, CRDTs merge)\nClients see         errors on one side                 success everywhere, divergence later","note":"The CP system never shows two different truths but refuses service on the minority side; the AP system always answers but pushes conflict resolution into your application or data model."},{"title":"PostgreSQL choosing consistency over availability","lang":"properties","code":"# postgresql.conf on the primary\n# Commit returns only after at least one standby has applied the WAL\nsynchronous_standby_names = 'ANY 1 (replica_a, replica_b)'\nsynchronous_commit = remote_apply\n\n# If both standbys are unreachable, commits wait instead of succeeding:\n# the primary chooses C over A for writes until a standby returns\n# or an operator relaxes the setting.","note":"Synchronous replication is a concrete CP knob: during a partition from its standbys the primary stops acknowledging commits rather than risking acknowledged writes that a failover would lose."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What would a ledger choose during partition?","a":"A ledger chooses **consistency** for the posting path: the minority side must not accept debits it cannot validate against the authoritative balance, so it rejects them or records them only as pending intents that are not yet posted. Read-only views such as statements can be served from a stale replica if they are labeled with an as-of time. Card networks show the controlled exception: stand-in authorization during outages accepts limited risk with explicit amount limits and reconciles afterward, which is a deliberate, bounded AP decision rather than a default."}],"extra":["**PACELC:** even without a partition you trade latency for consistency; synchronous cross-region replication adds a network round trip to every write, which is often the decision that actually matters day to day.","**CAP availability is strict:** it requires every non-failing node to answer, so a CP system that keeps serving from the majority side can still be highly available in the SRE sense of meeting its SLO.","**Quorums are not automatically linearizable:** `R + W > N` makes read and write sets overlap, but sloppy quorums, hinted handoff and concurrent read repair can still return stale or reordered values.","**Tunable per request:** Cassandra consistency levels (`QUORUM` vs `ONE`), DynamoDB `ConsistentRead`, and MongoDB `readConcern`/`writeConcern` `majority` let one system make different CAP choices per query.","**Detection is a timeout:** a node cannot distinguish a slow peer from a partitioned one, which is why leases, fencing tokens and GC pause monitoring matter in CP designs."],"redFlags":["Says choose any two at all times","confuses partition with node failure"]},{"id":104,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"What does eventual consistency mean in practice?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Replicas and read models may temporarily disagree but converge once updates stop flowing; in practice you design pending states, idempotent versioned updates, reconciliation and lag monitoring, and keep invariants on a strongly consistent store.","answer":["Eventual consistency is a **convergence** guarantee: if no new writes arrive, all replicas eventually return the same value. It shows up everywhere in a backend: async PostgreSQL read replicas, CQRS read models fed by events, caches, search indexes, and data copied between services over Kafka or RabbitMQ. It does not mean no guarantees; it means no bound on staleness unless you add one, plus convergence you must actually engineer.","Design for the window. Model explicit **pending states** (`PENDING_PAYMENT`, a 202 Accepted with a status URL) instead of pretending the result is final. Make projections idempotent and version-guarded so duplicates and reordering still converge. Provide session guarantees where users notice: read-your-writes by returning the created resource from the command, or routing that user to the primary briefly after a write.","Add **reconciliation** because convergence can silently fail: a dropped event, a poison message in a DLQ or a bug leaves a read model permanently wrong. Periodic jobs compare the source of truth with derived copies and repair them, and you monitor replica lag, consumer lag and the age of the oldest unprocessed event against an SLO.","Finally, apply **stronger guarantees where invariants require them**: uniqueness, balances, inventory and idempotency keys live in one authoritative store with transactions or conditional writes; everything derived can be eventual."],"keyPoints":["Replicas/read models may temporarily differ but converge","design pending states, reconciliation and stronger guarantees where required"],"examples":[{"title":"Version-guarded projection that converges despite duplicates and reordering","lang":"java","code":"record OrderStatusChanged(UUID orderId, String status, long version) {}\n\n@Component\nclass OrderSummaryProjector {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    OrderSummaryProjector(JdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"order-summary.projection\")\n    void on(OrderStatusChanged e) {\n        // GOOD: upsert only if the event is newer than what the read model holds\n        jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO order_summary (order_id, status, version, updated_at)\n            VALUES (?, ?, ?, now())\n            ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE\n               SET status = EXCLUDED.status,\n                   version = EXCLUDED.version,\n                   updated_at = now()\n             WHERE order_summary.version < EXCLUDED.version\n            \"\"\", e.orderId(), e.status(), e.version());\n    }\n}","note":"The `WHERE order_summary.version < EXCLUDED.version` clause turns duplicates and late events into no-ops, so the read model always converges to the latest source version."},{"title":"Exposing the pending state instead of hiding it","lang":"http","code":"POST /payments HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example.com\nIdempotency-Key: 7f3c9a2e-1b4d-4c1e-9f7a-2d8e6b0c5a11\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"orderId\": \"ord_1042\", \"amount\": \"149.00\", \"currency\": \"SEK\"}\n\nHTTP/1.1 202 Accepted\nLocation: /payments/pay_88231\nRetry-After: 2\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"id\": \"pay_88231\", \"status\": \"PENDING\"}","note":"The client gets an honest intermediate state and a place to poll, so the UI can say processing rather than showing a stale or wrong final status."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which operations require stronger consistency?","a":"Anything that protects an invariant or is irreversible: debiting money and balance checks, uniqueness (usernames, idempotency keys, one capture per payment), inventory or seat reservation, permission revocation (a removed role must stop working now), and calls that move money at an external provider. Implement these on one authoritative store with transactions, unique constraints or conditional writes such as a debit `UPDATE account SET balance = ... WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?` whose row count you check. Dashboards, search, notifications, analytics and most read models can be eventual."}],"extra":["**Staleness needs an SLO:** alert on `pg_stat_replication.replay_lag`, Kafka consumer lag and oldest event age; eventually without a number is how a read model ends up hours behind unnoticed.","**Read-your-writes via LSN:** return the commit LSN (`pg_current_wal_lsn()`) to the client and have replica reads wait until `pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()` has passed it, instead of pinning all reads to the primary.","**Replica routing gotcha:** routing `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` to replicas with `AbstractRoutingDataSource` silently breaks read-your-writes for flows that write and then read in separate transactions.","**Conflict resolution:** with multiple writers, last-write-wins by wall clock loses updates under clock skew; prefer a single writer per key, version numbers or CRDTs."],"redFlags":["Equates eventual consistency with no guarantees"]},{"id":105,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"At-most-once, at-least-once and exactly-once delivery.","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"At-most-once can lose messages, at-least-once can duplicate them, and exactly-once only holds inside a scoped boundary such as Kafka transactions; end to end you get effectively-once from at-least-once plus idempotent processing.","answer":["The semantics come from **when you acknowledge relative to the side effect**. At-most-once acks (or commits the offset) before processing: a crash after the ack loses the message, and a producer that never retries loses on network errors. At-least-once processes first and acks after: a crash between commit and ack causes redelivery, and a producer retrying after a lost confirm publishes twice, so duplicates are normal.","Exactly-once is **scoped**. Kafka's idempotent producer dedupes retries per partition using producer IDs and sequence numbers, and Kafka transactions make consume, transform and produce atomic, including the consumer offset commit, for readers using `read_committed`. That guarantee ends at Kafka's boundary: once the handler writes to PostgreSQL, sends an email or calls a payment provider, the broker cannot make that side effect and the ack atomic. No broker gives arbitrary end-to-end exactly-once, and RabbitMQ does not claim it at all.","The practical design is **at-least-once delivery plus idempotent consumers**: publisher confirms, persistent messages and durable (quorum) queues on the way in, a transactional outbox on the producer side, and a dedupe record written in the same database transaction as the business change on the consumer side. At-most-once is acceptable only where loss is cheap: metrics, telemetry, cache invalidation hints."],"keyPoints":["At-most-once may lose","at-least-once may duplicate","exactly-once is usually scoped, not arbitrary end-to-end side effects"],"examples":[{"title":"Where a crash lands decides loss or duplication","lang":"text","code":"Consumer crash point                 | Ack BEFORE work   | Ack AFTER work\n-------------------------------------+-------------------+----------------------------\nBefore business commit               | message LOST      | redelivered, processed once\nAfter business commit, before ack    | n/a (acked)       | redelivered: DUPLICATE\nAfter ack                            | done              | done\n\nProducer: no retry after timeout     -> possible LOSS\nProducer: retry after lost confirm   -> possible DUPLICATE","note":"Every row has either a loss or a duplicate case, which is why you choose at-least-once and make duplicates harmless instead of trying to eliminate them."},{"title":"Kafka exactly-once scope in Spring Boot","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  kafka:\n    producer:\n      acks: all\n      transaction-id-prefix: ledger-tx-   # enables transactional producer\n      properties:\n        enable.idempotence: true\n    consumer:\n      enable-auto-commit: false\n      isolation-level: read-committed      # skip aborted transactional writes","note":"This makes a Kafka-to-Kafka pipeline atomic; a PostgreSQL write inside the same listener is outside the Kafka transaction and still needs its own idempotency."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if a consumer writes DB then crashes before ack?","a":"The broker never received the ack, so it redelivers: RabbitMQ requeues the unacked delivery when the channel closes, and a Kafka consumer resumes from the last committed offset. The DB write then happens twice unless the consumer is idempotent. The fix is to insert the message ID into a `processed_message` table with a unique constraint in the same transaction as the business write; on redelivery the insert conflicts, you skip the work and ack. With Kafka you can alternatively store the offset in the same DB transaction and `seek` to it on partition assignment."}],"extra":["**Kafka defaults:** since Kafka 3.0 producers default to `enable.idempotence=true` and `acks=all`, which removes producer retry duplicates per partition within a producer session, not duplicates from your own application retries.","**Stable message IDs:** assign the event ID when the business fact is created (for example in the outbox row), not at send time, so every retry and replay carries the same ID for dedupe.","**Dedupe windows are bounded:** broker-side dedupe such as SQS FIFO deduplication works only within a short window (5 minutes for SQS FIFO), so it cannot replace consumer idempotency for replays days later.","**Redelivered flag is a hint:** RabbitMQ sets `redelivered=true` on redelivery, but it does not tell you whether processing completed; only your dedupe record can."],"redFlags":["Claims broker guarantees end-to-end exactly once"]},{"id":106,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"How do you make a message consumer idempotent?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use a stable producer-assigned ID and, in the same transaction as the business change, insert it into an inbox table with a unique constraint, so duplicates fail atomically and are simply acked.","answer":["Start with a **stable ID**: an event ID assigned by the producer when the fact was created, or a natural business key such as `paymentId` plus event type. Do not use broker artifacts like the delivery tag or a Kafka offset, which change when a message is republished or replayed from a DLQ.","Then make **dedupe and the business update atomic**. In one local transaction, `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` into an inbox table keyed by `(message_id, consumer)`, check the affected row count, and only if it is 1 apply the business change. Either both commit or neither does. The classic bugs are the alternatives: an in-memory set of processed IDs is lost on restart and not shared across replicas; check-then-insert (`SELECT` then `INSERT`) races when two consumers get the same redelivered message; and a Redis `SETNX` outside the DB transaction leaves windows where the ID is marked but the business write rolled back, or the reverse.","Where possible, make the operation **naturally idempotent** as a second line of defense: conditional state transitions (`UPDATE ... WHERE status = 'PENDING'`), upserts by business key, or a unique constraint such as one ledger entry per `(payment_id, entry_type)`. Ack only after the transaction commits, retain inbox rows longer than any redelivery or replay window, and handle external side effects separately because they cannot join the transaction."],"keyPoints":["Use stable message/business ID, unique constraint or inbox table, and atomic dedupe plus business update"],"examples":[{"title":"Inbox claim and business write in one transaction","lang":"java","code":"record PaymentCaptured(UUID eventId, UUID paymentId, BigDecimal amount, String currency) {}\n\n@Component\npublic class PaymentCapturedConsumer {\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n    private final LedgerService ledger;\n\n    public PaymentCapturedConsumer(JdbcTemplate jdbc, LedgerService ledger) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n        this.ledger = ledger;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"ledger.payment-captured\")\n    @Transactional\n    public void on(PaymentCaptured event) {\n        // BAD: if (processedIds.contains(event.eventId())) return;  in memory, racy, lost on restart\n\n        // GOOD: atomic claim in the same transaction as the ledger posting\n        int claimed = jdbc.update(\"\"\"\n            INSERT INTO inbox (message_id, consumer, received_at)\n            VALUES (?, 'ledger', now())\n            ON CONFLICT (message_id, consumer) DO NOTHING\n            \"\"\", event.eventId());\n        if (claimed == 0) {\n            return; // duplicate: nothing to do, the container acks it\n        }\n        ledger.postCapture(event.paymentId(), event.amount(), event.currency());\n    }\n}","note":"The listener returns only after the transaction commits, and Spring AMQP acks on return, so a crash at any point either rolls back both writes or leaves a committed claim that makes the redelivery a no-op."},{"title":"Schema: inbox plus a natural business key","lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE inbox (\n    message_id  uuid        NOT NULL,\n    consumer    text        NOT NULL,\n    received_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    PRIMARY KEY (message_id, consumer)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ledger_entry (\n    id          bigserial     PRIMARY KEY,\n    payment_id  uuid          NOT NULL,\n    entry_type  text          NOT NULL,\n    amount      numeric(19,4) NOT NULL,\n    currency    char(3)       NOT NULL,\n    created_at  timestamptz   NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n    CONSTRAINT uq_ledger_payment_entry UNIQUE (payment_id, entry_type)\n);","note":"The composite key lets several consumer groups share one inbox table, and the business unique constraint still blocks a double posting even if a message is republished with a new ID."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you handle side effects such as email?","a":"An email or provider call cannot join the database transaction, so record the intent instead: in the business transaction insert an `outbound_email` row with a unique key such as `(order_id, template)`, and let a separate worker send it and mark it sent. The worker can still crash after sending and before marking, so delivery is at-least-once; pass an idempotency key to the provider when its API supports one and accept rare duplicates for low-risk messages. For money-moving calls always send a provider idempotency key derived from your business ID, and query status on uncertain outcomes rather than resending."}],"extra":["**Spring AMQP ack timing:** with the default `AcknowledgeMode.AUTO` the container acks after the listener returns and nacks on exception, requeueing by default; set `default-requeue-rejected: false` or throw `AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException` so failures dead-letter instead of looping.","**Same ID, different payload:** if a duplicate ID arrives with a different body, that is a producer bug; store a payload hash in the inbox and alert on mismatch instead of silently skipping.","**Inbox retention:** keep rows longer than the longest possible redelivery, including DLQ replays days later; partition the table by day and drop old partitions rather than running large `DELETE`s.","**Dedupe is not ordering:** an idempotent consumer still applies events in arrival order, so combine it with version checks when events for one aggregate can be reordered."],"redFlags":["Stores processed IDs only in memory","check-then-insert is not atomic"]},{"id":107,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"What ordering guarantees can a distributed system provide?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Distributed systems give ordering per key or partition, not globally; retries, redelivery and parallel consumers reorder events, so route related events by a key and have consumers enforce order with sequence or version checks.","answer":["What you actually get is **scoped ordering**: Kafka orders records within a partition, a RabbitMQ queue delivers FIFO to a single consumer, SQS FIFO orders within a message group, and a database orders commits within itself. A true global order needs a single sequencer (one partition, one leader), which caps throughput and availability, so most systems do not provide it and most business logic does not need it.","Order breaks in predictable places: producers retrying with several requests in flight, multiple producers for the same entity, competing consumers or a thread pool inside one consumer, nack and redelivery, delayed retry queues, DLQ replays, and repartitioning that changes which partition a key maps to. Any of these lets event 12 be processed before event 11.","The design answer has two parts. First, **choose the ordering key** as the aggregate whose invariants depend on order (`accountId`, `orderId`) and use it as the partition or routing key, so all its events share one ordered lane. Second, **make consumers verify order**: the source assigns a per-aggregate sequence or version (the aggregate's optimistic lock version or an outbox sequence), and the consumer applies only the expected next value, ignores older ones and parks gaps. State-carrying events with versions are more forgiving than deltas, because a newer snapshot can simply win."],"keyPoints":["Usually ordering is per key/partition, not global","retries and parallelism reorder","use keys and sequence/version checks"],"examples":[{"title":"Consumer enforcing per-account sequence","lang":"java","code":"record AccountEvent(UUID accountId, long seq, String type, BigDecimal amount) {}\n\nclass OutOfOrderException extends RuntimeException {\n    OutOfOrderException(UUID account, long expected, long got) {\n        super(\"account \" + account + \" expected seq \" + expected + \" but got \" + got);\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass AccountProjection {\n    private final AccountRepository accounts;\n\n    AccountProjection(AccountRepository accounts) {\n        this.accounts = accounts;\n    }\n\n    @Transactional\n    public void apply(AccountEvent e) {\n        Account acc = accounts.findByIdForUpdate(e.accountId()).orElseThrow();\n        long expected = acc.getLastSeq() + 1;\n        if (e.seq() < expected) {\n            return; // duplicate or already applied\n        }\n        if (e.seq() > expected) {\n            // gap: 11 is missing, do not apply 12 yet; retry later or park\n            throw new OutOfOrderException(e.accountId(), expected, e.seq());\n        }\n        acc.apply(e.type(), e.amount());\n        acc.setLastSeq(e.seq());\n    }\n}","note":"The row lock plus `lastSeq` makes the consumer the final arbiter of order, so broker reordering can delay processing but can never corrupt the balance."},{"title":"How a retry topic reorders one key","lang":"text","code":"Partition 3, key acct-42, single consumer, retry topic with 30 s delay\n\nt0    consume seq 11 (debit 500)  -> provider timeout -> sent to retry topic\nt1    consume seq 12 (credit 200) -> applied\nt31   consume seq 11 from retry   -> applied after 12\n\nWithout a sequence check: the overdraft rule evaluated at t1 used the wrong balance.\nWith a sequence check: 12 is parked at t1 and applied right after 11 at t31.","note":"The partition itself was perfectly ordered; the retry path is what broke per-key order."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if event 12 arrives before 11?","a":"It depends on the event type. If events carry full state with a version, apply 12 and later discard 11 as stale because its version is lower. If they are deltas such as debits and credits, do not apply 12: park it (a pending table keyed by aggregate and sequence, or a delayed retry), apply 11 when it arrives, then drain 12. Put a timeout on the gap that alerts or triggers a refetch of the authoritative state from the source service, and never silently skip a gap for money-moving events."}],"extra":["**Kafka producer ordering:** with idempotence enabled (the default since Kafka 3.0) order is preserved per partition with up to 5 in-flight requests; without it, retries plus `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection` above 1 can reorder.","**Repartitioning:** adding partitions changes the key to partition mapping, so events for one key can straddle two partitions during the switch; size partitions up front or drain producers before changing them.","**Spring Kafka concurrency:** a listener container processes each partition serially, so per-key order holds until you hand records to an async executor; concurrency above the partition count only leaves idle consumers.","**More parallelism than partitions:** the Confluent Parallel Consumer supports key-level ordering, processing different keys from one partition concurrently while keeping each key sequential.","**Timestamps are not order:** clocks skew across hosts, so decide order by source-assigned sequence numbers or versions, never by `occurredAt`."],"redFlags":["Assumes global ordering with many consumers"]},{"id":108,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"Distributed lock vs database constraint: which should enforce correctness?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Enforce correctness in the authoritative datastore with unique constraints, conditional updates and transactions; a distributed lock only reduces duplicate work, because leases expire, holders pause and the lock is not atomic with the write.","answer":["A Redis lock (`SET key value NX PX 10000`) is a **lease**: if the holder stalls longer than the TTL because of a GC pause, a VM freeze or a slow network, the lock expires, a second node acquires it, and both proceed. Redis failover with asynchronous replication can also lose a lock outright. Even a perfect lock lives in a different system from your data, so acquiring it and committing the write are never atomic.","The database, by contrast, enforces invariants **atomically with the write**: a unique constraint or partial unique index (one capture per payment), a conditional state transition (`UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'AUTHORIZED'` and check the row count), optimistic locking with `@Version`, `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, or serializable isolation. Whatever races happen above it, only one writer can win.","Locks are still useful as an **optimization**: stopping two nodes from running the same expensive report, spreading scheduled jobs (ShedLock), or avoiding thundering herds on a cache miss. When a lock must protect an external resource, add **fencing tokens**: a monotonically increasing number issued with the lock that the resource checks, rejecting writes from an older holder.","In interviews and production alike, the rule is: the lock may reduce how often you hit the constraint, but the constraint is what makes it correct."],"keyPoints":["Prefer authoritative datastore constraints/transactions for invariants","locks may reduce duplicate work but are not sole correctness boundary"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: lock as the only guard; GOOD: database claims the transition","lang":"java","code":"@Service\nclass CaptureService {\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n    private final PaymentProvider provider;\n\n    CaptureService(StringRedisTemplate redis, JdbcTemplate jdbc, PaymentProvider provider) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n        this.provider = provider;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: a GC pause longer than 10 s lets a second node capture as well\n    void captureWithLockOnly(UUID paymentId) {\n        Boolean got = redis.opsForValue()\n            .setIfAbsent(\"capture:\" + paymentId, \"node-a\", Duration.ofSeconds(10));\n        if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(got)) {\n            provider.capture(paymentId, \"capture-\" + paymentId);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: only one caller can move AUTHORIZED to CAPTURE_PENDING\n    void capture(UUID paymentId) {\n        int claimed = jdbc.update(\n            \"UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'AUTHORIZED'\",\n            paymentId);\n        if (claimed == 0) {\n            return; // already captured or in progress elsewhere\n        }\n        provider.capture(paymentId, \"capture-\" + paymentId); // idempotency key\n        jdbc.update(\"UPDATE payment SET status = 'CAPTURED' WHERE id = ? AND status = 'CAPTURE_PENDING'\",\n            paymentId);\n    }\n}","note":"The claim commits before the external call (no long transaction), and the provider idempotency key protects against the one remaining gap: a crash between the provider call and the final update."},{"title":"Database-level guarantees","lang":"sql","code":"-- At most one capture operation per payment, whatever the application does\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX uq_payment_single_capture\n    ON payment_operation (payment_id)\n    WHERE operation_type = 'CAPTURE';\n\n-- Serialize work on one key inside the database, released at commit, no TTL\nBEGIN;\nSELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended('payment:' || '9b2f0c1e-6a7d-4f3b-8c2e-1d5a7e9f0b44', 0));\n-- ... check state and insert the operation ...\nCOMMIT;","note":"The partial unique index is the true invariant; the advisory lock is optional coordination that lives in the same transaction, so it cannot outlive the work the way a Redis lease can."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you prevent two captures for one payment?","a":"Layer it. Claim the transition in the database (`AUTHORIZED` to `CAPTURE_PENDING` with a row count check, backed by a partial unique index on capture operations per payment), so only one node proceeds. Send the provider an idempotency key derived from the payment ID, so retries from any node are deduplicated at the provider. Record the result and move to `CAPTURED`; a recovery job for payments stuck in `CAPTURE_PENDING` queries the provider for status instead of blindly retrying. Finally, reconciliation against the provider's settlement report catches anything that still slipped through."}],"extra":["**Redlock critique:** Martin Kleppmann's analysis showed that lease-based locks like Redlock depend on bounded process pauses and clock drift, which JVMs and VMs do not guarantee, so they are unsafe as a correctness mechanism.","**Advisory locks and poolers:** PostgreSQL session-level advisory locks break under PgBouncer transaction pooling because the session changes between transactions; transaction-level `pg_advisory_xact_lock` avoids that.","**ShedLock still needs idempotency:** `lockAtMostFor` expires if a job runs too long, so a second instance can start the same job; the job itself must tolerate overlap.","**Optimistic locking as default:** JPA `@Version` turns lost updates into `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException` in Spring, a cheap guard for aggregate updates that needs no lock service at all."],"redFlags":["Uses Redis lock instead of a unique constraint"]},{"id":109,"category":"Distributed Systems","question":"What is reconciliation, and why is it essential in financial systems?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Reconciliation regularly compares independent records (provider, ledger, orders, bank) to detect missing, duplicate or mismatched states, then drives an owned, audited repair workflow; it catches what retries and idempotency cannot.","answer":["Retries only fix failures you observed. Financial flows also fail silently: a provider charged the card but the response timed out, an event was dropped, a bug posted the wrong amount, an operator changed something manually, or the provider later applied a chargeback, refund, fee or FX adjustment. **Reconciliation** compares independently produced records of the same money movement to find these: missing on one side, duplicated, amount or currency mismatch, or status mismatch. Auditors and regulators expect it as a financial control.","Define **authority per fact** first: the provider or bank is authoritative for whether money moved, the ledger for accounting, the order service for customer intent. Match on stable references (your payment ID sent to the provider as merchant reference, plus the provider transaction ID), compare amount, currency and status, and allow a tolerance window for items still in flight across cutoffs.","Then the **repair workflow**: auto-resolve only well-understood break types, route the rest to an owned ops queue with evidence, and apply fixes as compensating ledger entries, never edits, each linked to the break with who, when and why. That gives an **audit trail**. Track **metrics**: open breaks by type and age, value at risk, auto-resolution rate and time to resolve, and alert on aged or growing breaks because they usually signal a systemic bug."],"keyPoints":["Compare independent records to find missing, duplicate or mismatched states","define authority, repair workflow, audit trail and metrics"],"examples":[{"title":"Daily provider versus ledger match that records breaks","lang":"sql","code":"WITH p AS (\n    SELECT merchant_ref, currency, sum(amount) AS amount, count(*) AS n\n    FROM provider_settlement\n    WHERE settlement_date = DATE '2026-09-25'\n    GROUP BY merchant_ref, currency\n), l AS (\n    SELECT payment_ref, currency, sum(amount) AS amount, count(*) AS n\n    FROM ledger_entry\n    WHERE entry_type = 'CAPTURE' AND booking_date = DATE '2026-09-25'\n    GROUP BY payment_ref, currency\n), matched AS (\n    SELECT coalesce(p.merchant_ref, l.payment_ref) AS ref,\n           p.amount AS provider_amount,\n           l.amount AS ledger_amount,\n           CASE\n             WHEN l.payment_ref IS NULL  THEN 'MISSING_IN_LEDGER'\n             WHEN p.merchant_ref IS NULL THEN 'MISSING_AT_PROVIDER'\n             WHEN l.n > 1 OR p.n > 1     THEN 'DUPLICATE'\n             WHEN p.amount <> l.amount   THEN 'AMOUNT_MISMATCH'\n           END AS break_type\n    FROM p FULL OUTER JOIN l\n      ON l.payment_ref = p.merchant_ref AND l.currency = p.currency\n)\nINSERT INTO recon_break (ref, break_type, provider_amount, ledger_amount, run_date)\nSELECT ref, break_type, provider_amount, ledger_amount, DATE '2026-09-25'\nFROM matched\nWHERE break_type IS NOT NULL;","note":"The `FULL OUTER JOIN` is the heart of it: rows with no partner are exactly the missing items; a real job also widens the date window so late settlements age before they count as breaks."},{"title":"Break types, authority and repair policy","lang":"text","code":"Break type            Authority   Auto repair?              Action\nMISSING_IN_LEDGER     provider    yes, if order is PENDING  post capture entry, mark order PAID\nMISSING_AT_PROVIDER   provider    no                        hold fulfilment, query provider API, ops case\nDUPLICATE             provider    no                        refund at provider, reversing ledger entry\nAMOUNT_MISMATCH       provider    no                        check fees and FX, ops case with evidence\nIN_FLIGHT (< 2 days)  none yet    wait                      re-check next run, age the item","note":"Writing the policy down per break type is what turns reconciliation from a report nobody reads into an operational process with owners."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you reconcile provider, ledger and order?","a":"Do a three-way match on a shared reference: your payment ID, sent to the provider as merchant reference, stored on the order and on every ledger entry. Provider versus ledger checks that money moved exactly as booked (amounts, fees, currency, refunds, chargebacks); ledger versus order checks that every paid order has a capture entry and every capture belongs to a paid order, and that refunds match cancellations. Each mismatch is classified by type with the provider authoritative for money movement, auto-repaired through compensating postings and state transitions where the case is known, and otherwise routed to an ops queue, with open breaks tracked by age and value."}],"extra":["**Unknown outcome state:** model provider timeouts as `UNKNOWN`, not failed, and resolve them by status query or reconciliation; treating a timeout as failure and retrying as a new request is how double charges happen.","**Control totals first:** compare record counts and sums per file before line matching, and treat a missing or late settlement file as a break in itself.","**Continuous checks:** besides the nightly batch, per-transaction checks shortly after an event (no webhook within 15 minutes, so query status) cut detection time from a day to minutes.","**Four-eyes on manual repairs:** manual adjustments above a threshold should need a second approver, and the engineers who write ledger code should not be the sole approvers of corrections."],"redFlags":["Assumes retries remove all inconsistency"]},{"id":110,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"Explain direct, topic, fanout and headers exchanges.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Publishers send to exchanges, which route to queues via bindings: direct matches the exact routing key, topic matches dotted wildcard patterns, fanout copies to every bound queue, headers matches header values.","answer":["The model is publisher to **exchange**, exchange to **queues** via **bindings**, queues to consumers. An exchange stores nothing; it only evaluates bindings. If nothing matches, the message is dropped unless you publish with `mandatory` and handle returns, or configure an alternate exchange. The queue is what buffers and delivers. The default exchange (`\"\"`) is a direct exchange where every queue is bound by its own name.","**Direct** routes when the binding key equals the routing key exactly; good for commands and work queues (`refund.requested`). **Topic** matches dot-separated words where `*` is exactly one word and `#` is zero or more (`payment.*.failed`, `payment.#`); the usual choice for domain events. **Fanout** ignores the routing key and copies to every bound queue; right for true broadcast such as cache invalidation, wrong for targeted delivery because every consumer then receives and discards messages it does not want. **Headers** ignores the routing key and matches message headers with `x-match` set to `all` or `any`; flexible but rarely needed, slower and harder to see.","**Topology should mirror business routing.** A common shape is one durable topic exchange per domain (`payments.events`), routing keys built from stable business attributes (`payment.<event>.<merchant>`), and each consuming service owning its queue and bindings, which gives pub/sub between services and competing consumers inside one. Declare it as code so it is reviewed and versioned."],"keyPoints":["Direct routes by exact key","topic by patterns","fanout broadcasts","headers uses header matching","topology should match business routing"],"examples":[{"title":"Topic exchange for payment events in Spring AMQP","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass PaymentTopology {\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables paymentEvents() {\n        TopicExchange events = ExchangeBuilder.topicExchange(\"payments.events\").durable(true).build();\n        FanoutExchange invalidate = ExchangeBuilder.fanoutExchange(\"config.invalidate\").build();\n\n        Queue ledger = QueueBuilder.durable(\"ledger.payment-events\").quorum().build();\n        Queue fraud = QueueBuilder.durable(\"fraud.payment-failures\").quorum().build();\n        Queue localCache = new AnonymousQueue(); // one per instance, deleted on disconnect\n\n        return new Declarables(\n            events, invalidate, ledger, fraud, localCache,\n            // routing key format: payment.<event>.<merchantId>\n            BindingBuilder.bind(ledger).to(events).with(\"payment.captured.*\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(ledger).to(events).with(\"payment.refunded.*\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(fraud).to(events).with(\"payment.failed.*\"),\n            // true broadcast: every instance must drop its cache\n            BindingBuilder.bind(localCache).to(invalidate));\n    }\n}","note":"Each consuming service declares its own queue and only the keys it needs; fanout is reserved for the one case where every instance really must get every message."},{"title":"Which queue receives which routing key","lang":"text","code":"Published routing key          ledger (payment.captured.*)   fraud (payment.failed.*)\npayment.captured.m_1042        yes                           no\npayment.failed.m_1042          no                            yes\npayment.failed.m_1042.retry    no                            no  (* matches one word only)\npayment.refunded.m_7            yes (payment.refunded.*)      no","note":"The third row is the classic surprise: `*` matches exactly one word, so use `#` when the key may grow extra segments."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you route events by merchant and event type?","a":"Use a topic exchange `payments.events` with routing key `payment.<eventType>.<merchantId>`. Consumers bind by pattern: all captures with `payment.captured.*`, everything for one merchant with `payment.*.m_1042`, everything with `payment.#`. Avoid a queue per merchant for thousands of merchants because each queue has broker overhead; if a few large merchants need isolation, add a tier segment such as `payment.captured.dedicated.m_1042` so they get dedicated queues while the rest share `payment.captured.shared.*`, since topic bindings cannot express exclusions."}],"extra":["**Unroutable messages:** without `mandatory=true` plus `RabbitTemplate.setReturnsCallback`, or an `alternate-exchange` argument, a typo in a routing key silently discards messages.","**Exchange to exchange bindings:** RabbitMQ can bind exchanges to exchanges, which lets you layer a domain topic exchange into per-team exchanges without publishers knowing about them.","**Streams for replay:** if consumers need to re-read history or you have very high fan-out, RabbitMQ Streams (and partitioned super streams) give an append-only log with non-destructive reads, closer to Kafka.","**Routing keys are not a data contract:** also put the merchant ID and event type in the body or headers, because consumers should not parse routing keys for business data and keys may be restructured."],"redFlags":["Cannot distinguish exchange from queue","uses fanout for targeted delivery"]},{"id":111,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"Ack, nack, reject and requeue: what are the risks?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use manual acks and ack only after the work is durably committed; nack or reject can requeue or dead-letter, so requeue only transient failures, bound the attempts, and send poison messages to a DLQ.","answer":["With manual acknowledgement each delivery carries a delivery tag. `basicAck` removes the message; `basicReject(tag, requeue)` rejects one message and `basicNack(tag, multiple, requeue)` is RabbitMQ's extension that can reject many. With `requeue=true` the message goes back to the queue for redelivery; with `requeue=false` it is dead-lettered if the queue has a dead-letter exchange, otherwise discarded. Unacked deliveries are redelivered when the channel or connection closes. Auto-ack mode treats a message as done the moment it is sent, which is at-most-once.","The risks follow directly. **Ack before processing** loses the message if the consumer crashes. **Ack after durable processing** is correct but means a crash between commit and ack produces a duplicate, so the consumer must be idempotent. **Requeue on a permanent error** (deserialization failure, validation error, a bug) creates a hot loop: the same message is redelivered immediately, burns CPU, floods logs and can starve everything behind it. `multiple=true` can ack or nack messages that another thread is still processing.","Guardrails: classify errors, reject permanent ones without requeue into a DLQ, retry transient ones with a delay and a bounded count (retry queues, Spring Retry, or quorum queue delivery limits), and keep processing well under the broker's consumer timeout. In Spring AMQP, note that the default `AUTO` mode requeues on any exception unless you disable `default-requeue-rejected`."],"keyPoints":["Ack after durable processing","nack/reject can requeue or dead-letter","endless requeue creates hot loops","manual ack controls delivery safety"],"examples":[{"title":"Manual ack with error classification","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass RefundConsumer {\n    private final RefundService refunds;\n    private final MessageConverter converter;\n    private final RabbitTemplate rabbit;\n\n    RefundConsumer(RefundService refunds, MessageConverter converter, RabbitTemplate rabbit) {\n        this.refunds = refunds;\n        this.converter = converter;\n        this.rabbit = rabbit;\n    }\n\n    @RabbitListener(queues = \"refunds.requests\", ackMode = \"MANUAL\")\n    void on(Message message, Channel channel) throws IOException {\n        long tag = message.getMessageProperties().getDeliveryTag();\n        // BAD: channel.basicAck(tag, false) here, before the work, loses the refund on a crash\n        try {\n            refunds.process((RefundRequested) converter.fromMessage(message)); // commits DB tx\n            channel.basicAck(tag, false);            // GOOD: ack after the durable commit\n        } catch (TransientDataAccessException e) {\n            channel.basicNack(tag, false, false);    // DLX routes it to a delayed retry queue\n        } catch (Exception e) {\n            rabbit.send(\"refunds\", \"refund.parked\", message); // poison: park it for humans\n            channel.basicAck(tag, false);            // never requeue forever\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Transient and permanent failures take different paths; nothing uses `requeue=true`, so a bad message can never spin in a hot loop."},{"title":"Safer defaults for Spring AMQP auto mode","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  rabbitmq:\n    listener:\n      simple:\n        acknowledge-mode: auto           # ack when the listener returns normally\n        default-requeue-rejected: false  # exceptions dead-letter instead of requeueing\n        prefetch: 20","note":"With `default-requeue-rejected: false` an unexpected exception goes to the queue's dead-letter exchange instead of being redelivered immediately forever."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What happens if the consumer crashes after DB commit but before ack?","a":"The connection drops, the broker still sees the delivery as unacked, and it requeues it with `redelivered=true` for the same or another consumer. The business effect is already committed, so processing it again duplicates the effect unless the consumer is idempotent, for example with an inbox table keyed by message ID inserted in the same transaction, or a conditional state transition that becomes a no-op the second time. This is the normal at-least-once case, so design for it rather than trying to prevent it."}],"extra":["**Consumer timeout:** RabbitMQ closes the channel if a delivery stays unacked longer than `consumer_timeout` (30 minutes by default), which redelivers everything that channel held; long-running jobs need a different pattern.","**Quorum queue delivery limit:** quorum queues count redeliveries and dead-letter a message once it exceeds `delivery-limit` (RabbitMQ 4.0 made 20 the default), turning a hot loop into a DLQ entry.","**Tags belong to a channel:** acks must go on the channel that received the delivery; after a reconnect old tags are invalid and acking them closes the channel with an unknown delivery tag error.","**Consumer acks are half the story:** use publisher confirms (`spring.rabbitmq.publisher-confirm-type: correlated`) and persistent messages, otherwise messages can be lost before they ever reach the queue."],"redFlags":["Acknowledges before processing","requeues poison messages forever"]},{"id":112,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"How do prefetch and consumer concurrency affect throughput and fairness?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Prefetch caps unacked messages buffered per consumer; size it from processing time, latency and memory so consumers stay busy without hoarding, and add consumers only while downstream capacity allows.","answer":["Prefetch is set with `basic.qos`: the broker pushes up to N unacknowledged deliveries to a consumer and then waits for acks. Prefetch 0 means unlimited, so the broker pushes as fast as it can, the consumer's memory grows with the backlog and fairness disappears. Spring AMQP containers default to 250, which is fine for millisecond messages and far too high for slow ones.","**Throughput:** a consumer needs enough messages in flight to hide the round trip between ack and next delivery. For fast handlers (about 1 ms) with a few ms of network latency, a prefetch of tens keeps it busy; for slow tasks (seconds) a prefetch of 1 to 5 is plenty, and more only hoards work. Memory matters on both sides because every unacked message is held by the broker and the client.","**Fairness and redelivery:** RabbitMQ round-robins between consumers, but a consumer with a large prefetch grabs a big batch; a slow or stuck consumer then sits on hundreds of messages while others idle, a newly scaled pod gets nothing until buffers drain, and a crash redelivers the whole batch at once, which shows up as a duplicate and latency burst.","**Concurrency multiplies:** total in flight is instances times consumers times prefetch. Adding consumers beyond what the database pool or a rate-limited provider can absorb just moves the queue into thread pools, causing timeouts, retries and more load."],"keyPoints":["Prefetch limits unacked messages per consumer","tune with processing time and memory","high values can create unfairness and large redelivery bursts"],"examples":[{"title":"Slow task queue sized to downstream capacity","lang":"yaml","code":"# Statement PDF generation: about 2 s per message, each needs one DB connection\nspring:\n  rabbitmq:\n    listener:\n      type: simple\n      simple:\n        acknowledge-mode: auto\n        prefetch: 2           # small buffer: fair spreading, small redelivery burst\n        concurrency: 4        # consumers per pod at start\n        max-concurrency: 8    # never more consumers than the pool below can serve\n  datasource:\n    hikari:\n      maximum-pool-size: 10","note":"The ceiling of 8 consumers per pod is derived from the connection pool, not from the queue depth, so scaling out cannot exhaust the database."},{"title":"Why the default can hurt a slow queue","lang":"text","code":"Pods 6, consumers per pod 10, prefetch 250 (Spring AMQP default)\nUnacked in flight     = 6 x 10 x 250 = 15,000 messages\nProcessing time       = 200 ms, so each consumer buffers 50 s of work\nOne pod restarts      = up to 2,500 messages redelivered at once\nNew pod added         = idle while old consumers drain their buffers\n\nPrefetch 5            = 300 in flight, about 1 s buffered per consumer, fair spreading","note":"The same queue with a small prefetch reacts to scaling within seconds instead of minutes and limits the blast radius of a crash."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you tune a slow task queue?","a":"Measure processing time (p50 and p99) and identify the bottleneck first. Set a low prefetch (1 to 5) so work spreads evenly and a crash redelivers little, and cap total consumers at what the downstream budget allows (DB pool size, provider rate limit), scaling pods on backlog or message age, for example with the KEDA RabbitMQ scaler, rather than CPU. Keep each task well under `consumer_timeout`, or switch long tasks to a job table where the message only triggers work tracked in the database. Put slow tasks on their own queue so they do not delay fast messages."}],"extra":["**Per-consumer semantics:** RabbitMQ applies `basic.qos` with `global=false` per consumer, unlike the AMQP spec's per-channel meaning, and quorum queues do not support global QoS at all.","**Consumer utilisation:** the management UI and Prometheus expose how often the queue could deliver immediately; below 100% with a backlog means prefetch is too low or consumers too slow.","**Container choice:** `SimpleMessageListenerContainer` supports dynamic scaling between `concurrency` and `max-concurrency`, while `DirectMessageListenerContainer` uses `consumers-per-queue` and runs the listener on the client library's threads.","**Single active consumer:** with `x-single-active-consumer` for ordering, only one consumer works at a time, so prefetch and handler speed are your only throughput levers."],"redFlags":["Sets unlimited prefetch","increases consumers without downstream capacity"]},{"id":113,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"How should retries and dead-letter queues be designed?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Classify failures, retry only transient ones a bounded number of times with backoff (via delayed retry queues), and dead-letter everything else to an owned DLQ with alerting, inspection and safe replay tooling.","answer":["Start with **classification**. Transient failures (timeouts, connection resets, HTTP 503 or 429, lock timeouts) may succeed later; permanent failures (deserialization errors, validation failures, business rule violations, unknown entity, a null pointer bug) never will, so they go straight to the DLQ. Unknown errors are treated as transient but with a bounded attempt count.","RabbitMQ has no built-in retry delay, so the usual pattern is **retry queues with a per-queue TTL**: the main queue dead-letters rejected messages into a retry exchange, the retry queue holds them for its TTL and dead-letters them back to the work exchange. Several tiers (10 s, 1 min, 10 min) give exponential backoff. The consumer counts attempts from the `x-death` header or its own header and, once the limit is hit, parks the message. In-process retry with Spring Retry is simpler for short delays but blocks the consumer thread.","The **DLQ (parking lot)** is an operational product, not a trash can: keep original headers, the exception, attempt count and first failure time; give it an owning team, alerts on depth and oldest message age, a runbook, and a **replay tool** that can inspect, filter and republish to the original exchange at a controlled rate. Replays must go through the same idempotent consumer."],"keyPoints":["Use bounded retries with delay/backoff, classify transient vs permanent errors, dead-letter poison messages and provide replay/inspection workflow"],"examples":[{"title":"Retry and parking topology","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass RefundRetryTopology {\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables refundQueues() {\n        DirectExchange work = new DirectExchange(\"refunds\");\n        DirectExchange retry = new DirectExchange(\"refunds.retry\");\n\n        Queue main = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.requests\").quorum()\n            .deadLetterExchange(\"refunds.retry\")   // rejected messages go to the retry tier\n            .build();\n        Queue wait30s = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.retry.30s\")\n            .ttl(30_000)\n            .deadLetterExchange(\"refunds\")          // expired messages return to work\n            .deadLetterRoutingKey(\"refund\")\n            .build();\n        Queue parkingLot = QueueBuilder.durable(\"refunds.parking-lot\").quorum().build();\n\n        return new Declarables(work, retry, main, wait30s, parkingLot,\n            BindingBuilder.bind(main).to(work).with(\"refund\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(wait30s).to(retry).with(\"refund\"),\n            BindingBuilder.bind(parkingLot).to(work).with(\"refund.parked\"));\n    }\n}","note":"A rejected message waits 30 s in a queue nobody consumes, then flows back to the main queue; nothing spins, and the broker does the delay."},{"title":"Consumer that bounds attempts and parks poison messages","lang":"java","code":"@RabbitListener(queues = \"refunds.requests\")\nvoid on(Message message) {\n    try {\n        refunds.process((RefundRequested) converter.fromMessage(message));\n    } catch (ProviderUnavailableException | TransientDataAccessException e) {\n        if (attempts(message) >= 5) {\n            park(message, e);   // exhausted: stop retrying, container acks\n            return;\n        }\n        throw new AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException(\"transient, retry later\", e);\n    } catch (RuntimeException e) {\n        park(message, e);       // permanent: validation error, bug, unknown refund\n    }\n}\n\nprivate long attempts(Message m) {\n    List<Map<String, ?>> deaths = m.getMessageProperties().getXDeathHeader();\n    return deaths == null || deaths.isEmpty() ? 0 : (Long) deaths.get(0).get(\"count\");\n}\n\nprivate void park(Message m, Exception e) {\n    m.getMessageProperties().setHeader(\"x-exception\", e.getClass().getName() + \": \" + e.getMessage());\n    rabbit.send(\"refunds\", \"refund.parked\", m);\n}","note":"Throwing `AmqpRejectAndDontRequeueException` routes the message through the dead-letter exchange into the retry tier; the exception header gives whoever triages the parking lot the context they need."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you avoid a retry storm?","a":"Bound the attempts and use exponential backoff with jitter so failed messages do not return in synchronized waves, and cap retry traffic to a small fraction of normal traffic (a retry budget). Put a circuit breaker such as Resilience4j in front of the failing dependency and slow or pause consumers (stop the listener container or lower concurrency) while it is open, instead of hammering a service that is down. Retry at one layer only, because retries in the client, service, consumer and gateway multiply. Once the root cause is fixed, drain the DLQ with a rate-limited replay."}],"extra":["**Per-message TTL trap:** in classic queues expired messages are dead-lettered only when they reach the head, so mixing different per-message TTLs in one retry queue stalls short delays behind long ones; use one queue per delay tier.","**Delayed message plugin:** `rabbitmq-delayed-message-exchange` supports per-message `x-delay`, but delayed messages are not replicated and it is not meant for large volumes, so TTL tiers are the more predictable choice.","**Spring's recoverers:** with `spring.rabbitmq.listener.simple.retry.enabled`, exhausted retries go to a `MessageRecoverer`; `RepublishMessageRecoverer` republishes to a parking exchange with exception message and stack trace headers.","**Kafka equivalent:** Spring Kafka's `@RetryableTopic` creates delayed retry topics and a dead-letter topic automatically, the same design with different plumbing.","**Replay safety:** replays days later can meet changed schemas and changed state, so replay through the normal idempotent consumer, throttle it, and record who replayed what and why."],"redFlags":["Immediate endless retry","DLQ with no ownership or replay process"]},{"id":114,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"What ordering does RabbitMQ provide?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"A single queue delivers FIFO, but multiple consumers, prefetch, requeues and retry queues change processing order; strict order needs one active consumer per ordering key or sequence checks in the consumer.","answer":["RabbitMQ guarantees that messages published on one channel, through one exchange, to one queue are enqueued in publish order, and the queue delivers them FIFO. There is no ordering across different publishers or channels, and delivery order is not processing order.","Processing order breaks as soon as you add **competing consumers** (message 2 on a fast consumer finishes before message 1 on a slow one), **concurrency inside a consumer** with prefetch above 1, **requeue after nack** (the message is redelivered after later ones may already be done), **redelivery after a crash**, **retry queues with TTL** (the message rejoins much later), **DLQ replays**, and priority queues. Parallel consumers and strict end-to-end order are fundamentally at odds.","For strict ordering you have three tools. A **single consumer** with sequential processing, or `x-single-active-consumer` so one consumer is active and others stand by for failover; this caps throughput. **Partitioning by key**: hash the ordering key (account ID) to N queues, each with a single active consumer, giving per-key order with N-way parallelism; the consistent hash exchange or super streams do this in the broker. **Sequence control**: the producer stamps a per-key sequence and the consumer applies only the next expected value, parking gaps. Most designs combine partitioning with sequence checks, because retries can still reorder within a partition."],"keyPoints":["A queue is ordered, but multiple consumers, redelivery and retries can change processing order","strict ordering usually requires one consumer or sequence control"],"examples":[{"title":"Per-account ordering with hashed queues and single active consumer","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass AccountEventPartitions {\n    static final int PARTITIONS = 8;\n\n    @Bean\n    Declarables accountPartitions() {\n        DirectExchange ex = new DirectExchange(\"accounts.events\");\n        List<Declarable> all = new ArrayList<>(List.of(ex));\n        for (int p = 0; p < PARTITIONS; p++) {\n            Queue q = QueueBuilder.durable(\"accounts.events.\" + p)\n                .quorum()\n                .singleActiveConsumer()   // one consumer processes, others wait for failover\n                .build();\n            all.add(q);\n            all.add(BindingBuilder.bind(q).to(ex).with(String.valueOf(p)));\n        }\n        return new Declarables(all);\n    }\n}\n\n@Component\nclass AccountEventPublisher {\n    private final RabbitTemplate rabbit;\n\n    AccountEventPublisher(RabbitTemplate rabbit) {\n        this.rabbit = rabbit;\n    }\n\n    void publish(AccountEvent e) {\n        int partition = Math.floorMod(e.accountId().hashCode(), AccountEventPartitions.PARTITIONS);\n        rabbit.convertAndSend(\"accounts.events\", String.valueOf(partition), e);\n    }\n}","note":"All events for one account land in the same queue with exactly one active consumer, so they are processed in order while 8 accounts' streams run in parallel; the consumer must still process sequentially (one thread per queue)."},{"title":"How two consumers reorder one account","lang":"text","code":"Queue order:  A1 (debit 500)   A2 (credit 200)   A3 (debit 100)\nConsumer X takes A1, slow DB call   (400 ms)\nConsumer Y takes A2, fast path      (20 ms)   -> A2 committed first\nConsumer Y takes A3                 (20 ms)   -> A3 committed second\nConsumer X finishes A1                        -> A1 committed last","note":"The queue delivered in perfect FIFO order, yet the commits happened as A2, A3, A1."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you preserve order per account?","a":"Route by account: hash the account ID into N queues (consistent hash exchange or publisher-side hashing), each with single active consumer and strictly sequential processing, which gives per-account order with N-way parallelism. Stamp each event with a per-account sequence from the source, such as the aggregate version or an outbox sequence, and have the consumer apply only the next expected value, parking gaps. On a transient failure, retry in place and block that partition, or park all later events for that account, because sending one event to a delayed retry queue lets later events overtake it."}],"extra":["**Consistent hash exchange:** the `rabbitmq_consistent_hash_exchange` plugin routes by a hash of the routing key or a header across bound queues, with the binding key acting as weight, so publishers do not need to know the partition count.","**Super streams:** RabbitMQ Streams support partitioned super streams with single active consumer per partition, giving Kafka-like per-key ordering plus replay.","**Priority queues:** with `x-max-priority`, higher-priority messages overtake lower ones, so FIFO holds only within one priority level.","**Publisher retries:** a publisher that republishes an unconfirmed message after later ones were confirmed creates reordering at the source, which only a per-key sequence number lets the consumer detect."],"redFlags":["Assumes strict end-to-end order with parallel consumers"]},{"id":115,"category":"RabbitMQ","question":"A queue has millions of messages. How do you investigate?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Treat it as a flow problem: compare publish and ack rates, check consumer health, processing latency, redeliveries, prefetch, downstream saturation and oldest message age before you scale consumers or purge anything.","answer":["First establish the shape: is the queue growing, flat or draining, and since when? Compare **publish rate with ack rate**, split depth into ready versus unacked, check **consumer count** and consumer utilisation, and look at the **age of the oldest message** against the business SLA. Correlate the start time with deploys, traffic spikes and downstream incidents.","Then narrow down the cause. No or few consumers: pods crashed, lost connections, or channels closed by the consumer timeout. Consumers present but slow: **processing latency** went up because a **downstream** dependency is saturated (DB locks or pool exhaustion, a provider returning 429). A high redeliver rate on the same message IDs: a **poison message** hot loop. Huge unacked counts on one consumer while others idle: **prefetch** too high. Publish rate jumped: an upstream bug or retry storm. Publishers blocked: a broker memory or disk alarm.","Act on the bottleneck. Scaling consumers only helps if the downstream has headroom; otherwise it deepens the outage. If fresh messages matter most, divert the backlog to a side queue with a shovel and process new traffic first. **Never purge** without impact analysis: the queue may hold payments or refunds, so export or shovel to an archive first and get the owning team's sign-off. Afterward, add alerts on oldest message age and consumer count, not just depth."],"keyPoints":["Check publish/ack rates, consumer health, processing latency, poison messages, prefetch, downstream saturation and age of oldest message before scaling"],"examples":[{"title":"First commands during the investigation","lang":"bash","code":"# Depth split, consumers and utilisation per queue\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers consumer_utilisation\n\n# Who consumes, with which prefetch and ack mode\nrabbitmqctl list_consumers queue_name channel_pid prefetch_count ack_required\n\n# Broker alarms that block publishers or slow the node\nrabbitmq-diagnostics alarms\nrabbitmq-diagnostics memory_breakdown\n\n# Peek at a few messages to spot poison payloads (requeues them, sets redelivered)\nrabbitmqadmin get queue=refunds.requests count=5 ackmode=ack_requeue_true","note":"Rates come from the management UI or Prometheus; these commands answer the structural questions of who is consuming, how much is in flight, and whether the broker itself is under pressure."},{"title":"Symptom to cause to first action","lang":"text","code":"Signal                                    Likely cause                     First action\npublish rate up, ack rate flat            traffic spike or upstream bug    check producer, rate limit\nack rate dropped, consumers = 0           consumers crashed or timed out   pods, logs, consumer_timeout\nack rate dropped, consumers healthy       downstream slow (DB, provider)   latency, pools, 429s; do not scale\nhigh redeliver rate, same message IDs     poison message hot loop          reject to DLQ, fix handler\nhuge unacked on one consumer              prefetch too high                lower prefetch\npublishers blocked, memory or disk alarm  broker resource alarm            free resources, check other queues","note":"Every row except the first is fixed by something other than adding consumers, which is why scaling is the last step, not the first."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which metric matters more than queue depth?","a":"The **age of the oldest message** (or end-to-end lag from publish timestamp to processing) matters more, because it maps directly to the business SLA: a million small messages draining at 50,000 per second is healthy, while 200 refunds stuck for an hour may be a breach. Pair it with the net drain rate (ack rate minus publish rate) to know whether the backlog is shrinking and when it will clear. Depth alone says nothing about either."}],"extra":["**Little's law for stakeholders:** backlog divided by net drain rate is time to recovery; 3,000,000 messages draining at a net 500 per second clear in about 100 minutes.","**Measure lag at the consumer:** set the AMQP `timestamp` property when publishing and emit now minus timestamp as a processing lag metric, because oldest message age is not exposed uniformly for every queue type.","**Protect the broker:** very long queues cost memory, disk and recovery time; where dropping or refusing is acceptable, set `x-max-length` or a TTL with `x-overflow` such as `reject-publish` so one stuck consumer cannot take down the cluster.","**Autoscaling cap:** scaling consumers with KEDA on backlog needs a maximum replica count tied to downstream capacity, or the autoscaler becomes a denial of service against your own database."],"redFlags":["Only adds consumers","purges queue without impact analysis"]},{"id":116,"category":"Security","question":"Authentication vs authorization: where should each be enforced?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Authentication establishes who the caller is; authorization decides whether that identity may perform this action on this resource. A gateway can authenticate and do coarse checks, but every service must enforce resource and domain authorization.","answer":["**Authentication** verifies credentials or a token and establishes a principal: a user, a service, a tenant. Failure is a 401. **Authorization** is a policy decision over subject, action, resource and context: scopes and roles, but also ownership, tenant, resource state and limits (a merchant may refund only its own captured payments, up to the captured amount). Failure is a 403, or a 404 when you do not want to confirm the resource exists.","At the **edge**, a gateway terminates TLS, validates tokens (signature, `exp`, `iss`, `aud`), rate limits, and can do coarse checks such as requiring the `payments:refund` scope on a route. It cannot do domain authorization because it does not know who owns payment 9f2c or what state it is in. So each **service** must validate the token itself (do not trust an `X-User-Id` header anyone inside the network could forge) and enforce object and domain rules in its own code, ideally in the data access path so a missing check fails closed.","In Spring Boot 3 that means a resource server per service, URL rules for coarse checks, `@PreAuthorize` for action level checks, and ownership built into queries (`findByIdAndMerchantId`). Default deny, log denials for audit, and write negative tests. Broken object level authorization is number one in the OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023) precisely because teams stop at authentication."],"keyPoints":["Authentication establishes identity","authorization decides allowed actions","gateway may authenticate, but services must enforce resource/domain authorization"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: authenticated means allowed; GOOD: scope plus ownership in the query","lang":"java","code":"@RestController\n@RequestMapping(\"/payments\")\nclass PaymentController {\n    private final PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    PaymentController(PaymentRepository payments) {\n        this.payments = payments;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: any authenticated merchant can read any payment by guessing or leaking IDs\n    @GetMapping(\"/legacy/{id}\")\n    PaymentView legacy(@PathVariable UUID id) {\n        return payments.findById(id).map(PaymentView::from).orElseThrow();\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: action checked by scope, object ownership enforced by the query itself\n    @GetMapping(\"/{id}\")\n    @PreAuthorize(\"hasAuthority('SCOPE_payments:read')\")\n    PaymentView get(@PathVariable UUID id, @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt) {\n        String merchantId = jwt.getClaimAsString(\"merchant_id\");\n        return payments.findByIdAndMerchantId(id, merchantId)\n            .map(PaymentView::from)\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND));\n    }\n}","note":"Another merchant's payment ID returns 404, exactly like a nonexistent one, so the endpoint neither leaks data nor confirms which IDs exist."},{"title":"Each service is its own resource server","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\n@EnableMethodSecurity\nclass SecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n                .requestMatchers(\"/actuator/health/**\").permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, \"/payments/*/refunds\").hasAuthority(\"SCOPE_payments:refund\")\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(rs -> rs.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .build();\n    }\n}","note":"The service validates the JWT itself even behind a gateway; URL rules give coarse checks, while domain rules stay in the service and repository code."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you prevent IDOR/BOLA?","a":"Never treat a client-supplied ID as proof of access: every read and write checks that the object belongs to the caller's tenant or user, preferably by scoping the query itself (`findByIdAndMerchantId`, or PostgreSQL row-level security) so a forgotten check fails closed. Random UUIDs make enumeration harder but are not authorization. Centralize the rule in repository methods or a policy component rather than per controller, and add automated tests that call each endpoint with a second tenant's token and expect 403 or 404."}],"extra":["**Confused deputy:** when service A calls B with its own powerful credentials on behalf of a user, B cannot apply the user's permissions; propagate user context via OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693) or pass a downscoped user token.","**Relationship-based access control:** for complex sharing models (teams, delegated merchant users), Zanzibar-style systems such as OpenFGA or a policy engine like OPA keep rules consistent across services.","**Row-level security:** PostgreSQL RLS policies keyed on a per-transaction tenant setting give a second, database-enforced tenant boundary if application code forgets a filter.","**Cheap negative tests:** `spring-security-test` lets MockMvc requests carry a `jwt()` with another tenant's claims, so every endpoint can assert a 403 or 404 for foreign resources."],"redFlags":["Assumes authenticated users may access everything or gateway is enough"]},{"id":117,"category":"Security","question":"What are common JWT design mistakes?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Typical JWT mistakes are long-lived unrevocable tokens, weak validation of algorithm, signature, issuer, audience and expiry, sensitive data in readable claims, no key rotation or revocation plan, and tokens leaking into logs.","answer":["**Validation mistakes** come first. Letting the token header choose the algorithm enables `alg: none` and algorithm confusion (an RS256 public key reused as an HS256 secret), so pin the expected algorithm. Skipping `aud` lets a token minted for one API be replayed against another; skipping `iss` accepts tokens from the wrong authorization server; ignoring `exp` and `nbf` makes stolen tokens live forever. Decoding the payload in a filter without verifying the signature is the same as no authentication.","**Content mistakes:** a JWS is signed, not encrypted. Anyone holding it can base64url-decode the claims, so no personal data, account numbers or internal details belong there. Keep tokens small because they travel on every request.","**Lifecycle mistakes:** a self-contained token cannot be revoked before `exp` unless you build for it. Use short-lived access tokens (minutes), refresh tokens with rotation and reuse detection, and a revocation path for high-risk cases. Plan **key rotation**: publish keys in a JWKS with `kid`, add the new key before signing with it, and keep old keys until their tokens expire.","**Leakage mistakes:** logging `Authorization` headers, putting tokens in URLs, or storing them in `localStorage` where any XSS can read them. Mask tokens in logs and APM, and prefer a backend-for-frontend with HttpOnly cookies for browser apps."],"keyPoints":["Long-lived tokens, weak validation, sensitive claims, no rotation/revocation plan, accepting wrong issuer/audience/algorithm and logging tokens"],"examples":[{"title":"Strict JWT validation in Spring Security","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass JwtConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(@Value(\"${auth.issuer}\") String issuer,\n                          @Value(\"${auth.jwk-set-uri}\") String jwkSetUri) {\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri)\n            .jwsAlgorithm(SignatureAlgorithm.RS256)   // pin the algorithm, never trust the header\n            .build();\n\n        OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> audience = new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(\n            JwtClaimNames.AUD, aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(\"payments-api\"));\n\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\n            JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer), // exp, nbf with small skew, iss\n            audience));                                    // reject tokens minted for other APIs\n        return decoder;\n    }\n}","note":"The default validators check time and issuer, but audience must be added explicitly; without it any token from the same issuer is accepted by every API."},{"title":"A reasonable access token payload","lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"iss\": \"https://auth.example.com\",\n  \"sub\": \"usr_8812\",\n  \"aud\": \"payments-api\",\n  \"iat\": 1790000000,\n  \"exp\": 1790000900,\n  \"jti\": \"b1c8f0e2-4a57-4d0c-9d36-51f1a7e0c3aa\",\n  \"scope\": \"payments:read payments:refund\",\n  \"merchant_id\": \"m_1042\"\n}","note":"Fifteen minutes of lifetime, a single audience, a `jti` for revocation lists, and only identifiers and scopes: no email, IBAN or role dumps that anyone holding the token could read."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Access vs refresh token?","a":"An **access token** is short-lived (minutes), audience-restricted, sent to resource servers on every call and validated statelessly. A **refresh token** is longer-lived, sent only to the authorization server's token endpoint to obtain new access tokens, and must be stored more carefully (server-side in a BFF or an HttpOnly cookie). Rotate refresh tokens on every use and detect reuse: if an old refresh token is presented again, revoke the whole token family. Because revocation is enforced at refresh time, the access token lifetime bounds how long a revoked session keeps working."},{"q":"How do you revoke compromised sessions?","a":"Revoke at the authorization server first (refresh tokens and the session or `sid`), so no new access tokens are issued. Outstanding access tokens stay valid until `exp`, which is why they must be short; for immediate cut-off on sensitive APIs, check a denylist of `jti` or `sid` in Redis with a TTL equal to the remaining lifetime, compare `iat` against a per-user not-before timestamp, or use opaque tokens with introspection. If a signing key is compromised, remove it from the JWKS, which invalidates everything signed with it. OIDC back-channel logout propagates the termination to relying parties."}],"extra":["**Header parameters are attacker input:** never fetch keys from a `jku` or `x5u` URL in the token, and resolve `kid` only against your configured JWKS; injection through `kid` lookups has happened in real libraries.","**Sender-constrained tokens:** DPoP (RFC 9449) or mutual TLS bound tokens (RFC 8705) make a stolen access token useless without the client's private key.","**JWKS caching:** resource servers cache the key set; a decoder should refetch when it sees an unknown `kid`, otherwise a key rotation causes a burst of 401s until the cache expires.","**Permission bloat:** stuffing full permission lists into tokens inflates headers past proxy limits and means permission changes lag until expiry; carry coarse scopes and resolve fine-grained permissions server-side."],"redFlags":["Treats signed JWT as encrypted","never validates audience or expiry"]},{"id":118,"category":"Security","question":"OAuth 2.0 vs OpenID Connect.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"OAuth 2.0 is delegated authorization: a client gets an access token to call APIs; OpenID Connect adds authentication on top with the `openid` scope, an ID token, UserInfo and standard claims.","answer":["**OAuth 2.0** defines a resource owner, a client, an authorization server and a resource server. The client obtains an **access token** with scopes and uses it to call the resource server on the user's behalf, or on its own behalf with client credentials. The access token is meant for the API, its format is unspecified (opaque or JWT), and it tells the client nothing reliable about who the user is. Using an access token as proof of login is the classic mistake: a token issued to a different client can be substituted.","**OpenID Connect** adds authentication. The client requests `scope=openid`, and the provider returns an **ID token**: a signed JWT addressed to the client (`aud` is the client ID) with `iss`, `sub`, `exp`, `iat`, `nonce` and optionally `auth_time`, `acr` and `amr`. OIDC also standardizes the UserInfo endpoint, standard profile claims, discovery at `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, JWKS and logout.","In practice: user login to a web app is OIDC with the authorization code flow and PKCE; APIs are OAuth resource servers that accept access tokens for their audience and never ID tokens; machine to machine calls use client credentials with no user and no OIDC at all. In Spring Boot that is `oauth2Login()` from the OAuth2 client starter for the app, and the resource server starter for APIs."],"keyPoints":["OAuth delegates authorization to access resources","OIDC adds identity/authentication with ID tokens and user-info semantics"],"examples":[{"title":"Authorization code flow with PKCE and OIDC (query wrapped for readability)","lang":"http","code":"GET /authorize?response_type=code&client_id=merchant-portal\n    &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fportal.example.com%2Flogin%2Foauth2%2Fcode%2Fidp\n    &scope=openid%20profile%20payments%3Aread&state=af0ifjsldkj&nonce=n0S6WzA2Mj\n    &code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&code_challenge_method=S256 HTTP/1.1\nHost: idp.example.com\n\nPOST /token HTTP/1.1\nHost: idp.example.com\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\ngrant_type=authorization_code&code=SplxlOBeZQQYbYS6WxSbIA&client_id=merchant-portal\n&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fportal.example.com%2Flogin%2Foauth2%2Fcode%2Fidp\n&code_verifier=dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"access_token\": \"eyJhbGciOi...\", \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \"expires_in\": 900,\n \"refresh_token\": \"8xLOxBtZp8\", \"id_token\": \"eyJraWQiOi...\", \"scope\": \"openid profile payments:read\"}","note":"The `openid` scope is what makes this OIDC: the response carries an `id_token` for the portal to log the user in, and a separate `access_token` for calling the payments API."},{"title":"OIDC login client in Spring Boot","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      client:\n        registration:\n          idp:\n            client-id: merchant-portal\n            client-secret: ${IDP_CLIENT_SECRET}\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\n            scope: openid, profile, payments:read\n            redirect-uri: \"{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}\"\n        provider:\n          idp:\n            issuer-uri: https://idp.example.com","note":"With `issuer-uri`, Spring discovers endpoints and keys from the provider's discovery document and validates the ID token's signature, issuer, audience and nonce for you."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Authorization code with PKCE: why?","a":"The authorization code travels through the browser's redirect, where it can be intercepted by a malicious app registered for the same custom URI scheme or leak via logs, history or referrers. With PKCE the client generates a random `code_verifier`, sends its SHA-256 hash as `code_challenge` on the authorize request, and must present the verifier at the token endpoint, so a stolen code alone cannot be redeemed. It lets public clients (SPAs, mobile apps) that cannot keep a secret use the code flow and replaces the implicit flow, and current guidance recommends it for confidential clients too because it also defeats authorization code injection."}],"extra":["**state, nonce, PKCE:** `state` protects the redirect back to the client against CSRF, `nonce` binds the ID token to the login session to stop replay, and PKCE binds the code to the client instance that started the flow.","**User identity key:** `sub` is unique only per issuer, so key local users by the pair (`iss`, `sub`), never by email, which can change or be reassigned.","**OAuth 2.1 draft:** consolidates current best practice: PKCE for all clients, no implicit or password grants, and exact redirect URI matching.","**Browser apps:** the current recommendation for SPAs is a backend-for-frontend that runs the code flow server-side and keeps tokens out of JavaScript, exposing only an HttpOnly session cookie."],"redFlags":["Calls OAuth an authentication protocol without qualification"]},{"id":119,"category":"Security","question":"CSRF vs CORS: how do they differ?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"CSRF tricks the victim's browser into sending an authenticated request with ambient cookies; CORS controls which origins may read cross-origin responses in the browser, so it is not a CSRF defense.","answer":["**CSRF** exploits the fact that browsers attach cookies (and HTTP basic credentials or client certificates) to requests for your domain regardless of which page triggered them. A hidden auto-submitting form on `evil.example` can POST to your payout endpoint with the victim's session cookie. The attacker never sees the response, but the side effect happens. It requires cookie-based (ambient) authentication and a state-changing endpoint.","**CORS** relaxes the same-origin policy: by default scripts cannot read responses from another origin, and the server opts in with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and related headers. For non-simple requests (JSON bodies, custom headers, PUT or DELETE) the browser sends a preflight `OPTIONS` first. But simple requests, such as a form POST with `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, are sent without any preflight; CORS only hides the response. That is why CORS does not stop CSRF. CORS is also enforced only by browsers: curl ignores it, so it is never server-side access control.","Real **CSRF defenses**: synchronizer or double-submit tokens (on by default in Spring Security for unsafe methods), `SameSite` cookies, checking `Origin` or `Sec-Fetch-Site`, and never changing state on GET. **CORS** should be an explicit allowlist of trusted origins; never reflect arbitrary origins with credentials, and never \"disable CORS\" by allowing everything."],"keyPoints":["CSRF abuses browser credentials to send unwanted requests","CORS controls browser reading of cross-origin responses and is not a CSRF defense by itself"],"examples":[{"title":"Cookie-session app: CSRF on, CORS as a strict allowlist","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass WebSecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain web(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .cors(Customizer.withDefaults())       // uses the CorsConfigurationSource bean below\n            .csrf(Customizer.withDefaults())       // on by default; do not disable for cookie auth\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\n            .build();\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {\n        CorsConfiguration cfg = new CorsConfiguration();\n        cfg.setAllowedOrigins(List.of(\"https://portal.example.com\")); // BAD: reflecting any Origin\n        cfg.setAllowedMethods(List.of(\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"DELETE\"));\n        cfg.setAllowedHeaders(List.of(\"Content-Type\", \"X-CSRF-TOKEN\"));\n        cfg.setAllowCredentials(true);\n        cfg.setMaxAge(3600L);\n        UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();\n        source.registerCorsConfiguration(\"/api/**\", cfg);\n        return source;\n    }\n}","note":"CSRF protection and CORS are configured independently because they solve different problems; the allowlist names exact origins, which is mandatory when credentials are allowed."},{"title":"What a CSRF request looks like on the wire","lang":"http","code":"POST /api/payouts HTTP/1.1\nHost: portal.example.com\nOrigin: https://evil.example\nSec-Fetch-Site: cross-site\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\nCookie: SESSION=5f2c9a17e3b04d8c\n\niban=SE4550000000058398257466&amount=5000","note":"This is a simple request, so no preflight happens and CORS never gets a say; the browser attached the session cookie on its own, and only a CSRF token, `SameSite`, or an `Origin` or `Sec-Fetch-Site` check stops it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When does SameSite help?","a":"`SameSite` tells the browser not to send the cookie on cross-site requests: `Strict` never sends it cross-site, `Lax` sends it only on top-level GET navigations, and Chrome treats cookies without the attribute as `Lax`. That blocks most CSRF because cross-site POSTs, iframes and fetches arrive without the session. It does not help against attackers on the same site (a compromised or user-content subdomain counts as same-site), against state-changing GET endpoints under `Lax`, or when you need `SameSite=None` for embedded third-party contexts. Treat it as defense in depth alongside CSRF tokens."},{"q":"Why are bearer-token APIs different?","a":"CSRF depends on credentials the browser attaches automatically. A token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header is added by your own JavaScript, a cross-site page cannot make the browser add it, and adding that header cross-origin triggers a CORS preflight anyway. So pure bearer-token APIs do not need CSRF tokens, which is why stateless Spring resource servers typically disable CSRF. The risk shifts to token theft through XSS when tokens sit in `localStorage`, and the protection vanishes if the API also accepts the token from a cookie."}],"extra":["**Fetch Metadata:** modern browsers send `Sec-Fetch-Site`; rejecting state-changing requests marked `cross-site` is a cheap, strong layer on top of tokens.","**Login CSRF:** an attacker can log the victim into the attacker's account so saved cards or documents land there; login forms need CSRF protection too, which Spring Security applies.","**CORS misconfigurations:** reflecting `Origin` with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, trusting the `null` origin, or loose suffix matching that accepts `example.com.evil.net` turn CORS into a data exfiltration hole.","**Preflight caching:** `Access-Control-Max-Age` cuts OPTIONS traffic, but browsers cap it (Chromium at two hours), so do not expect a longer value to help."],"redFlags":["Treats CORS as server-side authentication or disables it globally"]},{"id":120,"category":"Security","question":"How do you prevent SQL injection and XSS?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Stop SQL injection by binding every value and allowlisting identifiers; stop XSS with context-aware output encoding, allowlist sanitizing where HTML is allowed, and CSP; validation helps but is never the primary defense.","answer":["**SQL injection** happens when data is concatenated into query code. Bind values with `PreparedStatement`, `JdbcTemplate` placeholders, named parameters, or JPA parameters and the Criteria API. An ORM is not automatically safe: string-built JPQL or HQL, native queries, and dynamic `ORDER BY` or column names are injectable. Identifiers cannot be bound, so map user input to an allowlist of known columns. Manual escaping is a red flag: it breaks on encodings, backslash rules and dialect differences. Run the application with a least-privilege database role to limit the blast radius.","**XSS** happens when untrusted data reaches the browser as markup or script. The core defense is **output encoding for the context** (HTML body, attribute, JavaScript, URL), which auto-escaping templates such as Thymeleaf `th:text` and React JSX do for you; the bugs come from escape hatches like `th:utext`, `innerHTML` and `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. When users may submit rich text, **sanitize** with an allowlist library such as the OWASP Java HTML Sanitizer, never a homemade regex. Add a **Content Security Policy** without `unsafe-inline` as defense in depth, and `HttpOnly` cookies so a successful XSS cannot read the session.","**Validation** of type, length and format shrinks the attack surface, but perfectly valid data can still be dangerous (a name like O'Brien, a `<` in a comment), so you bind and encode regardless of validation."],"keyPoints":["Use parameterized queries and safe ORM binding","output encoding, sanitization where required and CSP for XSS","validate but do not rely on validation alone"],"examples":[{"title":"BAD: concatenated value and sort column; GOOD: bound value and allowlisted identifier","lang":"java","code":"@Repository\nclass PaymentSearchRepository {\n    private static final Map<String, String> SORTABLE = Map.of(\n        \"createdAt\", \"created_at\",\n        \"amount\", \"amount\",\n        \"status\", \"status\");\n\n    private final NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    PaymentSearchRepository(NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc) {\n        this.jdbc = jdbc;\n    }\n\n    // BAD: merchantId and sort go straight into the SQL text\n    List<Map<String, Object>> searchUnsafe(String merchantId, String sort) {\n        return jdbc.getJdbcOperations().queryForList(\n            \"SELECT id, amount, status FROM payment WHERE merchant_id = '\" + merchantId\n                + \"' ORDER BY \" + sort);\n    }\n\n    // GOOD: value bound as a parameter, identifier chosen from a fixed allowlist\n    List<Map<String, Object>> search(String merchantId, String sort, boolean desc) {\n        String column = SORTABLE.getOrDefault(sort, \"created_at\");\n        String sql = \"SELECT id, amount, status FROM payment WHERE merchant_id = :merchantId\"\n            + \" ORDER BY \" + column + (desc ? \" DESC\" : \" ASC\") + \" LIMIT 100\";\n        return jdbc.queryForList(sql, Map.of(\"merchantId\", merchantId));\n    }\n}","note":"The only concatenated fragments in the good version come from constants in the code, never from the request."},{"title":"XSS layers: sanitizer for rich text and a CSP header","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\nclass XssDefenses {\n\n    // Merchant descriptions may contain basic formatting and links, nothing else\n    static final PolicyFactory MERCHANT_DESCRIPTION = Sanitizers.FORMATTING.and(Sanitizers.LINKS);\n\n    static String sanitizeDescription(String untrustedHtml) {\n        return MERCHANT_DESCRIPTION.sanitize(untrustedHtml);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    SecurityFilterChain web(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        return http\n            .headers(h -> h\n                .contentSecurityPolicy(csp -> csp.policyDirectives(\n                    \"default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; \"\n                        + \"base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\"))\n                .contentTypeOptions(Customizer.withDefaults()))\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())\n            .build();\n    }\n}","note":"Encoding in templates is still the first line; the sanitizer covers the one field that must contain HTML, and the CSP limits damage if something slips through."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Stored vs reflected XSS?","a":"**Reflected XSS** comes from the current request (a query parameter or form field) echoed back in the response, usually delivered to one victim via a crafted link. **Stored XSS** is saved first (a product review, a merchant name, a support ticket) and served to everyone who views it, often including admins in back-office tools, so its impact is larger and no crafted link is needed. **DOM-based XSS** is a third kind where client-side JavaScript moves data into a dangerous sink. The defenses are the same, context-aware encoding, sanitization and CSP, but stored XSS forces you to treat data from your own database as untrusted."},{"q":"Can ORDER BY be parameterized normally?","a":"No. Bind parameters represent values, not identifiers or keywords, so `ORDER BY ?` either fails or sorts by a constant, which does nothing. Map the user's choice to an allowlist of known column names plus a fixed `ASC` or `DESC`, as in the example, or build the sort with a type-safe API such as the JPA Criteria API or jOOQ fields from a known set. Inside PL/pgSQL, quote identifiers with `format('%I', column)` after validating them."}],"extra":["**LIKE wildcards:** binding a search term prevents injection, but `%` and `_` in user input still act as wildcards and can force expensive scans; escape them and declare an `ESCAPE` character.","**Second-order injection:** data stored safely can become dangerous when a later job or PL/pgSQL function concatenates it into dynamic SQL; there, use `EXECUTE format('... %I ...', col) USING value` so identifiers are quoted and values bound.","**DOM-based XSS:** the payload may never reach the server (for example `location.hash` written into `innerHTML`), so server encoding cannot help; use `textContent`, safe DOM APIs and Trusted Types.","**CSP rollout:** start with `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` and a report endpoint, then enforce; nonce-based `script-src` with `strict-dynamic` holds up better than long host allowlists.","**Tooling:** SAST rules (Semgrep, Find Security Bugs) that flag string concatenation into query methods catch most injection regressions in code review."],"redFlags":["Escapes strings manually","assumes ORM makes every dynamic query safe"]},{"id":121,"category":"Security","question":"How should secrets be stored and rotated?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Keep secrets out of Git, images and logs; store them in a secret manager or encrypted Kubernetes Secrets with least-privilege access and audit, prefer short-lived dynamic credentials, and rotate automatically with apps able to reload.","answer":["**Storage:** a central secret manager (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager, Azure Key Vault) encrypts with KMS keys, applies per-workload policies and audits every read. Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded by default, so enable encryption at rest in etcd with a KMS provider, restrict RBAC tightly, and preferably sync from the external manager with the External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver.","**Access and least privilege:** workloads authenticate by identity instead of a bootstrap secret: IAM roles for service accounts or EKS Pod Identity, GKE Workload Identity, Vault's Kubernetes auth. Each service reads only its own secrets, read-only; humans get break-glass access with audit, not standing access.","**Short-lived credentials and rotation:** prefer credentials that expire on their own: Vault's database secrets engine issues a per-instance DB user with a TTL, and cloud IAM roles replace long-lived access keys. For static secrets, automate rotation with overlap (create new, roll out or reload, then revoke old) and rehearse it, because the day a key leaks is not the day to discover the runbook fails.","**Exposure:** never commit secrets (pre-commit and CI scanning), never bake them into images or ConfigMaps, and keep them out of logs, `/actuator/env` output, crash dumps and `kubectl describe`. File mounts are safer than environment variables, which leak into child processes and diagnostics."],"keyPoints":["Use secret manager/Kubernetes Secret with encryption and least privilege","short-lived credentials, rotation, audit and no source-control/log exposure"],"examples":[{"title":"Spring Boot with Vault: Kubernetes auth and dynamic DB credentials","lang":"yaml","code":"spring:\n  application:\n    name: payments\n  config:\n    import: vault://\n  cloud:\n    vault:\n      uri: https://vault.internal:8200\n      authentication: KUBERNETES          # pod's service account token, no static secret\n      kubernetes:\n        role: payments\n      database:\n        enabled: true                     # fills spring.datasource.username and password\n        role: payments-rw                 # Vault creates a DB user with a TTL per instance\n        backend: database","note":"No password exists in Git, the image or the manifest: the pod proves its identity, receives a short-lived DB user, and Vault revokes it when the lease ends."},{"title":"Mounting a synced secret as a read-only file","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: {app: payments}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: {app: payments}\n    spec:\n      serviceAccountName: payments        # workload identity, no cloud keys in the pod\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: registry.example.com/payments:1.42.0\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: provider-api-key\n              mountPath: /etc/secrets/provider\n              readOnly: true\n      volumes:\n        - name: provider-api-key\n          secret:\n            secretName: payments-provider-api-key   # synced from the secret manager","note":"A mounted Secret volume is refreshed by the kubelet after rotation, while an env var would stay frozen at container start and show up in process dumps."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do applications reload rotated secrets?","a":"Three common patterns. Restart-based: a controller such as Stakater Reloader, or a checksum annotation in the Helm chart, triggers a rolling restart when the secret changes; simple and works for any library. File-based: read the mounted file on use or watch it and rebuild the client, since the kubelet updates mounted Secret files after a delay (not with `subPath`, and never for env vars). Library-based: Spring Cloud Vault renews and rotates leases, and cloud SDK caching clients refresh secrets on a schedule. For database passwords, rotate with overlap: issue the new credential, let the pool open new connections with it (or recreate the pool), and revoke the old one only after the pool's maximum connection lifetime has passed."}],"extra":["**Pod creation implies secret read:** anyone who can create pods in a namespace can mount and read every Secret there, so namespace RBAC for workloads is effectively secret RBAC.","**Leaked means rotated:** once a secret reaches Git history, treat it as compromised and rotate it; rewriting history does not undo clones, forks or CI caches. Tools like gitleaks and GitHub push protection stop most leaks before they land.","**Records leak too:** Spring Boot 3 masks `/actuator/env` and `/actuator/configprops` values by default, but a Java `record` holding a password prints it in its generated `toString()`, so override it or wrap secrets in a type that masks itself.","**Dual-key rotation:** for secrets verified by a peer, such as webhook HMAC keys or partner API keys, accept two active versions during the overlap so rotation needs no coordinated cutover."],"redFlags":["Stores secrets in Git or environment dumps","no rotation process"]},{"id":122,"category":"Security","question":"How should user passwords be stored?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Store only a slow adaptive hash (Argon2id, or bcrypt) with a unique random salt per user and a tuned work factor, optionally plus a separately stored pepper; never a fast digest or reversible encryption.","answer":["Passwords must be stored with a **slow, adaptive password hashing function**, not encrypted and not hashed with a general purpose digest. SHA-256 and MD5 are designed to be fast, so a GPU rig can test billions of candidates per second against a leaked table; encryption is worse, because whoever obtains the key recovers every password at once. The current first choice is **Argon2id** (memory-hard, so GPU and ASIC cracking gets expensive); **bcrypt** with a cost of at least 10 is still acceptable, and PBKDF2 with a high iteration count when FIPS validated primitives are mandatory.","Every hash gets a **unique random salt** (16 bytes from a CSPRNG), which defeats precomputed rainbow tables and makes identical passwords produce different hashes. The salt is not secret: algorithm, parameters and salt are encoded in the stored string, for example `$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$<salt>$<hash>`. Tune the **work factor** (memory, iterations, cost) to what your login fleet can afford at peak, and raise it as hardware improves.","A **pepper** is an extra secret applied as an HMAC before hashing, or used to encrypt the resulting hash, and stored outside the database in a KMS, HSM or secrets manager, so a SQL injection or a leaked backup alone is not enough to start cracking. It adds key management and rotation work, so treat it as defense in depth. Never invent your own scheme: in Spring use the `PasswordEncoder` implementations, verify with `matches()` (constant time), and rate limit login, because each attempt is deliberately expensive in CPU and memory."],"keyPoints":["Use a slow adaptive password hash such as Argon2id or bcrypt with unique salts and appropriate work factor","optional pepper separately protected"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good password storage","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: fast, unsalted digest; a leaked table is cracked at GPU speed\nString sha = HexFormat.of().formatHex(\n        MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(rawPassword.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8)));\n\n// BAD: reversible; whoever gets the key gets every password\nString encrypted = aesGcm.encrypt(rawPassword);\n\n// GOOD: Argon2id with a fresh random salt; parameters travel with the hash\n// (saltLength, hashLength, parallelism, memoryKiB, iterations)\nPasswordEncoder encoder = new Argon2PasswordEncoder(16, 32, 1, 19 * 1024, 2);\nString stored = encoder.encode(rawPassword);\n// stored looks like $argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1$<salt>$<hash>\n\nboolean ok = encoder.matches(candidate, stored); // constant time comparison","note":"The good version never stores anything reversible, and because the parameters are embedded in the string you can raise them later without a schema change. Spring's Argon2 encoder needs BouncyCastle on the classpath."},{"title":"DelegatingPasswordEncoder with transparent rehash on login","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\npublic class PasswordConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {\n        Map<String, PasswordEncoder> encoders = Map.of(\n                \"argon2\", new Argon2PasswordEncoder(16, 32, 1, 19 * 1024, 2),\n                \"bcrypt\", new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12));\n        // New hashes are stored as {argon2}...; old {bcrypt}... hashes still verify\n        return new DelegatingPasswordEncoder(\"argon2\", encoders);\n    }\n\n    @Bean\n    UserDetailsPasswordService passwordUpgrader(UserAccountRepository accounts) {\n        // Called by DaoAuthenticationProvider after a successful login\n        // when upgradeEncoding() reports that the stored hash is outdated\n        return (user, newHash) -> {\n            accounts.updatePasswordHash(user.getUsername(), newHash);\n            return User.withUserDetails(user).password(newHash).build();\n        };\n    }\n}","note":"The `{id}` prefix lets old and new algorithms coexist, and the `UserDetailsPasswordService` bean makes Spring Security re-hash with current parameters the next time each user logs in."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you upgrade work factor over time?","a":"Store the algorithm and its parameters with every hash (PHC string format or Spring's `{id}` prefix) so old and new settings coexist. On each successful login you briefly have the plaintext, so verify against the old hash, check `PasswordEncoder.upgradeEncoding(stored)` and re-hash with current parameters; Spring's `DaoAuthenticationProvider` does this automatically when a `UserDetailsPasswordService` bean exists. For dormant accounts, wrap the legacy hash in a batch job (for example `argon2id(bcrypt_hash)`) so weak hashes disappear immediately and unwrap on the next login, or expire those passwords and force a reset. Re-benchmark on production hardware periodically and watch login latency and CPU after each increase."}],"extra":["**OWASP baselines:** Argon2id with at least 19 MiB of memory, 2 iterations and parallelism 1; bcrypt with cost 10 or more; PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 600,000 iterations when FIPS 140 compliance forces it.","**Spring defaults:** `Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8()` uses 16 MiB and 2 iterations, slightly below the OWASP minimum, so pass explicit constructor parameters if you want to match the guidance.","**bcrypt 72-byte limit:** bcrypt only uses the first 72 bytes of input, and current Spring Security versions reject longer passwords instead of silently truncating; naive pre-hashing with a raw binary digest can introduce null bytes, so prefer Argon2id for long passphrases.","**User enumeration and DoS:** when the username does not exist, still run a dummy hash so timing does not reveal valid accounts (Spring's `DaoAuthenticationProvider` does this), and put rate limiting, lockout or CAPTCHA in front of login because every attempt burns real memory and CPU.","**Policy side:** NIST SP 800-63B favors length over composition rules, drops forced periodic rotation, and requires screening new passwords against breached-password lists (for example the Have I Been Pwned k-anonymity range API)."],"redFlags":["Uses SHA-256/MD5 or reversible encryption","invents custom crypto"]},{"id":123,"category":"Observability","question":"Logs, metrics and traces: what question does each answer?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Metrics show that something is wrong and how much (cheap trends for alerting), traces show where across services the time or error occurs, and logs explain exactly what happened in one event.","answer":["**Metrics** answer \"is the system healthy and how is it trending?\": numeric time series such as request rate, error ratio, latency histograms, pool usage and GC time. They are cheap to store and query over long periods, so they drive dashboards, SLOs and alerts, but they are aggregated: they tell you the P99 of `POST /payments` jumped at 14:05, not which request suffered or why.","**Logs** answer \"what exactly happened in this event?\": a discrete, detailed record such as a validation failure with its reason, a stack trace or an audit entry. They can carry high-cardinality detail (order IDs, error messages) but are the most expensive signal per event and poor for trend detection, which is why using logs for all monitoring is a red flag. **Traces** answer \"where did the time go across services for this request?\": a tree of spans with timing, stitched together by trace context (W3C `traceparent`) propagated through HTTP and message headers, showing for instance that 1.8 s of a checkout was spent waiting on the fraud service.","They work best **together** as a workflow: an SLO burn alert fires from metrics, an exemplar or a slow-trace query leads to a representative trace, the trace pinpoints the slow span and service, and the trace ID in that service's structured logs gives the precise error. The glue is consistent resource attributes (service, version, environment) and the trace ID on every log line."],"keyPoints":["Metrics show system trends/health","logs explain discrete events","traces connect latency and calls across services","use them together"],"examples":[{"title":"Which signal answers which question","lang":"text","code":"Signal   Question it answers                         Typical content                          Cost\nMetrics  Healthy? How much? Trending where?          rate, errors, latency histogram, pools   cheap, labels must stay bounded\nTraces   Where did the time go across services?      spans, timings, parent/child, attrs      medium, usually sampled\nLogs     What exactly happened here, and why?        structured JSON with traceId, orderId    expensive per event, full detail\n\nWorkflow for \"checkout is slow\":\n1. Metric alert: P99 of POST /api/checkout above 1.5 s, error budget burning\n2. Exemplar on the latency histogram links to trace 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n3. Trace shows the fraud-service /score span at 1.3 s, retried twice\n4. Logs filtered by that traceId: \"read timeout after 600 ms, retrying\" in FraudClient","note":"Each step narrows the search: metrics find when and where, traces find which hop, logs find why."},{"title":"One Micrometer Observation produces a metric, a span and linked logs","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CreditDecisionService {\n\n    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(CreditDecisionService.class);\n    private final ObservationRegistry registry;\n    private final DecisionEngine engine;\n\n    public CreditDecisionService(ObservationRegistry registry, DecisionEngine engine) {\n        this.registry = registry;\n        this.engine = engine;\n    }\n\n    public Decision decide(CreditApplication app) {\n        return Observation.createNotStarted(\"credit.decision\", registry)\n                .lowCardinalityKeyValue(\"product\", app.product().name())        // metric tag and span attribute\n                .highCardinalityKeyValue(\"application.id\", app.id().toString()) // span only, never a metric tag\n                .observe(() -> {\n                    Decision decision = engine.evaluate(app);\n                    // traceId and spanId are in the MDC, so this line links to the trace\n                    log.info(\"credit decision outcome={} applicationId={}\", decision.outcome(), app.id());\n                    return decision;\n                });\n    }\n}","note":"With Spring Boot 3 Actuator and Micrometer Tracing on the classpath, this records a `credit.decision` timer tagged only with bounded values, a span carrying the application ID, and a log line that carries the same trace ID."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which one would you start with for rising P99 latency?","a":"Start with metrics: a latency histogram broken down by endpoint, instance and dependency tells you whether the rise is global or isolated to one route, pod or downstream, and when it began relative to deploys and traffic. Then pull traces from the slow tail (exemplars, or a tracing backend query for duration above the P99) to see which span grew, or whether time disappears into a gap such as pool wait or GC. Finally read the logs for those trace IDs to get the concrete cause, such as timeouts, retries or one tenant's oversized payload."}],"extra":["**Exemplars:** Prometheus and OpenMetrics can attach a trace ID to individual histogram samples, so a Grafana latency panel links straight to a representative slow trace; Micrometer's Prometheus registry adds them when tracing is active.","**Averages lie:** record latency as histograms (for example `management.metrics.distribution.percentiles-histogram.http.server.requests=true`) so percentiles can be aggregated across instances; client-side precomputed percentiles cannot be averaged meaningfully.","**Structured logs:** Spring Boot 3.4 added built-in structured JSON logging (`logging.structured.format.console=ecs`, `logstash` or `gelf`), which makes filtering by `traceId` or `orderId` reliable instead of regex parsing.","**Sampling blind spot:** traces are usually sampled, so a rare failure may only exist in logs and metrics; tail sampling that keeps all errors and slow traces closes most of that gap.","**Fourth signal:** continuous profiling (JFR, async-profiler, the emerging OpenTelemetry profiles signal) answers \"which code is burning CPU or allocating\", which none of the other three shows directly."],"redFlags":["Uses logs for all monitoring","cannot explain trace context"]},{"id":124,"category":"Observability","question":"What is a correlation ID, and how is it different from trace/span IDs?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"A correlation ID is an application-level key grouping related work, possibly across many traces and days; a trace ID identifies one distributed trace and span IDs single operations in it; both must cross async hops.","answer":["A **correlation ID** is a grouping key that the application defines: an `X-Correlation-Id` sent by a client, a loan application or order reference, or a support reference that ties together a checkout request, the Kafka events it emits, the settlement batch that runs hours later and the customer's retry the next day. It can outlive any single request, often spans several traces, and is frequently shown to customers or support staff.","A **trace ID** (16 bytes, 32 hex characters in W3C Trace Context) identifies one distributed trace: the causal tree of work triggered by one entry point. Every operation in that tree has its own **span ID** (8 bytes) plus a parent span ID, which is how the backend rebuilds timing and call hierarchy. The tracing SDK creates the trace ID once at the root and every downstream service continues it from the incoming `traceparent` header; generating a fresh trace ID in each service breaks the trace into disconnected fragments.","Both kinds of context must cross **async boundaries**: inject them into Kafka record headers or AMQP message properties when publishing, extract them when consuming, and carry them across thread pools. In Spring Boot 3 the simplest approach is to carry the correlation ID as tracing **baggage**, so it travels with the trace context and lands in the MDC. Keep identifiers opaque: never use an email, national ID or account number as a correlation ID, because it gets logged everywhere and forwarded to downstream parties."],"keyPoints":["Correlation ID is an application grouping identifier","trace ID identifies a distributed trace and span IDs identify operations","propagate through async messages too"],"examples":[{"title":"Servlet filter that accepts, validates and echoes a correlation ID","lang":"java","code":"@Component\n@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)\npublic class CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {\n\n    static final String HEADER = \"X-Correlation-Id\";\n    private static final Pattern SAFE = Pattern.compile(\"[A-Za-z0-9-]{8,64}\");\n\n    @Override\n    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,\n                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {\n        String incoming = request.getHeader(HEADER);\n        // Accept only short opaque values: prevents log injection and PII smuggling\n        String id = (incoming != null && SAFE.matcher(incoming).matches())\n                ? incoming\n                : UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n        MDC.put(\"correlationId\", id);\n        response.setHeader(HEADER, id);\n        try {\n            chain.doFilter(request, response);\n        } finally {\n            MDC.remove(\"correlationId\");\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"The correlation ID is independent of the trace ID: a client can reuse it across retries and later calls, while each request still gets its own trace. Echoing it back lets customers and support quote it."},{"title":"Propagating trace context and a correlation field through HTTP, Kafka and RabbitMQ","lang":"yaml","code":"management:\n  tracing:\n    sampling:\n      probability: 0.1\n    baggage:\n      remote-fields: x-correlation-id     # sent to downstream services with the trace context\n      correlation:\n        fields: x-correlation-id          # copied into the MDC for every log line\nspring:\n  kafka:\n    template:\n      observation-enabled: true           # KafkaTemplate writes trace headers\n    listener:\n      observation-enabled: true           # @KafkaListener continues the producer's trace\n  rabbitmq:\n    template:\n      observation-enabled: true\n    listener:\n      simple:\n        observation-enabled: true","note":"With Micrometer Tracing, `remote-fields` makes the value travel as baggage next to `traceparent`, and the observation flags make Spring Kafka and Spring AMQP inject and extract context in message headers."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you propagate context through Kafka/RabbitMQ?","a":"The producer injects the current context (`traceparent`, `tracestate`, `baggage` and any correlation header) into Kafka record headers or AMQP message headers, and the consumer extracts it to start a consumer span that is a child of the producer span, or linked to it for batch consumption. In Spring Boot 3 enable `spring.kafka.template.observation-enabled` and `spring.kafka.listener.observation-enabled` (and the RabbitMQ equivalents), or let the OpenTelemetry Java agent instrument the clients. After extraction the context must also follow any hand-off to other threads, for example via a `ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator` on your executor, or MDC fields vanish in async work. With a transactional outbox, persist the headers alongside the event row so the relay restores them when it publishes."}],"extra":["**W3C format:** `traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01` holds version, trace ID, parent span ID and flags (the sampled bit); `tracestate` carries vendor data and the separate `baggage` header carries your key/value pairs.","**Span links for batches:** when one consumer processes a batch of messages from many producers, OpenTelemetry messaging conventions recommend a consumer span with **links** to each producer context rather than arbitrarily choosing one parent.","**Edge trust boundary:** at public ingress decide whether to honor an incoming `traceparent`; a client that sets the sampled flag on every request can inflate tracing cost, so many teams start a new trace at the edge and keep the client's value only as an attribute.","**Baggage leaks:** baggage is sent on every outbound call, including to third-party APIs, so allowlist the fields, keep values small and strip them at egress.","**Give it back to callers:** include the trace or correlation ID in error responses (for example as a property on a `ProblemDetail`) so a customer complaint maps straight to the exact trace and logs."],"redFlags":["Generates a new trace ID at every service","logs sensitive IDs indiscriminately"]},{"id":125,"category":"Observability","question":"SLI, SLO and SLA: explain the difference.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"An SLI is a measured ratio of good to valid events, an SLO is the internal target for it over a window, and an SLA is a looser external contract with financial or legal consequences.","answer":["An **SLI** (service level indicator) is a precisely defined measurement of what users experience, best expressed as good events divided by valid events: the proportion of `POST /credit-decisions` requests that return a non-5xx response within 800 ms, measured at the gateway. An **SLO** (service level objective) is the internal target for that SLI over a window, such as 99.5% over a rolling 28 days. The remainder is the **error budget** (0.5%, about 3.4 hours of full outage at uniform traffic), which the team spends on releases and experiments and protects with an agreed error budget policy.","An **SLA** (service level agreement) is an external commitment in a contract, with consequences such as service credits or penalties. It is owned by business and legal, and it must be looser than the SLO so that you are warned, and can react, long before a contract is breached.","Indicators and windows must be **user-centric**. Plain uptime or \"the pod is running\" misses a service that is up but returning errors or taking 10 s, and it misses partial outages affecting one region or product. Measure as close to the user as practical (load balancer or client), exclude invalid events such as client validation errors, define SLOs per critical user journey, and use a rolling window that matches how users perceive reliability rather than a calendar month that resets the pain on the first."],"keyPoints":["SLI is a measured indicator","SLO is an internal reliability target","SLA is an external commitment with consequences","define user-centric windows"],"examples":[{"title":"The three terms for one credit decision API","lang":"text","code":"Term  Owner               Example                                                   When missed\nSLI   Engineering         good  = POST /credit-decisions, non-5xx, under 800 ms     (just a measurement)\n                          valid = all requests except 4xx validation rejects\nSLO   Engineering+Product 99.5% of valid requests are good, rolling 28 days         error budget policy:\n                                                                                    freeze risky releases\nSLA   Business, Legal     99.0% monthly availability in the partner contract        service credits paid\n\nError budget: 0.5% of valid requests per 28 days\nAt 2,000,000 requests per 28 days that is 10,000 bad requests","note":"The SLA is deliberately looser than the SLO, and the SLI is a ratio of events rather than server uptime."},{"title":"Prometheus recording rules for a request-based latency SLI","lang":"yaml","code":"# Needs a histogram bucket exactly at 800 ms, for example:\n# management.metrics.distribution.slo.http.server.requests=800ms\ngroups:\n  - name: credit-decision-sli\n    rules:\n      - record: credit_decision:good:rate5m\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket{uri=\"/credit-decisions\", method=\"POST\",\n                   outcome=\"SUCCESS\", le=\"0.8\"}[5m]))\n      - record: credit_decision:valid:rate5m\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{uri=\"/credit-decisions\", method=\"POST\",\n                   outcome!=\"CLIENT_ERROR\"}[5m]))\n      - record: credit_decision:sli:ratio_28d\n        expr: |\n          sum_over_time(credit_decision:good:rate5m[28d])\n          /\n          sum_over_time(credit_decision:valid:rate5m[28d])","note":"Good and valid rates are recorded separately and summed over the window before dividing, which weights the SLI by traffic instead of averaging ratios."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What SLI would you define for credit decision latency?","a":"The proportion of valid credit decision requests (excluding client validation rejections) that receive a final decision within a threshold, measured at the API gateway or load balancer so queueing and network time count: good means non-5xx and under 800 ms, valid means every non-4xx request. If the shape of the distribution matters, use two thresholds (for example 95% under 500 ms and 99% under 2 s) and configure histogram buckets exactly at those boundaries. Applications routed to asynchronous manual review deserve their own SLI, such as the proportion receiving a final decision within 24 hours, instead of being mixed into the synchronous latency SLI."}],"extra":["**Error budget policy:** agree in advance what happens when the budget is exhausted (release freeze, reliability work first, stricter change review); without a policy an SLO is just a dashboard.","**Dependency math:** a service that synchronously needs three dependencies at 99.9% each cannot credibly promise more than about 99.7% itself, which is why SLOs drive timeouts, fallbacks and asynchronous design.","**Not 100%:** a 100% target means no releases and no risk, and users cannot tell 99.99% from 100% when their own mobile network is less reliable than that.","**Thresholds, not averages:** latency SLIs are \"proportion faster than X\", possibly with two thresholds (90% under 300 ms and 99% under 1 s), which maps directly to histogram buckets and to error budget burn.","**Low traffic:** with a few hundred requests a day one failure moves the SLI a lot, so combine request-based SLIs with synthetic probes or longer windows."],"redFlags":["Uses uptime alone or treats all three as synonyms"]},{"id":126,"category":"Observability","question":"What is metric-cardinality explosion?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Unbounded label values (user ID, order ID, raw URLs) multiply time series until the backend runs out of memory, slows down or gets very expensive; keep labels bounded and put unique IDs in logs and traces.","answer":["In Prometheus style systems every unique combination of metric name and label values is a separate **time series** with its own index entry and in-memory head chunk. Cardinality is the product of distinct values per label: `http_server_requests_seconds_bucket` with 20 routes, 5 methods, 8 status codes and 30 buckets across 50 pods can already reach 1.2 million series. Add a `customerId` label with 100,000 customers and growth becomes unbounded.","The consequences are operational, not cosmetic: Prometheus memory grows until it is OOMKilled, ingestion and compaction slow down, dashboards and alert queries time out or return partial data, and SaaS vendors that bill per series send a surprising invoice. It hurts the application as well, because Micrometer keeps a meter object per tag combination, so an unbounded tag is a memory leak inside your service too.","The rule is **bounded dimensions only** on metrics: method, route template, status or status class, outcome, exception type, region, a small enum of products or payment methods. Unique identifiers (user, order, request, trace) belong in structured logs and span attributes, and **exemplars** can link a metric sample to a specific trace. The classic trap is tagging with a raw path such as `/orders/98231` instead of the template `/orders/{id}`, usually from a custom filter or hand-built meter."],"keyPoints":["Unbounded labels such as customer/order ID create huge time series, cost and query instability","use bounded dimensions and logs/traces for unique identifiers"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good Micrometer tags, plus a safety net","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: one series per customer and per order, grows forever\nCounter.builder(\"payments.processed\")\n        .tag(\"customerId\", payment.customerId().toString())\n        .tag(\"orderId\", payment.orderId().toString())\n        .register(registry)\n        .increment();\n\n// BAD: raw path as a tag, so /orders/98231 becomes its own series\nTimer.builder(\"orders.api\").tag(\"path\", request.getRequestURI()).register(registry);\n\n// GOOD: bounded tags on the metric, identifiers in the log line and the span\nCounter.builder(\"payments.processed\")\n        .tag(\"method\", payment.method().name())    // CARD, SEPA, SWISH\n        .tag(\"outcome\", result.outcome().name())    // APPROVED, DECLINED, ERROR\n        .register(registry)\n        .increment();\nlog.info(\"payment processed orderId={} customerId={}\", payment.orderId(), payment.customerId());\n\n// GOOD: route template instead of the raw path\nString route = (String) request.getAttribute(HandlerMapping.BEST_MATCHING_PATTERN_ATTRIBUTE);\n\n// Safety net: stop registering new values beyond a cap instead of hurting Prometheus\n@Bean\nMeterFilter paymentMethodCap() {\n    return MeterFilter.maximumAllowableTags(\"payments.processed\", \"method\", 20, MeterFilter.deny());\n}","note":"Every tag value you add multiplies series; the `MeterFilter` turns a future bug into dropped meters instead of an outage of the monitoring system."},{"title":"Finding the offending metric and label in Prometheus","lang":"bash","code":"# Top metrics and labels by series count in the TSDB head block\ncurl -s http://prometheus:9090/api/v1/status/tsdb \\\n  | jq '.data.seriesCountByMetricName[:5], .data.labelValueCountByLabelName[:5]'\n\n# Typical output that points at the culprit:\n# {\"name\": \"payments_processed_total\", \"value\": 412877}\n# {\"name\": \"orderId\", \"value\": 398112}\n\n# Watch total head series over time; alert on sudden growth\ncurl -s 'http://prometheus:9090/api/v1/query?query=prometheus_tsdb_head_series' \\\n  | jq '.data.result[0].value[1]'","note":"The TSDB status endpoint shows which metric names and label names own the most series, which usually identifies the bad deploy within minutes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which labels are safe for HTTP metrics?","a":"Safe labels have a small, known set of values: `method`, the route template (`uri=\"/orders/{id}\"`, never the raw path), `status` or status class, `outcome`, the `exception` class name, and target labels such as service, instance, region and version added at scrape time. For outbound calls add the client name or target service, again with a URI template rather than a full URL. Avoid user ID, unbounded tenant ID, request or trace ID, query strings, user agent and client IP; when you need to jump from a metric to a specific request, use exemplars."}],"extra":["**Histograms multiply:** each label combination on a histogram costs one series per bucket plus `_sum` and `_count`, so enabling percentile histograms on a meter with a moderately wide tag set can dominate your series count.","**Churn is cardinality too:** labels like `pod` or `container_id` change on every deploy, so frequent rollouts create many short-lived series that still occupy the head block and index for hours.","**Scrape guards:** `sample_limit`, `label_limit` and `label_value_length_limit` in a Prometheus scrape config fail a misbehaving target's scrape instead of letting it exhaust the server.","**Framework defaults help:** Spring MVC server observations tag `uri` with the matched route pattern, and Spring Framework 6 client observations use the URI template (falling back to `none`), so explosions usually come from custom meters rather than the framework.","**Tenant IDs:** a label with tens of tenants is fine and often essential; with thousands it is not, so aggregate by plan or tier and do per-tenant analytics in logs or a columnar store."],"redFlags":["Adds userId or requestId as Prometheus labels"]},{"id":127,"category":"Observability","question":"How do you design alerts that are actionable?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Page only on user-facing symptoms such as SLO burn rate, give every alert a severity, an owning team and a runbook, suppress duplicate downstream alerts, and tune windows so each page is urgent, real and actionable.","answer":["An actionable alert means a human must do something now and knows what to do. So page on **user impact**: error ratio or latency SLO burn rate, failing checkouts, consumer lag measured in time. Causes such as high CPU, a restarted pod or a long GC are not pages by themselves: CPU at 90% while the SLO is healthy just means the system is busy doing its job. Put causes on dashboards or low severity tickets, where they help diagnosis without waking anyone.","Every alert needs metadata: a **severity** that maps to a response (page now vs ticket for the next business day), an **owner** label that routes it to the team able to fix it, and a **runbook** link covering impact, first diagnostic steps, safe mitigations and escalation. The summary should state what users experience (\"card payments failing for 4% of requests\"), not only which metric crossed a line.","Then control noise: group related alerts, use **inhibition** so a database outage does not also fire twenty dependent service alerts, and avoid alerting on several symptoms of the same failure. **Tune windows** with `for:` durations and multi-window burn rates so short blips stay quiet but real incidents are caught quickly. Treat alerts as code: review every page after the on-call shift, delete or demote alerts nobody acted on, and unit test rules with `promtool test rules`."],"keyPoints":["Alert on user impact/SLO burn, include severity, ownership and runbook","avoid noisy symptom duplication and tune windows"],"examples":[{"title":"Multi-window burn-rate alert with owner and runbook","lang":"yaml","code":"# SLO 99.9% over 30 days, so the allowed error ratio is 0.001\ngroups:\n  - name: payments-api-slo-alerts\n    rules:\n      - alert: PaymentsApiErrorBudgetFastBurn\n        # 14.4x burn spends 2% of the monthly budget in one hour\n        expr: |\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate1h{job=\"payments-api\"} > (14.4 * 0.001)\n          and\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate5m{job=\"payments-api\"} > (14.4 * 0.001)\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Payments API failing {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of requests\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/payments-api/error-budget-burn\"\n          dashboard: \"https://grafana.internal/d/payments-api-slo\"\n      - alert: PaymentsApiErrorBudgetSlowBurn\n        # 6x burn over 6 hours spends 5% of the budget\n        expr: |\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate6h{job=\"payments-api\"} > (6 * 0.001)\n          and\n          job:http_error_ratio:rate30m{job=\"payments-api\"} > (6 * 0.001)\n        labels:\n          severity: page\n          team: payments\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Payments API burning its error budget 6x faster than sustainable\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/payments-api/error-budget-burn\"","note":"The `job:http_error_ratio:*` series are recording rules computing errors divided by requests over each window. The long window proves significance, the short window makes the alert resolve quickly once the incident is over."},{"title":"Alertmanager routing and inhibition to avoid duplicate pages","lang":"yaml","code":"route:\n  receiver: team-tickets\n  group_by: [alertname, service, cluster]\n  group_wait: 30s\n  group_interval: 5m\n  repeat_interval: 4h\n  routes:\n    - matchers: ['severity=\"page\"', 'team=\"payments\"']\n      receiver: payments-oncall\n    - matchers: ['severity=\"ticket\"']\n      receiver: team-tickets\ninhibit_rules:\n  # The database is down: do not also page for every payments service that depends on it\n  - source_matchers: ['alertname=\"PaymentsDbUnavailable\"']\n    target_matchers: ['team=\"payments\"', 'severity=\"page\"']\n    equal: [cluster]","note":"Routing uses the owner and severity labels from the rule, and inhibition keeps a single root-cause page instead of a storm of symptom pages."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why are multi-window burn-rate alerts useful?","a":"A single long window (say 1 hour) is statistically solid but keeps firing long after recovery, while a single short window reacts fast but pages on brief blips. Requiring both a long window and a short window (about one twelfth of it, such as 1h and 5m) to exceed the same burn rate gives precision from the long window and a fast reset from the short one, because the short window drops as soon as the incident ends. Stacking tiers (14.4x over 1h and 5m, 6x over 6h and 30m as pages, 1x over 3d and 6h as a ticket) makes detection time proportional to severity: a total outage pages within minutes, while a slow budget leak produces a ticket instead of a 3 a.m. page."}],"extra":["**Burn rate math:** burn rate is the observed error ratio divided by the allowed one (1 minus the SLO); at 14.4x a 30 day budget is gone in about two days, which justifies a page, while 1x only warrants a ticket.","**Dead man's switch:** keep an always-firing `Watchdog` alert routed to an external heartbeat service so you learn when Prometheus or Alertmanager itself is broken, and use `absent()` for metrics that silently disappear.","**Low traffic trap:** at 50 requests an hour a single failure is a 2% error ratio, so require a minimum request count in the expression or rely on synthetic checks for quiet services.","**Measure the alerting:** track pages per shift, time to acknowledge and the share of pages that led to action; the Google SRE book argues for at most about two incidents per 12 hour on-call shift.","**Runbook content:** what the alert means for users, the dashboard, likely causes ranked by frequency, safe mitigations (rollback, feature flag, scale out), how to verify recovery and when to escalate; a runbook that only says \"investigate\" is not a runbook."],"redFlags":["Alerts on every CPU spike","no owner or runbook"]},{"id":128,"category":"Observability","question":"What value does OpenTelemetry provide?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"OpenTelemetry gives you one vendor-neutral API, SDK, wire protocol (OTLP) and set of semantic conventions for traces, metrics and logs with standard context propagation, and its Collector decouples services from whichever backend you choose.","answer":["OpenTelemetry (OTel) standardizes the **instrumentation layer**: an API and SDK per language, the OTLP protocol, and W3C Trace Context plus Baggage for **context propagation**, covering traces, metrics and logs. Instrument once and the same data can flow to Tempo, Jaeger, Prometheus, Datadog, Honeycomb or Elastic, so switching vendors or running two in parallel does not touch application code. For Java you get the **Java agent** for zero-code instrumentation of HTTP servers and clients, JDBC, Kafka and more, or Spring Boot 3 with Micrometer Tracing's OTel bridge and an OTLP exporter.","The **Collector** is the other half of the value: a separate process built from receivers, processors and exporters. Services send OTLP to a node-local or gateway collector, which batches, retries, enriches with Kubernetes metadata, redacts PII, applies tail sampling and exports to one or more backends. Backend credentials, routing and cost controls then live in collector configuration owned by the platform team, not in every service.","It is not magic, though. Auto-instrumentation gives transport-level spans, but useful telemetry also needs business spans and attributes (decision outcome, product), consistent naming through **semantic conventions** (`http.request.method`, `http.route`, `http.response.status_code`) so dashboards work across teams and languages, a deliberate **sampling plan** so cost stays predictable, and care with cardinality and sensitive data. Without that you get expensive, inconsistent telemetry nobody can query."],"keyPoints":["Vendor-neutral instrumentation and context propagation for traces, metrics and logs","collectors decouple applications from backends"],"examples":[{"title":"Zero-code instrumentation with the OpenTelemetry Java agent","lang":"bash","code":"java -javaagent:/opt/otel/opentelemetry-javaagent.jar \\\n  -Dotel.service.name=credit-decision-service \\\n  -Dotel.resource.attributes=service.namespace=lending,service.version=1.42.0 \\\n  -Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://otel-collector:4318 \\\n  -Dotel.exporter.otlp.protocol=http/protobuf \\\n  -Dotel.traces.sampler=parentbased_traceidratio \\\n  -Dotel.traces.sampler.arg=0.25 \\\n  -jar credit-decision-service.jar","note":"The application only knows the collector address; the parent-based sampler honors the upstream sampling decision so traces are never cut in half."},{"title":"Collector gateway with memory limiter and tail sampling","lang":"yaml","code":"receivers:\n  otlp:\n    protocols:\n      grpc:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4317\n      http:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318\nprocessors:\n  memory_limiter:\n    check_interval: 1s\n    limit_percentage: 80\n    spike_limit_percentage: 20\n  tail_sampling:\n    decision_wait: 10s\n    policies:\n      - name: keep-errors\n        type: status_code\n        status_code: { status_codes: [ERROR] }\n      - name: keep-slow\n        type: latency\n        latency: { threshold_ms: 1000 }\n      - name: baseline\n        type: probabilistic\n        probabilistic: { sampling_percentage: 5 }\n  batch: {}\nexporters:\n  otlp:\n    endpoint: tempo.observability:4317\n    tls: { insecure: true }\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [memory_limiter, tail_sampling, batch]\n      exporters: [otlp]","note":"Swapping Tempo for another backend is a change to the exporters block only. `tail_sampling` ships in the contrib distribution and keeps every error and slow trace plus 5% of the rest."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Head vs tail sampling?","a":"Head sampling decides at the root span before anything is known about the request, typically with `parentbased_traceidratio` at some percentage; it is cheap, stateless and consistent across services because the decision travels in the `traceparent` sampled flag, but errors and slow requests are kept at the same low rate as everything else. Tail sampling decides after the whole trace has been collected (the collector waits `decision_wait`, for example 10 s), so it can keep all errors and slow traces plus a small share of the rest. The cost is collector memory and complexity, a short delay before traces appear, and the requirement that every span of a trace reaches the same collector instance."},{"q":"Where should sampling occur?","a":"Head sampling should happen once, at the entry point (gateway or edge service), and every downstream service should use a parent-based sampler so it honors that decision; independent random sampling per service produces broken, partial traces. Tail sampling belongs in a dedicated collector gateway tier fed by a trace-ID-aware load balancer, not inside application processes. Compute metrics before any sampling; a common setup sends all or most spans from the apps and lets the gateway keep errors, slow traces and a baseline percentage."}],"extra":["**Ports and protocols:** OTLP uses 4317 for gRPC and 4318 for HTTP/protobuf; the 2.x Java agent defaults to HTTP/protobuf, a common cause of \"no data\" when the collector only listens on gRPC.","**Deployment pattern:** run collectors as a DaemonSet or sidecar for batching and Kubernetes metadata (`k8sattributes` processor), plus a gateway tier for tail sampling and export; because tail sampling needs all spans of a trace on one instance, the first tier routes by trace ID with the `loadbalancing` exporter.","**Metrics before sampling:** derive RED metrics from all spans (the `spanmetrics` connector) or from application metrics before tail sampling drops traces, otherwise latency numbers are biased toward the traces you kept.","**Semantic convention migrations:** HTTP attribute names changed when the conventions stabilized (`http.method` became `http.request.method`); during the transition `OTEL_SEMCONV_STABILITY_OPT_IN=http/dup` let instrumentation emit both so dashboards could migrate.","**Memory limiter first:** put `memory_limiter` at the start of every pipeline so a traffic spike makes the collector refuse data (and senders retry) instead of being OOMKilled and losing everything in flight."],"redFlags":["Treats instrumentation as automatic with no semantic conventions or sampling plan"]},{"id":129,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"API latency suddenly increases. What is your investigation sequence?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Confirm scope and start time, correlate with deploys, config and traffic changes, read RED metrics per endpoint and instance, use traces to find the slow span or dependency, check saturation, mitigate first, then find root cause.","answer":["First **confirm and scope**: which endpoints, which percentile (averages hide tail problems), since when, all instances or one pod, one region or tenant, and whether the server sees it or only a client reports it. Then line it up against a **change timeline**: deploys, config and feature flag changes, traffic shape (a new client, a batch job, a retry storm), dependency incidents, node pool or database maintenance.","Next read **RED metrics** (rate, errors, duration) per endpoint and per instance, and **USE** metrics (utilization, saturation, errors) for resources: CPU throttling, GC pause time, Tomcat or executor queueing, HikariCP pending connections and acquire time, database and downstream latency. Pull **traces** of slow requests: either one span grew (a query, a dependency) or there is an unexplained gap, which usually means waiting on a pool, a lock, GC or CPU. Separate **saturation** (queueing because something is full) from **errors** (timeouts and retries that inflate latency).","**Mitigate before you root cause**: roll back the suspect deploy, disable the flag, shed or rate limit the abusive traffic, scale the saturated tier. Change one thing at a time and watch the metric respond, and capture evidence (thread dumps, slow traces, pool stats) before any restart, because a restart destroys state. Once users are safe, find the root cause and add whatever alert or dashboard would have shortened the investigation."],"keyPoints":["Confirm scope/time, compare deploy/traffic changes, inspect RED metrics, traces and dependencies, isolate saturation/errors, mitigate then find root cause"],"examples":[{"title":"Investigation command sequence on Kubernetes and Spring Boot","lang":"bash","code":"# 1. Scope: what changed, and is it every pod or one?\nkubectl -n payments rollout history deploy/payments-api\nkubectl -n payments get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20\nkubectl -n payments top pods -l app=payments-api\n\n# 2. Saturation inside the JVM (Spring Boot Actuator)\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.pending | jq '.measurements'\ncurl -s localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/jvm.gc.pause | jq '.measurements'\ncurl -s 'localhost:8080/actuator/metrics/http.server.requests?tag=uri:/api/payments' | jq '.measurements'\n\n# 3. Thread state on a slow pod: blocked on the pool, a lock, or a remote call?\nkubectl -n payments exec payments-api-7d9f-abcde -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > threads-1.txt\nsleep 10\nkubectl -n payments exec payments-api-7d9f-abcde -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > threads-2.txt\n\n# 4. If a deploy correlates, mitigate first and keep investigating afterwards\nkubectl -n payments rollout undo deploy/payments-api","note":"Evidence (events, pool metrics, two thread dumps) is captured before the rollback, so the root cause analysis still has data after users are safe."},{"title":"Reading a slow trace: the gap is the clue","lang":"text","code":"POST /api/payments    total 2.41 s    (same endpoint last week: 180 ms)\npayments-api  handler                            2.41 s\n  (no child span)                                2.05 s   <== unexplained: pool wait, lock, GC or CPU throttling\n  SELECT payment ... (JDBC)                      0.09 s\n  POST fraud-service /score                      0.21 s\n\nNext checks for the gap:\n  hikaricp.connections.pending > 0 and acquire time up  -> pool exhaustion\n  jvm.gc.pause spikes at the same timestamps            -> GC\n  container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total rising      -> CPU limit","note":"When no child span explains the time, the request was waiting for a resource inside the service, which is a saturation problem rather than slow code or a slow dependency."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if P50 is normal but P99 is high?","a":"The typical request is fine, so look for something that hits a subset: long GC pauses, pool or lock queueing during bursts, one slow pod or node, a slow shard or partition, cache misses, big tenants or oversized payloads, cold JIT after a deploy, or a dependency that occasionally times out and gets retried. Break the latency histogram down by instance, endpoint, tenant tier and payload size, look for a bimodal distribution (a second peak at a timeout value is a strong clue), and pull exemplar traces from the tail instead of random ones. Correlate tail spikes with GC logs and pool pending metrics at the same timestamps."}],"extra":["**Little's law:** concurrency equals throughput times latency, so if latency doubles at constant traffic the in-flight requests double, which can exhaust thread and connection pools and turn one slow dependency into a full outage.","**Latency at the timeout value:** a cluster of requests at exactly 1 s or 2 s usually means a timeout followed by a retry somewhere; look for the configured timeout, not for slow code.","**Coordinated omission:** load generators and clients that wait for each response before sending the next request under-report tail latency, so trust server histograms and gateway timings over client averages.","**Virtual threads (Java 21):** they remove the servlet thread pool as a queueing point, so saturation moves to the HikariCP pool or the downstream; on JDK 21 detect carrier pinning in `synchronized` blocks with the JFR `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event (JEP 491 removed most pinning in JDK 24).","**Compare with a baseline:** the same hour last week, or a healthy canary or region, often shows the difference faster than absolute numbers."],"redFlags":["Restarts first","changes many things","checks only averages"]},{"id":130,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Memory usage grows every day and drops after restart. What do you do?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"First determine whether the heap live set, native memory or a cache is growing, confirm with GC and NMT data, then take comparable heap dumps hours apart and compare dominators and paths to GC roots.","answer":["Start by identifying **which memory** grows. Container working set or RSS includes Java heap, metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, direct and mapped buffers and malloc arenas. Compare `jvm.memory.used` per pool and the **live set after GC** (old generation after a full or mixed collection, or `jvm.gc.live.data.size`) with the container metric. A heap sawtooth is normal; a post-GC floor that rises every day is a heap leak. A flat heap floor with rising RSS points to **native memory**: enable Native Memory Tracking and diff summaries, and check direct buffers, thread count and metaspace (class loader leaks from dynamic proxies or scripting).","For a heap problem, confirm the trend in GC logs (`-Xlog:gc*`), then take **comparable heap dumps**: same instance, similar load, a few hours apart, live objects only. One dump shows what is big, not what is growing; two or three show the delta. In Eclipse MAT compare the histograms, open the **dominator tree** for the largest retained sizes, and follow **paths to GC roots** (excluding weak and soft references) to the static field, cache, `ThreadLocal`, listener list or meter registry holding the objects.","Typical culprits are unbounded maps used as caches, per-request `ThreadLocal` values on pooled threads, Micrometer meters with unbounded tags, a Hibernate persistence context in a long batch, or listeners never deregistered. Raising `-Xmx` only delays the restart and makes each dump bigger. A controlled rolling restart is fine as a mitigation while you investigate, not as the fix."],"keyPoints":["Separate heap/native/cache growth","inspect GC and live set, take comparable heap dumps, analyze dominators and retention paths"],"examples":[{"title":"Separating heap, native and live-set growth with jcmd","lang":"bash","code":"POD=orders-api-6c8d9-x2k4p\n\n# Which one grows: container memory or the heap?\nkubectl exec $POD -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.current      # cgroup v2\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_info\n\n# Native memory (JVM started with -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary)\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 VM.native_memory baseline\n# ... several hours of normal traffic later ...\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 VM.native_memory summary.diff\n\n# Cheap trend: live-object histogram (forces a full GC), repeated later, then diff\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > histo-morning.txt\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > histo-evening.txt\n\n# Comparable heap dumps of live objects; take the pod out of rotation first\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-morning.hprof\nkubectl exec $POD -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_dump /dumps/orders-evening.hprof","note":"`summary.diff` shows which native category (threads, class, internal, other) grew since the baseline; the two histograms or dumps turn \"what is big\" into \"what is growing\"."},{"title":"A classic leak and its bounded replacement","lang":"java","code":"@Component\npublic class FxRateService {\n\n    // BAD: static map keyed by pair and day, gains new entries forever\n    private static final Map<String, BigDecimal> RATES = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    // GOOD: bounded, expiring and observable, so its size plateaus\n    private final Cache<String, BigDecimal> rates = Caffeine.newBuilder()\n            .maximumSize(50_000)\n            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofHours(12))\n            .recordStats()\n            .build();\n\n    private final FxClient client;\n\n    public FxRateService(FxClient client, MeterRegistry registry) {\n        this.client = client;\n        CaffeineCacheMetrics.monitor(registry, rates, \"fx-rates\");\n    }\n\n    public BigDecimal rate(String pair, LocalDate day) {\n        return rates.get(pair + \":\" + day, key -> client.fetch(pair, day));\n    }\n}","note":"In a heap dump the static `ConcurrentHashMap` shows up in the dominator tree with a GC root path through a class; the Caffeine cache exposes size, hit ratio and evictions so you can prove it is bounded."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you distinguish a leak from a legitimate cache?","a":"A legitimate cache is bounded (by size, weight or time), has an eviction policy, and its size **plateaus** under steady load while the hit ratio stays useful; Caffeine with `recordStats()` exposes size, hit ratio and eviction counts through Micrometer. A leak grows roughly monotonically with time or traffic and never levels off, and its entries are never read again (for example keys containing timestamps or request IDs). Between two dumps, a cache sitting at its configured maximum is a sizing question, while a `HashMap` in a static field or a growing `ThreadLocalMap` is a leak. You can also confirm by clearing the cache or waiting for TTL expiry and checking that the post-GC live set actually drops."}],"extra":["**Low overhead leak profiling:** JFR's `OldObjectSample` event, dumped with `path-to-gc-roots=true`, samples long-lived objects with their allocation stack and reference chain, cheap enough for production when a full heap dump is too disruptive.","**Dumps are disruptive and sensitive:** a heap dump pauses the JVM for seconds and contains customer data, so remove the pod from rotation first, write to a volume with enough space, and store the file encrypted with restricted access.","**Native surprises:** glibc malloc arenas can inflate RSS with many threads (`MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2` is a common mitigation), and Netty or NIO direct buffers are capped by `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize`, not by `-Xmx`.","**Container sizing:** with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` around 70 to 75 there must still be room for non-heap memory; if native growth crosses the container limit the kernel OOMKills the pod without any `OutOfMemoryError` in the logs.","**Automatic capture:** run with `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/dumps` on a persistent volume, so the next failure leaves evidence instead of a clean restart."],"redFlags":["Only increases heap","takes one dump with no baseline"]},{"id":131,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Database CPU is 95%. How do you investigate?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Find which queries consume the CPU (pg_stat_statements by total time, pg_stat_activity right now), check plan regressions, stale statistics, locks, connection storms and recent deploy or traffic changes, and stop the expensive work before scaling.","answer":["First establish **what changed and when**: a deploy, a new batch or reporting job, a traffic spike, a cache outage, a data migration or bulk load. Then find **where the CPU goes**: `pg_stat_statements` ordered by `total_exec_time` (not mean time) shows which statements consume the most in aggregate, with calls, rows and buffers touched, while `pg_stat_activity` shows what runs right now and whether sessions are on CPU (`wait_event` is null) or waiting on locks or I/O.","For the top statements look for a **plan change** with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`: a sequential scan or a hash join over millions of rows where an index scan used to run, caused by **stale statistics** after a bulk load, a shifted data distribution, a dropped index or a prepared statement switching to a generic plan. Compare rows scanned with rows returned per call. Also check **locks** (`pg_blocking_pids`) and the **connection count**: hundreds of active sessions on a 16 core server create contention and context switching, so raising pool sizes makes it worse.","**Stop the expensive work first**: cancel runaway queries, pause the batch or reporting job, disable the feature flag, rate limit the endpoint, move analytics to a replica. Then fix the cause: `ANALYZE`, rewrite the query or add a targeted index validated with `EXPLAIN`, remove N+1 access in code. Scaling the instance up is a last resort: it usually needs a failover, costs money and does not fix a query whose cost grows with the data."],"keyPoints":["Check top queries, plan changes, rows scanned, locks, connections, traffic/deploy changes and statistics","stop expensive work before random scaling"],"examples":[{"title":"Finding the CPU consumers in PostgreSQL","lang":"sql","code":"-- Top statements by total execution time (PostgreSQL 13+ column names)\nSELECT queryid,\n       calls,\n       round(total_exec_time::numeric)       AS total_ms,\n       round(mean_exec_time::numeric, 2)     AS mean_ms,\n       rows / NULLIF(calls, 0)               AS rows_per_call,\n       shared_blks_hit + shared_blks_read    AS blocks_touched,\n       left(query, 100)                      AS query\nFROM pg_stat_statements\nORDER BY total_exec_time DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- What runs right now: on CPU (wait_event IS NULL) or blocked?\nSELECT pid, now() - query_start AS runtime, state, wait_event_type, wait_event,\n       pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by, left(query, 80) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE state <> 'idle' AND backend_type = 'client backend'\nORDER BY runtime DESC;\n\n-- Stale statistics or unexpected sequential scans?\nSELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_mod_since_analyze, last_autoanalyze, seq_scan, idx_scan\nFROM pg_stat_user_tables\nORDER BY n_mod_since_analyze DESC\nLIMIT 10;","note":"Sorting by total time finds both the slow statement and the fast one executed a million times; `blocks_touched` divided by rows returned reveals scans that read far more than they return."},{"title":"Stopping the bleeding, then checking the plan","lang":"sql","code":"-- Cancel long running reporting queries (cancel first, terminate only if needed)\nSELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid)\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE usename = 'reporting'\n  AND state = 'active'\n  AND now() - query_start > interval '2 minutes';\n\n-- Guardrail for new sessions of that role\nALTER ROLE reporting SET statement_timeout = '60s';\n\n-- Plan check with real row counts and buffer usage (this executes the query)\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT id, amount, created_at\nFROM payment\nWHERE merchant_id = 4711 AND status = 'PENDING'\nORDER BY created_at\nLIMIT 50;\n\n-- Estimates far from actual rows: refresh statistics\nANALYZE payment;","note":"Mitigation comes before optimization: the role-level timeout keeps one reporting tool from saturating the primary again while you fix the underlying plan."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What if one query is fast individually but called 100x more?","a":"Then total time, not mean time, is the problem: 2 ms times 5,000 calls per second is 10 CPU seconds per second, which saturates the server even though each trace looks fine. `pg_stat_statements` sorted by `calls` and `total_exec_time` exposes it, and comparing query calls with HTTP requests shows the multiplier. Typical sources are N+1 access from lazy loading (fix with a fetch join, an entity graph or `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size`), a cache that expired or went down so every request hits the database, a retry storm, or a client polling too aggressively. Fix it at the caller by batching, caching, deduplicating or rate limiting; an index will not help a query that is already fast."}],"extra":["**Generic plan trap:** PostgreSQL uses custom plans for the first five executions of a prepared statement and may then switch to a cached generic plan that ignores skewed parameter values; pgJDBC switches to server-side prepared statements after `prepareThreshold` (default 5) uses, so a plan can go bad minutes after a deploy, and `plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan` is a targeted fix.","**auto_explain:** setting `auto_explain.log_min_duration` logs plans of slow statements as they happen, which catches intermittent plan flips you cannot reproduce later.","**Windowed statistics:** `pg_stat_statements` is cumulative since the last reset, so snapshot it every few minutes (or call `pg_stat_statements_reset()` when the investigation starts) to see today's top consumers rather than last month's.","**Pool sizing:** HikariCP's guidance is a small pool, roughly cores times two plus effective spindles on the database, shared across all application instances; 40 pods with 20 connections each is 800 sessions, far beyond what a 16 core server runs efficiently.","**Other CPU sinks:** autovacuum on heavily updated tables, JIT compilation for complex queries (the `jit` setting), and bloated tables or indexes that make every scan touch more pages."],"redFlags":["Adds indexes blindly or increases connections"]},{"id":132,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"One pod is slow while other replicas are healthy. What could cause it?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"One slow replica means a local cause: CPU throttling or a noisy node, GC pressure, a bad pooled connection, cold caches, uneven load, disk or network trouble, stuck threads or config drift; compare pod-level signals.","answer":["If one replica is slow while the others run the same code on the same traffic mix, look for something **local**. On the node side: a noisy neighbor, CPU steal, **CFS throttling** from a tight CPU limit, memory pressure, a slow or full local disk, a flaky NIC, a full conntrack table, DNS trouble, or simply a node in a different availability zone from the database. On the pod side: **GC pressure** because its heap is fuller, a **stuck or deadlocked** thread pool, a pooled **connection** to a degraded database replica or a stale DNS entry, a **cold cache** or unwarmed JIT after a recent restart, or **version and config drift** (a different image digest, ConfigMap or feature flag).","**Uneven load** is the other big category: long-lived HTTP/2 or gRPC connections pinned to one pod by L4 load balancing, sticky sessions, a hot Kafka partition assigned to that consumer, or one heavy tenant routed there.","Investigate by **comparing pod-level signals** side by side: latency and error rate by `pod`, request rate by `pod` (is it simply getting more?), CPU usage and throttling, GC pause time, heap after GC, thread and connection pool metrics, uptime and restarts, node and zone, image digest. Capture a thread dump and a short JFR recording from the slow pod before touching it, then take it out of rotation, or cordon the node if the problem follows the node. Restarting alone erases the evidence and the problem often returns."],"keyPoints":["Node/resource throttling, bad connection, GC, cache state, uneven load, local disk/network, stuck threads or version/config drift","compare pod-level signals"],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing the slow pod with its siblings","lang":"bash","code":"NS=credit; SLOW=credit-api-5f7c-q8z2m\n\n# Same image, same node type? Where does each replica run?\nCOLS='POD:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName,IMAGE:.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'\nkubectl -n $NS get pods -l app=credit-api -o custom-columns=\"$COLS\"\n\n# Node pressure and neighbors\nNODE=$(kubectl -n $NS get pod $SLOW -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')\nkubectl describe node \"$NODE\" | grep -A8 -E \"Conditions|Allocated resources\"\nkubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -15\n\n# CPU throttling from inside the container (cgroup v2)\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\n\n# JVM view on the slow pod only: heap, GC and blocked threads\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- jcmd 1 GC.heap_info\nkubectl -n $NS exec $SLOW -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > slow-threads.txt\ngrep -c \"java.lang.Thread.State: BLOCKED\" slow-threads.txt","note":"The goal is a diff between the slow pod and a healthy one: image digest, node, throttling counters and thread states usually expose the local cause quickly."},{"title":"Alerting on per-pod CPU throttling","lang":"yaml","code":"groups:\n  - name: credit-api-pods\n    rules:\n      - alert: CreditApiPodCpuThrottled\n        expr: |\n          sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\", container=\"credit-api\"}[5m]))\n          /\n          sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\", container=\"credit-api\"}[5m]))\n          > 0.25\n        for: 10m\n        labels:\n          severity: ticket\n          team: credit\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"{{ $labels.pod }} is throttled in more than 25% of CFS periods\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.internal/credit-api/cpu-throttling\"","note":"Grouping by `pod` is the point: an aggregate over all replicas hides the one pod that is throttled while the others are fine."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you detect CPU throttling?","a":"Kubernetes enforces a CPU limit as a CFS quota per 100 ms period, so a container can be throttled even when its average CPU looks low. From cAdvisor metrics, compute `rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total[5m]) / rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total[5m])` per pod and look at `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_seconds_total`; inside the container, `/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat` (cgroup v2) shows `nr_periods`, `nr_throttled` and `throttled_usec`. In the application it looks like latency spikes in roughly 100 ms steps and long GC pauses without heavy allocation. Fix it by raising or removing the CPU limit (keep the request), reducing parallel GC and JIT threads, or scaling out."}],"extra":["**Tail from one pod:** with round-robin across 10 replicas, one bad pod affects about 10% of requests, which shows up as a normal P50 and a terrible P99 in the aggregate; always break latency down by `pod` before blaming the code.","**JVM CPU awareness:** the JVM sizes GC threads, JIT compiler threads and `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` from the container CPU limit, and parallel GC phases can burn a whole quota period in milliseconds; many teams remove CPU limits while keeping requests, or set `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount` explicitly.","**Quarantine instead of kill:** removing the Service selector label from the pod makes the ReplicaSet start a replacement while the sick pod stays alive, out of the endpoints, for debugging.","**Outlier ejection:** a service mesh or proxy with outlier detection (Envoy, Istio `outlierDetection`) automatically ejects a replica returning consecutive errors, limiting blast radius while you investigate.","**Node signals:** node-exporter's CPU `steal` mode, pressure stall information (PSI) and kubelet eviction events reveal node trouble that pod-level metrics hide."],"redFlags":["Assumes application code affects every pod equally","only restarts"]},{"id":133,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"Pods restart every few minutes. Where do you look?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Read the pod's events and last termination state (reason, exit code, OOMKilled), the previous container's logs, probe failures and resource limits, then check startup timing, dependencies and graceful shutdown.","answer":["Start with `kubectl describe pod`: **Last State** shows the termination **reason** and **exit code**, and **Events** show probe failures, back-off, scheduling and eviction. Exit code 137 means SIGKILL: with reason `OOMKilled` the container exceeded its memory limit, otherwise it was killed after the grace period. Exit code 143 means the JVM handled SIGTERM (typical after a liveness failure), and 1 or another small code means the application exited on its own, usually a startup failure. Then read **`kubectl logs --previous`**, because the current container's logs begin after the crash and hide the cause.","Map causes to those signals. **OOMKilled**: heap plus metaspace, thread stacks and direct buffers exceed the limit; fix sizing with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` and a realistic limit. **Liveness failures**: a tight probe timeout during GC pauses or CPU throttling, or a liveness check that includes the database, so one DB blip restarts every pod. **Startup timing**: Spring Boot starting slowly under a low CPU limit exceeds the liveness delay; use a `startupProbe`. **Dependencies and config**: a missing secret, an unreachable database or a failed Flyway migration makes the app exit, ending in `CrashLoopBackOff`.","Finally check **graceful shutdown** and limits: if SIGTERM is not completed within `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, the kubelet sends SIGKILL, in-flight requests fail and you see 137 without an OOM. Compare requests and limits with real usage, and check whether restarts correlate with deploys, node events or traffic peaks."],"keyPoints":["Events and termination reason, exit code, OOMKilled, probe failures, logs, resource limits, dependency/startup timing and graceful shutdown"],"examples":[{"title":"Where to look, in order","lang":"bash","code":"NS=orders; POD=orders-api-7b9c-kx2lp\n\nkubectl -n $NS get pods -l app=orders-api            # RESTARTS column, CrashLoopBackOff?\nkubectl -n $NS describe pod $POD                     # Last State, Reason, Exit Code, Events\n\n# Termination details of the previous container as JSON\nkubectl -n $NS get pod $POD \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated}'\n# {\"exitCode\":137,\"reason\":\"OOMKilled\",\"startedAt\":\"...\",\"finishedAt\":\"...\"}\n\n# Logs of the container that died, not the fresh one\nkubectl -n $NS logs $POD --previous --tail=200\n\n# Probe failures, kills and back-off for this pod only\nkubectl -n $NS get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=$POD --sort-by=.lastTimestamp","note":"The termination reason and exit code usually decide the branch of the investigation within a minute: OOM, probe kill, application crash or shutdown timeout."},{"title":"Probes, memory and shutdown configured to avoid restart loops","lang":"yaml","code":"spec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45       # longer than preStop plus Spring's shutdown phase\n  containers:\n    - name: orders-api\n      image: registry.internal/orders-api:1.18.3\n      resources:\n        requests: { cpu: \"1\", memory: 1Gi }\n        limits: { memory: 1Gi }\n      env:\n        - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS\n          value: \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=70 -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError\"\n      startupProbe:                         # protects slow starts from liveness kills\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n        failureThreshold: 36                # up to 3 minutes\n      livenessProbe:                        # process health only, no database check\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        timeoutSeconds: 3\n        failureThreshold: 3\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec: { command: [\"sleep\", \"5\"] }  # let endpoints update before shutdown","note":"Heap is capped at 70% of the container limit to leave room for native memory, the startup probe covers slow boots, and liveness never depends on external systems."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Liveness failure vs OOMKilled: how do symptoms differ?","a":"OOMKilled shows `Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137`, no probe events, logs that stop abruptly without shutdown messages, and a container memory graph that climbs to the limit just before each restart. A liveness failure shows events such as `Liveness probe failed: ... context deadline exceeded` followed by `Container orders-api failed liveness probe, will be restarted`; the kubelet sends SIGTERM, so you usually see Spring's graceful shutdown logs and exit code 143, or 137 if it did not stop within the grace period. Memory is typically normal in that case, while GC pauses, CPU throttling or an exhausted thread pool line up with the probe timeouts."}],"extra":["**Back-off timing:** the kubelet restart delay doubles from 10 s up to a 5 minute cap and resets after the container runs cleanly for 10 minutes, so `CrashLoopBackOff` pods look idle between attempts.","**Alert on it:** kube-state-metrics exposes `kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total` and `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason`, so you can alert on restart rate per reason instead of finding restarts by accident.","**Java OOM vs kernel OOM:** a heap `OutOfMemoryError` appears in the logs and leaves the JVM in an undefined state, so `-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` makes it exit and restart cleanly; a kernel OOMKill leaves no Java stack trace at all.","**Probes on the right port:** if Actuator runs on a separate management port, `management.endpoint.health.probes.add-additional-paths=true` also serves `/livez` and `/readyz` on the main port, so probes reflect the server that actually handles traffic.","**Graceful shutdown in Spring Boot:** it is on by default since 3.4 and bounded by `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase` (30 s by default), so `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` must exceed that plus any `preStop` sleep."],"redFlags":["Only reads current logs","ignores previous container logs and events"]},{"id":134,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"An external API becomes very slow. How should your service behave?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Fail fast and contain it: strict timeouts within an overall deadline, few retries with backoff for idempotent calls only, a circuit breaker, a bulkhead capping concurrency, a degraded fallback, and per-dependency metrics.","answer":["A slow dependency is more dangerous than a dead one, because every waiting request holds a thread, a pooled HTTP connection and sometimes a database connection. By Little's law, if the API goes from 100 ms to 10 s at the same traffic, in-flight calls grow a hundredfold and your service falls over as well. **Bound the damage**: set **connect and read timeouts** on every client (the JDK `HttpClient` and several request factories wait indefinitely by default) and derive them from an overall **deadline** for the inbound request, so a call never outlives its caller's own timeout.","**Retry** only transient failures of idempotent operations, at one layer, with one or two extra attempts, exponential backoff and jitter; retrying a slow service synchronously multiplies its load exactly when it is struggling. A **circuit breaker** (Resilience4j, using both failure rate and slow-call rate) opens after sustained problems so calls fail immediately and the dependency gets room to recover, then probes in half-open state. A **bulkhead** (semaphore or dedicated pool) caps concurrent calls to that dependency so it can only consume its own slice of capacity, which still matters with Java 21 virtual threads because connection pools and memory remain finite.","Choose the **fallback** per use case: cached data, a default, a degraded response, or queue the work and answer asynchronously (a credit application goes to manual review instead of failing). Never hold a database transaction open around the remote call. Emit **metrics** per dependency: latency histogram, timeouts, breaker state, bulkhead rejections and fallback rate."],"keyPoints":["Apply deadlines, bounded retry, circuit breaker, bulkhead, fallback/degraded response and metrics","protect threads and connection pools"],"examples":[{"title":"Resilience4j configuration for a slow credit bureau","lang":"yaml","code":"resilience4j:\n  circuitbreaker:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        sliding-window-type: COUNT_BASED\n        sliding-window-size: 50\n        minimum-number-of-calls: 20\n        failure-rate-threshold: 50\n        slow-call-duration-threshold: 500ms\n        slow-call-rate-threshold: 60\n        wait-duration-in-open-state: 20s\n        permitted-number-of-calls-in-half-open-state: 5\n  retry:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        max-attempts: 2                    # one retry only\n        wait-duration: 200ms\n        enable-exponential-backoff: true\n        exponential-backoff-multiplier: 2\n        retry-exceptions:\n          - org.springframework.web.client.ResourceAccessException\n          - org.springframework.web.client.HttpServerErrorException$ServiceUnavailable\n  bulkhead:\n    instances:\n      creditBureau:\n        max-concurrent-calls: 30           # this dependency can never hold more than 30 threads\n        max-wait-duration: 0ms             # reject immediately instead of queueing","note":"The breaker opens on slowness, not only on errors, and the bulkhead rejects instantly when full, so the bureau can never consume more than a fixed share of your capacity."},{"title":"Client with timeouts, retry, breaker, bulkhead and fallback","lang":"java","code":"@Service\npublic class CreditBureauClient {\n\n    private final RestClient restClient;\n\n    public CreditBureauClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {\n        var factory = new JdkClientHttpRequestFactory(\n                HttpClient.newBuilder().connectTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(300)).build());\n        factory.setReadTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(800));\n        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl(\"https://bureau.example.com\")\n                .requestFactory(factory)\n                .build();\n    }\n\n    // Fallback on the outermost aspect (Retry), so inner failures still reach the retry logic\n    @Retry(name = \"creditBureau\", fallbackMethod = \"manualReview\")\n    @CircuitBreaker(name = \"creditBureau\")\n    @Bulkhead(name = \"creditBureau\")\n    public BureauScore score(String applicationId, String customerRef) {\n        return restClient.get()\n                .uri(\"/v2/scores/{ref}\", customerRef)\n                .retrieve()\n                .body(BureauScore.class);\n    }\n\n    private BureauScore manualReview(String applicationId, String customerRef, Throwable cause) {\n        // Degraded answer: the decision engine routes the application to manual review\n        return BureauScore.unavailable(applicationId);\n    }\n}","note":"Worst case is bounded: 300 ms connect plus 800 ms read, at most two attempts, and an open breaker or full bulkhead goes straight to the fallback without touching the network."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which operations are safe to retry?","a":"Operations that are idempotent, meaning repeating them has the same effect as doing them once: reads (`GET`, `HEAD`), `PUT` and `DELETE` when the server implements HTTP semantics correctly, and `POST` only with an idempotency key that the server deduplicates (the standard pattern for payments and order creation). Retry only transient errors: connection failures, 503, 429 with `Retry-After`, and some 502 or 504 responses. A timeout on a non-idempotent call is ambiguous because the server may have completed it, so either send an idempotency key or query the status before retrying. Never retry validation errors (400, 422) or authentication and authorization failures."}],"extra":["**Retry amplification:** three layers each making three attempts turn one user request into up to 27 calls to the bottom dependency, so retry at one layer only and consider a retry budget (for example retries capped at 10% of normal traffic).","**Pool acquire timeouts count too:** set a connection request timeout on pooled HTTP clients and keep HikariCP's `connectionTimeout` short, otherwise requests queue invisibly for a connection before the read timeout even starts.","**Aspect order:** Resilience4j's Spring Boot default order is Retry around CircuitBreaker around RateLimiter around TimeLimiter around Bulkhead, so a fallback on an inner annotation swallows the exception and Retry never retries.","**Hedged requests:** for idempotent reads against replicated backends, sending a second request after roughly the P95 latency and taking the first answer cuts tail latency, at the price of extra load.","**Respect backpressure:** honor `Retry-After` on 429 and 503, and apply load shedding or adaptive concurrency on your own inbound side so you degrade gracefully instead of queueing."],"redFlags":["Waits indefinitely","retries synchronously many times"]},{"id":135,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"A message queue backlog grows continuously. What do you check?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Compare ingress rate with processing rate and watch the oldest message's age, then check consumer errors and rebalances, downstream saturation, partition or queue skew, poison messages and whether capacity really exceeds peak load.","answer":["Depth alone says little: a million messages may be two minutes of normal work or two days. First compare the **ingress rate** with the **processing rate** (acks or committed offsets per second): a backlog only grows while ingress exceeds throughput, so find out whether producers spiked (a campaign, a replay, an upstream batch) or consumption dropped. Then track the **age of the oldest message**, or lag expressed in time, which is what the business feels and what you should alert on.","If throughput dropped, inspect the consumers: **error and retry rates**, redeliveries, Kafka **rebalances** (processing longer than `max.poll.interval.ms` gets a consumer evicted and its batch redone), how many consumers are alive, and their CPU, GC and time per message. Very often the real bottleneck is **downstream saturation**: the database, an external API or a lock every message needs, and adding consumers then only adds contention.","Check **distribution**: lag concentrated on one partition means a hot key or a stuck consumer, and Kafka cannot use more consumers than partitions in a group; in RabbitMQ a large prefetch lets one slow consumer hoard messages. Look for **poison messages** that fail forever and block a partition or cycle through redelivery. Finally check **capacity**: per-consumer throughput times consumer count must exceed peak ingress with headroom, and drain time is backlog divided by (consume rate minus ingress rate). Scale consumers only as far as the downstream can absorb."],"keyPoints":["Ingress vs processing rate, oldest-message age, consumer errors, downstream saturation, partition/queue distribution, poison messages and capacity"],"examples":[{"title":"Where is the lag, and is it moving?","lang":"bash","code":"# Kafka: lag per partition for the consumer group\nkafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server kafka:9092 \\\n  --describe --group payment-settlement\n\n# GROUP               TOPIC     PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG      CONSUMER-ID\n# payment-settlement  payments  0          1840211         1840390         179      consumer-1\n# payment-settlement  payments  7          922004          1391887         469883   consumer-4\n\n# Run it again a minute later: if CURRENT-OFFSET on partition 7 has not moved,\n# suspect a poison record or a stuck consumer; if it moves slowly, suspect a hot key.\n\n# RabbitMQ: ready vs unacknowledged messages and consumers per queue\nrabbitmqctl list_queues name messages_ready messages_unacknowledged consumers","note":"Lag concentrated on one partition is a distribution or poison problem, not a capacity problem, and adding consumers will not help it."},{"title":"Bounded retries and a dead-letter topic in Spring Kafka","lang":"java","code":"@Configuration\npublic class SettlementConsumerConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    DefaultErrorHandler settlementErrorHandler(KafkaTemplate<Object, Object> template) {\n        // After retries are exhausted, publish the record to payments.DLT and move on\n        var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template);\n\n        var backOff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);\n        backOff.setInitialInterval(500);\n        backOff.setMultiplier(2.0);\n\n        var handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backOff);\n        // Retrying cannot fix bad data: send these to the DLT immediately\n        handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(InvalidSettlementException.class);\n        return handler;\n    }\n}","note":"Spring Boot wires a single `CommonErrorHandler` bean into the auto-configured listener container factory. Keep total backoff well below `max.poll.interval.ms`, or use `@RetryableTopic` for non-blocking retries."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What indicates a poison-message loop?","a":"The same message key, ID or offset appears in error logs again and again with the same exception (often a deserialization or validation error), while the committed offset of one partition stops moving and the others advance. In RabbitMQ you see the redelivered flag or a rising `x-delivery-count`, messages cycling between ready and unacked, and consumers busy with near-zero successful acks. If processing a batch exceeds `max.poll.interval.ms`, it can also appear as a rebalance loop. The fix is bounded retries followed by a dead-letter destination (`DefaultErrorHandler` with `DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer` in Spring Kafka, `delivery-limit` or a dead-letter exchange in RabbitMQ) plus an alert on DLQ growth."}],"extra":["**Kafka poll defaults:** `max.poll.records` defaults to 500 and `max.poll.interval.ms` to 5 minutes; if 500 records against a slow downstream exceed the interval, the group rebalances repeatedly and nothing commits, which looks like a stuck backlog with busy consumers.","**Lag in time:** tools such as Burrow or kafka-lag-exporter, or SQS `ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage`, turn offsets into seconds, which enables SLOs like \"settlement events processed within 5 minutes\".","**Autoscaling guard:** KEDA can scale consumers on lag, but set a maximum replica count derived from downstream capacity such as database connections, or the autoscaler will overload your own database.","**Rebalance hygiene:** the cooperative sticky assignor and static membership (`group.instance.id`) reduce stop-the-world rebalances during rolling deploys, which otherwise pause consumption across the whole group.","**RabbitMQ specifics:** quorum queues support a `delivery-limit` that dead-letters a message after repeated redeliveries, and Spring AMQP's default prefetch of 250 can let one slow consumer sit on work others could process."],"redFlags":["Looks only at depth","scales consumers beyond DB capacity"]},{"id":136,"category":"Production Troubleshooting","question":"A production issue disappears after restart. Is it solved?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"No: a restart is a mitigation that resets accumulated state; capture evidence first, find what accumulated (memory, threads, connections, locks, caches), fix the cause, and add detection and a runbook so it cannot silently return.","answer":["A restart that makes the symptom disappear proves only that the problem lives in **process state**: something accumulated or got stuck and the restart wiped it. The cause is still there and will come back, often at a worse moment, and every restart destroys the evidence needed to find it. So treat a restart as **mitigation, not root cause**: keep the incident open and make the root cause a tracked action item with an owner.","**Preserve evidence first** whenever you can: take the instance out of rotation instead of killing it, then capture thread dumps, a heap histogram or dump, the JFR recording, GC logs, pool, thread and file descriptor counts, and the logs and events. If users are hurting badly, restart the other replicas and keep one sick instance isolated for analysis.","Then look for **state or resource accumulation**: heap live set, native memory, threads, file descriptors, HTTP or database connections, a growing cache, a lock or stuck scheduled job, a DNS or connection cache pointing at a failed-over endpoint, or credentials and certificates that expired in memory. Plot them against time since start: a problem that appears N hours after every restart is a strong accumulation signal. Finally define **prevention** (bound the cache, add the timeout, fix the leak), add **detection** on the leading indicator so you alert before users notice, and write a **runbook** covering evidence capture and safe mitigation. Scheduled restarts are acceptable only as a documented stopgap with a ticket."],"keyPoints":["Restart is mitigation, not root cause","preserve evidence first, identify state/resource accumulation, define prevention and add detection/runbook"],"examples":[{"title":"Evidence capture script to run before restarting a sick pod","lang":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nNS=payments; POD=$1; OUT=evidence-$POD-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)\nmkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n\n# Remove the selector label: the Service stops routing to it, the ReplicaSet starts a replacement\nkubectl -n $NS label pod \"$POD\" app- quarantine=true\n\nkubectl -n $NS describe pod \"$POD\" > \"$OUT/describe.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=\"$POD\" > \"$OUT/events.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS logs \"$POD\" --since=2h > \"$OUT/app.log\"\nkubectl -n $NS logs \"$POD\" --previous > \"$OUT/app-previous.log\" 2>/dev/null || true\n\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  kubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 Thread.print > \"$OUT/threads-$i.txt\"\n  sleep 10\ndone\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 GC.class_histogram > \"$OUT/histogram.txt\"\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- jcmd 1 JFR.dump name=1 filename=/tmp/incident.jfr\nkubectl -n $NS cp \"$POD:/tmp/incident.jfr\" \"$OUT/incident.jfr\"\nkubectl -n $NS exec \"$POD\" -- sh -c 'ls /proc/1/fd | wc -l' > \"$OUT/open-fds.txt\"","note":"The quarantined pod keeps its broken state for analysis while capacity is restored by the replacement. `JFR.dump` assumes a continuous recording started with `-XX:StartFlightRecording`; delete the quarantined pod when done."},{"title":"Closing the incident properly: evidence, finding, actions","lang":"text","code":"Incident: payments-api P99 above 5 s, mitigated by restart at 14:32\nStatus after restart: MITIGATED, not resolved\n\nEvidence                            Finding\nthreads-1..3.txt                    180 request threads waiting in HikariPool.getConnection\nhistogram.txt                       heap stable, no growth\nhikaricp.connections.active         at max (20) for 40 minutes, 150 pending\napplication logs                    ReportExportJob opens connections and never closes them\n\nAction item                                                Owner      Type\nClose connections in ReportExportJob (try-with-resources)  payments   prevention\nEnable leakDetectionThreshold=30000 on the pool            payments   detection\nAlert: hikaricp pending > 10 for 5 minutes                 payments   detection\nRunbook: run evidence script before any restart            sre        runbook","note":"The incident is resolved only when prevention, detection and runbook items exist and are owned; the restart merely bought time."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What evidence should be captured before restart?","a":"At minimum three thread dumps about 10 s apart (`jcmd <pid> Thread.print`, or `jcmd <pid> Thread.dump_to_file -format=json <file>` to include virtual threads), a class histogram or heap dump if memory is involved, a dump of the continuous JFR recording, and the GC log. From the platform capture `kubectl describe pod`, events, current and `--previous` logs, restart count and resource usage. Also snapshot key metrics with their time range (pools, threads, file descriptors, connections, latency and errors by instance), the exact version and config (image digest, feature flags), and timestamps of every action taken. Treat dumps as sensitive data, since they contain customer information."}],"extra":["**Time to failure:** record uptime at the moment of failure across incidents; a consistent interval after restart (for example about three days) strongly suggests a leak, exhaustion or counter overflow, while random timing suggests an external trigger.","**Kubernetes hides restarts:** liveness probes and OOMKills restart pods automatically, so a problem can persist for months unnoticed; alert on restart rate and last termination reason, not only on availability.","**Continuous JFR:** running with `-XX:StartFlightRecording` and bounded `maxage` or `maxsize` keeps the last minutes before an incident in a ring buffer ready to dump, at low overhead.","**Leading indicators:** alert on the accumulating resource (post-GC live set trend, pool pending, thread or FD count) rather than on the eventual user-facing failure, so the next occurrence is caught hours earlier.","**Blameless postmortem:** record the timeline, the evidence, why detection did not fire earlier, and concrete action items with owners and dates; \"restarted, monitoring\" is not a closure."],"redFlags":["Closes incident after restart","collects no evidence"]},{"id":137,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"How do Docker image layers affect build speed, size and security?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Each Dockerfile instruction creates a cached, immutable layer, so put stable dependency layers before source code, use multi-stage builds on small trusted bases, run as non-root, and never put secrets in any layer.","answer":["Every `RUN`, `COPY` and `ADD` instruction produces an immutable, content-addressed layer, and the build cache reuses a layer only while its instruction and inputs are unchanged. The first cache miss invalidates every layer after it, so order instructions from least to most frequently changing: base image, OS packages, dependency manifests (`pom.xml`, `build.gradle`) plus a dependency resolution step, and only then the source code. Copying the whole repository before resolving dependencies means a one-line change re-downloads the entire Maven repository on every build.","Layers are additive, so size only goes up: deleting files in a later layer hides them but still ships the bytes. **Multi-stage builds** fix this by compiling with a full JDK and build tools in a builder stage and copying only the jar into a small runtime base such as `eclipse-temurin:21-jre` or a distroless Java image. Smaller images pull faster, which directly shortens scale-out and node recovery time.","For security, every layer is readable by anyone who can pull the image, so credentials must never appear in a copied file, `ENV` or `ARG`, even if removed later. Use BuildKit secret mounts (`RUN --mount=type=secret`) for build-time credentials and inject runtime secrets at deploy time. Prefer a minimal, trusted, pinned base (fewer packages, fewer CVEs), run as a **non-root user**, keep a `.dockerignore` so `.git`, `target/` and local `.env` files never enter the build context, and scan the final image in CI."],"keyPoints":["Order stable dependency layers before changing source","use multi-stage builds, small trusted bases, non-root user and no secrets in layers"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good Dockerfile for a Spring Boot service","lang":"dockerfile","code":"# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\n\n# BAD: any source change busts the dependency cache, JDK and Maven ship to prod,\n# the password lives forever in image metadata, and the process runs as root\n# FROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21\n# COPY . /app\n# ENV DB_PASSWORD=s3cr3t\n# RUN mvn -f /app/pom.xml package\n# CMD java -jar /app/target/orders-service.jar\n\n# GOOD: dependency layer cached, build tools left behind, non-root runtime\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-21 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY pom.xml .\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.m2 mvn -B -q dependency:go-offline\nCOPY src ./src\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.m2 mvn -B -q package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine\nRUN addgroup -S app && adduser -S app -G app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /src/target/orders-service.jar app.jar\nUSER app\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75\", \"-jar\", \"app.jar\"]","note":"Only `pom.xml` is copied before dependency resolution, so code edits reuse the cached dependency layer, and the BuildKit cache mount keeps `~/.m2` between builds without writing it into any layer. The exec-form `ENTRYPOINT` makes the JVM PID 1 so it receives SIGTERM directly."},{"title":"Build-time secret done right, and proof that a deleted secret still ships","lang":"bash","code":"# GOOD: the secret is mounted for a single RUN step and never written to a layer\n# Dockerfile line:\n#   RUN --mount=type=secret,id=maven_settings,target=/root/.m2/settings.xml mvn -B package\ndocker build --secret id=maven_settings,src=\"$HOME/.m2/settings.xml\" -t orders-service:1.4.2 .\n\n# BAD image: settings.xml was COPYed, then removed by a later \"RUN rm\"\ndocker history --no-trunc orders-service:bad     # ENV and ARG values are visible here\ndocker save orders-service:bad -o orders-bad.tar\nmkdir -p bad && tar -xf orders-bad.tar -C bad\nfor layer in $(find bad -type f); do\n  tar -tf \"$layer\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'settings.xml' && echo \"secret still in $layer\"\ndone","note":"The loop finds the file inside the earlier layer archive even though the final filesystem view no longer shows it. Anyone with pull access to the registry can do the same."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why does deleting a secret in a later layer not remove it?","a":"Image layers are immutable tar archives stacked by a union filesystem; `RUN rm secret.txt` does not modify the earlier layer, it adds a new layer containing a whiteout marker that hides the file in the merged view. The original layer, with the secret bytes, is still part of the image and is pushed, pulled and cached like any other. Anyone with pull access can extract it with `docker save` or registry tooling, and `ENV` or `ARG` values also remain visible in `docker history`. The fix is to never write the secret to a layer (BuildKit `--mount=type=secret`, or keep it in a builder stage that is never pushed), and to rotate any credential that already leaked."}],"extra":["**Pin by digest:** `FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre@sha256:...` makes builds reproducible because tags are mutable; let Renovate or Dependabot bump the digest so you still receive security patches.","**Spring Boot layered jars:** Boot can split the fat jar into dependencies, loader, snapshot-dependencies and application layers (`-Djarmode=tools` since Boot 3.3, `layertools` before), so a code change pushes only the small application layer; Buildpacks (`spring-boot:build-image`) and Jib do this automatically.","**Container-aware JVM:** JDK 21 reads cgroup v1 and v2 limits, so size the heap with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` instead of a fixed `-Xmx` that ignores the pod memory limit.","**Distroless trade-off:** no shell or package manager shrinks the attack surface and CVE count, but you debug with `kubectl debug` ephemeral containers instead of `exec` into a shell.","**Leaked means rotate:** once a secret was pushed in any layer, rebuilding does not help because old digests stay pullable from registries, mirrors and caches; rotate the credential first, then purge the images."],"redFlags":["Copies entire repo before dependency install","stores credentials in Dockerfile"]},{"id":138,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Liveness, readiness and startup probes: when does each run?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Startup probes gate slow boot, readiness decides whether the pod receives Service traffic, and liveness restarts a truly stuck process; only readiness may reflect hard dependencies, and liveness must stay local and cheap.","answer":["All three are HTTP, TCP, gRPC or exec checks that the kubelet runs periodically against each container. The **startup probe** runs first; until it succeeds, liveness and readiness are disabled, and if it fails `failureThreshold × periodSeconds` in a row the container is restarted. It protects slow initialization (JVM boot, Flyway migrations, cache warmup) so you need neither a huge `initialDelaySeconds` nor a lax liveness probe.","The **readiness probe** runs for the container's whole life. Failure removes the pod from the Service's EndpointSlices, so it stops receiving new traffic, but it is not restarted. Use it for \"cannot serve right now\": still warming up, draining at shutdown, or a hard dependency the pod genuinely cannot work without.","The **liveness probe** also runs for the whole life once startup has succeeded, and failure makes the kubelet kill and restart the container. It should detect only states a restart fixes: deadlocked threads, a wedged event loop, an unrecoverable internal error. It must not check the database, Kafka or downstream APIs, because a slow dependency would fail liveness on every replica at once and trigger mass restarts. Optional dependencies should not even fail readiness; degrade with fallbacks and circuit breakers instead.","Spring Boot 3 exposes `/actuator/health/liveness` and `/actuator/health/readiness`, backed by `ApplicationAvailability` and enabled automatically on Kubernetes. By default neither group includes external checks, and that default is usually right."],"keyPoints":["Startup protects slow initialization","readiness controls traffic","liveness restarts a stuck process","liveness should not depend on optional downstream services"],"examples":[{"title":"Three probes for a JVM service","lang":"yaml","code":"containers:\n  - name: payments-api\n    image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.8.3\n    ports:\n      - containerPort: 8080\n    startupProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 36      # up to 180s to boot before a restart\n    readinessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 2       # leave the load balancer quickly\n    livenessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 10\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3       # tolerate a GC pause or a throttled period","note":"Startup and liveness can share the cheap liveness endpoint; the startup probe simply allows a much larger failure budget. Readiness reacts fast, liveness is deliberately tolerant."},{"title":"Spring Boot probe groups","lang":"properties","code":"# Probe groups are auto-enabled on Kubernetes; this enables them locally too\nmanagement.endpoint.health.probes.enabled=true\n# With a separate management port, also expose /livez and /readyz on the main port\nmanagement.endpoint.health.probes.add-additional-paths=true\n# Liveness: internal state only, never external systems\nmanagement.endpoint.health.group.liveness.include=livenessState\n# Readiness: add a dependency only if the pod is useless without it\nmanagement.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include=readinessState,db","note":"Including `db` in readiness is a deliberate choice for a service that can do nothing without its database. Because all replicas share that database, an outage empties the Service of endpoints, so leave it out when the app can still serve cached reads or return controlled errors."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Why can a bad liveness probe amplify an outage?","a":"If liveness checks a shared dependency such as the database, Redis or a downstream API, a slowdown there makes every replica fail at the same moment, and the kubelet restarts them all. The restarted JVMs come back cold, without caches, JIT warmth or connection pools, and hammer the already struggling dependency with reconnects and warmup queries while serving capacity drops to zero and CrashLoopBackOff delays recovery. A tight `timeoutSeconds` combined with CPU throttling or a long GC pause can cause the same cascade with no dependency involved. Keep liveness local, cheap and tolerant (`failureThreshold` of 3 or more), and let readiness, timeouts and circuit breakers deal with dependency trouble."}],"extra":["**Detection math:** time to act is roughly `periodSeconds × failureThreshold` (plus `timeoutSeconds`); a liveness failure restarts only the container, not the pod, and repeated failures lead to CrashLoopBackOff with exponential backoff.","**Separate management port gotcha:** with `management.server.port` the probe hits a different connector and thread pool, so liveness can pass while request threads are exhausted; `add-additional-paths=true` serves `/livez` and `/readyz` on the main port.","**Shared dependency in readiness:** if every replica fails readiness because of the same database outage, the Service has zero endpoints and clients get connection errors instead of meaningful 503 responses from your app.","**Native gRPC probes:** Kubernetes supports `grpc` probes natively (GA in 1.27), so gRPC services no longer need the `grpc_health_probe` binary; exec probes fork a process every period and are costly, especially with JVM tools.","**Custom liveness signals:** publish `AvailabilityChangeEvent` with `LivenessState.BROKEN` when the app detects an unrecoverable state (for example a poisoned internal cache), letting Kubernetes restart it cleanly."],"redFlags":["Uses the same deep dependency check for all probes"]},{"id":139,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Requests vs limits: what happens when they are wrong?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Requests are what the scheduler reserves and what HPA percentages are based on; limits are enforced at runtime, where exceeding CPU means throttling and exceeding memory means OOMKill, so size both from measured usage.","answer":["**Requests** are what the scheduler reserves: a pod lands only on a node whose unreserved allocatable capacity covers its requests, regardless of actual usage. They also set the QoS class (Guaranteed, Burstable, BestEffort), which decides eviction order under node pressure, and CPU requests become the cgroup CPU weight used to share contended CPU. Requests set too low overpack nodes, causing noisy neighbors and evictions; set too high, they waste capacity and leave pods Pending. HPA utilization targets are percentages of requests too.","**Limits** are enforced by the kernel at runtime. CPU is compressible: a container that uses its quota within a CFS period (100 ms by default) is **throttled** until the next period, never killed. A JVM with many GC and JIT threads can burn a 1 CPU quota in 30 ms and then stall for 70 ms, which shows up as p99 latency spikes while average CPU looks fine. Memory is incompressible: exceeding the limit invokes the OOM killer and the container restarts with reason `OOMKilled` and exit code 137.","For Java, the memory limit must cover heap plus metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, direct buffers and GC structures, so size the heap with `-XX:MaxRAMPercentage` around 70 to 75 rather than heap equal to limit. A common production stance: memory request equal to limit, CPU request at measured p95 under realistic load, and a generous or no CPU limit for latency-sensitive services, as cluster policy allows. Derive numbers from metrics, load tests and VPA recommendations, then revisit after major changes."],"keyPoints":["Requests drive scheduling","CPU limit causes throttling, memory limit can cause OOMKill","tune from measurements and workload behavior"],"examples":[{"title":"Resources sized from measurements for a JVM service","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: credit-decision\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels: { app: credit-decision }\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels: { app: credit-decision }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: registry.example.com/credit-decision:2.4.1\n          env:\n            - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS\n              value: \"-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 -XX:ActiveProcessorCount=2\"\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"1\"          # measured p95 is about 0.7 cores under peak load\n              memory: 1Gi\n            limits:\n              memory: 1Gi       # equal to request: no memory overcommit\n              # no CPU limit: bursts are not throttled (if cluster policy allows)","note":"Heap is capped at 75% of the 1Gi limit, leaving room for native memory. `ActiveProcessorCount` stops the JVM from sizing GC and ForkJoin pools for every core on the node when no CPU limit is set."},{"title":"Spotting OOMKill and CPU throttling","lang":"bash","code":"# OOMKill: the kernel killed the container for exceeding its memory limit\nkubectl describe pod credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p | grep -A4 \"Last State\"\n#   Last State:  Terminated\n#     Reason:    OOMKilled\n#     Exit Code: 137\nkubectl get pod credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p \\\n  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'\n\n# Throttling: cgroup v2 counters from inside the container\nkubectl exec credit-decision-7d9f8c-x2k4p -- cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat\n#   nr_periods 120000\n#   nr_throttled 38000        (about 32% of periods throttled)\n#   throttled_usec 912000000\n\n# Same signal fleet-wide in Prometheus:\n#   sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\"}[5m]))\n#   / sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total{namespace=\"credit\"}[5m]))","note":"A throttled-period ratio above a few percent on a latency-sensitive service is worth acting on even if average CPU usage is far below the limit."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you identify CPU throttling or OOMKill?","a":"For OOMKill, `kubectl describe pod` shows `Last State: Terminated`, `Reason: OOMKilled`, `Exit Code: 137` and a rising restart count, and kube-state-metrics exposes `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason{reason=\"OOMKilled\"}` for alerting. Distinguish it from a JVM `OutOfMemoryError` (thrown inside the JVM, visible in logs, heap dump if `-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError` is set) and from node-pressure eviction (pod status `Evicted`). For throttling, compare `container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total` with `container_cpu_cfs_periods_total`, or read `nr_throttled` from `/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat`. A high throttled ratio combined with p99 latency spikes while average CPU sits below the limit is the classic signature, and average CPU graphs alone will never show it."}],"extra":["**QoS and eviction:** under node memory pressure the kubelet evicts BestEffort pods first, then Burstable pods using the most above their requests, and Guaranteed last; eviction deletes the pod (status `Evicted`), while an OOMKill restarts the container in place.","**JVM processor count:** modern JDKs no longer derive `availableProcessors()` from CPU requests (shares), so without a CPU limit the JVM sizes GC threads and the common `ForkJoinPool` for the whole node; pin it with `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount`.","**Where native memory goes:** `-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary` plus `jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary` breaks RSS into heap, metaspace, threads, code cache and GC, which explains OOMKills that happen with a healthy heap.","**LimitRange surprises:** a namespace LimitRange can silently inject default requests and limits into pods that declare none, for example a small CPU limit that throttles every JVM; check with `kubectl describe limitrange`.","**Startup bursts:** JIT compilation and class loading need far more CPU in the first minute than steady state, so tight CPU limits make startup slow and can trip startup or liveness probes."],"redFlags":["Sets request and limit arbitrarily","assumes CPU limit kills the pod"]},{"id":140,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"Rolling, blue-green and canary deployment trade-offs.","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Rolling updates replace pods gradually with little extra capacity, blue-green switches all traffic between two full environments for instant rollback at double cost, and canary exposes a small slice while metrics decide promotion.","answer":["**Rolling** (the Deployment default) replaces pods in batches bounded by `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`, so it needs almost no extra capacity and no extra tooling. The costs: old and new versions serve side by side for the whole rollout, rollback is just another rolling update, and readiness is the only gate, so a version that is ready but wrong (higher error rate, bad pricing logic) rolls out fully unless someone is watching.","**Blue-green** runs the new version at full capacity next to the old one, verifies it with smoke tests on a private route, then switches all traffic at once through a Service selector, load balancer target or DNS. Rollback is switching back, which takes seconds as long as blue is still running. The price is double capacity during the switch and exposing every user at the same moment.","**Canary** sends a small share of traffic (1%, 5%, 25%) to the new version and promotes step by step only if predefined criteria hold against the stable baseline: error rate, p99 latency, saturation and business signals such as payment authorization rate. It needs weighted traffic control (service mesh, ingress, Argo Rollouts or Flagger) and reliable metrics; one canary pod behind a Service with nobody evaluating it is just an unmonitored rolling update.","Rule of thumb: rolling for low-risk internal services, blue-green when you need an atomic cutover, canary for high-traffic, high-risk paths. In all three, two versions coexist at some point, so APIs, events and schemas must stay backward compatible."],"keyPoints":["Rolling is resource-efficient","blue-green gives fast switch/rollback with double capacity","canary limits exposure but needs metrics and traffic control"],"examples":[{"title":"Canary with automated analysis (Argo Rollouts)","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Rollout\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 10\n  workloadRef:                  # reuse the pod template of the existing Deployment\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: payments-api\n  strategy:\n    canary:\n      canaryService: payments-api-canary\n      stableService: payments-api-stable\n      trafficRouting:\n        nginx:\n          stableIngress: payments-api\n      analysis:                 # runs in the background; failure aborts and shifts traffic back\n        templates:\n          - templateName: payments-5xx-ratio\n        startingStep: 1\n      steps:\n        - setWeight: 5\n        - pause: { duration: 10m }\n        - setWeight: 25\n        - pause: { duration: 15m }\n---\napiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AnalysisTemplate\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-5xx-ratio\nspec:\n  metrics:\n    - name: error-ratio\n      interval: 1m\n      failureLimit: 2\n      successCondition: result[0] < 0.01\n      provider:\n        prometheus:\n          address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n          query: >\n            sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{service=\"payments-api-canary\",status=~\"5..\"}[2m]))\n            / sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{service=\"payments-api-canary\"}[2m]))","note":"The canary is judged by an explicit success condition (under 1% server errors) evaluated every minute; two failed measurements abort the rollout automatically. Real setups add latency and business metrics to the same template."},{"title":"Trade-offs at a glance","lang":"text","code":"Strategy     Extra capacity     Rollback             Blast radius           Needs\nRolling      maxSurge only      minutes (re-roll)    grows batch by batch   good readiness probes\nBlue-green   2x during switch   seconds (flip back)  100% at once           switchable routing, smoke tests\nCanary       small slice        seconds (weight 0)   1% to 5% at first      weighted routing, SLO metrics, analysis","note":"No strategy removes the need for backward compatible changes; they only change how many users see a bad version and how fast you can undo it."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do database changes remain backward compatible?","a":"Use expand and contract (parallel change): first ship an additive migration that the old code tolerates, such as a new nullable column, a new table, or an index built with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`. Then deploy code that writes both shapes and reads the new one, backfill in batches, and only after no old version runs and the rollback window has passed, drop or rename the old structure in a later release. Never rename or drop a column, or add a `NOT NULL` column without a default, in the same release as the code that needs it, because during rolling or canary rollouts old pods run against the new schema and a rollback would do the same. Run Flyway or Liquibase as a separate pre-deploy step rather than racing on every pod start, and keep each migration compatible with version N-1 of the code."}],"extra":["**Rolling knobs:** `maxUnavailable: 0` with `maxSurge: 1` keeps full capacity throughout; `progressDeadlineSeconds` marks a stuck rollout as failed but does not roll back by itself, so pair it with `kubectl rollout undo` or a progressive delivery controller.","**Deploy is not release:** feature flags let you ship code dark and enable it per tenant or percentage, so a behavioral rollback becomes a flag flip instead of a redeploy.","**Canary statistics:** at 1% traffic metrics are noisy; compare canary against a baseline over the same window, require a minimum request count, and prefer ratios over absolute counts.","**Consumers and workers:** Kafka consumers receive partitions, not traffic weights, so a canary consumer gets a partition share of messages; blue-green for consumers must ensure only one color consumes, typically by scaling the idle color to zero.","**Traffic mirroring:** a mesh can shadow production requests to the new version and discard its responses, which is great for read paths but dangerous for payments unless side effects are disabled."],"redFlags":["Calls canary 'deploy one pod' without evaluation criteria"]},{"id":141,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"How should a Java service perform graceful shutdown in Kubernetes?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"On SIGTERM, fail readiness, keep serving while endpoint removal propagates, stop accepting new work, finish or cancel in-flight requests and messages within the grace period, then close consumers, executors and pools before exiting.","answer":["On pod deletion two things start in parallel: the control plane removes the pod from EndpointSlices, and the kubelet runs the `preStop` hook and then sends SIGTERM to PID 1. Kube-proxy, ingress controllers and meshes learn about the endpoint removal asynchronously, a few seconds later, so an app that exits instantly on SIGTERM still receives requests and clients see resets and 502s. When `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30, counted from the start and including preStop) expires, the kubelet sends SIGKILL, which is the failure mode, not the plan.","The sequence a Java service should follow: fail readiness (Kubernetes marks terminating endpoints not ready, and publishing `ReadinessState.REFUSING_TRAFFIC` also covers external load balancers), keep serving during a short preStop delay, then stop accepting new work. SIGTERM runs JVM shutdown hooks and Spring Boot closes the context: with `server.shutdown=graceful` (the default since Boot 3.4) the web server stops accepting connections and waits for in-flight requests up to `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase`.","Background work matters as much: stop Kafka listener containers so no new records are polled, finish or abandon the current batch (unprocessed records are redelivered, so handlers must be idempotent), commit offsets, shut down executors with `shutdown()` plus `awaitTermination()` and `shutdownNow()` on timeout, then close pools and flush telemetry. Budget it so preStop delay plus drain fits inside the grace period, and use an exec-form `ENTRYPOINT` so the JVM, not a shell, receives SIGTERM."],"keyPoints":["Fail readiness, stop new traffic, honor termination grace period, finish/cancel in-flight work, close consumers/executors and handle SIGTERM"],"examples":[{"title":"Pod spec and Spring config sized together","lang":"yaml","code":"# Deployment pod template excerpt\nspec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45       # preStop 10s + app drain 25s + margin\n  containers:\n    - name: orders-api\n      image: registry.example.com/orders-api:3.2.0\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"sleep\", \"10\"]      # let endpoint removal propagate before SIGTERM\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n---\n# application.yaml of orders-api\nserver:\n  shutdown: graceful                      # explicit, although it is the default since Boot 3.4\nspring:\n  lifecycle:\n    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 25s","note":"The grace period is derived from its parts rather than guessed. An `exec` sleep needs a `sleep` binary in the image; distroless images can use the native `preStop.sleep` action available in newer Kubernetes versions."},{"title":"Draining a Kafka consumer and a worker pool on shutdown","lang":"java","code":"@Component\nclass SettlementWorker implements SmartLifecycle {\n\n    private final ExecutorService workers = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);\n    private final KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry;\n    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;\n    private volatile boolean running;\n\n    SettlementWorker(KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry registry, ApplicationEventPublisher events) {\n        this.registry = registry;\n        this.events = events;\n    }\n\n    @Override public void start() { running = true; }\n    @Override public boolean isRunning() { return running; }\n\n    // Default phase (Integer.MAX_VALUE) stops before the web server and listener containers\n    @Override\n    public void stop() {\n        AvailabilityChangeEvent.publish(events, this, ReadinessState.REFUSING_TRAFFIC);\n        registry.getListenerContainer(\"settlements\").stop();  // no new polls, offsets committed\n        workers.shutdown();                                   // reject new jobs, finish queued ones\n        try {\n            if (!workers.awaitTermination(20, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n                workers.shutdownNow();                        // interrupt; redelivery covers the rest\n            }\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            workers.shutdownNow();\n            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n        } finally {\n            running = false;\n        }\n    }\n}","note":"Spring calls `stop()` when the context closes after SIGTERM, before beans such as the DataSource are destroyed. The order is: refuse traffic, stop intake, drain with a deadline, cancel the rest and rely on idempotent redelivery."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What is the role of preStop and terminationGracePeriodSeconds?","a":"`preStop` is a hook (exec, HTTP, or the native sleep action in newer versions) that the kubelet runs to completion before sending SIGTERM. For Java services its main job is a short delay so kube-proxy, ingress controllers and meshes stop routing to the pod before the app starts shutting down; it can also trigger an application-level drain. `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30) is the total budget from the moment termination starts: the preStop hook and the post-SIGTERM shutdown both count against it, and when it runs out the kubelet sends SIGKILL (with only a small one-off extension if preStop is still running). Size it as preStop delay plus `timeout-per-shutdown-phase` plus consumer and executor drain time plus a margin."}],"extra":["**PID 1 and signals:** shell-form `ENTRYPOINT java -jar app.jar` runs under `/bin/sh -c`, which does not forward SIGTERM, so the JVM only ever sees SIGKILL; use exec form, `exec java` in scripts, or a tiny init such as tini.","**Kafka rebalances on deploy:** every stopping consumer triggers a rebalance; the cooperative sticky assignor and static membership (`group.instance.id`) reduce stop-the-world rebalances during rolling updates.","**Long jobs:** a grace period of minutes cannot protect hour-long work, so design jobs to checkpoint, hold leases and resume idempotently after interruption instead of stretching `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`.","**Node drains use the same path:** evictions during cluster upgrades trigger the same termination sequence, and a PodDisruptionBudget limits how many replicas drain at once.","**Test it:** run `kubectl rollout restart` under load and watch ingress 5xx, client connection resets and consumer lag; any spike means the shutdown sequence has a gap."],"redFlags":["Relies on SIGKILL","continues consuming new messages during shutdown"]},{"id":142,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"How should ConfigMaps and Secrets be used?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Keep images environment-agnostic and inject non-sensitive settings from ConfigMaps and credentials from Secrets, remembering that Secrets are only base64 encoded, so real protection comes from etcd encryption, tight RBAC and an external secret manager.","answer":["Build one immutable image and promote it unchanged through environments; everything environment-specific is injected at deploy time. Baking config into images forces a rebuild per environment (so production runs a binary you never tested) and pushes secrets into the registry. **ConfigMaps** hold non-sensitive settings: URLs, feature flags, pool sizes, log levels. **Secrets** hold credentials, API keys, TLS keys and keystores.","Secrets are **not inherently secure**. Values are only base64 encoded, stored unencrypted in etcd unless encryption at rest is configured, and readable by anyone with `get` on secrets or permission to create a pod in the namespace. Minimum hardening: etcd encryption with a KMS provider, least-privilege RBAC (no `list` or `watch` on secrets for humans or CI), a dedicated service account per workload, and audit logging. Better still, keep the source of truth in Vault or a cloud secret manager and sync it with External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver, which adds rotation and audit. Never commit plain Secret manifests; use Sealed Secrets or SOPS in GitOps repositories.","In the application, never log injected values or dump the environment: Spring Boot 3 masks values on `/actuator/env` by default, and `management.endpoint.env.show-values` should stay `NEVER`. Spring reads environment variables through relaxed binding and mounted secret files through `spring.config.import=configtree:`. Make ConfigMaps and Secrets immutable with a content hash in the name, so a config change triggers a controlled rollout instead of silent drift."],"keyPoints":["ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive config","Secrets require encryption/access controls and are not inherently secure","avoid baking environment config into images"],"examples":[{"title":"ConfigMap for settings, Secret mounted as files","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-config\ndata:\n  SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: prod\n  PAYMENTS_PSP_BASE_URL: https://psp.example.com\n  SPRING_DATASOURCE_HIKARI_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE: \"20\"\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-db\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:          # in real clusters created by External Secrets, never committed to Git\n  username: payments_app\n  password: change-me\n---\n# Pod template excerpt from the Deployment\nspec:\n  serviceAccountName: payments-api\n  containers:\n    - name: app\n      image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.8.3\n      envFrom:\n        - configMapRef: { name: payments-config }\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: db-creds\n          mountPath: /etc/secrets/db\n          readOnly: true\n  volumes:\n    - name: db-creds\n      secret:\n        secretName: payments-db","note":"Plain settings arrive as environment variables that Spring binds automatically; credentials arrive as files on a tmpfs volume, one file per key."},{"title":"Reading mounted secrets in Spring Boot without exposing them","lang":"properties","code":"# /etc/secrets/db/username becomes the property db.username\nspring.config.import=optional:configtree:/etc/secrets/\nspring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://payments-db:5432/payments\nspring.datasource.username=${db.username}\nspring.datasource.password=${db.password}\n# Never return raw values from actuator\nmanagement.endpoint.env.show-values=NEVER\nmanagement.endpoint.configprops.show-values=NEVER","note":"The `configtree` import maps directory and file names to property names, so the same image works with any secret backend that can produce files."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Environment variable vs mounted file trade-offs?","a":"Environment variables are simple and bind natively through Spring relaxed binding, but they are fixed at process start, readable through `/proc/<pid>/environ`, inherited by child processes, and they leak easily through crash reports, debug endpoints and careless startup logging. Mounted files (tmpfs-backed for Secrets) can be permission-restricted, are refreshed by the kubelet when the Secret changes (unless mounted with `subPath`), and suit multi-line material such as TLS keys and keystores. The trade-off is that the app must read them and, if you want rotation without restart, watch and reload them, for example for certificates. A good default: env vars via `envFrom` for plain config, mounted files via `configtree` for secrets."}],"extra":["**Update propagation:** environment variables are fixed at container start; mounted volumes are refreshed by the kubelet after a delay, except `subPath` mounts, which never update. Spring reloads neither automatically, so a hash-triggered rollout (Kustomize `configMapGenerator`, Helm checksum annotation) is the reliable pattern.","**Size limit:** ConfigMaps and Secrets are capped at 1 MiB because they live in etcd; large reference data belongs elsewhere.","**Pod creation is a read path:** anyone who can create pods or Deployments in a namespace can mount any Secret there, so RBAC on workloads matters as much as RBAC on secrets.","**Workload identity over static keys:** IRSA on EKS, Workload Identity on GKE or Azure Workload Identity give pods short-lived cloud credentials, removing long-lived access keys from Secrets entirely.","**Dynamic database credentials:** Vault's database secrets engine can issue per-application credentials with a TTL; the app must renew the lease or rebuild its connection pool before expiry, so test that path before relying on it."],"redFlags":["Stores secrets in ConfigMaps or logs mounted values"]},{"id":143,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"How does HPA decide to scale, and what can make it ineffective?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"HPA computes desired replicas as current replicas times current metric over target, with CPU and memory measured against requests, so wrong requests, slow startup, missing metrics or downstream bottlenecks make it ineffective.","answer":["The HPA controller runs a control loop (every 15 seconds by default), reads the metrics APIs (metrics-server for CPU and memory, a Prometheus adapter or KEDA for custom and external metrics) and computes `desired = ceil(currentReplicas × currentValue / targetValue)`. Resource targets are **relative to requests**: 70% CPU means 70% of the requested CPU averaged across pods, not of the node. With several metrics it takes the highest result, it ignores changes within a 10% tolerance, and scale-down is damped by a stabilization window configurable in `behavior`.","Things that make it ineffective: **wrong requests** (far below real usage pins it at max, far above means it never triggers); **slow startup** (a JVM that needs a minute plus warmup arrives after the spike, and JIT CPU can cause extra scaling); **missing metrics** (a broken adapter or pods without metrics mean no or conservative decisions); the **wrong signal** (CPU for an I/O-bound service waiting on a database); and `maxReplicas` or cluster capacity, where pending pods wait minutes for the cluster autoscaler.","Crucially, HPA only adds replicas of this service. If the bottleneck is the database or a rate-limited provider, more pods mean more connections and concurrent queries, which deepens the saturation; Hikari pool size times `maxReplicas` must fit the database's `max_connections`. Scale on a signal that reflects real work (requests per pod, queue lag), keep `minReplicas` with headroom for sudden spikes, and combine autoscaling with rate limiting and load shedding, because HPA reacts in minutes, not seconds."],"keyPoints":["Scales from resource/custom metrics relative to requests","slow startup, wrong requests, downstream bottlenecks and missing metrics limit value"],"examples":[{"title":"HPA on CPU and requests per second with tuned behavior","lang":"yaml","code":"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-api\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: orders-api\n  minReplicas: 4\n  maxReplicas: 20              # bounded by the DB: 20 pods x 10 Hikari connections = 200\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 65     # percent of the CPU request, not of the node\n    - type: Pods\n      pods:\n        metric:\n          name: http_server_requests_per_second   # served by a Prometheus adapter rule\n        target:\n          type: AverageValue\n          averageValue: \"150\"\n  behavior:\n    scaleUp:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0\n      policies:\n        - { type: Percent, value: 100, periodSeconds: 60 }\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n      policies:\n        - { type: Pods, value: 2, periodSeconds: 60 }","note":"Scale-up is aggressive (double per minute) while scale-down is slow and gradual, which avoids flapping. `maxReplicas` is a capacity decision about the database, not just about the cluster."},{"title":"How a wrong request breaks the calculation","lang":"text","code":"4 replicas, CPU request 500m, target 65%\nObserved average usage 455m per pod = 91% of request\ndesired = ceil(4 x 91 / 65) = ceil(5.6) = 6 replicas      (scales as intended)\n\nSame load, request mistakenly set to 2000m\n455m = 23% of request\ndesired = ceil(4 x 23 / 65) = ceil(1.4) = 2  -> clamped to minReplicas 4\nLatency is climbing, yet HPA sees idle pods and never scales.","note":"Because utilization is measured against requests, the request value is effectively part of the autoscaling policy."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What metric would you use for a queue consumer?","a":"Scale on backlog relative to throughput, not on CPU: Kafka consumer group lag (or SQS or RabbitMQ queue depth) per replica, ideally translated into time to drain, meaning lag divided by per-pod processing rate, compared with your latency objective. KEDA's Kafka scaler or an external metric through a Prometheus adapter both work, and `maxReplicas` should not exceed the partition count because extra consumers in a group sit idle. Also watch the age of the oldest unprocessed message, and remember that a downstream limit (database, PSP rate limit) caps useful replicas however large the lag. CPU is misleading here because a consumer blocked on I/O shows low CPU while lag explodes."}],"extra":["**Reaction time adds up:** metric scrape interval, HPA sync, scheduling, image pull, JVM start and readiness commonly total one to three minutes, so pre-scale for known peaks (a KEDA cron scaler or a scheduled `minReplicas` bump).","**Startup CPU is discounted:** the controller treats CPU samples from pods that just started or are not yet ready conservatively, which only works if readiness is accurate; AppCDS, CRaC or GraalVM native images shrink the startup gap itself.","**Memory is a poor signal for Java:** the heap rarely shrinks after load, so memory-based scaling goes up and never comes back down.","**HPA and VPA:** do not let both act on the same resource metric for one workload; use VPA in recommendation mode to size requests and HPA for the replica count.","**Scale to zero:** a plain HPA keeps at least one replica by default; KEDA can scale event-driven workloads to zero and manages an HPA underneath once there is work."],"redFlags":["Assumes HPA solves DB saturation or scales instantly"]},{"id":144,"category":"Docker, Kubernetes & CI/CD","question":"What quality gates belong in a CI/CD pipeline?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Use layered, risk-based gates: fast compile, unit, static and secret checks on every PR, then integration and contract tests, a scanned and signed immutable artifact promoted unchanged, and post-deploy verification with automated rollback.","answer":["Order gates by cost and signal so feedback stays fast. On every pull request, within minutes: compile, unit tests, static analysis on new code (Error Prone, SpotBugs, Sonar), formatting, dependency vulnerability and license checks, and **secret scanning**, ideally also as push protection so secrets never reach the remote. Then integration tests with Testcontainers and **contract verification**, which catch boundary bugs unit tests cannot. Failing tests fail the build; a flaky test is quarantined with an owner, never retried until green.","Build the artifact **once**: an immutable version (semver plus git SHA), an image scanned for vulnerabilities, an SBOM, and a signature with provenance. Promote the same digest from staging to production. Deploying a mutable `latest` tag breaks rollback, audit and reproducibility, and different nodes can end up running different code.","Deployment gates protect the environment: migration safety (backward compatible, tested against a production-like schema), an admission policy that accepts only signed digests, smoke tests after deploy, canary analysis on SLO metrics, and automated rollback when error rate or latency breaches. Manual approval and change windows belong on high-risk services such as payments, not on every internal tool.","The design principle is **risk-based**, not one huge gate. A 60-minute pipeline teaches people to batch changes and bypass controls. Parallelize and cache, keep the merge path to roughly 10 to 15 minutes, and move expensive suites (full E2E, performance, DAST) to nightly or release-candidate stages."],"keyPoints":["Fast unit/static/security checks, integration/contract tests, artifact scanning, immutable versioning, deploy verification and rollback controls","risk-based, not one huge slow gate"],"examples":[{"title":"Gate map for a payments service","lang":"text","code":"Stage            Gate                                          Blocks\nPR (< 10 min)    compile, unit tests, static analysis          merge\n                 formatting, secret scan, dependency policy    merge\nPR / main        Testcontainers ITs, contract verification     merge\n                 migration check on prod-like schema           merge\nBuild once       image scan, SBOM, signature, provenance       publish and promotion\nDeploy           admission policy: signed digest only          deployment\n                 smoke tests, canary SLO analysis              promotion, triggers auto rollback\nNightly / RC     full E2E, load test, DAST                     release candidate, not every merge","note":"Each gate sits at the cheapest point where it can catch its class of problem, and only the fast deterministic ones block merges."},{"title":"Build once, scan, sign, deploy by digest","lang":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nVERSION=\"1.12.${BUILD_NUMBER}-$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\"\nIMAGE=\"registry.example.com/payments/orders-service:${VERSION}\"\n\ndocker build -t \"$IMAGE\" .\n# Gate: fail on fixable critical or high CVEs before anything is pushed\ntrivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignore-unfixed \"$IMAGE\"\ndocker push \"$IMAGE\"\n\n# Resolve the immutable digest; everything downstream uses it, never a tag\nDIGEST=$(docker inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' \"$IMAGE\")\nsyft \"$DIGEST\" -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json\ncosign sign --yes \"$DIGEST\"\ncosign attest --yes --type spdxjson --predicate sbom.spdx.json \"$DIGEST\"\n\n# Promote the same digest; the cluster admission policy verifies the signature\nkubectl -n payments set image deployment/orders-service app=\"$DIGEST\"\nkubectl -n payments rollout status deployment/orders-service --timeout=5m \\\n  || { kubectl -n payments rollout undo deployment/orders-service; exit 1; }","note":"The tag is only a human-readable label; the digest is what gets signed, attested and deployed, so rollback and audit always refer to exact bytes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Which gates block merge vs deployment?","a":"Merge-blocking gates judge the change itself, so they must be fast and deterministic: compilation, unit and integration tests, contract verification against the consumers' published contracts, static analysis on new code, secret scanning, and dependency or license policy violations. Deployment-blocking gates judge the artifact and the target environment: image vulnerability policy (rescanned at promotion because new CVEs appear after merge), signature and provenance checks at admission, migration safety, smoke tests, canary analysis, and change freezes or approvals for high-risk services. Slow or environment-dependent suites such as full E2E and load tests usually gate release candidates rather than every merge, so they stop a bad release without blocking every developer."}],"extra":["**Pin CI dependencies:** third-party CI actions and plugins run with your deploy credentials, so pin them by commit SHA and use OIDC federation to the cloud instead of long-lived deploy keys.","**Coverage on changed lines:** a global coverage threshold rewards junk tests; gate on coverage of new code and use mutation testing (PIT) on critical modules to check that tests actually assert.","**Rescan at promotion:** new CVEs are published after an image was built, so rescan images in the registry continuously and at promotion time, not only at build.","**Migrations as a separate step:** run Flyway or Liquibase as a pre-deploy job with its own gate, so a failed migration stops the rollout before any new pod starts.","**Measure the pipeline:** track the DORA metrics (lead time, deployment frequency, change failure rate, time to restore) plus pipeline duration and flake rate to judge whether gates add value or only delay."],"redFlags":["Deploys mutable latest tag","ignores failed tests or secrets scanning"]},{"id":145,"category":"Testing","question":"Unit vs integration vs end-to-end tests: what should each cover?","difficulty":"Easy","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Unit tests check isolated logic in milliseconds, integration tests verify real boundaries such as the database, messaging and HTTP adapters, and a few end-to-end tests protect critical journeys; the mix trades confidence against feedback speed.","answer":["**Unit tests** exercise domain logic in isolation: no Spring context, no database, no network. Fee calculation, state transitions, validation and credit rules run in milliseconds, cover edge cases exhaustively with parameterized tests, and pinpoint the failing line. A `@SpringBootTest` is not a unit test, and neither is anything that needs a container.","**Integration tests** verify your code against real infrastructure and framework wiring at a boundary: repositories and SQL against real PostgreSQL via Testcontainers, Flyway migrations, transactions and locking, Kafka serialization, Jackson mapping, HTTP clients against WireMock, security configuration. Slices such as `@DataJpaTest`, `@WebMvcTest` and `@JsonTest` keep them focused and cheaper than a full context. They catch what mocks hide: wrong SQL, constraint violations, lazy loading and transaction boundary bugs.","**End-to-end tests** drive the deployed system through real entry points across services to validate a critical journey such as checkout or refund. They give the most realistic confidence but are slow, need environments and data, fail for reasons unrelated to the change and are hard to debug, so keep a handful for revenue-critical journeys and run them after deployment or as synthetic monitoring.","The balance is confidence per minute of feedback: a wide base of fast unit tests for logic, integration tests at every boundary you own, contract tests between services, and very few E2E tests. Relying mainly on E2E produces slow, flaky pipelines that people learn to ignore."],"keyPoints":["Unit isolates logic and is fast","integration validates boundaries/infrastructure","E2E validates critical journeys but is slow/brittle","balance confidence and feedback speed"],"examples":[{"title":"Unit test vs integration test for the same payment feature","lang":"java","code":"// Unit: pure domain logic, no Spring, runs in milliseconds\nclass RefundPolicyTest {\n\n    private final RefundPolicy policy = new RefundPolicy(Duration.ofDays(30));\n\n    @ParameterizedTest\n    @CsvSource({\"29, true\", \"30, true\", \"31, false\"})\n    void refundAllowedWithinWindow(int daysAgo, boolean expected) {\n        Instant now = Instant.parse(\"2026-03-01T10:00:00Z\");\n        Payment payment = Payment.captured(new BigDecimal(\"120.00\"), now.minus(Duration.ofDays(daysAgo)));\n        assertThat(policy.canRefund(payment, now)).isEqualTo(expected);\n    }\n}\n\n// Integration: real PostgreSQL, real schema from Flyway, real constraints\n@DataJpaTest\n@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)\n@Testcontainers\nclass PaymentRepositoryIT {\n\n    @Container\n    @ServiceConnection\n    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:16-alpine\");\n\n    @Autowired PaymentRepository payments;\n\n    @Test\n    void rejectsDuplicateIdempotencyKey() {\n        payments.saveAndFlush(PaymentEntity.pending(\"idem-123\", new BigDecimal(\"50.00\")));\n        assertThatThrownBy(() -> payments.saveAndFlush(PaymentEntity.pending(\"idem-123\", new BigDecimal(\"50.00\"))))\n            .isInstanceOf(DataIntegrityViolationException.class);\n    }\n}","note":"The unit test covers boundary days exhaustively in microseconds; the integration test proves something only the real database can: that the unique constraint on the idempotency key exists and is translated into Spring's exception hierarchy."}],"followUps":[{"q":"What would you test for a payment use case at each layer?","a":"Unit: amount and currency rules with `BigDecimal` rounding, fee calculation, the payment state machine (AUTHORIZED to CAPTURED, no capture after VOID), idempotency decisions, and classification of PSP error codes into retryable and final, all with a fixed `Clock`. Integration: the repository against PostgreSQL (unique idempotency key, optimistic locking on concurrent capture), Flyway migrations, the PSP client against WireMock (timeouts, 5xx, malformed bodies), the outbox publishing to Kafka, and `@WebMvcTest` for request validation and error mapping. E2E: one or two journeys in staging against the PSP sandbox, such as authorize, capture and refund, verifying the ledger entries and the customer notification. Contract tests cover the order service to payment service API in between, so E2E does not have to."}],"extra":["**Context caching:** Spring caches application contexts across test classes with identical configuration; each unique combination of properties, profiles or `@MockitoBean` (which replaces `@MockBean`, deprecated since Boot 3.4) creates a new context, so standardize test base classes.","**Component tests:** one service running in-process with real infrastructure containers and stubbed neighbors, tested through its HTTP or messaging API, is often the best value layer for microservices.","**Unit means behavior, not class:** sociable unit tests that use real collaborators inside the same module are less brittle than one test class per production class with everything else mocked.","**Split the build:** Maven Surefire runs `*Test` classes and Failsafe runs `*IT` classes in the `integration-test` phase, so CI can report fast unit feedback first.","**Assert outcomes:** integration tests should assert on observable state (rows, published events, HTTP responses), not on which internal methods were called."],"redFlags":["Calls every Spring context test a unit test","relies mainly on E2E"]},{"id":146,"category":"Testing","question":"What is the test pyramid, and when can its shape differ?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":false,"tldr":"The pyramid recommends many fast focused tests, fewer integration tests and very few end-to-end tests, but the real goal is reliable fast feedback, so architecture and risk can justify a different shape.","answer":["The test pyramid (popularized by Mike Cohn) recommends a broad base of fast, focused unit tests, fewer integration or service tests, and very few end-to-end tests. The reasoning: going up the pyramid, tests get slower, costlier to maintain, flakier and less precise. A failing unit test points to a line of code; a failing E2E test points to \"something in the system\".","The shape is a heuristic, not a law, and the percentages are not universal. The distribution should follow where risk and defects actually live. A thin CRUD or orchestration service has most of its bugs in SQL, mappings, serialization and configuration, so a \"honeycomb\" with more component and integration tests gives more confidence per minute than hundreds of unit tests on trivial code. A rule-heavy credit decision engine justifies a very wide unit base, including property-based tests. Microservice architectures shift weight to component and contract tests instead of cross-service E2E.","The goal is **reliable, fast feedback**: a PR suite that runs in minutes, fails only for real problems, and catches the defects that matter. Watch for the ice cream cone (mostly E2E or manual testing) and the hourglass (unit plus E2E with no integration layer). Reshape using data: suite duration, flake rate, and which layer would have caught each escaped production defect."],"keyPoints":["Many fast focused tests, fewer integration and few E2E","architecture and risk may justify more component/contract tests","goal is reliable fast feedback"],"examples":[{"title":"Two services, two justified shapes","lang":"text","code":"payments-gateway: thin orchestration, heavy integration risk\nLayer              Count    Time      Catches\nUnit               ~300     8 s       fee rules, state machine, PSP error classification\nComponent          ~120     90 s      SQL, migrations, Kafka outbox, PSP client via WireMock\nContract           ~40      20 s      breaking changes with order-service and ledger\nE2E (staging)      6        6 min     authorize, capture, refund journeys with PSP sandbox\n\ncredit-decision-engine: rule heavy, few boundaries\nLayer              Count    Time      Catches\nUnit + property    ~4000    25 s      rule combinations, rounding, score boundaries\nIntegration        ~30      40 s      rule repository, cache, API mapping\nE2E                2        3 min     application to decision journey","note":"Neither shape follows a fixed ratio; each puts most of its tests where that service's defects actually come from, while keeping PR feedback in minutes."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would microservices change the pyramid?","a":"Cross-service E2E tests become far more expensive and flaky, because they need many services at compatible versions, shared test data and a stable environment. So the middle grows: each service gets component tests that run it in isolation with real infrastructure containers and stubbed neighbors, plus consumer-driven contract tests for every API and event schema, which preserve independent deployability. E2E shrinks to a few critical journeys, often run post-deploy as smoke or synthetic checks. Resilience testing (timeouts, retries, fault injection with Toxiproxy or a mesh) also becomes its own concern, because the network is now inside every use case."}],"extra":["**Other shapes:** the testing trophy (static analysis at the base, integration tests largest) comes from the frontend world, and Spotify's honeycomb puts integrated service tests at the center for microservices.","**Static analysis is a layer:** the type system, Error Prone and nullness annotations (JSpecify) eliminate whole bug classes at zero runtime cost and belong at the bottom of any shape.","**Mutation testing:** PIT reveals whether a wide unit base actually asserts anything; high line coverage with weak assertions is a pyramid in name only.","**Production as a test layer:** synthetic monitoring, canary analysis and alerting on SLOs catch what no pre-production environment can, and can replace some staging E2E tests.","**Unit of behavior:** sociable unit tests that use real in-module collaborators blur the unit and integration boundary in a healthy way and survive refactoring better."],"redFlags":["Treats percentages as universal law"]},{"id":147,"category":"Testing","question":"Why use Testcontainers?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Testcontainers starts real, disposable dependencies such as PostgreSQL, Kafka or Redis in Docker from test code, so integration tests exercise production-like behavior while staying isolated, reproducible and runnable anywhere Docker is available.","answer":["Mocks and in-memory substitutes lie exactly at the boundary where many bugs live. H2 in PostgreSQL mode differs in SQL dialect, `jsonb`, `ON CONFLICT`, partial indexes, locking, isolation and error codes, so tests pass while production fails. **Testcontainers** starts the real engine (the same major version as production, such as `postgres:16`) in Docker from the test lifecycle, and your real Flyway migrations run against it.","It keeps tests **isolated and reproducible**: every run gets a fresh container on a random port, so there is no shared, dirty dev database, no dependence on another team's data, and the same test behaves identically on a laptop and in CI. The Ryuk sidecar removes containers even if the JVM crashes. Modules exist for PostgreSQL, Kafka, Redis, LocalStack, Keycloak and more, and since Spring Boot 3.1 `@ServiceConnection` wires connection details automatically, without `@DynamicPropertySource` boilerplate.","Trade-offs: CI needs a Docker-compatible runtime, containers add seconds of startup, and images should be cached. Use it for boundary tests (repositories, messaging, caches, HTTP clients), not for pure logic that unit tests cover faster. The production payoff is catching migration errors, constraint violations, and transaction or locking bugs before deploy rather than on the first production run."],"keyPoints":["Runs real disposable dependencies with production-like behavior, improving integration confidence while keeping tests isolated and reproducible"],"examples":[{"title":"Shared PostgreSQL container with @ServiceConnection","lang":"java","code":"@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\nclass ContainersConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    @ServiceConnection   // Boot builds the DataSource from the container's host, port and credentials\n    PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres() {\n        return new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:16-alpine\");\n    }\n}\n\n@SpringBootTest\n@Import(ContainersConfig.class)   // same config in every IT: one cached context, one container\nclass AccountLedgerIT {\n\n    @Autowired LedgerRepository ledger;\n    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    void cleanUp() {\n        jdbc.execute(\"TRUNCATE account_balance\");\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void upsertKeepsLatestBalance() {\n        // upsertBalance uses INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (account_id) DO UPDATE, PostgreSQL semantics\n        ledger.upsertBalance(\"acc-42\", new BigDecimal(\"100.00\"));\n        ledger.upsertBalance(\"acc-42\", new BigDecimal(\"75.50\"));\n\n        BigDecimal balance = jdbc.queryForObject(\n            \"SELECT balance FROM account_balance WHERE account_id = ?\", BigDecimal.class, \"acc-42\");\n        assertThat(balance).isEqualByComparingTo(\"75.50\");\n    }\n}","note":"The container is a Spring bean, so its lifecycle follows the cached application context and every test class importing the same configuration reuses it. Tests are isolated by data cleanup, not by new containers."},{"title":"Bad vs good test configuration","lang":"properties","code":"# BAD: src/test/resources/application-test.properties\n# spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;MODE=PostgreSQL\n# spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop\n# Schema generated from entities, Flyway skipped, PostgreSQL behavior never exercised\n\n# GOOD: no datasource URL at all, @ServiceConnection supplies it\nspring.flyway.enabled=true\nspring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate","note":"With `validate`, Hibernate checks the entity mapping against the schema Flyway built, so a missing migration fails the test instead of production startup."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How do you keep container tests fast?","a":"Start each container once per JVM and share it, either through a `@TestConfiguration` bean combined with Spring context caching or a static singleton, instead of starting one per test class; isolate tests by data (truncate tables, unique IDs per test) rather than by fresh containers. Keep Spring test configuration identical across IT classes so the context cache hits, and use slices such as `@DataJpaTest` where a full context is not needed. Start independent containers in parallel with `Startables.deepStart(postgres, kafka).join()`, use small images that are pre-pulled or cached on CI runners, and tune the database for tests, for example `withCommand(\"postgres\", \"-c\", \"fsync=off\")`. Locally, `withReuse(true)` plus `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` keeps containers alive between runs, but do not rely on it in CI."}],"extra":["**Dev-time containers:** since Boot 3.1 a test main method such as `SpringApplication.from(OrdersApplication::main).with(ContainersConfig.class).run(args)` runs the app locally against the same containers, replacing hand-maintained docker-compose files for development.","**Wait strategies:** modules ship sensible readiness checks, but a `GenericContainer` needs an explicit `waitingFor(Wait.forHttp(\"/health\"))` or log-based wait; testing before the dependency is ready is a classic flakiness source.","**Pin image tags:** use the production major and minor version, never `latest`, or an upstream release silently changes behavior under your tests.","**Failure injection:** the Toxiproxy module adds latency, bandwidth limits and dropped connections between the app and a real container, which is the honest way to test timeouts and retries.","**CI runtime options:** Testcontainers needs a Docker API (socket mount, Docker-in-Docker or a remote engine such as Testcontainers Cloud); locked-down runners that disable Ryuk must clean up containers another way."],"redFlags":["Mocks SQL behavior or shares one dirty external database"]},{"id":148,"category":"Testing","question":"What problem does contract testing solve?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Contract testing verifies that a consumer's expectations of an API or message match what the provider delivers, each side checked independently in its own build, provided contracts are owned, versioned and verified.","answer":["In distributed systems a frequent integration failure is a provider changing an API or event in a way a consumer did not expect: a renamed field, a changed type, a new required field, a different status code. Unit tests cannot catch it because each side mocks its own idea of the other, and full E2E environments catch it late, slowly and flakily. **Contract testing** turns the expectations into an executable artifact: the consumer tests against a stub generated from the contract, and the provider verifies the same contract against its real implementation, each in its own CI build.","With Pact, the consumer test records interactions (given account acc-42 exists, `GET /accounts/acc-42` returns 200 with `id` and `status`) into a pact file published to a broker with the consumer version and branch. Provider CI fetches the relevant pacts, sets up data through provider states and replays them. Before either side deploys, `can-i-deploy` checks that the two versions were verified together. Spring Cloud Contract is the Spring-native alternative that generates provider tests and WireMock stubs from contracts, and message contracts cover Kafka events.","Contracts need **ownership** (who fixes a broken verification), **versioning** tied to what is deployed where, and **provider verification** as a merge gate, otherwise they rot into stale documentation. They check the shape and semantics consumers rely on, not business correctness, performance or each side's own infrastructure, so they complement integration tests of adapters and a few E2E journeys rather than replacing them."],"keyPoints":["Validates consumer-provider API/message expectations without full E2E environments","contracts need ownership, versioning and provider verification"],"examples":[{"title":"Consumer side: Pact test in the payment service","lang":"java","code":"@ExtendWith(PactConsumerTestExt.class)\n@PactTestFor(providerName = \"account-service\")\nclass AccountClientPactTest {\n\n    @Pact(consumer = \"payment-service\")\n    V4Pact activeAccount(PactDslWithProvider builder) {\n        return builder\n            .given(\"account acc-42 exists and is active\")\n            .uponReceiving(\"a request for account acc-42\")\n                .path(\"/accounts/acc-42\")\n                .method(\"GET\")\n            .willRespondWith()\n                .status(200)\n                .headers(Map.of(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\"))\n                .body(new PactDslJsonBody()\n                    .stringValue(\"id\", \"acc-42\")\n                    .stringMatcher(\"status\", \"ACTIVE|BLOCKED\", \"ACTIVE\")\n                    .stringType(\"currency\", \"SEK\"))\n            .toPact(V4Pact.class);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    @PactTestFor(pactMethod = \"activeAccount\")\n    void readsActiveAccount(MockServer mockServer) {\n        var client = new AccountClient(RestClient.create(mockServer.getUrl()));\n        Account account = client.find(\"acc-42\");\n        assertThat(account.status()).isEqualTo(AccountStatus.ACTIVE);\n    }\n}","note":"The consumer only pins the fields it actually uses and uses type or regex matchers instead of exact values, so the provider stays free to add fields or change unrelated data."},{"title":"Provider side: verifying all consumer pacts from the broker","lang":"java","code":"@Provider(\"account-service\")\n@PactBroker   // broker URL and consumer version selectors come from system properties\n@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)\nclass AccountProviderPactTest {\n\n    @LocalServerPort int port;\n    @Autowired AccountRepository accounts;\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    void target(PactVerificationContext context) {\n        context.setTarget(new HttpTestTarget(\"localhost\", port));\n    }\n\n    @TestTemplate\n    @ExtendWith(PactVerificationInvocationContextProvider.class)\n    void verifyPact(PactVerificationContext context) {\n        context.verifyInteraction();\n    }\n\n    @State(\"account acc-42 exists and is active\")\n    void activeAccount() {\n        accounts.save(new AccountEntity(\"acc-42\", \"ACTIVE\", \"SEK\"));\n    }\n}","note":"Each interaction from every consumer becomes a test against the real provider; provider states are the agreed setup hooks, so the state names are part of the contract too."}],"followUps":[{"q":"Consumer-driven vs provider-driven contracts?","a":"Consumer-driven contracts (Pact) let each consumer publish exactly what it uses, and the provider verifies all of them; the provider knows which fields and endpoints are in use and can change everything else freely, which fits internal APIs with known consumers. Provider-driven contracts (OpenAPI, Spring Cloud Contract stubs published by the provider, Avro schemas) let the provider define the API and consumers test against its stubs; this fits public or widely used APIs with many unknown consumers, but the provider cannot tell which parts are safe to change, so it depends on strict versioning and deprecation. Spring Cloud Contract can also run consumer-driven, with consumers proposing contracts as pull requests to the provider repository. Many organizations combine both: schema compatibility checks for structure plus consumer-driven contracts for critical interactions."}],"extra":["**can-i-deploy and environments:** `pact-broker can-i-deploy --pacticipant payment-service --version $GIT_SHA --to-environment production` checks the verification matrix, and `record-deployment` tells the broker what runs where so providers verify against deployed and main-branch consumer versions.","**Loose matching on both sides:** consumers should assert only fields they use, and providers must tolerate unknown fields; Spring Boot's auto-configured `ObjectMapper` already disables `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES`.","**Event contracts:** for Kafka, combine message pacts or Spring Cloud Contract messaging with schema registry compatibility modes (BACKWARD or FULL for Avro and Protobuf); the registry checks structure, contracts check the semantics consumers rely on.","**Pending pacts:** a new consumer expectation can be marked pending so it reports failures to the provider without breaking its build until it has been verified once, which keeps consumer-driven workflows from blocking provider teams.","**Bi-directional option:** comparing consumer pacts with the provider's OpenAPI specification (offered by PactFlow) helps when the provider team cannot run Pact verification themselves."],"redFlags":["Treats contracts as replacements for all integration tests"]},{"id":149,"category":"Testing","question":"What are common mocking mistakes?","difficulty":"Medium","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Mock only the boundaries you do not own or cannot run cheaply and assert on observable outcomes; mocking internals, value objects and every call makes tests brittle, painful to refactor and blind to real bugs.","answer":["The common mistakes all couple the test to how the code works instead of what it does. **Mocking implementation details**: every collaborator, including internal helpers of the same module, is mocked, so the test mirrors the code, breaks on every refactor, and still passes when behavior is wrong. **Over-specifying calls**: verifying every interaction, exact argument matching, `verifyNoMoreInteractions` and `inOrder` where order does not matter, so harmless changes fail the build. **Mocking owned value objects**: `Money`, `Order`, records and DTOs are cheap to construct, and their behavior is part of what you should be testing.","Related traps: partial mocks or spies on the class under test; mocks returning mocks (a Law of Demeter smell); `any()` everywhere so wrong arguments pass; static mocks for time and UUIDs instead of injecting `Clock` and an ID generator; mocking third-party types you do not own such as `RestClient` or `KafkaTemplate`; and mocking the repository in a test whose real risk is the SQL.","The better approach: test **observable behavior** through the public API (return values, resulting state, messages sent across real boundaries). Mock or fake only the **external boundaries**: payment provider, email gateway, other services, the clock. Use `verify` only when the interaction is the behavior, such as a capture command sent to the PSP, and match only the arguments that matter. Mockito's strict stubs (the default with `MockitoExtension`) also flag unused stubbing that has gone stale."],"keyPoints":["Mocking implementation details, over-specifying calls and mocking owned value objects creates brittle tests","mock external boundaries and test observable behavior"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good test for capturing a payment","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: mocks value objects and owned pure logic, asserts implementation steps\n@Test\nvoid capture_bad() {\n    Money amount = mock(Money.class);\n    FeeCalculator fees = mock(FeeCalculator.class);\n    when(fees.feeFor(amount)).thenReturn(mock(Money.class));\n    var service = new CaptureService(fees, psp, repository);\n\n    service.capture(paymentId, amount);\n\n    InOrder order = inOrder(fees, psp, repository);\n    order.verify(fees).feeFor(amount);\n    order.verify(repository).findById(paymentId);\n    order.verify(psp).capture(any(), any());\n    verifyNoMoreInteractions(fees, psp, repository);\n}\n\n// GOOD: real domain objects, fake repository, mock only the external PSP\n@Test\nvoid capturesAuthorizedPaymentAndRecordsFee() {\n    var repository = new InMemoryPaymentRepository();\n    repository.save(Payment.authorized(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\")));\n    PaymentProvider psp = mock(PaymentProvider.class);\n    when(psp.capture(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\"))).thenReturn(CaptureResult.approved(\"psp-777\"));\n    var service = new CaptureService(new FeeCalculator(), psp, repository);\n\n    service.capture(paymentId, Money.of(\"100.00\", \"SEK\"));\n\n    Payment saved = repository.findById(paymentId).orElseThrow();\n    assertThat(saved.status()).isEqualTo(PaymentStatus.CAPTURED);\n    assertThat(saved.fee()).isEqualTo(Money.of(\"1.80\", \"SEK\"));\n    assertThat(saved.pspReference()).isEqualTo(\"psp-777\");\n}","note":"The bad test breaks if the service loads the payment before computing the fee, yet never checks the fee value or the final status. The good test survives refactoring and fails only when observable behavior changes."},{"title":"A fake: a small working implementation of a port you own","lang":"java","code":"class InMemoryPaymentRepository implements PaymentRepository {\n\n    private final Map<PaymentId, Payment> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n    @Override\n    public Payment save(Payment payment) {\n        store.put(payment.id(), payment);\n        return payment;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public Optional<Payment> findById(PaymentId id) {\n        return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id));\n    }\n}","note":"Written once and reused by many tests, it lets assertions target resulting state instead of recorded calls."}],"followUps":[{"q":"When is a fake better than a mock?","a":"A fake is a lightweight working implementation, such as an in-memory repository, an in-memory event bus or a fake PSP that keeps state. It is better when many tests share the same collaborator, when the collaborator is stateful, and when you care about the resulting state rather than a specific call: tests get shorter, survive changes to call order and read like behavior. Mocks remain better for one-off interactions, for asserting that a command crossed a boundary, and for simulating hard-to-produce failures such as a timeout on the third call. The risk with fakes is drift from the real implementation, so run one shared contract test suite (an abstract test class) against both the fake and the real adapter backed by Testcontainers."}],"extra":["**Don't mock what you don't own:** a guideline from Growing Object-Oriented Software; wrap third-party APIs behind your own port, mock or fake the port in unit tests, and test the adapter against the real thing with WireMock or Testcontainers.","**Spring context cost:** every distinct set of `@MockitoBean` or `@MockBean` fields yields a separate cached application context, so scattering them across tests multiplies startup time.","**Coverage illusion:** a mock-heavy suite can show near 100% line coverage while SQL, serialization and transactions are broken; mutation testing with PIT exposes tests that execute code without asserting on it.","**Strictness is a feature:** `UnnecessaryStubbingException` from strict stubs points to dead setup; reaching for `lenient()` should be rare and explained.","**Stub queries, verify commands:** stub calls that return data and verify only calls with side effects; verifying a call you already stubbed adds brittleness without adding information."],"redFlags":["Mocks every collaborator and asserts private interactions"]},{"id":150,"category":"Testing","question":"How do you diagnose and eliminate flaky tests?","difficulty":"Hard","mustKnow":true,"tldr":"Treat a flaky test as a real bug: quarantine and measure it, reproduce it under controlled conditions, find the nondeterminism (time, randomness, ordering, concurrency, shared state, external dependencies) and remove it instead of adding retries.","answer":["A flaky test passes and fails on the same code, and the cause is always nondeterminism. **Time**: `now()` calls, time zones, DST, midnight and month end, timeouts too tight for a slow CI runner. **Randomness**: random test data, unseeded generators, iteration order of `HashMap` and `HashSet`. **Ordering**: tests that depend on execution order, or queries without `ORDER BY`. **Concurrency**: asserting before async work finishes, `Thread.sleep` as synchronization, or real races in the code. **Shared state**: static fields, mutated singleton beans in a cached Spring context, shared database rows, fixed ports, leftover files. **External dependencies**: real networks, third-party sandboxes, containers that are not yet ready.","Diagnose with data first: CI history, failure rate, and what differs between passing and failing runs (runner, parallelism, time of day, test order). Then reproduce: run the test in a loop with `@RepeatedTest`, run it alone and inside the full suite to reveal order dependence, randomize order with a logged seed, add parallelism or CPU pressure to expose races, and bisect the suite to find the test that pollutes state.","Fix the root cause: inject `Clock`, seed and log randomness, assert order-independently, replace sleeps with Awaitility polling on an observable condition, give each test its own data, use random ports and proper container wait strategies, and stub external systems. Meanwhile quarantine the test (tagged, excluded from the blocking gate, with an owner and a ticket) so the pipeline stays trusted. Blind retries hide real race conditions that also happen in production and teach the team that red builds are normal."],"keyPoints":["Find nondeterminism from time, randomness, ordering, concurrency, shared state and external dependencies","reproduce, isolate and fix rather than blind retry"],"examples":[{"title":"Bad vs good: async assertion and wall-clock time","lang":"java","code":"// BAD: races the async listener and depends on the machine clock and zone\n@Test\nvoid publishesInvoiceEvent_flaky() throws Exception {\n    orderService.complete(orderId);\n    Thread.sleep(500);                                   // sometimes too short on CI\n    assertThat(invoiceListener.received()).hasSize(1);\n    assertThat(invoiceRepository.findByOrder(orderId).dueDate())\n        .isEqualTo(LocalDate.now().plusDays(30));        // wrong around midnight or in another zone\n}\n\n// GOOD: fixed Clock injected, bounded polling on the observable outcome\n@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)\nstatic class FixedClockConfig {\n    @Bean\n    @Primary\n    Clock testClock() {\n        // 23:59:30 UTC is already 00:59:30 on Feb 1 in Stockholm: exercises the zone edge\n        return Clock.fixed(Instant.parse(\"2026-01-31T23:59:30Z\"), ZoneId.of(\"Europe/Stockholm\"));\n    }\n}\n\n@Test\nvoid publishesInvoiceEventWithDueDateInThirtyDays() {\n    orderService.complete(orderId);\n\n    await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10))\n        .untilAsserted(() -> assertThat(invoiceListener.received()).hasSize(1));\n    assertThat(invoiceRepository.findByOrder(orderId).dueDate())\n        .isEqualTo(LocalDate.of(2026, 3, 3));\n}","note":"The service must call `LocalDate.now(clock)`, never the no-argument variant. Awaitility returns as soon as the event arrives, so the good test is both faster on average and robust on a slow runner."},{"title":"Exposing order dependence and shared state","lang":"properties","code":"# src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties\n# Randomize class and method order; the seed is logged, reuse it to reproduce a failure\njunit.jupiter.testclass.order.default=org.junit.jupiter.api.ClassOrderer$Random\njunit.jupiter.testmethod.order.default=org.junit.jupiter.api.MethodOrderer$Random\njunit.jupiter.execution.order.random.seed=1726823411\n# Run concurrently to surface static state, shared rows and fixed ports\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=true\njunit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default=concurrent","note":"Running in a random but reproducible order turns hidden order dependence into a deterministic failure you can bisect."}],"followUps":[{"q":"How would you test time-dependent code?","a":"Make time an explicit dependency: inject `java.time.Clock` (as a Spring bean) and always call `Instant.now(clock)` or `LocalDate.now(clock)`, never the no-argument versions. In unit tests use `Clock.fixed(...)` for exact assertions, or a small mutable test clock with an `advance(Duration)` method to test expiry, TTLs and schedules without sleeping. Choose instants that hit edges: midnight in the business time zone, DST transitions, month end, leap day and year boundaries. For scheduled jobs, keep the `@Scheduled` method thin and test the job logic directly with the clock; for reactive timeouts, use virtual time such as Reactor's `StepVerifier.withVirtualTime` instead of real waiting."}],"extra":["**Detect, don't hide:** Surefire's `rerunFailingTestsCount` and Gradle's test-retry plugin report tests that pass on rerun as flaky; use that signal to feed a flake dashboard, not as a permanent cover.","**Transactional rollback hides behavior:** `@Transactional` tests roll back, which isolates data but skips commit-time behavior such as deferred constraints, triggers and `@TransactionalEventListener` handlers that run after commit.","**Unordered SQL results:** PostgreSQL guarantees no row order without `ORDER BY`, and the order can change after updates, vacuum or with parallel plans; use `containsExactlyInAnyOrder` or add the `ORDER BY`.","**Time zone in CI:** runners usually run in UTC while developers sit in CET, so set the JVM zone explicitly (`-Duser.timezone`) and periodically run the suite in a non-UTC zone to flush out hidden assumptions.","**Context pollution:** tests that mutate singleton beans or static state leak into Spring's cached context; `@DirtiesContext` fixes it at a large speed cost, so prefer not mutating shared beans at all."],"redFlags":["Adds retries and accepts flakiness as normal"]}];
